4a8d4e67-4acf-45b7-b162-82231152f0c2	xCyPbFx0Ktg	conference	2025-04-24	{"Jameson Lopp",Shinobi}	{}	{}	## Intro & Casa rebrand\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHey everybody, I'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine sitting down with Jamison Lop from Casa.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:05\n\nDid you guys drop the huddle officially?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:07\n\nWe're still trying to get the Casa X handle without the Huddle.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:12\n\nMaybe someday.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:14\n\nBut yeah, so to start, I guess just wanted to pick Jamison's brain about a new type of address poisoning attack to get their hands on your Bitcoin.\n\n\n\n## Bitcoin address poisoning attack explained\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:24\n\nEven though you won't see it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:26\n\nUntil after he presents officially at the conference shortly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:29\n\nHe talked about it here first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nSo I guess you wanna just kind of break down the high level of that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:35\n\nYeah, sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:36\n\nSo in January, I actually saw a post from Mono Naut who pointed out a transactional admin pool space block explorer that basically looked like an address poisoning attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:51\n\nAnd what does that actually mean?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nWell it means that someone is sending a small amount of dust, you know, 500 to 1,000 Satoshis from an address that they generated, Vanity generated, that has the same starting and ending characters as the target address that they're sending it to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nAnd why are they doing this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:14\n\nWell, effectively, they're hoping to trick the target recipient to send money to their address rather than to the actual target recipient's address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:26\n\nAnd how would this happen?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nIt would basically require them to be using wallet software that made it really easy for them to go back in their transaction history and accidentally copy and paste the wrong address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:40\n\nAs you can imagine, this is a speculative kind of social engineering attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:46\n\nSo you have to expect the vast majority of these attacks will fail.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:51\n\nBut if you're only spending 1,000 sats per try, then you could probably get away with a pretty low success rate if, for example, you trick someone into sending you several Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:04\n\nYeah, it's kind of just like pepper it around and you know see what comes back.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nLike it's not like generating the address or dusting it's costing anything really practical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nYeah, so pay very close attention to the address that you enter in the send to field when you make a transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:21\n\nYeah, I mean there's several very easy ways you can prevent this from ever happening.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:24\n\nFirst, don't reuse Bitcoin addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:27\n\nThat's a very easy one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nSecond, Don't copy-paste addresses from your transaction history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:33\n\nSo, you know, some services and tools and stuff offer address whitelisting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:38\n\nThat would be another way to get around doing this to make sure that you're not shooting yourself in the foot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:43\n\nLet's dive into the sticky fun topic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:46\n\nSo, no surprise in this space, but the topic of conversations and what the next soft work is going to be is highly contentious.\n\n\n\n## Easy ways to protect yourself\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nSo um you know w what what do you think in terms of just the general conversational dynamic is a practical way to try at least to move forward with things?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:09\n\nBecause it seems to me like everybody is just in a Mexican standoff with completely different priorities, completely different analysis of like trade-offs between different proposals.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:20\n\nLike nobody can really agree on anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:23\n\nSo like where do we go from here?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:25\n\nYeah, I think part of the problem right now is that there's too many proposals, they're spread too thin, and there's a lot of overlap between some of the proposals.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:34\n\nSo I think it would behoove some of the soft fork proponents who are interested in getting a proposal implemented that Has overlap with the functionality of other proposals to try to consolidate.\n\n\n\n## The gridlock in Bitcoin protocol development\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:53\n\nAnd of course, this is going to require, I think, people to, some people to give up on what they're doing and kind of put their support behind another thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:01\n\nBut that's mainly, I think, why we don't really have enough velocity for any one given thing right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:09\n\nI myself, I would be fine with seeing multiple proposals go through.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:15\n\nIt's actually kind of funny to me because I remember sitting in the room in Hong Kong in 2017, I think when Peter Woola was announcing how we were actually improving the soft forking process so that we could have dozens of soft forked proposals in flight at any given time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:32\n\nAnd of course, here we are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:34\n\nUm, eight years later, and it's hard enough just to have a single proposal in flight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nYeah, kind of like uh how TapScript uh had all of these nice unassigned opcodes to fill with things that uh aren't getting told.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:50\n\nSo, part of the kind of contention that I see at least is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:58\n\nKind of completely contradictory motivations from different parties.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:02\n\nLike some people putting forward proposals are trying to think very conservative and incremental.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:09\n\nLike here are a few basic primitives to work with that are very constrained, they only do like the single thing they're designed to do, and are kind of trying to push forward with that incremental attitude.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:22\n\nWhereas a lot of the response to that that I'm seeing, the core of the criticism is that like these incremental improvements aren't really doing enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:32\n\nAnd so why should we spend the time and the coordination effort and all the development resources to actually push a fork through for something that's just a tiny incremental change?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:43\n\nAnd so like these are just two completely different philosophies of how to approach this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:48\n\nWe should wait until we actually have something that massively improves functionality versus the step-by-step incrementalism.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:56\n\nAnd I'm not really sure how those two philosophies can kind of reconcile.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:01\n\nThey're completely like polar opposed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:04\n\nYeah, I mean, the idea of having an incredibly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:09\n\nSmall change is that theoretically it should be in a better position to make it through the Bitcoin improvement proposal gauntlet because it should be easier to review.\n\n\n\n## Incrementalism vs. big sweeping upgrades\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:22\n\nIt should be simpler to understand from an adversarial perspective of how it might be abused, how it might cause harm in some way if it's used for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nUnanticipated things, whereas the more generic stuff just has a much broader potential attack surface and is just much more complicated to think through what could potentially go wrong.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:48\n\nBut the flip side of that is that, as should be very obvious now, it's an incredibly long and arduous process to get anything through.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nSo maybe it is better to have a Broader proposal where you could add 10 different major pieces of functionality in one fell swoop rather than adding one every few years and us being here decades later, still trying to get some other basic functionality added.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:20\n\nI think part of the broader kind of issue, I kind of think of this in layers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:26\n\nIf we're discussing the developments, Scope of things, like people actually working on the protocol, contributing, like that has its own problems here in terms of different attitudes, like different philosophies to approach things from in the first place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:41\n\nBut then we also have the wider problem of the ecosystem beyond.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:46\n\nLike eventually, if we can address all these issues and get developers on the same page, whatever that is, to move forward, there's still the necessity to.\napproach and kind of sell the entire rest of the ecosystem on or something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:01\n\nBut right now you have this very ill educated, I would say, attitude that Bitcoin doesn't need to be improved, that our victory is guaranteed no matter what we do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:15\n\nAnd so why even bother kind of having these conversations or moving towards this in the first place?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nSo how do we address that problem if we can get past the first level of conflict between developers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nI think a lot of this complexity and difficulty to understand who are the stakeholders who need to be in the room, having these conversations, can also be difficult because I suspect the vast majority of Bitcoin holders, first of all, don't actually participate in anything, not even social media stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:54\n\nMost people that are into Bitcoin, they've just got their Bitcoin and they're living their life.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:58\n\nThen there's the broad set of people who are into Bitcoin and they're into it deep enough that it's a part of their personality.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nIt's a part of their social network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:11\n\nAnd so they talk about it all the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:14\n\nAnd these people may buy into any number of different narratives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:20\n\nI would say, unfortunately, some of the more broadly adopted narratives tend to be the simpler and more naive ones, the things that are really catchy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nBitcoin is inevitable type of stuff, which I I wrote about a bit in my breakdown of Bitcoin maximalism a few years ago, how we've gotten to that point.\n\n\n\n## Narrative cycles and ideological dilution\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:47\n\nEssentially, each new cohort that comes into the space generally is brought in by a new set of narratives from the previous cohort.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:57\n\nSo you could argue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:59\n\nThat Michael Saylor and the sort of corporate adoption of Bitcoin is one of the strongest narratives that's happening right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:05\n\nAnd these people are, by all accounts, the newbies in the Bitcoin space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:11\n\nAnd so they've come at it from a different perspective, and they bring their own ideas and their own catchphrases.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:18\n\nAnd then those things kind of matriculate throughout the ecosystem and the world, and then eventually bring in.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:24\n\nThe next cohort.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:26\n\nAnd I think that this is true of really any mainstream adoption of anything: is that the early adopters are the really sophisticated, nerdy, or more ideological folks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd then as they improve the technology or good or service or whatever, that brings in another cohort that's less sophisticated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:47\n\nAnd they come in because it's more user-friendly and they can understand it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:52\n\nCycle continues to repeat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nSo then you eventually have to ask yourself: Are we going to get to a sort of idiocracy-level mainstream adoption?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:00\n\nAnd it very well may be inevitable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:03\n\nAnd do the later cohorts of people who aren't as nuanced in their opinions or may not have the same set of ideals or interests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nHow much does their opinion matter, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nAnd so I think a lot of that then can explain why, you know, there was this really long report that came out, I think, a few months ago, that was trying to explain the governance of Bitcoin and who all the stakeholders are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nThe 1A, 1Z development funding report?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:35\n\nI think so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nAnd so that was interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:39\n\nI think that you can mostly dumb it down to if you look at what happened in the fork wars, it's the economic power is really what matters if you're talking about consensus changes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:51\n\nAnd so then you have to ask yourself, well, if the vast majority of economic power is actually shifting into the hands of a few large corporations and institutions.\n\n\n\n## Corporate power and protocol ossification\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:02\n\nHow is that going to change the governance and the future evolution of the protocol?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:07\n\nIt very well may be that those institutions don't want to see any changes because they don't really care about any of the cypherpunk aspects and the individual sovereignty of Bitcoin holders and trying to scale that sovereignty out to the entire world.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:22\n\nYeah, I mean, this is something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:25\n\nI think it is kind of at the heart of this dynamic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:28\n\nAnd it really kind of frustrates and scares me a little bit to see how many developers either seem to not care about this dynamic or seem to even just dismiss that this is an empirically observable dynamic occurring right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:47\n\nBecause that transition to larger corporate powers and You know, service providers as the economically dominant actor on the network, I see that as a massive existential risk to Bitcoin because it is their natural incentive to simply monetize shortcomings of things into services.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:09\n\nAnd when you're talking about an open protocol like Bitcoin, whose whole purpose was to be this open access, decentralized, censorship-resistant network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:18\n\nThere, if you want the economic benefits of the asset and that use of Bitcoin in that way doesn't scale, like this naturally creates an incentive for rent seeking and just capture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:33\n\nBecause if they become the economic dominant actor, they Spin up all these services to monetize the shortcomings and the failings of the protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:42\n\nWhat incentive would they ever have in the world to adopt any type of change that would alter that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:49\n\nLike that disrupts their ability to just sit back comfortably and rent-seek on these shortcomings.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:55\n\nAnd really, what you've described to a T is what happened with SMTP, with the email protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nAnd And that can be difficult to talk about as well, because I think if you ask the average person, are you an email user?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nThey'll say, oh, yeah, you know, I use email all the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nWell, actually, you don't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:15\n\nYou're using a trusted third party who then uses SMTP on your behalf, and they are a gatekeeper of really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:24\n\nA number of layers of meta protocols that are enforced at more of a social layer between the dozen or so gatekeepers that control like 95% of all email traffic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:35\n\nYeah, it's impossible to essentially spin up your own SMTP server right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:39\n\nIt'll be blacklisted and ignored by any major domain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nSo, like that, that network effect like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:47\n\nIs moted off.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nLike anybody who wants to do that, 95% of the people using email are behind these few service providers that refuse to accept messages from lesser-known services just as a denial of service protection.\n\n\n\n## Bitcoin vs. the SMTP trap\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:02\n\nAnd then you can't even join this network effect anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:06\n\nYeah, and I think that people don't realize how easily that could happen to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:12\n\nIf you have a handful of corporations that are the custodians and a handful of corporations that are the miners, and if most of them are in America and the American government, which is actually getting really interested in Bitcoin right now, decides that it's in their best interest to sort of push the finger on the scale, it's It's not hard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:36\n\nI mean, corporations have to comply with the governments that are in charge of whatever jurisdiction that they operate out of.\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:46\n\nI mean, especially a public company, there is just absolutely no legal recourse.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:52\n\nLike, I mean, at least with a private company, like, they are capable of internally making decisions that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:00\n\nEntail taking on risks of the government penalizing them or punishing them, but that's just not possible in the structure of a public company.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:08\n\nAnd every company in this space that isn't already public is eyeballing their path to going public.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:14\n\nYeah, and I mean, this is something that I thought about when I was starting CASA.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:18\n\nYou know, we are a legal entity, you know, we have attorneys, and even though we are not a regulated financial Entity, we pay attorneys a lot of money to try to navigate through a very gray area of the legal code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:33\n\nAnd it's always, you know, certainly before the most recent administration, it was always possible that the hammer was going to come down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nAnd, you know, the government can force us to do pretty much whatever they want.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nAnd so, from CASA's perspective, our main safeguard is that even if the government Put a gun to our heads.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:54\n\nWe can't take our clients' money because we simply don't have the keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:58\n\nYeah, it's yeah, I feel like we've kinda actually I feel like the trajectory we just took in this conversation is actually the the perfect trajectory for unfamiliar people to really think through these issues and how they relate, like the internal development process, the wider ecosystem and the the risk of capture there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:17\n\nBut it's like this to me kind of lays out an environment where we have a gun to our head and a ticking clock.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:26\n\nLike that, this is not a situation where we can just sit back and calmly observe and analyze things to act whenever we feel like getting around to it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:36\n\nLike there is a deadline, even if it is ambiguous and not clearly defined.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:41\n\nWe are asymptotically approaching a point where this dynamic of corporate capture will come.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:49\n\nAnd if we do not improve things at the protocol level to the point where we can keep building structures that tend more to a decentralized form versus just falling into these pressures of centralization, there is no way to fix that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:05\n\nWe need the building blocks to accomplish that before we get to that point, or there's no way to wind it back.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:12\n\nYeah, and this is why I started a self-custody company, even though it's not the best business model.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:22\n\nRunning a custody company is the best way to make money in this space, generally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:27\n\nBut I felt like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:31\n\nWe're fighting against human nature.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:33\n\nAnd if we didn't put a lot of resources into trying to make self-custody more user-friendly, then the inevitable outcome is that almost everybody goes to a third-party custodian.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:44\n\nAnd that creates systemic risk within the system and weaknesses that we've been talking about here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:50\n\nAnd really, the open question right now is: are we doing enough?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:56\n\nIt doesn't seem to be going in the right direction.\n\n\n\n## Misleading on-chain metrics\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:00\n\nAnother thing that I have a big problem with is that a lot of people on social media keep posting these charts about how the amount of Bitcoin held at exchanges is going down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:12\n\nAnd that metric is terribly flawed by design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:16\n\nThe way the metric works, it always looks like it's going down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:19\n\nBut then the metric gets updated in hindsight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:21\n\nAnd if you go back and look at it from like a year or two in the past after it's been updated, you see actually, no, the number didn't go down, it might have actually gone up just due to the nature of how chain analysis and trying to understand ownership of addresses works.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:36\n\nYeah, I mean, you know, especially like you have so much institutional buying pressure these days, there's no way to kind of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:45\n\nReally know like something left to custodians' control.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:48\n\nLike, if you have a lot of these institutions who are moving their funds into segregated accounts at these custodians, like coming out of the general pool of the hot wallet and what's actually in their order books, like that looks like it's leaving the exchange, but it's really just going to a different address of the custodian.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:07\n\nWe kind of have a lot of work ahead of us this year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:11\n\nWe actually want the things that make Bitcoin important and valuable to us to remain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:18\n\nWell, you know, the most that we can do is continue to propose improvements.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:23\n\nAnd then the real question is whether or not ultimately the game theory of Bitcoin plays out in our favor, or it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:35\n\nIt may be a problem of just human coordination.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:43\n\nAnd I've been talking about ossification for a long time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:46\n\nWe all understand that as networks grow, the coordination problem eventually becomes intractable to the point that no one can agree on anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nAnd so a lot of.\nof us who have been around for a while now are basically asking, are we already past that point?\n\n\n\n## Why apathy is Bitcoin’s biggest risk\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:03\n\nWe won't really know for sure until we have sufficient hindsight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:07\n\nBut I think actually Jeremy Rubin just posted a few minutes ago how between the past several major soft forks, there was like 1390 days between forks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:17\n\nAnd we we just passed 1390 days, I think, since the taproot fork and we don't even have a real proposal on the table for activation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:26\n\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nYou know, despite the uh sailor driven mantra on Twitter, like this is not guaranteed and it's definitely not going to be easy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:36\n\nBut if anybody out there actually cares about this staying a censorship resistant network that anybody can access, like we we need to do something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:47\n\nTo move forward.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:48\n\nYes, I think caring is actually the main point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:52\n\nI think I've said for a long time: when people have asked me what is the greatest long-term risk to Bitcoin, I say apathy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nIf we do nothing, yeah, Bitcoin keeps moving on and blocks keep coming through, but the rest of the world doesn't ossify.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:09\n\nThe rest of the world is gonna continue changing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:12\n\nAnd other entities throughout the world, like nation states, are going to start exerting pressure and poking and seeing what control can they have over this network to their own advantage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:26\n\nThat's, I think, the perfect way to say it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:29\n\nThe internal game theory of the network is in a way a result of its technical architecture and how that interacts with the real world.\n\n\n\n## Final thoughts: participate, don’t parrot\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nAnd so as the real world evolves and changes, if we want to maintain that same game theoretic equilibrium point, like Bitcoin itself also has to change and stuff with the world around it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:51\n\nOr like that equilibrium is just going to shift because the other part of the equation changed, even though Bitcoin stayed the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:58\n\nBitcoin is far from perfect.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:02\n\nThere's a ton of room of improvement for security, privacy, scalability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:08\n\nAnd I don't think that we should rest upon our laurels and say, oh, we've done so well to this point and success is guaranteed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:18\n\nYeah, if anything, success is going to be harder to achieve the bigger this gets.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:23\n\nWell I guess um this has been a fun conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:26\n\nDo you have any uh fun and depressing conversation?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:30\n\nThey aren't necessarily mutually exclusive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nBut um did you have any parting words of wisdom for the viewers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:36\n\nUh well, like I said, uh care, participate, um interact with people, continue educating yourself, uh don't just blindly repeat mantras and expect that that is gonna somehow be helpful for Bitcoin's long-term health.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:52\n\nI don't think there's a better word you could have left us with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:55\n\nThanks for sitting down, Jameson.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:56\n\nYou bet.	## Intro & Casa rebrand\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHey everybody, I'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine sitting down with Jameson Lopp from Casa.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:05\n\nDid you guys drop the handle officially?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:07\n\nWe're still trying to get the Casa X handle without the Huddle.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:12\n\nMaybe someday.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:14\n\nBut yeah, so to start, I guess just wanted to pick Jameson's brain about a new type of address poisoning attack to get their hands on your Bitcoin.\n\n\n\n## Bitcoin address poisoning attack explained\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:24\n\nEven though you won't see it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:26\n\nUntil after he presents officially at the conference shortly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:29\n\nHe talked about it here first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nSo I guess you wanna just kind of break down the high level of that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:35\n\nYeah, sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:36\n\nSo in January, I actually saw a post from Mononut who pointed out a transactional admin pool space block explorer that basically looked like an address poisoning attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:51\n\nAnd what does that actually mean?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nWell it means that someone is sending a small amount of dust, you know, 500 to 1,000 Satoshis from an address that they generated, Vanity generated, that has the same starting and ending characters as the target address that they're sending it to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nAnd why are they doing this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:14\n\nWell, effectively, they're hoping to trick the target recipient to send money to their address rather than to the actual target recipient's address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:26\n\nAnd how would this happen?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nIt would basically require them to be using wallet software that made it really easy for them to go back in their transaction history and accidentally copy and paste the wrong address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:40\n\nAs you can imagine, this is a speculative kind of social engineering attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:46\n\nSo you have to expect the vast majority of these attacks will fail.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:51\n\nBut if you're only spending 1,000 sats per try, then you could probably get away with a pretty low success rate if, for example, you trick someone into sending you several Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:04\n\nYeah, it's kind of just like pepper it around and you know see what comes back.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nLike it's not like generating the address or dusting it's costing anything really practical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nYeah, so pay very close attention to the address that you enter in the send to field when you make a transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:21\n\nYeah, I mean there's several very easy ways you can prevent this from ever happening.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:24\n\nFirst, don't reuse Bitcoin addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:27\n\nThat's a very easy one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nSecond, Don't copy-paste addresses from your transaction history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:33\n\nSo, you know, some services and tools and stuff offer address whitelisting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:38\n\nThat would be another way to get around doing this to make sure that you're not shooting yourself in the foot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:43\n\nLet's dive into the sticky fun topic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:46\n\nSo, no surprise in this space, but the topic of consensus and what the next soft fork is going to be is highly contentious.\n\n\n\n## Easy ways to protect yourself\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nSo um you know w what what do you think in terms of just the general conversational dynamic is a practical way to try at least to move forward with things?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:09\n\nBecause it seems to me like everybody is just in a Mexican standoff with completely different priorities, completely different analysis of like trade-offs between different proposals.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:20\n\nLike nobody can really agree on anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:23\n\nSo like where do we go from here?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:25\n\nYeah, I think part of the problem right now is that there's too many proposals, they're spread too thin, and there's a lot of overlap between some of the proposals.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:34\n\nSo I think it would behoove some of the soft fork proponents who are interested in getting a proposal implemented that has overlap with the functionality of other proposals to try to consolidate.\n\n\n\n## The gridlock in Bitcoin protocol development\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:53\n\nAnd of course, this is going to require, I think, people to, some people to give up on what they're doing and kind of put their support behind another thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:01\n\nBut that's mainly, I think, why we don't really have enough velocity for any one given thing right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:09\n\nI myself, I would be fine with seeing multiple proposals go through.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:15\n\nIt's actually kind of funny to me because I remember sitting in the room in Hong Kong in 2017, I think when Peter Wuill was announcing how we were actually improving the soft-forking process so that we could have dozens of soft-forked proposals in flight at any given time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:32\n\nAnd of course, here we are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:34\n\nUm, eight years later, and it's hard enough just to have a single proposal in flight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nYeah, kind of like uh how TapScript uh had all of these nice unused opcodes to fill with things that aren't getting touched.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:50\n\nSo, part of the kind of contention that I see at least is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:58\n\nKind of completely contradictory motivations from different parties.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:02\n\nLike some people putting forward proposals are trying to think very conservative and incremental.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:09\n\nLike here are a few basic primitives to work with that are very constrained, they only do like the single thing they're designed to do, and are kind of trying to push forward with that incremental attitude.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:22\n\nWhereas a lot of the response to that that I'm seeing, the core of the criticism is that like these incremental improvements aren't really doing enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:32\n\nAnd so why should we spend the time and the coordination effort and all the development resources to actually push a fork through for something that's just a tiny incremental change?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:43\n\nAnd so like these are just two completely different philosophies of how to approach this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:48\n\nWe should wait until we actually have something that massively improves functionality versus the step-by-step incrementalism.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:56\n\nAnd I'm not really sure how those two philosophies can kind of reconcile.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:01\n\nThey're completely like polar opposed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:04\n\nYeah, I mean, the idea of having an incredibly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:09\n\nSmall change is that theoretically it should be in a better position to make it through the Bitcoin Improvement Proposal gauntlet because it should be easier to review.\n\n## Incrementalism vs. big sweeping upgrades\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:22\n\nIt should be simpler to understand from an adversarial perspective of how it might be abused, how it might cause harm in some way if it's used for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nUnanticipated things, whereas the more generic stuff just has a much broader potential attack surface and is just much more complicated to think through what could potentially go wrong.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:48\n\nBut the flip side of that is that, as should be very obvious now, it's an incredibly long and arduous process to get anything through.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nSo maybe it is better to have a Broader proposal where you could add 10 different major pieces of functionality in one fell swoop rather than adding one every few years and us being here decades later, still trying to get some other basic functionality added.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:20\n\nI think part of the broader kind of issue, I kind of think of this in layers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:26\n\nIf we're discussing the developments, Scope of things, like people actually working on the protocol, contributing, like that has its own problems here in terms of different attitudes, like different philosophies to approach things from in the first place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:41\n\nBut then we also have the wider problem of the ecosystem beyond.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:46\n\nLike eventually, if we can address all these issues and get developers on the same page, whatever that is, to move forward, there's still the necessity to.\napproach and kind of sell the entire rest of the ecosystem on or something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:01\n\nBut right now you have this very ill educated, I would say, attitude that Bitcoin doesn't need to be improved, that our victory is guaranteed no matter what we do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:15\n\nAnd so why even bother kind of having these conversations or moving towards this in the first place?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nSo how do we address that problem if we can get past the first level of conflict between developers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nI think a lot of this complexity and difficulty to understand who are the stakeholders who need to be in the room, having these conversations, can also be difficult because I suspect the vast majority of Bitcoin holders, first of all, don't actually participate in anything, not even social media stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:54\n\nMost people that are into Bitcoin, they've just got their Bitcoin and they're living their life.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:58\n\nThen there's the broad set of people who are into Bitcoin and they're into it deep enough that it's a part of their personality.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nIt's a part of their social network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:11\n\nAnd so they talk about it all the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:14\n\nAnd these people may buy into any number of different narratives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:20\n\nI would say, unfortunately, some of the more broadly adopted narratives tend to be the simpler and more naive ones, the things that are really catchy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nBitcoin is inevitable type of stuff, which I I wrote about a bit in my breakdown of Bitcoin maximalism a few years ago, how we've gotten to that point.\n\n\n\n## Narrative cycles and ideological dilution\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:47\n\nEssentially, each new cohort that comes into the space generally is brought in by a new set of narratives from the previous cohort.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:57\n\nSo you could argue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:59\n\nThat Michael Saylor and the sort of corporate adoption of Bitcoin is one of the strongest narratives that's happening right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:05\n\nAnd these people are, by all accounts, the newbies in the Bitcoin space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:11\n\nAnd so they've come at it from a different perspective, and they bring their own ideas and their own catchphrases.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:18\n\nAnd then those things kind of permeate throughout the ecosystem and the world, and then eventually bring in.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:24\n\nThe next cohort.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:26\n\nAnd I think that this is true of really any mainstream adoption of anything: is that the early adopters are the really sophisticated, nerdy, or more ideological folks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd then as they improve the technology or good or service or whatever, that brings in another cohort that's less sophisticated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:47\n\nAnd they come in because it's more user-friendly and they can understand it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:52\n\nCycle continues to repeat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nSo then you eventually have to ask yourself: Are we going to get to a sort of idiocracy-level mainstream adoption?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:00\n\nAnd it very well may be inevitable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:03\n\nAnd do the later cohorts of people who aren't as nuanced in their opinions or may not have the same set of ideals or interests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nHow much does their opinion matter, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nAnd so I think a lot of that then can explain why, you know, there was this really long report that came out, I think, a few months ago, that was trying to explain the governance of Bitcoin and who all the stakeholders are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nThe P2P Foundation?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:35\n\nI think so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nAnd so that was interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:39\n\nI think that you can mostly dumb it down to if you look at what happened in the fork wars, it's the economic power is really what matters if you're talking about consensus changes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:51\n\nAnd so then you have to ask yourself, well, if the vast majority of economic power is actually shifting into the hands of a few large corporations and institutions.\n\n\n\n## Corporate power and protocol ossification\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:02\n\nHow is that going to change the governance and the future evolution of the protocol?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:07\n\nIt very well may be that those institutions don't want to see any changes because they don't really care about any of the cypherpunk aspects and the individual sovereignty of Bitcoin holders and trying to scale that sovereignty out to the entire world.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:22\n\nYeah, I mean, this is something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:25\n\nI think it is kind of at the heart of this dynamic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:28\n\nAnd it really kind of frustrates and scares me a little bit to see how many developers either seem to not care about this dynamic or seem to even just dismiss that this is an empirically observable dynamic occurring right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:47\n\nBecause that transition to larger corporate powers and you know, service providers as the economically dominant actor on the network, I see that as a massive existential risk to Bitcoin because it is their natural incentive to simply monetize shortcomings of things into services.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:09\n\nAnd when you're talking about an open protocol like Bitcoin, whose whole purpose was to be this open access, decentralized, censorship-resistant network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:18\n\nThere, if you want the economic benefits of the asset and that use of Bitcoin in that way doesn't scale, like this naturally creates an incentive for rent seeking and just capture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:33\n\nBecause if they become the economic dominant actor, they spin up all these services to monetize the shortcomings and the failings of the protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:42\n\nWhat incentive would they ever have in the world to adopt any type of change that would alter that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:49\n\nLike that disrupts their ability to just sit back comfortably and rent-seek on these shortcomings.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:55\n\nAnd really, what you've described to a T is what happened with SMTP, with the email protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nAnd And that can be difficult to talk about as well, because I think if you ask the average person, are you an email user?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nThey'll say, oh, yeah, you know, I use email all the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nWell, actually, you don't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:15\n\nYou're using a trusted third party who then uses SMTP on your behalf, and they are a gatekeeper of really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:24\n\nA number of layers of meta protocols that are enforced at more of a social layer between the dozen or so gatekeepers that control like 95% of all email traffic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:35\n\nYeah, it's impossible to essentially spin up your own SMTP server right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:39\n\nIt'll be blacklisted and ignored by any major domain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nSo, like that, that network effect like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:47\n\nIs mooted off.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nLike anybody who wants to do that, 95% of the people using email are behind these few service providers that refuse to accept messages from lesser-known services just as a denial of service protection.\n\n## Bitcoin vs. the SMTP trap\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:02\n\nAnd then you can't even join this network effect anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:06\n\nYeah, and I think that people don't realize how easily that could happen to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:12\n\nIf you have a handful of corporations that are the custodians and a handful of corporations that are the miners, and if most of them are in America and the American government, which is actually getting really interested in Bitcoin right now, decides that it's in their best interest to sort of push the finger on the scale, it's not hard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:36\n\nI mean, corporations have to comply with the governments that are in charge of whatever jurisdiction that they operate out of.\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:46\n\nI mean, especially a public company, there is just absolutely no legal recourse.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:52\n\nLike, I mean, at least with a private company, like, they are capable of internally making decisions that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:00\n\nEntail taking on risks of the government penalizing them or punishing them, but that's just not possible in the structure of a public company.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:08\n\nAnd every company in this space that isn't already public is eyeballing their path to going public.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:14\n\nYeah, and I mean, this is something that I thought about when I was starting CASA.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:18\n\nYou know, we are a legal entity, you know, we have attorneys, and even though we are not a regulated financial Entity, we pay attorneys a lot of money to try to navigate through a very gray area of the legal code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:33\n\nAnd it's always, you know, certainly before the most recent administration, it was always possible that the hammer was going to come down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nAnd, you know, the government can force us to do pretty much whatever they want.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nAnd so, from CASA's perspective, our main safeguard is that even if the government put a gun to our heads.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:54\n\nWe can't take our clients' money because we simply don't have the keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:58\n\nYeah, it's yeah, I feel like we've kinda actually I feel like the trajectory we just took in this conversation is actually the perfect trajectory for unfamiliar people to really think through these issues and how they relate, like the internal development process, the wider ecosystem and the risk of capture there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:17\n\nBut it's like this to me kind of lays out an environment where we have a gun to our head and a ticking clock.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:26\n\nLike that, this is not a situation where we can just sit back and calmly observe and analyze things to act whenever we feel like getting around to it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:36\n\nLike there is a deadline, even if it is ambiguous and not clearly defined.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:41\n\nWe are asymptotically approaching a point where this dynamic of corporate capture will come.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:49\n\nAnd if we do not improve things at the protocol level to the point where we can keep building structures that tend more to a decentralized form versus just falling into these pressures of centralization, there is no way to fix that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:05\n\nWe need the building blocks to accomplish that before we get to that point, or there's no way to wind it back.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:12\n\nYeah, and this is why I started a self-custody company, even though it's not the best business model.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:22\n\nRunning a custody company is the best way to make money in this space, generally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:27\n\nBut I felt like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:31\n\nWe're fighting against human nature.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:33\n\nAnd if we didn't put a lot of resources into trying to make self-custody more user-friendly, then the inevitable outcome is that almost everybody goes to a third-party custodian.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:44\n\nAnd that creates systemic risk within the system and weaknesses that we've been talking about here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:50\n\nAnd really, the open question right now is: are we doing enough?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:56\n\nIt doesn't seem to be going in the right direction.\n\n\n\n## Misleading on-chain metrics\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:00\n\nAnother thing that I have a big problem with is that a lot of people on social media keep posting these charts about how the amount of Bitcoin held at exchanges is going down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:12\n\nAnd that metric is terribly flawed by design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:16\n\nThe way the metric works, it always looks like it's going down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:19\n\nBut then the metric gets updated in hindsight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:21\n\nAnd if you go back and look at it from like a year or two in the past after it's been updated, you see actually, no, the number didn't go down, it might have actually gone up just due to the nature of how chain analysis and trying to understand ownership of addresses works.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:36\n\nYeah, I mean, you know, especially like you have so much institutional buying pressure these days, there's no way to kind of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:45\n\nReally know like something left to custodians' control.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:48\n\nLike, if you have a lot of these institutions who are moving their funds into segregated accounts at these custodians, like coming out of the general pool of the hot wallet and what's actually in their order books, like that looks like it's leaving the exchange, but it's really just going to a different address of the custodian.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:07\n\nWe kind of have a lot of work ahead of us this year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:11\n\nWe actually want the things that make Bitcoin important and valuable to us to remain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:18\n\nWell, you know, the most that we can do is continue to propose improvements.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:23\n\nAnd then the real question is whether or not ultimately the game theory of Bitcoin plays out in our favor, or it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:35\n\nIt may be a problem of just human coordination.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:43\n\nAnd I've been talking about ossification for a long time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:46\n\nWe all understand that as networks grow, the coordination problem eventually becomes intractable to the point that no one can agree on anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nAnd so a lot of.\nof us who have been around for a while now are basically asking, are we already past that point?\n\n\n\n## Why apathy is Bitcoin's biggest risk\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:03\n\nWe won't really know for sure until we have sufficient hindsight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:07\n\nBut I think actually Jeremy Rubin just posted a few minutes ago how between the past several major soft forks, there was like 1390 days between forks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:17\n\nAnd we we just passed 1390 days, I think, since the taproot fork and we don't even have a real proposal on the table for activation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:26\n\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nYou know, despite the Sailor driven mantra on Twitter, like this is not guaranteed and it's definitely not going to be easy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:36\n\nBut if anybody out there actually cares about this staying a censorship resistant network that anybody can access, like we we need to do something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:47\n\nTo move forward.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:48\n\nYes, I think caring is actually the main point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:52\n\nI think I've said for a long time: when people have asked me what is the greatest long-term risk to Bitcoin, I say apathy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nIf we do nothing, yeah, Bitcoin keeps moving on and blocks keep coming through, but the rest of the world doesn't ossify.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:09\n\nThe rest of the world is gonna continue changing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:12\n\nAnd other entities throughout the world, like nation states, are going to start exerting pressure and poking and seeing what control can they have over this network to their own advantage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:26\n\nThat's, I think, the perfect way to say it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:29\n\nThe internal game theory of the network is in a way a result of its technical architecture and how that interacts with the real world.\n\n\n\n## Final thoughts: participate, don't parrot\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nAnd so as the real world evolves and changes, if we want to maintain that same game theoretic equilibrium point, like Bitcoin itself also has to change and stuff with the world around it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:51\n\nOr like that equilibrium is just going to shift because the other part of the equation changed, even though Bitcoin stayed the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:58\n\nBitcoin is far from perfect.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:02\n\nThere's a ton of room of improvement for security, privacy, scalability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:08\n\nAnd I don't think that we should rest upon our laurels and say, oh, we've done so well to this point and success is guaranteed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:18\n\nYeah, if anything, success is going to be harder to achieve the bigger this gets.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:23\n\nWell I guess um this has been a fun conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:26\n\nDo you have any fun and depressing conversation?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:30\n\nThey aren't necessarily mutually exclusive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nBut um did you have any parting words of wisdom for the viewers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:36\n\nUh well, like I said, uh care, participate, um interact with people, continue educating yourself, uh don't just blindly repeat mantras and expect that that is gonna somehow be helpful for Bitcoin's long-term health.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:52\n\nI don't think there's a better word you could have left us with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:55\n\nThanks for sitting down, Jameson.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:56\n\nYou bet.	Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine interviews Jameson Lopp of Casa regarding a new "address poisoning" attack, where attackers generate vanity addresses that partially match target addresses and dust them to trick recipients into accidentally sending funds to the attacker via copy-paste errors in wallet software. Lopp advises users to avoid reusing addresses, pasting from history, or relying on whitelist features not designed for this specific threat.\n\nThe conversation shifts to the gridlock in Bitcoin protocol development, contrasting two opposing philosophies: incremental changes via multiple soft forks versus broad, sweeping upgrades that add significant functionality in one go. Lopp argues that while small changes are easier to review against security risks, the current process is too slow and arduous, whereas large proposals are often dismissed despite being theoretically superior for scaling capability.\n\nA major theme is the growing conflict between Bitcoin's decentralized, cypherpunk ideals and the reality of increasing institutional adoption. Lopp warns that as economic power concentrates in a few corporations and institutions, the network risks "ossification" or capture. He draws a parallel to the email (SMTP) protocol, where centralized service providers now act as gatekeepers rather than users running their own servers, arguing Bitcoin faces a similar existential risk if large custodians and miners prioritize compliance and monetization over censorship resistance and user sovereignty.\n\nLopp critiques misleading on-chain metrics regarding exchange outflows, noting they are often flawed because chain analysis updates in hindsight make funds moving into institutional segregated accounts look like exits from exchanges when they are merely redistributions within custody pools. Ultimately, he identifies apathy as the greatest long-term risk to Bitcoin's evolution. To prevent the network from stagnating while the real world changes and external pressures mount, Lopp urges the community to actively participate in governance and development rather than blindly repeating ideological mantras or assuming success is guaranteed.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xCyPbFx0Ktg	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{"Address Poisoning","Protocol Governance",Ossification,"Protocol Development"}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:01:35.533269+05:30	2026-04-21 23:12:43.499414+05:30
6ab2502b-a683-40d3-a882-a78ca0a30df2	eEtxKbERWyA	conference	2025-04-09	{"Jeremy Rubin"}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nFirst, just to introduce myself a little bit, I'm Jeremy Rubin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:03\n\nI am, I guess, the founder of this expo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:06\n\nLike many, many years ago in 2014, we had the first one just a few buildings away.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:13\n\nAnd it's really great to see that it is still going on and in such good shape.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nAnd I wanted to say, before I get into my own content, that I'm really happy about the Freedom Tech theme this year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nA couple years ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:25\n\nYeah, exactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:25\n\nRound of applause for the organizers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nI was going to have to prompt you to do that, and then you guys all do that for yourself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nIt's really great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nA few years ago, when I was here, I looked at the agenda of who else was talking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:35\n\nI was like, oh, I probably just need to do a talk about why we're even here in the first place, because it was all sorts of we could build this or build that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:41\n\nAnd it's like, well, it's all about freedom technology and seeing the expo get back to those roots.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:46\n\nIt's really phenomenal and really great work from the team running the expo this year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:50\n\nSo that said, what is the char network?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:54\n\nI think I've been building on it for a little.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nPeople always ask me what is the char network, and I always respond tongue-in-cheek.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:59\n\nI'm excited to find out as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:02\n\nChar is a system to deploy and run decentralized applications and layer twos on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:08\n\nIt is a network of staked bonds that allows you to sequence applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nWe've heard a little bit about roll-ups and sequencing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nSolving that problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nIt allows for multiple bonders that you can reconcile a various sequence of messages, makes it expensive to cheat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:24\n\nIt's auditable, it's a set of SDKs to build those applications, and a system of incentives to guarantee that it's secure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:31\n\nA lot of that is still work in progress, and this is a talk maybe a little bit aspirationally about how the system is coming together.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:39\n\nSo, because we're focused here on freedom technology, I wanted to talk about maybe a single application.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:46\n\nSo, it's not the entire char network, but this is something that you could build on top of char, is a decentralized mining pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nSo, why do we care about decentralized mining?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:57\n\nWell, the sponsor, the lead sponsor of this event, is the Human Rights Foundation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:00\n\nSo, I thought, why not take the word from, you know, Directly from the horse's mouth.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:04\n\nCentralization in Bitcoin mining threatens its censorship resistance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nSo if we want Bitcoin to be censorship resistant, we better decentralize mining.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nAnd if you look at the actual distribution of hash rate and you do a little bit of snooping, what you'll see is it seems like mining just from a first glance is centralized.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:25\n\nBut if you look a little deeper, it's actually even more centralized than you might think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nIt seems like A lot of the pools that seem to be in different entities may actually be running identical software with identical databases.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:36\n\nMaybe that means that it's actually all just one company under the hood running all of Bitcoin mining infrastructure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:41\n\nThat's not really good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:43\n\nMaybe we can build an alternative.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:45\n\nSo before we get into how the alternative gets built, let me introduce some of the core concepts behind the JAR network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:52\n\nAnd the basic thing is an attestation chain bonding protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:56\n\nSo what is an attestation chain?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nThe core of that is just a linked sequence of signed messages.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nSo you sign a new message, and that new message commits to all the previous messages that you've ever had.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:09\n\nSo you get a nice linearization.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nIt's basically a blockchain, but instead of proof of work, you have a signature.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nSo one person could maybe forge a message.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:17\n\nSo how do we know if we see two conflicting statements in our Chain of messages, which one is the real one?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\n\nAnd the answer is: we can't really know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:27\n\nBut what we can do is, if we detect two conflicting statements, we can extract the secret key that was used for that chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:35\n\nAnd we can basically build on top of extracting a secret key a smart contract gadget that, with the knowledge of that secret key, allows the bonded oracle's funds to be burned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nSo, what this lets you do is, it lets you launch as an Oracle your own little blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:52\n\nAnd instead of having a baked-in reward to build the chain, you have a punishment if you don't build the chain in the honest way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:00\n\nAnd now we can kind of be sure if we see one of these chains, yeah, there might be another one floating around, but if you ever learned that other one, you'd be able to inflict a heavy penalty onto the provider of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nSequencing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:13\n\nInside of each of those sequences, it's not just a chain of signatures, you also have some data that's committed to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nAnd then you get a linked sequence of data that you can reference.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:23\n\nAnd in Char, the way that we architect that data is as a sparse Merkle tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:27\n\nA sparse Merkle tree is, you know, Bitcoin has a Merkle tree, a Merkle tree of transactions in every block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nIt lets you look up and see if there is a transaction at any given point without necessarily looking at the whole block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nSparse Merkle trees are a little bit different because they also allow you to give an exclusion proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nYou can look up and see if, for a given key, there's no data present, which is a nice and useful property.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:50\n\nThe way they work, a little bit out of scope for this talk, but you can think of it as just like a regular hash map that you can check if an entity is there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:56\n\nYou can retrieve it, or you can check and see that there's nothing there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:00\n\nSo, with that, we then use our special hash map with a key that is the hash of some piece of code that makes up our smart contract logic, and a value that's some argument that we want to call with that function.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:19\n\nAnd each of those functions that we have should have kind of two pieces of an API.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:23\n\nAt core, you'd have some sort of process update that takes a piece of call data and runs it over the function.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:30\n\nAnd then we have some reading function that says, well, given that we have a state of our application, can we query for, let's say, that you had a DNS application as we just heard about?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:43\n\nYou would say, given this name, which Tor hidden service should we use?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nYou know, actually go and visit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:49\n\nThat's an example of reading data once it's been updated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:52\n\nAnd so our sparse Merkle tree ends up looking like this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:55\n\nInstead of having keys that are arbitrary, you look up by program X, and then you find the call data that's associated with program X.\nStitching that, I guess I'll just show this is kind of in these visuals how we'll show an exclusion proof or how we'll show an inclusion proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:11\n\nYou can keep in mind that there's a sparse Merkle tree there, but we can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:13\n\nFilter, because you can do this exclusion proof to look up the data for a single key, which is a nice scalable property if you don't care about all the data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:21\n\nSo now we have our sequence of messages that are signed, and we filter by application.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nSo we're just looking at program X, some general program, and we get a list of data that's relevant for that channel.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nAnd we can take that list of Call data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:39\n\nActually, this slide maybe should be a little bit later.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:43\n\nBut throughout all of this, I'll just say before we go further, we're going to make a simplifying assumption.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:47\n\nYou can use multiple bonds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:49\n\nSo you can have multiple sequencers, and you can reconcile them into a single linearization or serialization of all of the history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nIt's a little bit more complicated to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:00\n\nSo for this presentation, we're just going to focus on imagining there's one single bonded oracle that everyone's using, but keep it in the back of your head that in a real world system, you'll use multiple, and you'll find a way to coalesce them into one view of consensus at a user application level.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nSo we take those program call data, and we call our process update function on them sequentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:25\n\nSo, this is basically a replicated state machine, and we're doing a functional fold over all the call data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:31\n\nSo, we keep calling this process update function, and we take the result of that with the first call data, and we run it with the second, and we run it with the third as sort of like an accumulator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:42\n\nAnd we do this not just a couple times, we do this every single time there's a new piece of data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:46\n\nAnd in between, every time we call it, there's an opportunity to read some piece of data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:52\n\nAnd where is this call data coming from?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:55\n\nLike, is it just random?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:56\n\nWell, maybe you read something that's interesting to you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nYou see, oh, there's actually something that I could respond to, and then you yourself would write a piece of call data that then goes into the next update.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nIt's a generic kind of smart contracting paradigm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nSo now that we have those basics on the char architecture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:15\n\nLet's look at what it would look like to put in a program that's specifically a decentralized mining pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nSo, the basic API that we would need is to be able to ask the contract for a block template.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nWe'd say, hey, we are wanting to mine these new transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:37\n\nPlease tell us what the block template should look like in order for the contract to recognize this as a valid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:42\n\nBlock, and you would call this get block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:44\n\nThat's a readable API.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:47\n\nAnd you'd want a write whenever you find a share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:52\n\nSo in a Bitcoin mining pool, I'm sure many of you are familiar with how that works.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nBut you have an idea of blocks that are valid blocks, which can go directly to the blockchain, and blocks that they've done some amount of work, but it's not enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nAnd you can use them to show how much hash rate you have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:07\n\nWhat is your participation share in the pool?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nSo you can submit those fractional shares as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:12\n\nAnd the pool will keep that replicated state machine and keep track of how much hash rate each person is contributing to the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:19\n\nAnd you could also request a payout.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:21\n\nYou could say, okay, I'm done participating in the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:22\n\nPlease pay me out in the next sequence of blocks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nSo that's all a little bit abstract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:28\n\nSo let's kind of map this out visually.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nHere we have a sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:35\n\nWe have some initial state of this has been running for some time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nAnd we're going to query and we're going to ask all of our miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:41\n\nThey're going to ask for a block template to start mining on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:44\n\nAnd then they're all going to start doing their thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:46\n\nThe data at this point in time, let's just say randomly: miner A has 20 shares, miner B has five shares, and miner C has 10 shares.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:54\n\nThat's the data that the contract is storing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:56\n\nAnd then, Miner A might find a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:59\n\nWhen miner A finds a block, they're going to send that to the sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:03\n\nThe sequencer is then going to form a new commitment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:06\n\nThey're going to form that new root, and that root is going to contain the call data for that program X.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nAnd one thing to keep in mind is we're showing a single application.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:16\n\nThat root is for a sparse Merkle tree, and there can be many applications happening simultaneously in this bond, but we're just looking at a single application running inside of there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nSo then, after a block is found, everyone will call to get a new block template.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:31\n\nThey want the new data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:32\n\nThey want to know what the new set of payouts should look like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:36\n\nAnd then they'll continue processing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nThis is what the data would look like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:39\n\nThere's not been any shares found since that block was found.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:43\n\nThen, maybe there's some other update that happens in the system, and so you'll see no data committed to, but there's some other commitment for some other applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:51\n\nYou're ignoring that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nThen miner C finds a share, and they say, Hey, I found a partial work share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:57\n\nThey send that to the sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:59\n\nThe sequencer then creates a new commitment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nThat new commitment contains the work share that miner C found, and everybody queries for what the latest block template is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:10\n\nThis type of design can be made resilient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:12\n\nYou don't necessarily need to be working on the latest template for it to still work, but you generally would be.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nProcessing on whatever the latest template is for the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:21\n\nOur updated table at this point, when you call for that template, looks like A has zero shares, B has zero shares, and C has one share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:29\n\nAnd you just keep processing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nSome other data comes up, it's not really relevant for you, you ignore it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:34\n\nThen B finds a share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nB submits the share to the sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nThe sequencer sequences the message, includes it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:42\n\nAnd then this is what our state looks like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:45\n\nI'll stop showing all of the extra data around getting templates and stuff like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:50\n\nIt's just happening in the background.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:51\n\nKeep that in mind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:52\n\nC as one share, B as one share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:55\n\nAnd then minor C finds a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:57\n\nSo C submits that block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:59\n\nNow, that block is based on the block template that was suggested by the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nThe call data, when it's processed by our process data function, is going to check that that block template was a valid find if it's going to be included in the pool, and then update the tables accordingly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:15\n\nAnd those blocks can directly pay out to the miners that have requested their payouts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:21\n\nOkay, so this whole design, I put the slide in there just for the benefit of, I think sometimes people like to play a game when I'm talking about something, which is find the covenant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:32\n\nAnd there's no covenants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nIt's a separate design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:37\n\nI do other things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:39\n\nThey would help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:40\n\nThose payouts that we're doing to miners, if they were done inside of a payment protocol like Arc, would be much more scalable and private efficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nSo that would decrease the size of miner that would be economic to participate in the pool and decrease the amount of custodial risk that you bear as a miner using this pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:58\n\nSo that would be really great to have ARC.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:00\n\nIt turns out, even though there's a version of ARC that works without covenants, if you want to use them in a mining pool, you really need this non-interactive form of arc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:08\n\nBecause every time you make a template, you can't really interact with all the miners to get a signature from them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:13\n\nSo we need a non-interactive arc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nThe bonding protocol for Char, it does not use covenants at all, doesn't use covenant emulation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nThere are different ways of structuring some of these bonding proof of stake contracts that don't require covenants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nThe overall game theory for something like this is that if you didn't have the bonding, then you're relying on a centralized pool operator, and the pool operator can lie to you about what the payoff should be, lie to you about how much work is done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nThis, you're logging publicly, what all the work shares are, so everybody can audit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:47\n\nEverybody has the same picture, and if there's any equivocation over that, then the pool operator can be punished, or the sequencers can be punished.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:56\n\nSo there's not going to be equivocation, especially in this design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:00\n\nIf the bonded collateral exceeds one block reward, there's no incentive to be able to cheat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nAnd then in terms of censoring, you might wonder: well, what if I find a work share and then it isn't getting sequenced?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:12\n\nYou can also provide a tip to the bond operators, and the tip to the bond operators guarantees that they have incentive to include your message.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nKind of like a transaction fee.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nSo now I'll loop back kind of to the very beginning of this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:24\n\nWhat is the char network?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:26\n\nIt's a system to deploy and run decentralized applications in layer twos.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:30\n\nSo, whatever you're interested in building, this is a new primitive that we're building into Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:38\n\nSo it'll be right alongside, and you can use this for coordinating all sorts of different applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nAnd you'll have an SDK and sort of way of incentivizing participation in these that will allow you to launch all sorts of new things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:52\n\nAnd there's a lot of research that we're doing to make it even more efficient, stuff with zero-knowledge proofs that let you kind of compact how that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:01\n\nReplicated state machine runs, a lot of good research directions there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nJust examples of other applications that can benefit from being able to use this sequencing primitive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:10\n\nRoll-ups and sidechains, BitVM, those are kind of the obvious ones.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:13\n\nYou hear them talk all the time about how they're going to solve their sequencing problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nBut it also works really well for things that are just as simple as a multi-sig wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nIf you have a multi-sig and you're trying to coordinate with a couple friends around how you should spend money, You all kind of need to agree on which PSPT you're going to sign.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nAnd this is a way that you could use this as a broadcast channel to be able to say, are we all on the same page?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nAnd are we dealing with the same underlying data set?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:36\n\nIt can be useful for all sorts of service providers like ASPS.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:39\n\nIf you're signing off on partial PSPTs, your peers might want to know that there's no other version floating out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:45\n\nThis is a way of making sure that that data is deduplicated and consistent.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:50\n\nAs well as things like routing tables or name services.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nThere's a lot of useful applications for this new data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:56\n\nAnd it's also not data that has to live inside of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:59\n\nSo the sort of core ask that I have right now is: we are hiring.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:04\n\nSo if this is stuff that's exciting for you, we have an in-person office in Miami.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:08\n\nAnd we're hiring for people who want to do low-level systems engineering, Bitcoin Core, C, Rust.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:14\n\nDevelopment, just reach out and let us know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:16\n\nAnd we're really excited about what we can build to empower the next layer of freedom technology for Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:22\n\nAnd I think I've got good time for questions if people want to ask anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:32\n\nHi, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:33\n\nHey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nSo a couple of things that weren't clear.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:39\n\nWhere is contract data kept?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:43\n\nLike, where is the contract data being kept?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nSo, depending on the application, the contract data doesn't need to be like the contract code or contract data?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:54\n\nThe state.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:56\n\nThe stake or the?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:58\n\nThe state.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nState.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nSo, for the state, there's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:03\n\nThere's the call data, which is like the arguments, and then there's the state that's generated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:07\n\nYou really only need to store the call data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:11\n\nAnd there are ways of compacting that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:13\n\nBecause of our sparse Merkle Tree structure, for applications you don't particularly care about, you can prune that out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:18\n\nIf you're running a node, it would be stored on your node in a database, but it's not consensus required.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:25\n\nFor a given application, you can just rerun the validation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:30\n\nIt's like kind of client-side validation style.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:33\n\nDoes that answer your question?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:35\n\nI think that does.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:36\n\nDo you need to store everybody's state or just yours?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nSo if you're like a full node, you would want to store as much data as possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nBut because of the Sparse Merkle Tree format, you really only destore for applications that you're interested in.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:53\n\nWhat would incentivize a full node runner?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:57\n\nSo, something that, just in terms of any incentive whatsoever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:03\n\nSo, for something like a mining pool, you can incentivize sequencing by demarcating that in your work share, you're paying an amount to the set of Like stakers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:15\n\nAnd then there are other ways also of longer term incentivizing this sort of network as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:24\n\nIt's hard to do it fully Bitcoin denominated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:26\n\nSo sometimes there has to be potentially other incentive mechanisms.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:30\n\nBut there are a lot of applications that can just be pure Bitcoin denominated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:33\n\nIt might make sense to integrate full node or at least stake like Hosting with the mining pool capability to kind of like couple those incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:45\n\nYeah, absolutely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:46\n\nAnd also, the other question I have is: okay, so now you have smart contracts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:50\n\nHow how do you where do you keep the the SATs?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:54\n\nLike where are those?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:55\n\nSo the mining pool is a particularly interesting application because you have the SATs wrapped up in a future block to be mined.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:05\n\nIt's actually non-custodial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:07\n\nAnd so, at the point of mining, you just have to send in order for it to be recognized into the address that's been told to you as the block template coming out of the contract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:17\n\nSo, that's actually a fully non-custodial type of setup.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:21\n\nIf you wanted a custodial setup, you could also say it goes to this address, and then that address would be like a multi-sig or something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:29\n\nThat multi-sig could also be coordinated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nBy char, but then it would be multi-sig custodial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:33\n\nFor the mining pool itself, it it's fully non-custodial though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:37\n\nSo it always boils down to a DAO is essentially a multi-sig.\nYeah, i and and for like roll-ups, a lot of them have like a big federation set, but they still need like a sequencing layer to decide their consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:50\n\nEven if it's a big federation, they might not be able to agree on which transaction they're doing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nVery clever stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:55\n\nThank you, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:56\n\nYeah, of course.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:57\n\nUh is there a paper for the implementation of the bonding uh for the multi-party for the sequencers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:04\n\nUm there is a paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:06\n\nWe haven't published it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:08\n\nUm and there's like an implementation which we're you know sort of getting production grade before we publish.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:14\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:14\n\nUm and then also as far as uh the bonding, um is there uh like interactivity, like is there like variability that can you slash part part of it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:26\n\nOr is it more of like a cryptographic uh like reflexive kind of thing that you you mess up somewhere in the uh state machine and that you know breaks the crypto or h how is that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:36\n\nUm so basically the right right now the only way that you get punished is by equivocating.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:41\n\nUm so general, like if you just committed to garbage data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:47\n\nIt should be ignored in the client-side validation model.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:50\n\nThere are ways of guaranteeing that you commit to valid data under the keys like isData valid function.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:01\n\nBut those are not a current part of the design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:03\n\nIt's sort of related to future work around fraud proofs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:08\n\nOkay, and when's that paper coming out, and where can we find it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:11\n\nThat's a good question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:13\n\nHopefully, soon.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:14\n\nWe're aiming to put out a lot more by May, but it is a little bit early, early preview.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:22\n\nHi, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:23\n\nSo I did Harvard CS on MIT CS, so some of this was over my head.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nBut I was curious if you could explain the value proposition again in really simple terms.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:32\n\nIf you're working on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:35\n\nAny sort of protocol on top of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:40\n\nYou need some way to share data with your peers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nAnd if you have peers who are not particularly trustworthy, it can be extraordinarily difficult to make sure that you all have the same data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:51\n\nThis is a way of getting data that can be sort of consensus guaranteed against a Bitcoin collateral.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nThat, if you got incorrect records, would be slashable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:04\n\nAnd so you could basically say if you have an application that's running, it's incentivized that those are the records that everyone has.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:11\n\nAnd if those records get falsified, you get a public signal of Bitcoin getting stolen from those bonds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:18\n\nSo you'd see that there's kind of an attack on the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:20\n\nSo this is a collaboration tool, fundamentally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:22\n\nYeah, fundamentally a collaboration tool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:25\n\nIn a way of building scaling solutions for Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:29\n\nHey, this looks pretty cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:31\n\nI'm curious exactly what functionality you need to implement that touches the internals of Bitcoin Core compared to using the existing Rust ecosystem, or what pieces are missing that you're probably not gonna build because you're busy building this instead of tooling?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:49\n\nTooling, like REST tooling, is missing for what you want to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:53\n\nSo, I think that one thing that is missing, and I know that there's some work on it for like IPC interfaces with a node, but we really want this system to run in perfect lockstep with Bitcoin consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:07\n\nAnd so, because we want that, we implement inside of Bitcoin Core the C validation interface.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:13\n\nAnd every time a block is mined or submitted across the network, we are able to read that signal and react to it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:22\n\nIt's not real-time software, but basically real-time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:24\n\nAnd most of the other interfaces, you end up implementing a long polling-based solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:29\n\nAnd then also, in terms of writing the Bitcoin transactions that we're doing, they are standard, so they're reliable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:36\n\nThere's no problem with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:38\n\nA lot of the tooling is not quite there for at least in my opinion, for building things that are a little bit outside of the commonly used patterns.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:48\n\nSo we're doing some stuff that's not maybe a little bit more bleeding edge, so that's why we're building inside of core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:54\n\nIn terms of how we're actually built, one other notable thing is we use Bitcoin's peer-to-peer layer for broadcasting the messages for attestations.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:02\n\nNot in a way that if you're not participating, it would have any impact on you, but that is able to then sort of inherit some of Bitcoin's network properties, which I think is also nice.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:14\n\nSo those are some of the reasons why we're not just building a completely separate system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:18\n\nThanks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:20\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:20\n\nThank you, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:21\n\nThank you, everybody.	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nFirst, just to introduce myself a little bit, I'm Jeremy Rubin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:03\n\nI am, I guess, the founder of this expo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:06\n\nLike many, many years ago in 2014, we had the first one just a few buildings away.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:13\n\nAnd it's really great to see that it is still going on and in such good shape.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nAnd I wanted to say, before I get into my own content, that I'm really happy about the Freedom Tech theme this year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nA couple years ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:25\n\nYeah, exactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:25\n\nRound of applause for the organizers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nI was going to have to prompt you to do that, and then you guys all do that for yourself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nIt's really great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nA few years ago, when I was here, I looked at the agenda of who else was talking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:35\n\nI was like, oh, I probably just need to do a talk about why we're even here in the first place, because it was all sorts of ways we could build this or build that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:41\n\nAnd it's like, well, it's all about freedom technology and seeing the expo get back to those roots.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:46\n\nIt's really phenomenal and really great work from the team running the expo this year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:50\n\nSo that said, what is the Char network?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:54\n\nI think I've been building on it for a while.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nPeople always ask me what is the Char network, and I always respond tongue-in-cheek.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:59\n\nI'm excited to find out as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:02\n\nChar is a system to deploy and run decentralized applications and layer two's on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:08\n\nIt is a network of staked bonds that allows you to sequence applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nWe've heard a little bit about roll-ups and sequencing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nSolving that problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nIt allows for multiple bonders that you can reconcile a various sequence of messages, makes it expensive to cheat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:24\n\nIt's auditable, it's a set of SDKs to build those applications, and a system of incentives to guarantee that it's secure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:31\n\nA lot of that is still work in progress, and this is a talk maybe a little bit aspirationally about how the system is coming together.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:39\n\nSo, because we're focused here on freedom technology, I wanted to talk about maybe a single application.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:46\n\nSo, it's not the entire Char network, but this is something that you could build on top of Char, is a decentralized mining pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nSo, why do we care about decentralized mining?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:57\n\nWell, the sponsor, the lead sponsor of this event, is the Human Rights Foundation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:00\n\nSo, I thought, why not take the word from, you know, Directly from the horse's mouth.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:04\n\nCentralization in Bitcoin mining threatens its censorship resistance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nSo if we want Bitcoin to be censorship resistant, we better decentralize mining.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nAnd if you look at the actual distribution of hash rate and you do a little bit of snooping, what you'll see is it seems like mining just from a first glance is centralized.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:25\n\nBut if you look a little deeper, it's actually even more centralized than you might think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nIt seems like a lot of the pools that seem to be in different entities may actually be running identical software with identical databases.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:36\n\nMaybe that means that it's actually all just one company under the hood running all of Bitcoin mining infrastructure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:41\n\nThat's not really good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:43\n\nMaybe we can build an alternative.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:45\n\nSo before we get into how the alternative gets built, let me introduce some of the core concepts behind the Char network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:52\n\nAnd the basic thing is an attestation chain bonding protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:56\n\nSo what is an attestation chain?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nThe core of that is just a linked sequence of signed messages.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nSo you sign a new message, and that new message commits to all the previous messages that you've ever had.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:09\n\nSo you get a nice linearization.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nIt's basically a blockchain, but instead of proof of work, you have a signature.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nSo one person could maybe forge a message.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:17\n\nSo how do we know if we see two conflicting statements in our Chain of messages, which one is the real one?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\n\nAnd the answer is: we can't really know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:27\n\nBut what we can do is, if we detect two conflicting statements, we can extract the secret key that was used for that chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:35\n\nAnd we can basically build on top of extracting a secret key, a smart contract gadget that, with the knowledge of that secret key, allows the bonded oracle's funds to be burned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nSo, what this lets you do is, it lets you launch as an Oracle your own little blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:52\n\nAnd instead of having a baked-in reward to build the chain, you have a punishment if you don't build the chain in the honest way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:00\n\nAnd now we can kind of be sure if we see one of these chains, yeah, there might be another one floating around, but if you ever learn that other one, you'd be able to inflict a heavy penalty onto the provider of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nSequencing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:13\n\nInside of each of those sequences, it's not just a chain of signatures, you also have some data that's committed to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nAnd then you get a linked sequence of data that you can reference.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:23\n\nAnd in Char, the way that we architect that data is as a sparse Merkle tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:27\n\nA sparse Merkle tree is, you know, Bitcoin has a Merkle tree, a Merkle tree of transactions in every block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nIt lets you look up and see if there is a transaction at any given point without necessarily looking at the whole block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nSparse Merkle trees are a little bit different because they also allow you to give an exclusion proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nYou can look up and see if, for a given key, there's no data present, which is a nice and useful property.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:50\n\nThe way they work, a little bit out of scope for this talk, but you can think of it as just like a regular hash map that you can check if an entity is there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:56\n\nYou can retrieve it, or you can check and see that there's nothing there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:00\n\nSo, with that, we then use our special hash map with a key that is the hash of some piece of code that makes up our smart contract logic, and a value that's some argument that we want to call with that function.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:19\n\nAnd each of those functions that we have should have kind of two pieces of an API.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:23\n\nAt core, you'd have some sort of process update that takes a piece of call data and runs it over the function.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:30\n\nAnd then we have some reading function that says, well, given that we have a state of our application, can we query for, let's say, that you had a DNS application as we just heard about?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:43\n\nYou would say, given this name, which Tor hidden service should we use?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nYou know, actually go and visit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:49\n\nThat's an example of reading data once it's been updated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:52\n\nAnd so our sparse Merkle tree ends up looking like this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:55\n\nInstead of having keys that are arbitrary, you look up by program X, and then you find the call data that's associated with program X.\nStitching that, I guess I'll just show this is kind of in these visuals how we'll show an exclusion proof or how we'll show an inclusion proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:11\n\nYou can keep in mind that there's a sparse Merkle tree there, but we can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:13\n\nFilter, because you can do this exclusion proof to look up the data for a single key, which is a nice scalable property if you don't care about all the data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:21\n\nSo now we have our sequence of messages that are signed, and we filter by application.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nSo we're just looking at program X, some general program, and we get a list of data that's relevant for that channel.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nAnd we can take that list of Call data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:39\n\nActually, this slide maybe should be a little bit later.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:43\n\nBut throughout all of this, I'll just say before we go further, we're going to make a simplifying assumption.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:47\n\nYou can use multiple bonds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:49\n\nSo you can have multiple sequencers, and you can reconcile them into a single linearization or serialization of all of the history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nIt's a little bit more complicated to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:00\n\nSo for this presentation, we're just going to focus on imagining there's one single bonded oracle that everyone's using, but keep it in the back of your head that in a real world system, you'll use multiple, and you'll find a way to coalesce them into one view of consensus at a user application level.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nSo we take those program call data, and we call our process update function on them sequentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:25\n\nSo, this is basically a replicated state machine, and we're doing a functional fold over all the call data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:31\n\nSo, we keep calling this process update function, and we take the result of that with the first call data, and we run it with the second, and we run it with the third as sort of like an accumulator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:42\n\nAnd we do this not just a couple times, we do this every single time there's a new piece of data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:46\n\nAnd in between, every time we call it, there's an opportunity to read some piece of data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:52\n\nAnd where is this call data coming from?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:55\n\nLike, is it just random?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:56\n\nWell, maybe you read something that's interesting to you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nYou see, oh, there's actually something that I could respond to, and then you yourself would write a piece of call data that then goes into the next update.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nIt's a generic kind of smart contracting paradigm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nSo now that we have those basics on the chain architecture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:15\n\nLet's look at what it would look like to put in a program that's specifically a decentralized mining pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nSo, the basic API that we would need is to be able to ask the contract for a block template.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nWe'd say, hey, we are wanting to mine these new transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:37\n\nPlease tell us what the block template should look like in order for the contract to recognize this as a valid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:42\n\nBlock, and you would call this get block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:44\n\nThat's a readable API.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:47\n\nAnd you'd want a write whenever you find a share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:52\n\nSo in a Bitcoin mining pool, I'm sure many of you are familiar with how that works.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nBut you have an idea of blocks that are valid blocks, which can go directly to the blockchain, and blocks that they've done some amount of work, but it's not enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nAnd you can use them to show how much hash rate you have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:07\n\nWhat is your participation share in the pool?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nSo you can submit those fractional shares as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:12\n\nAnd the pool will keep that replicated state machine and keep track of how much hash rate each person is contributing to the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:19\n\nAnd you could also request a payout.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:21\n\nYou could say, okay, I'm done participating in the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:22\n\nPlease pay me out in the next sequence of blocks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nSo that's all a little bit abstract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:28\n\nSo let's kind of map this out visually.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nHere we have a sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:35\n\nWe have some initial state of this chain has been running for some time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nAnd we're going to query and we're going to ask all of our miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:41\n\nThey're going to ask for a block template to start mining on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:44\n\nAnd then they're all going to start doing their thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:46\n\nThe data at this point in time, let's just say randomly: miner A has 20 shares, miner B has five shares, and miner C has 10 shares.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:54\n\nThat's the data that the contract is storing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:56\n\nAnd then, Miner A might find a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:59\n\nWhen miner A finds a block, they're going to send that to the sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:03\n\nThe sequencer is then going to form a new commitment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:06\n\nThey're going to form that new root, and that root is going to contain the call data for that program X.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nAnd one thing to keep in mind is we're showing a single application.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:16\n\nThat root is for a sparse Merkle tree, and there can be many applications happening simultaneously in this bond, but we're just looking at a single application running inside of there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nSo then, after a block is found, everyone will call to get a new block template.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:31\n\nThey want the new data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:32\n\nThey want to know what the new set of payouts should look like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:36\n\nAnd then they'll continue processing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nThis is what the data would look like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:39\n\nThere's not been any shares found since that block was found.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:43\n\nThen, maybe there's some other update that happens in the system, and so you'll see no data committed to, but there's some other commitment for some other applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:51\n\nYou're ignoring that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nThen miner C finds a share, and they say, Hey, I found a partial work share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:57\n\nThey send that to the sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:59\n\nThe sequencer then creates a new commitment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nThat new commitment contains the work share that miner C found, and everybody queries for what the latest block template is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:10\n\nThis type of design can be made resilient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:12\n\nYou don't necessarily need to be working on the latest template for it to still work, but you generally would be.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nProcessing on whatever the latest template is for the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:21\n\nOur updated state at this point, when you call for that template, looks like A has zero shares, B has zero shares, and C has one share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:29\n\nAnd you just keep processing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nSome other data comes up, it's not really relevant for you, you ignore it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:34\n\nThen B finds a share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nB submits the share to the sequencer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nThe sequencer sequences the message, includes it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:42\n\nAnd then this is what our state looks like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:45\n\nI'll stop showing all of the extra data around getting templates and stuff like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:50\n\nIt's just happening in the background.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:51\n\nKeep that in mind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:52\n\nC as one share, B as one share.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:55\n\nAnd then minor C finds a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:57\n\nSo C submits that block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:59\n\nNow, that block is based on the block template that was suggested by the pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nThe call data, when it's processed by our process_data function, is going to check that that block template was a valid find if it's going to be included in the pool, and then update the tables accordingly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:15\n\nAnd those blocks can directly pay out to the miners that have requested their payouts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:21\n\nOkay, so this whole design, I put the slide in there just for the benefit of, I think sometimes people like to play a game when I'm talking about something, which is find the covenant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:32\n\nAnd there's no covenants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nIt's a separate design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:37\n\nI do other things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:39\n\nThey would help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:40\n\nThose payouts that we're doing to miners, if they were done inside of a payment protocol like Arc, would be much more scalable and private efficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nSo that would decrease the size of miner that would be economic to participate in the pool and decrease the amount of custodial risk that you bear as a miner using this pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:58\n\nSo that would be really great to have ARC.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:00\n\nIt turns out, even though there's a version of ARC that works without covenants, if you want to use them in a mining pool, you really need this non-interactive form of ARC.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:08\n\nBecause every time you make a template, you can't really interact with all the miners to get a signature from them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:13\n\nSo we need a non-interactive arc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nThe bonding protocol for Char, it does not use covenants at all, doesn't use covenant emulation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nThere are different ways of structuring some of these bonding proof of stake contracts that don't require covenants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nThe overall game theory for something like this is that if you didn't have the bonding, then you're relying on a centralized pool operator, and the pool operator can lie to you about what the payoff should be, lie to you about how much work is done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nThis, you're logging publicly, what all the work shares are, so everybody can audit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:47\n\nEverybody has the same picture, and if there's any equivocation over that, then the pool operator can be punished, or the sequencers can be punished.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:56\n\nSo there's not going to be equivocation, especially in this design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:00\n\nIf the bonded collateral exceeds one block reward, there's no incentive to be able to cheat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nAnd then in terms of censoring, you might wonder: well, what if I find a work share and then it isn't getting sequenced?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:12\n\nYou can also provide a tip to the bond operators, and the tip to the bond operators guarantees that they have incentive to include your message.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nKind of like a transaction fee.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nSo now I'll loop back kind of to the very beginning of this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:24\n\nWhat is the Char network?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:26\n\nIt's a system to deploy and run decentralized applications in layer twos.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:30\n\nSo, whatever you're interested in building, this is a new primitive that we're building into Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:38\n\nSo it'll be right alongside, and you can use this for coordinating all sorts of different applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nAnd you'll have an SDK and sort of way of incentivizing participation in these that will allow you to launch all sorts of new things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:52\n\nAnd there's a lot of research that we're doing to make it even more efficient, stuff with zero-knowledge proofs that let you kind of compact how that.\n\nReplicated state machine runs, a lot of good research directions there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nJust examples of other applications that can benefit from being able to use this sequencing primitive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:10\n\nRoll-ups and sidechains, BitVM, those are kind of the obvious ones.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:13\n\nYou hear them talk all the time about how they're going to solve their sequencing problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nBut it also works really well for things that are just as simple as a multi-sig wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nIf you have a multi-sig and you're trying to coordinate with a couple friends around how you should spend money, You all kind of need to agree on which P2SH or PSBT you're going to sign.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nAnd this is a way that you could use this as a broadcast channel to be able to say, are we all on the same page?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nAnd are we dealing with the same underlying data set?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:36\n\nIt can be useful for all sorts of service providers like ASGs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:39\n\nIf you're signing off on partial PSBTs, your peers might want to know that there's no other version floating out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:45\n\nThis is a way of making sure that that data is deduplicated and consistent.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:50\n\nAs well as things like routing tables or name services.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nThere's a lot of useful applications for this new data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:56\n\nAnd it's also not data that has to live inside of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:59\n\nSo the sort of core ask that I have right now is: we are hiring.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:04\n\nSo if this is stuff that's exciting for you, we have an in-person office in Miami.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:08\n\nAnd we're hiring for people who want to do low-level systems engineering, Bitcoin Core, C, Rust.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:14\n\nDevelopment, just reach out and let us know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:16\n\nAnd we're really excited about what we can build to empower the next layer of freedom technology for Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:22\n\nAnd I think I've got enough time for questions if people want to ask anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:32\n\nHi, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:33\n\nHey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nSo a couple of things that weren't clear.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:39\n\nWhere is contract data kept?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:43\n\nLike, where is the contract data being kept?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nSo, depending on the application, the contract data doesn't need to be like the contract code or contract data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:54\n\nThe state.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:56\n\nThe stake or the?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:58\n\nThe state.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nState.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nSo, for the state, there's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:03\n\nThere's the call data, which is like the arguments, and then there's the state that's generated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:07\n\nYou really only need to store the call data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:11\n\nAnd there are ways of compacting that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:13\n\nBecause of our sparse Merkle Tree structure, for applications you don't particularly care about, you can prune that out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:18\n\nIf you're running a node, it would be stored on your node in a database, but it's not consensus required.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:25\n\nFor a given application, you can just rerun the validation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:30\n\nIt's like kind of client-side validation style.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:33\n\nDoes that answer your question?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:35\n\nI think that does.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:36\n\nDo you need to store everybody's state or just yours?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nSo if you're like a full node, you would want to store as much data as possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nBut because of the Sparse Merkle Tree format, you really only restore for applications that you're interested in.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:53\n\nWhat would incentivize a full node runner?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:57\n\nSo, something that, just in terms of any incentive whatsoever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:03\n\nSo, for something like a mining pool, you can incentivize sequencing by demarcating that in your work share, you're paying an amount to the set of Like stakers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:15\n\nAnd then there are other ways also of longer term incentivizing this sort of network as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:24\n\nIt's hard to do it fully Bitcoin denominated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:26\n\nSo sometimes there has to be potentially other incentive mechanisms.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:30\n\nBut there are a lot of applications that can just be pure Bitcoin denominated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:33\n\nIt might make sense to integrate full node or at least stake like hosting with the mining pool capability to kind of like couple those incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:45\n\nYeah, absolutely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:46\n\nAnd also, the other question I have is: okay, so now you have smart contracts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:50\n\nHow how do you where do you keep the sats?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:54\n\nLike where are those?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:55\n\nSo the mining pool is a particularly interesting application because you have the Sats wrapped up in a future block to be mined.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:05\n\nIt's actually non-custodial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:07\n\nAnd so, at the point of mining, you just have to send in order for it to be recognized into the address that's been told to you as the block template coming out of the contract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:17\n\nSo, that's actually a fully non-custodial type of setup.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:21\n\nIf you wanted a custodial setup, you could also say it goes to this address, and then that address would be like a multi-sig or something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:29\n\nThat multi-sig could also be coordinated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nBy Charlie, but then it would be multi-sig custodial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:33\n\nFor the mining pool itself, it it's fully non-custodial though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:37\n\nSo it always boils down to a DAO is essentially a multi-sig.\nYeah, I think for like roll-ups, a lot of them have like a big federation set, but they still need like a sequencing layer to decide their consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:50\n\nEven if it's a big federation, they might not be able to agree on which transaction they're doing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nVery clever stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:55\n\nThank you, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:56\n\nYeah, of course.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:57\n\nUh, is there a paper for the implementation of the bonding, uh, for the multi-party for the sequencers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:04\n\nUm, there is a paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:06\n\nWe haven't published it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:08\n\nUm, and there's like an implementation which we're, you know, sort of getting production grade before we publish.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:14\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:14\n\nUm, and then also as far as uh the bonding, um, is there uh like interactivity, like is there like variability? That can you slash part of it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:26\n\nOr is it more of like a cryptographic uh, like reflexive kind of thing that you mess up somewhere in the uh state machine and that you know breaks the crypto or how is that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:36\n\nUm, so basically the right now, the only way that you get punished is by equivocating.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:41\n\nUm, so general, like if you just committed to garbage data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:47\n\nIt should be ignored in the client-side validation model.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:50\n\nThere are ways of guaranteeing that you commit to valid data under the keys like isDataValid function.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:01\n\nBut those are not a current part of the design.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:03\n\nIt's sort of related to future work around fraud proofs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:08\n\nOkay, and when's that paper coming out, and where can we find it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:11\n\nThat's a good question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:13\n\nHopefully, soon.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:14\n\nWe're aiming to put out a lot more by May, but it is a little bit early, early preview.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:22\n\nHi, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:23\n\nSo I did Harvard CS on MIT CS, so some of this was over my head.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nBut I was curious if you could explain the value proposition again in really simple terms.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:32\n\nIf you're working on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:35\n\nAny sort of protocol on top of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:40\n\nYou need some way to share data with your peers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nAnd if you have peers who are not particularly trustworthy, it can be extraordinarily difficult to make sure that you all have the same data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:51\n\nThis is a way of getting data that can be sort of consensus guaranteed against a Bitcoin collateral.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nThat, if you got incorrect records, would be slashable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:04\n\nAnd so you could basically say if you have an application that's running, it's incentivized that those are the records that everyone has.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:11\n\nAnd if those records get falsified, you get a public signal of Bitcoin getting stolen from those bonds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:18\n\nSo you'd see that there's kind of an attack on the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:20\n\nSo this is a collaboration tool, fundamentally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:22\n\nYeah, fundamentally a collaboration tool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:25\n\nIn a way of building scaling solutions for Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:29\n\nHey, this looks pretty cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:31\n\nI'm curious exactly what functionality you need to implement that touches the internals of Bitcoin Core compared to using the existing Rust ecosystem, or what pieces are missing that you're probably not gonna build because you're busy building this instead of tooling?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:49\n\nTooling, like REST tooling, is missing for what you want to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:53\n\nSo, I think that one thing that is missing, and I know that there's some work on it for like IPC interfaces with a node, but we really want this system to run in perfect lockstep with Bitcoin consensus.\n\nAnd so, because we want that, we implement inside of Bitcoin Core the C validation interface.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:13\n\nAnd every time a block is mined or submitted across the network, we are able to read that signal and react to it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:22\n\nIt's not real-time software, but basically real-time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:24\n\nAnd most of the other interfaces, you end up implementing a long polling-based solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:29\n\nAnd then also, in terms of writing the Bitcoin transactions that we're doing, they are standard, so they're reliable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:36\n\nThere's no problem with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:38\n\nA lot of the tooling is not quite there for at least in my opinion, for building things that are a little bit outside of the commonly used patterns.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:48\n\nSo we're doing some stuff that's not maybe a little bit more bleeding edge, so that's why we're building inside of core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:54\n\nIn terms of how we're actually built, one other notable thing is we use Bitcoin's peer-to-peer layer for broadcasting the messages for attestations.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:02\n\nNot in a way that if you're not participating, it would have any impact on you, but that is able to then sort of inherit some of Bitcoin's network properties, which I think is also nice.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:14\n\nSo those are some of the reasons why we're not just building a completely separate system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:18\n\nThanks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:20\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:20\n\nThank you, Jeremy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:21\n\nThank you, everybody.	Jeremy Rubin introduces Char, a Layer 2 system designed to deploy decentralized applications on Bitcoin by addressing sequencing challenges through staked bonds and an attestation chain bonding protocol. Char aims to decentralize Bitcoin mining to ensure censorship resistance, countering current risks of hash rate centralization without relying on traditional proof-of-work for sequencer incentives. The architecture utilizes signed message chains and sparse Merkle trees to function as a replicated state machine, leveraging standard Bitcoin transactions and the existing peer-to-peer network layer to broadcast attestation messages robustly while imposing no cost on non-participants. Distinct from other protocols requiring covenants, Char ensures security through cryptographic equivalence and public auditability, punishing malicious operators within a non-custodial framework where payouts are handled via partial shares and block templates. As a consensus-guaranteed collaboration tool, Char supports Bitcoin scaling solutions like roll-ups and multi-signature coordination, and the team is currently integrating these systems directly into Bitcoin Core C++ while seeking to publish technical papers on future developments.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eEtxKbERWyA	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{"Layer 2",BitVM,Scaling,Consensus}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:03:45.169562+05:30	2026-04-21 22:50:34.83789+05:30
465e6549-b83c-43df-ac49-f9d22246a7fd	QvtnQfVdLYU	conference	2025-05-02	{}	{}	{}	## Intro: Sjors Provoost at MIT Bitcoin Expo\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHello everybody, I'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine sitting down with Shore's Provost from Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nSo we're going to talk a little bit today about kind of the internal organization and workflow of Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:17\n\nHopefully leaving everybody at the end of it with a little bit of an understanding of how developers approach their work and Maybe, just maybe a little bit of understanding that they are not a homogeneous group that all have the same opinions about everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nOh, they certainly don't have the same opinions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:37\n\nI think five minutes on GitHub would convince you of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\n\nSo, um, yeah, you you've been contributing to Bitcoin as far back as I can remember.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:45\n\nI think the entire time I've been in this space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:47\n\nUh Bitcoin Core specifically in the middle of twenty seventeen I I made my first contribution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:53\n\nI was playing with uh Paul Storz's drive change project.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nOh no.\nTrying to see if it if it was actually running code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:00\n\nAnd then I ran into error messages that were nonsensical and I tried to improve some of the Bitcoin tests, Bitcoin Core tests to give better uh error messages.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:09\n\nAnd I think those were merged within a year or so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:13\n\nBut but once you once you you enter that rabbit hole, it's there's no way out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:19\n\nSo, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:20\n\nWhat what what's it like contributing to core?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:23\n\nLike just from a you know, a personal perspective?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nMainly it it varies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:28\n\nI mean sometimes it can be a little frustrating, but also it's very interesting.\n\n\n\n## What It’s Actually Like to Contribute to Bitcoin Core\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:32\n\nYou I found it nice to meet some really smart people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:35\n\nUm You know, because most of these peop most people are doing it for not for the money, they're doing it because they find the project interesting or whatever it is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nSo you just meet a lot of uh yeah, very smart people with good ideas.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:48\n\nAnd some of the sub projects you could almost say are super interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nUh there's also just very boring plumbing stuff that needs to happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:58\n\nI think that's very underappreciated uh from just your average user's point of view.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:02\n\nYeah, but of course, you know, not everybody is doing the boring plumbing stuff either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nI guess I'm I'm still somewhat lucky.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\n\nUm I'm just a reviewer, I'm not a maintainer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nSo, you know that's don't sell yourself short.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:14\n\nThat's that's important too.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nNo, but th th the thing is if you're the maintainer and the release is broken, like people are gonna complain and you can't ignore the problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:24\n\nWhereas if you're just a developer, you're like, well, I'm just gonna review things that are interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nSo I don't care about all these problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:30\n\nLet other people deal with the bugs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:32\n\nSo you know I'm not saying I'm doing that, but not my fault.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:37\n\nBut some s somebody gets all the complaints or at least, you know, can't you know, does doesn't want to ship broken code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\n\nSo there there is some and I think the specialty maintainers have to do some of the uh more ungrateful work of like somebody has to make the backports and actually ship them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:53\n\nAnd somebody has to you know, when when there's an actual bug in the release, can't just ignore it and then they'll have to cajole other developers to to be interested in in looking at it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:03\n\n'Cause ultimately nobody's in charge, so there's no CEO that says, like, you guys, get in a room and fix these fifteen bugs before you go home.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:10\n\nPeople can just decide to not fix any bugs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nIt's ki kind of a thankless job.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:15\n\nYou know, es especially um since Vladimir And some others have kind of stepped down, and we've seen new maintainers take those roles.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:23\n\nIt's a lot of animosity, frankly, just from the average user base in terms of demands made on maintainers or criticisms of how they handled an issue that's not really taking into consideration like that free formed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nOrganizational aspect of core, where it's the there are no bosses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:44\n\nLike you can't tell anybody to do anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nYou can either do something, especially not the maintainers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nYou can't tell the maintainers to just implement feature X or merge software Y because the maintainers, when they see a pull request that says, you know, double the coin supply, let's pick something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nThe maintainers may have an opinion about that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:06\n\nBut from their role as a maintainer, they need to sort of see what other developers think of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\n\nAnd so they need to wait for somebody else to review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:13\n\nThey can't just merge something because they like it or they don't like it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:17\n\nYeah, they're they're it's supposed to be somewhat janitorial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:20\n\nNow, of course, you know, things are always more nuanced than that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:23\n\nBut you know, ideally, a pull request appears on GitHub because somebody opens it and Maintainer sees it and says, Okay, that's just a pull request, but it hasn't had any review, so they just ignore it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nAnd then at some point, you know, it's got a few acts on it, and then a maintainer might see, Okay, I see an X or acknowledge, like, good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:42\n\nAnd then, okay, there's three of them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nBut then the second question is, Okay, who are those three acts?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:47\n\nAre those people who actually know what they're talking about?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:49\n\nOr this just drive-by comments?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:51\n\nBecause you don't wanna you know, it's not that the drive-by review is It's bad, it's useful, but there's, you know, you want to make sure that the code has been looked at by people who actually understand it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:03\n\nSo it's not just about the number.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:04\n\nAnd then hopefully, you know, there's two, maybe one or two experienced reviewers and a few others that have acted, and then the maintainer just says, okay, that meets the bar for quality, and just hits the merge button, and then they go to the next pull request, and that's all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:19\n\nAnd I think that's also usually how it goes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:21\n\nUh there's just a few exceptions where there's a bunch of drama on it, especially if somebody wants to get something in and then start promoting it on Twitter, then you've got 50,000 acts of by people who have never seen a line of code, probably haven't actually run it.\n\n\n\n## How a Pull Request Gets Merged\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:36\n\nCan't even read a line of code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:37\n\nAnd then you've got one neck, as in not acknowledged, like bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:41\n\nAnd if that one neck comes from somebody who does understand the code, then I might get a lot of weight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:45\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:46\n\nBecause the other aspect is, yeah, you you you only want to merge stuff that has gotten positive review and then does not have any objections.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:54\n\nSo w you you fair would you say it's fair to kind of characterize like their role and their job as kind of a responsibility to sideline their own opinions or attitudes and chiefly weigh and concern themselves with others?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:10\n\nLike it's definitely not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:11\n\nNo, no, that's that noble.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:13\n\nI I think there's often the case where if if a pull quest is is within the expertise of a maintainer, then that maintainer might review it and then merge it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:21\n\nSo their opinion does then matter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nWell then to say I guess maybe like a a chief aspect uh of their job is to consider and weigh other people's opinions and not simply react based on their own.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nYeah, they also have to consider other people's opinion.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:37\n\nI guess that's a better way to put it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:38\n\nSo they might have expertise in the area and they might themselves think it's fine and in that sense they're just another reviewer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:45\n\nUh but then they also have to put up I guess their more neutral maintainer hat and say, Okay, if I'm the only one who looked at this, then maybe, you know, other people should have looked at it as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:54\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:54\n\nBut I wouldn't go as far as that they're like Supreme Court justices that are like completely neutral on on everything.\n\n\n\n## GitHub Drama & The Problem with Drive-by Reviews\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:00\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:02\n\nDo you you wanna kinda like walk us through abstractly like the life cycle of a a pull request on Core?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nI think the example I just used, right, so uh it just appears because somebody's opened it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:13\n\nWhat happens is if if let's say if I open a pull request, well I need people to review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nAnd there's again, there's no CEO that says you shall review this pull request.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:22\n\nSo it might maybe nobody reviews it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:25\n\nBut usually, what happens is when a pull request is new, it sort of shows up on the first page of the GitHub list, and there's some notifications going into the IRC channel, like, hey, Shores opened the pull request.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nSo, usually, in the first couple of hours after you open something, you'll get a bunch of people's first impressions on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:43\n\nAnd then the art is to kind of keep them engaged.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:49\n\nIf the broadcast fixes something that other reviewers really care about, then they'll most likely review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:56\n\nAnd so you'll get feedback, and the feedback might be okay, just a few details like, hey, you know, you're not using indentation here, or this function name is wrong, or maybe add a test here or there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:09\n\nOr it can be more fundamental.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nIt's like, no, this approach is completely wrong, or you're solving the wrong problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:13\n\nAnd then You as the author of the pull request then have to deal with all that feedback either by fixing what they say you should fix or by explaining that they're they're asking for the wrong thing or sometimes even saying, Okay, this is reasonable, but it can wait for a follow-up, so I'm not gonna fix it here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nSo you've made those changes, then people will come back and review it again.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nAnd that could go on for a few rounds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:36\n\nAnd what can happen is it can sorta start stalling where You've got the first review, you fix the things, but then the reviewers just disappear for months at a time because they're doing other things.\n\n\n\n## Rebase Hell & Why PRs Stall\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:47\n\nOr as a reviewer, you've you've done all the review on the pull request and the person who wrote it disappears for three months and doesn't do anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:54\n\nSo things can get a little stuck and because people get notifications for new pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:58\n\nSo so a pull request can get completely forgotten almost and can be quite frustrating, especially if you're the author of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nIt it has this rebase hell, I guess is a term for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nBut the the main br uh the master branch of Bitcoin Core keeps changing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:12\n\nAnd so you may suddenly have to update your pull request to account for all the other changes that people have been making from under you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:19\n\nAnd you can be stuck in that situation for a while.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:22\n\nSo then it can be helpful to try and find reviewers more actively.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:24\n\nMaybe if you know who else worked on this area of the code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:29\n\nYou can ping them through some uh from one channel or the other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nAnd sometimes you just give up on it, I guess.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:34\n\nThat's another option.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:35\n\nNot a good one though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nNo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nWell, yeah, and some or sometimes you decide it's the wrong approach, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:42\n\nYeah, well I that I'd say it isn't killing up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:46\n\nThat it's it's smartening up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:50\n\nYeah, but it it is a tricky part to divide attention because again, there is no project manager or boss of Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:55\n\nSo you can have three hundred pull requests open and you kind of wanna I kinda like to imagine pull requests as rooms in a giant office building.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:03\n\nSo you get these one of these nice modernist buildings with big amounts of concrete and small windows and and if you walk through the hallways, there's not cubicles, there's like actual offices.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nSomebody can be in one of those offices for years.\nand nobody ever talks to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nAnd because you don't see each other, you don't see through the walls, it's all s uh siloed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nSo and this creates a problem where yeah, some of these offices are super crowded and there's like twenty people in there chatting about, but then other offices are completely abandoned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:31\n\nAnd you you can kind of paint people to to make them come to your office and and take a look.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:35\n\nAnd they might and then they might disappear again and you're al again alone in your little office for another year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:40\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:41\n\nUm yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:42\n\nSo it it it kinda sounds like, you know, th this dynamic isn't purely like a a resource issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:48\n\nI mean like you know, obviously if there were more developers with time, like this would alleviate maybe or you just end up with even more pull requests and more distraction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:58\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:58\n\n'Cause you need enough reviewers, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nI think the bottleneck tends to be review.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nSo there's just more pull requests and there's people willing to review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:06\n\nAnd so more developers could just mean more pull requests, which would mean less review per pull request, which would be bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nI think there's other dynamics.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:15\n\nIt's I mean I even slightly blame GitHub because they have this notification system that kind of draws your attention to the thing that changed most recently, whereas really ideally you should have your own list of projects in a separate system and decide what you want to work on, not based on what is pinging you all the time.\n\n\n\n## Is More Devs Always Better?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nAnd this can create a um a uh compounding effect where the pull requests that are active, they trigger the most notifications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nAnd so that's what you can spend your attention on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:40\n\nWhereas the pull requests that are just sitting quietly in a corner not changing, they don't notify you, so they get forgotten.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:46\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:48\n\nSo there is a there's a dynamic that's not just about the number of developers, but it's really about the the way people work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:53\n\nAnd again, nobody in charge to to change that way.\nMhm.\nYeah, it's it's m an incentive issue at the end of the day.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:59\n\nThere's no not even any politics involved, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:01\n\nThis isn't like nobody likes your software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThis is just you've made a change that some people find interesting, but they they're you're in this room and they haven't seen you for a while.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:12\n\nYou know, everybody only has so much time and when you're self-organizing, like y y you can't put yourself two places at once.\n\n\n\n## Why GitHub Notifications Skew Developer Attention\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:19\n\nYeah, but not everybody has the same way of self-organizing either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:23\n\nSome people might be super structured and getting things done and all these fancy methodologies, GTD, and others might just only look at GitHub notifications and only respond to those as they come in, and still others will have another system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:38\n\nI think something, you know, maybe not entirely created for this reason, but something Core has been doing to.\nkind of address this type of problem is like the working groups for different subprojects and kind of trying to get groups of developers focused on like a specific area or like end goal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nDo you want to talk a little bit about like how that came to be and you know what whether you see that kind of making a difference at least when somebody can get a working group together like Progress with what they're working on versus just another way to get people to pay attention to a s subset of the pull requests out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:18\n\nSo you basically a working group is could be a group of people that are focused on a specific feature that they want, say silent payments or something else, or the kernel.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:27\n\nAnd then those people might be in a signal group together or an IRC group together, depends on how they want to work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:33\n\nThen there might be somebody saying, Hey, we've got ten pull requests and somebody should look at number nine again.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:39\n\nSo I I yeah, it's it's another way to coordinate the work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:41\n\nAnd and some people like to do that in a more closed setting 'cause they feel m more comfortable about that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:47\n\nI mean the the the the actual code is on GitHub, but the the just nagging each other like who should work on what might be happening in a more private chat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:56\n\nOr they might even meet physically, which is always a good way to get stuff done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:59\n\nBut if the working group itself, you know, d doesn't have to work, you could y your signal group could just go dead or everybody's just waiting on this one person to do something and that person doesn't do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nSo it's not a ma it's not a silver bullet either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:11\n\nBut i i it could make sense.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nAnd I guess, you know, wh where do you see in in the longer term, like, core going in terms of how it organizes itself?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nI don't think much is gonna change.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:23\n\nI think the Main thing that will hopefully change is that core will be a more narrowly focused thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:30\n\nSo there might be separate projects.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:32\n\nBecause that I do think it's a problem now that Bitcoin Core is just too many things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:36\n\nI think a lot of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nLike the wallet versus the the consensus logic versus the the databases and just the different the RPCs and a bunch of other things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:46\n\nBut then it sounds nice in theory, but then if you think about how to actually split it and if that actually works, I'm not sure either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nBut I think the way people work together in an open source project is kind of like a company culture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:57\n\nIt just tends to stay pretty static.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:00\n\nPeople have their own workflows and they'll change a little bit over time, but there's no big intervention manager out there that could change the culture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:09\n\nSo I don't expect much to change.\n\n\n\n## Why Bitcoin Core Culture Is Hard to Change\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nI guess to kinda wrap everything up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:14\n\nWhat do you think People outside of core could kind of do to help alleviate some of the the tension and slow pace of things besides review more pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nOh, I was gonna say, review more pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nI mean, in general, the whole world should not be entitled.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:37\n\nThere is this thing where people might think, Oh, Bitcoin Core is this organization like this company or like the government.\nthat should just do things for me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:45\n\nIt's like, well, if you're not happy with what Bitcoin Core is doing, you should do it yourself, or you should set up, you know, fund an organization that does these things for you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:56\n\nYou don't have to write code yourself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:57\n\nYou can set up a the equivalent of OpenSATs that has a goal to make an alternative for Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:03\n\nAnd maybe Bitcoin Core would even welcome some competition to get some focus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:09\n\nComplaining that people are not doing the thing that you want them to do is uh just just a lot of negative energy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:15\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:15\n\nThen again, yeah, it's a free world, you can do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:17\n\nYeah, it's you know, like like I've said, whenever this topic comes up, like the people doing that, they're not paying any developer's salary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:26\n\nLike, what what are you doing?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:28\n\nYeah, if you're using something that other people made for you and you're not paying for it, then you don't really get to demand anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:34\n\nYeah, I mean it's people people need to think about how to support developers rather than just demand things from them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nYeah, and I don't know if the answer is trivial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:43\n\nI mean like I said, review can help, but also just testing can help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:46\n\nAnd filing good bug reports can help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:50\n\nBut there's other issues that are much more difficult to solve that you just can't unless you're involved with it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:56\n\nWell start contributing to Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nSounds good.\n\n\n\n## Final Thoughts & Call to Action\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:00\n\nThanks for sitting down and talking, Shorts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:01\n\nThank you.	## Intro: Sjors Provoost at MIT Bitcoin Expo\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHello everybody, I'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine sitting down with Sjors Provost from Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nSo we're going to talk a little bit today about kind of the internal organization and workflow of Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:17\n\nHopefully leaving everybody at the end of it with a little bit of an understanding of how developers approach their work and maybe, just maybe a little bit of understanding that they are not a homogeneous group that all have the same opinions about everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nOh, they certainly don't have the same opinions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:37\n\nI think five minutes on GitHub would convince you of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\n\nSo, um, yeah, you you've been contributing to Bitcoin as far back as I can remember.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:45\n\nI think the entire time I've been in this space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:47\n\nUh Bitcoin Core specifically in the middle of twenty seventeen I I made my first contribution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:53\n\nI was playing with uh Pieter Wuille's segwit project.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nOh no.\nTrying to see if it if it was actually running code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:00\n\nAnd then I ran into error messages that were nonsensical and I tried to improve some of the Bitcoin tests, Bitcoin Core tests to give better uh error messages.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:09\n\nAnd I think those were merged within a year or so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:13\n\nBut but once you once you you enter that rabbit hole, it's there's no way out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:19\n\nSo, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:20\n\nWhat what what's it like contributing to core?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:23\n\nLike just from a you know, a personal perspective?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nMainly it it varies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:28\n\nI mean sometimes it can be a little frustrating, but also it's very interesting.\n\n\n\n## What It’s Actually Like to Contribute to Bitcoin Core\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:32\n\nYou I found it nice to meet some really smart people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:35\n\nUm You know, because most of these peop most people are doing it for not for the money, they're doing it because they find the project interesting or whatever it is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nSo you just meet a lot of uh yeah, very smart people with good ideas.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:48\n\nAnd some of the sub projects you could almost say are super interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nUh there's also just very boring plumbing stuff that needs to happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:58\n\nI think that's very underappreciated uh from just your average user's point of view.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:02\n\nYeah, but of course, you know, not everybody is doing the boring plumbing stuff either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nI guess I'm I'm still somewhat lucky.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\n\nUm I'm just a reviewer, I'm not a maintainer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nSo, you know that's don't sell yourself short.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:14\n\nThat's that's important too.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nNo, but th th the thing is if you're the maintainer and the release is broken, like people are gonna complain and you can't ignore the problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:24\n\nWhereas if you're just a developer, you're like, well, I'm just gonna review things that are interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nSo I don't care about all these problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:30\n\nLet other people deal with the bugs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:32\n\nSo you know I'm not saying I'm doing that, but not my fault.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:37\n\nBut some s somebody gets all the complaints or at least, you know, can't you know, does doesn't want to ship broken code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\n\nSo there there is some and I think the specialty maintainers have to do some of the uh more ungrateful work of like somebody has to make the backports and actually ship them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:53\n\nAnd somebody has to you know, when when there's an actual bug in the release, can't just ignore it and then they'll have to cajole other developers to to be interested in in looking at it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:03\n\n'Cause ultimately nobody's in charge, so there's no CEO that says, like, you guys, get in a room and fix these fifteen bugs before you go home.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:10\n\nPeople can just decide to not fix any bugs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nIt's ki kind of a thankless job.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:15\n\nYou know, es especially um since Pieter Wuille and some others have kind of stepped down, and we've seen new maintainers take those roles.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:23\n\nIt's a lot of animosity, frankly, just from the average user base in terms of demands made on maintainers or criticisms of how they handled an issue that's not really taking into consideration like that free formed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nOrganizational aspect of core, where it's the there are no bosses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:44\n\nLike you can't tell anybody to do anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nYou can either do something, especially not the maintainers.\n\nYou can't tell the maintainers to just implement feature X or merge software Y because the maintainers, when they see a pull request that says, you know, double the coin supply, let's pick something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nThe maintainers may have an opinion about that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:06\n\nBut from their role as a maintainer, they need to sort of see what other developers think of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\n\nAnd so they need to wait for somebody else to review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:13\n\nThey can't just merge something because they like it or they don't like it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:17\n\nYeah, they're they're it's supposed to be somewhat janitorial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:20\n\nNow, of course, you know, things are always more nuanced than that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:23\n\nBut you know, ideally, a pull request appears on GitHub because somebody opens it and Maintainer sees it and says, Okay, that's just a pull request, but it hasn't had any review, so they just ignore it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nAnd then at some point, you know, it's got a few thumbs up on it, and then a maintainer might see, Okay, I see an X or acknowledge, like, good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:42\n\nAnd then, okay, there's three of them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nBut then the second question is, Okay, who are those three thumbs up?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:47\n\nAre those people who actually know what they're talking about?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:49\n\nOr this just drive-by comments?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:51\n\nBecause you don't wanna you know, it's not that the drive-by review is bad, it's useful, but there's, you know, you want to make sure that the code has been looked at by people who actually understand it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:03\n\nSo it's not just about the number.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:04\n\nAnd then hopefully, you know, there's two, maybe one or two experienced reviewers and a few others that have thumbs uped, and then the maintainer just says, okay, that meets the bar for quality, and just hits the merge button, and then they go to the next pull request, and that's all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:19\n\nAnd I think that's also usually how it goes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:21\n\nUh there's just a few exceptions where there's a bunch of drama on it, especially if somebody wants to get something in and then start promoting it on Twitter, then you've got 50,000 thumbs up by people who have never seen a line of code, probably haven't actually run it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:36\n\nCan't even read a line of code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:37\n\nAnd then you've got one thumb down, as in not acknowledged, like bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:41\n\nAnd if that one thumb down comes from somebody who does understand the code, then I might get a lot of weight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:45\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:46\n\nBecause the other aspect is, yeah, you you you only want to merge stuff that has gotten positive review and then does not have any objections.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:54\n\nSo would you say it's fair to kind of characterize like their role and their job as kind of a responsibility to sideline their own opinions or attitudes and chiefly weigh and concern themselves with others?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:10\n\nLike it's definitely not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:11\n\nNo, no, that's not noble.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:13\n\nI I think there's often the case where if if a pull request is within the expertise of a maintainer, then that maintainer might review it and then merge it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:21\n\nSo their opinion does then matter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nWell then to say I guess maybe like a chief aspect uh of their job is to consider and weigh other people's opinions and not simply react based on their own.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nYeah, they also have to consider other people's opinion.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:37\n\nI guess that's a better way to put it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:38\n\nSo they might have expertise in the area and they might themselves think it's fine and in that sense they're just another reviewer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:45\n\nUh but then they also have to put up I guess their more neutral maintainer hat and say, Okay, if I'm the only one who looked at this, then maybe, you know, other people should have looked at it as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:54\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:54\n\nBut I wouldn't go as far as that they're like Supreme Court justices that are like completely neutral on everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:00\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:02\n\nDo you wanna kinda like walk us through abstractly like the life cycle of a pull request on Core?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nI think the example I just used, right, so uh it just appears because somebody's opened it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:13\n\nWhat happens is if let's say if I open a pull request, well I need people to review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nAnd there's again, there's no CEO that says you shall review this pull request.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:22\n\nSo it might maybe nobody reviews it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:25\n\nBut usually, what happens is when a pull request is new, it sort of shows up on the first page of the GitHub list, and there's some notifications going into the IRC channel, like, hey, Shores opened the pull request.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nSo, usually, in the first couple of hours after you open something, you'll get a bunch of people's first impressions on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:43\n\nAnd then the art is to kind of keep them engaged.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:49\n\nIf the broadcast fixes something that other reviewers really care about, then they'll most likely review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:56\n\nAnd so you'll get feedback, and the feedback might be okay, just a few details like, hey, you know, you're not using indentation here, or this function name is wrong, or maybe add a test here or there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:09\n\nOr it can be more fundamental.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nIt's like, no, this approach is completely wrong, or you're solving the wrong problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:13\n\nAnd then You as the author of the pull request then have to deal with all that feedback either by fixing what they say you should fix or by explaining that they're they're asking for the wrong thing or sometimes even saying, Okay, this is reasonable, but it can wait for a follow-up, so I'm not gonna fix it here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nSo you've made those changes, then people will come back and review it again.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nAnd that could go on for a few rounds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:36\n\nAnd what can happen is it can sorta start stalling where You've got the first review, you fix the things, but then the reviewers just disappear for months at a time because they're doing other things.\n\n\n\n## Rebase Hell & Why PRs Stall\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:47\n\nOr as a reviewer, you've you've done all the review on the pull request and the person who wrote it disappears for three months and doesn't do anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:54\n\nSo things can get a little stuck and because people get notifications for new pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:58\n\nSo so a pull request can get completely forgotten almost and can be quite frustrating, especially if you're the author of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nIt it has this rebase hell, I guess is a term for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nBut the the main br uh the master branch of Bitcoin Core keeps changing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:12\n\nAnd so you may suddenly have to update your pull request to account for all the other changes that people have been making from under you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:19\n\nAnd you can be stuck in that situation for a while.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:22\n\nSo then it can be helpful to try and find reviewers more actively.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:24\n\nMaybe if you know who else worked on this area of the code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:29\n\nYou can ping them through some uh from one channel or the other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nAnd sometimes you just give up on it, I guess.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:34\n\nThat's another option.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:35\n\nNot a good one though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nNo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nWell, yeah, and some or sometimes you decide it's the wrong approach, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:42\n\nYeah, well I that I'd say it isn't killing up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:46\n\nThat it's it's smartening up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:50\n\nYeah, but it it is a tricky part to divide attention because again, there is no project manager or boss of Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:55\n\nSo you can have three hundred pull requests open and you kind of wanna I kinda like to imagine pull requests as rooms in a giant office building.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:03\n\nSo you get these one of these nice modernist buildings with big amounts of concrete and small windows and and if you walk through the hallways, there's not cubicles, there's like actual offices.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nSomebody can be in one of those offices for years.\nand nobody ever talks to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nAnd because you don't see each other, you don't see through the walls, it's all s uh siloed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nSo and this creates a problem where yeah, some of these offices are super crowded and there's like twenty people in there chatting about, but then other offices are completely abandoned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:31\n\nAnd you you can kind of paint people to to make them come to your office and and take a look.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:35\n\nAnd they might and then they might disappear again and you're al again alone in your little office for another year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:40\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:41\n\nUm yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:42\n\nSo it it it kinda sounds like, you know, th this dynamic isn't purely like a a resource issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:48\n\nI mean like you know, obviously if there were more developers with time, like this would alleviate maybe or you just end up with even more pull requests and more distraction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:58\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:58\n\n'Cause you need enough reviewers, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nI think the bottleneck tends to be review.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nSo there's just more pull requests and there's people willing to review it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:06\n\nAnd so more developers could just mean more pull requests, which would mean less review per pull request, which would be bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nI think there's other dynamics.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:15\n\nIt's I mean I even slightly blame GitHub because they have this notification system that kind of draws your attention to the thing that changed most recently, whereas really ideally you should have your own list of projects in a separate system and decide what you want to work on, not based on what is pinging you all the time.\n\n\n\n## Is More Devs Always Better?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nAnd this can create a um a uh compounding effect where the pull requests that are active, they trigger the most notifications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nAnd so that's what you can spend your attention on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:40\n\nWhereas the pull requests that are just sitting quietly in a corner not changing, they don't notify you, so they get forgotten.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:46\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:48\n\nSo there is a there's a dynamic that's not just about the number of developers, but it's really about the way people work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:53\n\nAnd again, nobody in charge to change that way.\nMhm.\nYeah, it's it's an incentive issue at the end of the day.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:59\n\nThere's no not even any politics involved, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:01\n\nThis isn't like nobody likes your software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThis is just you've made a change that some people find interesting, but they they're you're in this room and they haven't seen you for a while.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:12\n\nYou know, everybody only has so much time and when you're self-organizing, like y y you can't put yourself two places at once.\n\n\n\n## Why GitHub Notifications Skew Developer Attention\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:19\n\nYeah, but not everybody has the same way of self-organizing either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:23\n\nSome people might be super structured and getting things done and all these fancy methodologies, GTD, and others might just only look at GitHub notifications and only respond to those as they come in, and still others will have another system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:38\n\nI think something, you know, maybe not entirely created for this reason, but something Core has been doing to kind of address this type of problem is like the working groups for different subprojects and kind of trying to get groups of developers focused on like a specific area or like end goal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nDo you want to talk a little bit about like how that came to be and you know what whether you see that kind of making a difference at least when somebody can get a working group together like Progress with what they're working on versus just another way to get people to pay attention to a subset of the pull requests out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:18\n\nSo you basically a working group is could be a group of people that are focused on a specific feature that they want, say silent payments or something else, or the kernel.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:27\n\nAnd then those people might be in a Signal group together or an IRC group together, depends on how they want to work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:33\n\nThen there might be somebody saying, Hey, we've got ten pull requests and somebody should look at number nine again.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:39\n\nSo I I yeah, it's it's another way to coordinate the work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:41\n\nAnd and some people like to do that in a more closed setting 'cause they feel m more comfortable about that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:47\n\nI mean the the the the actual code is on GitHub, but the the just nagging each other like who should work on what might be happening in a more private chat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:56\n\nOr they might even meet physically, which is always a good way to get stuff done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:59\n\nBut if the working group itself, you know, doesn't have to work, you could y your Signal group could just go dead or everybody's just waiting on this one person to do something and that person doesn't do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nSo it's not a ma it's not a silver bullet either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:11\n\nBut I I it could make sense.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nAnd I guess, you know, where do you see in in the longer term, like, Core going in terms of how it organizes itself?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nI don't think much is gonna change.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:23\n\nI think the Main thing that will hopefully change is that Core will be a more narrowly focused thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:30\n\nSo there might be separate projects.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:32\n\nBecause that I do think it's a problem now that Bitcoin Core is just too many things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:36\n\nI think a lot of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nLike the wallet versus the consensus logic versus the databases and just the different RPCs and a bunch of other things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:46\n\nBut then it sounds nice in theory, but then if you think about how to actually split it and if that actually works, I'm not sure either.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nBut I think the way people work together in an open source project is kind of like a company culture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:57\n\nIt just tends to stay pretty static.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:00\n\nPeople have their own workflows and they'll change a little bit over time, but there's no big intervention manager out there that could change the culture.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:09\n\nSo I don't expect much to change.\n\n\n\n## Why Bitcoin Core Culture Is Hard to Change\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nI guess to kind of wrap everything up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:14\n\nWhat do you think? People outside of core could kind of do to help alleviate some of the tension and slow pace of things besides reviewing more pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nOh, I was gonna say, review more pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nI mean, in general, the whole world should not be entitled.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:37\n\nThere is this thing where people might think, Oh, Bitcoin Core is this organization like a company or like the government.\nthat should just do things for me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:45\n\nIt's like, well, if you're not happy with what Bitcoin Core is doing, you should do it yourself, or you should set up, you know, fund an organization that does these things for you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:56\n\nYou don't have to write code yourself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:57\n\nYou can set up the equivalent of OpenSats that has a goal to make an alternative for Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:03\n\nAnd maybe Bitcoin Core would even welcome some competition to get some focus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:09\n\nComplaining that people are not doing the thing that you want them to do is just a lot of negative energy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:15\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:15\n\nThen again, yeah, it's a free world, you can do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:17\n\nYeah, it's you know, like like I've said, whenever this topic comes up, like the people doing that, they're not paying any developer's salary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:26\n\nLike, what are you doing?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:28\n\nYeah, if you're using something that other people made for you and you're not paying for it, then you don't really get to demand anything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:34\n\nYeah, I mean it's people need to think about how to support developers rather than just demand things from them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nYeah, and I don't know if the answer is trivial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:43\n\nI mean like I said, reviewing can help, but also just testing can help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:46\n\nAnd filing good bug reports can help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:50\n\nBut there's other issues that are much more difficult to solve that you just can't unless you're involved with it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:56\n\nWell start contributing to Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nSounds good.\n\n\n\n## Final Thoughts & Call to Action\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:00\n\nThanks for sitting down and talking, Shorts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:01\n\nThank you.	Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine interviews Sjors Provoost, a long-time contributor to Bitcoin Core, regarding the internal organization, workflow, and culture of the open-source project. The conversation highlights that Bitcoin Core is not a homogeneous group but a decentralized community where maintainers act as "janitors" rather than bosses; they review pull requests based on community consensus and expert opinion rather than individual preference or top-down commands.\n\nThe dialogue details the lifecycle of a pull request, noting that while initial reviews are common, many projects stall due to the "rebase hell" caused by changes in the master branch, author disengagement, or a lack of follow-up from reviewers. A significant bottleneck is identified as the mismatch between available reviewers and the volume of active pull requests, exacerbated by GitHub's notification system which favors recent activity over quiet but stalled work.\n\nTo address coordination issues, Provoost explains the role of working groups (e.g., for specific features like silent payments) where developers self-organize via private chats or IRC to manage their specific areas of code. However, he warns that these groups can go dormant if key members drop off and notes that the core project culture is slow to change due to the lack of a central manager to intervene.\n\nFinally, Provoost advises against external users expecting Bitcoin Core developers to cater to every demand without compensation. He suggests that instead of complaining, users should contribute through code reviews, testing, and bug reports, or alternatively, fund their own development efforts or create competing projects (like OpenSats) to foster competition and focus within the ecosystem.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvtnQfVdLYU	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:30:34.231629+05:30	2026-04-21 16:55:17.792541+05:30
f68caab9-9750-4390-b6ca-1e0731732e94	e_yg6cLsQHE	conference	2025-04-06	{"Jameson Lopp"}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:02\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:03\n\nYou may know me from previous dramatic episodes such as the dismantling of the software thesis, or the griefing of the Bitcoin testnet, or more recently, the burning of quantum vulnerable Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nBut today, I'm not here to provide controversy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:21\n\nI'll do that next week at OpNext.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:25\n\nSo why are we here?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:27\n\nIt actually all started with a post from our favorite space monkey, Mononaut.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:33\n\nAnd Mononaut pointed out this interesting transaction that happened back in January, said, hey, this looks like address poisoning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:43\n\nAnd I had heard of this term before, but it's not really something that had come up much in the context of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nAnd you might ask yourself, well, what is address poisoning?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:55\n\nAnd of course, there's a clue there with the highlighting that Mono Naut provided in the screenshot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:03\n\nSo what is it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:05\n\nEssentially, this is an attack where someone goes out and they find a bunch of target Bitcoin addresses that look like juicy targets.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nThey probably have a decent amount of Bitcoin in them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:18\n\nAnd then they'll use a script to generate a vanity address that looks very similar to the target address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:25\n\nAnd in particular, it'll have similar starting characters and similar ending characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:30\n\nAnd this, of course, is just a simple brute forcing type of algorithm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:34\n\nYou just keep generating private keys until you get one that produces a script with an address that has those similar characteristics.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nThen they put a little bit of money into their newly generated address, and then they send that money to the target address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:49\n\nAnd we're usually talking like 500 to 1,000 Satoshi.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nSo not a ton of money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:54\n\nJust enough to get that transaction onto the blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:58\n\nAnd then they wait.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:00\n\nAnd they hope that what happens is that this victim eventually makes a mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nThe victim has to be naive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:10\n\nThey have to go back, look at their wallet transaction history, and be using a wallet that makes this easy and possible to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:18\n\nAnd they hope that that victim basically copies and pastes the wrong address from their transaction history and end up sending it to the attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:27\n\nSo, some example transactions here, just to show you that this hits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:32\n\nPretty much every different type of address that you'll see out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:36\n\nAnd on the left will be the poison address of the attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:41\n\nAnd you can see them sending a few hundred sats over to the target victim address, which has those similar starting and ending characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:51\n\nSo why does it work?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:54\n\nLike I said, people are not checking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:57\n\nAll of the address necessarily when they're sending it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:01\n\nThere are a number of especially mobile apps out there that may truncate a lot of the address, so it's actually very difficult to check the entire thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:09\n\nOr, you know, they're just lazy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nThis is long.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nWe're talking like what, 30 to 50 characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nNobody wants to check all of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:17\n\nAnd the UX may not make it easy to do so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nAnd then, of course, If their wallet makes it easy to copy it and paste from the transaction history, then that could make it easier for someone to make this mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:31\n\nSo, what did I do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:33\n\nWell, I wanted to understand what is the practicality of this attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:38\n\nIt clearly seems to be happening, but how often is it happening?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:42\n\nAnd is the attack actually economically feasible?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:46\n\nSo, I.\nWrote a script, I scanned the entire blockchain, and I tried to find every transaction that I thought was meeting these characteristics of address poisoning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:57\n\nAnd what in particular was I looking for?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:59\n\nI was looking for transactions with one input and one output.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nThose in and of themselves are fairly rare.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:06\n\nYou almost always have a second change output when you're sending money on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:12\n\nAnd then, of course, looking for having at least four characters at the beginning and the end of the exact same, and of course, not being the exact same address sending to itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:22\n\nAnd it's possible that there may be a few false positives in here, but I don't think that that's really the case because I scanned the entire Bitcoin blockchain and did not see any hits until just about a year ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nIn particular, Block 797570, which was July of 2023, is when we start seeing this happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nAnd it's not just one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:45\n\nIt's usually dozens of them happening in any given block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:48\n\nThis is happening in bursts, not in a consistent pattern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:52\n\nDefinitely seems to be a human-driven type of activity where they're probably doing some research, finding a new batch.\nof target victim addresses and then just sending all at once.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:05\n\nFirst, they're doing a big fan out transaction that'll have hundreds and hundreds of outputs to fund all of these poison addresses, and then each of those will have the one-to-one actual deposit address into the victim's account.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:20\n\nSo it was pretty quiet for several months, and then we saw another bash of them happening.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:25\n\nAnd it was quiet again, saw some more batches.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nAnd then there was a two-month break, and then they actually just started doing this again this week.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:33\n\nSo what were the stats?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:35\n\nWhat did we find from this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:37\n\nFound about 48,000 different transactions and unique Bitcoin addresses that seem to have been targeted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:46\n\nAnd you could easily sum up how much Block space did they buy?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:51\n\nThey bought about four blocks worth of block space over that year and a half, two-year period.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:58\n\nAnd they ended up spending about 0.3 Bitcoins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:01\n\nSo this attacker, they didn't spend a ton.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:05\n\nI mean, $24,000 isn't nothing, but considering that they were able to hit nearly 50,000 different targets, that's pretty decent.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:14\n\nYou know, 50 cents per target.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:17\n\nSo looking at some of the breakdowns, I was trying to understand, you know, how is this attacker trying to target the different addresses?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:26\n\nAnd this is sort of a breakdown of the wallet balances or the address balances that they were attacking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:32\n\nI don't know if there's too much to learn from this, because this is also fairly consistent with the overall Distribution of balances on the blockchain, other than the fact that, of course, they're ignoring dust addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:46\n\nSo they're generally ignoring any addresses that have less than one Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:52\n\nIf you throw out all of the less than one Bitcoin addresses, this is generally what the distribution looks like anyway.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nOne thing that I found really interesting, because I put myself in the shoes of the attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:05\n\nAnd I think for an attack like this, you're going to want to go after addresses that are pretty consistently and regularly spending.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:13\n\nBecause each time they're making a spin, that's a potential mistake that they might make.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nThey might choose the wrong address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:20\n\nAnd this is where I think the attacker, from my perspective, they did not optimize this very well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:26\n\nMaybe they had some other reason for doing this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:29\n\nBut the biggest surprise to me is that of the 48,000 addresses they targeted, 12,000 of those addresses had never spent funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:39\n\nSo maybe this is really just a spray and pray, and they're hoping maybe eventually someday, of course, that person's going to spend the coin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:46\n\nBut about a quarter of these addresses to this day have never spent those funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:54\n\nSo maybe they're just playing the really long game, but I think that that is a suboptimal attribute that they probably should have filtered out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:02\n\nAnd other than that, most of these addresses are very sparingly actually spending funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:09\n\nSo I was kind of surprised.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:11\n\nI thought that they would have gone after more addresses that have a higher velocity, a higher rate of spending.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:18\n\nSimilar to that, what is the amount of Bitcoin?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nLike I said, a quarter of them, zero spending, and then most of them are spending less than one Bitcoin per transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nSo what about script types?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nThis was also kind of interesting because they're targeting the pay-to-public key hash addresses, which are not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:43\n\nThe most commonly used addresses these days, but um you know perhaps that's just because they uh are older and you know they have a lot of funds sitting in them from long time ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:57\n\nAnd I didn't try to break down uh like uh SegWit wrapped P2SH.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:02\n\nI was just looking at the high-level scripts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:05\n\nI did not see any taproot addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nThat have been targeted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nMaybe that's just because taproot addresses tend not to have a lot of funds in them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:14\n\nThat could be another potential interesting thing to try to understand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:17\n\nBut for whatever reason, taproot is not being targeted by address poisoning, at least not yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:24\n\nSo is it successful?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nIs this a positive ROI?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:29\n\nWell, That took some more research because then I had to look at all of these addresses, and then I had to look to see if any of the victim addresses had sent funds back to a poison address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:43\n\nAnd that was tricky because you basically need to have an indexer where you've indexed the whole blockchain by address, not a lot of Local indexing solution to make it easy for that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nYou can't just call the Bitcoin Core RPC to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nBut I did find one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:05\n\nAnd the reason why I'm quite sure that this is actually a successful attack is because we can actually look and see what happened.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nAnd so this first transaction is the attacker sending from the poison address to Uh the target address, and you can see the time stamps in the upper right there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nAnd let's see, that was on January 18th.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:28\n\nAnd then a month and a half later, the target address sent money to the poison address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd within about 12 hours, the attacker, the owner of that poison address, moved the funds to some.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:45\n\nOther wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:47\n\nSo why do I think that this was an attack and not just, you know, s something else going on?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nWell, we can then see that same victim address wallet realize they made a mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:00\n\nAnd about twelve hours after they sent that first uh 0.1 bitcoin to the attacker, you can see they sent um 0.1 Bitcoin to some other address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nSo it looks like that second transaction was the one that they had actually intended to do, but they screwed up the first time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:21\n\nSo if we look at the ROI, it's not great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:26\n\nThey're down about 0.2 Bitcoin right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:30\n\nBut I think that this still is not great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:33\n\nThe fact that that it's a one out of forty eight thousand uh success rate is good in and of itself, but just due to the nature of Bitcoin, this very easily could have been a highly profitable attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:47\n\nThe attacker was unlucky that the one person who has been tricked so far only sent 0.1 Bitcoin, because they had eight Bitcoin in that address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:56\n\nThey very easily could have sent that entire balance.\nwhich would have made this overall a highly, highly profitable attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nAnd of course, like I said, this is an ongoing thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:08\n\nWe should expect that given enough transactions and enough target victims, someone else will probably make the same mistake and possibly for a lot more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:20\n\nSo from a practical perspective, how do you ensure that you don't shoot yourself in the foot here?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nWell, really the easiest thing and the most important thing, you just follow the best practice of not reusing addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nLike you shouldn't be copying and pasting addresses from your own transaction history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:38\n\nIf you don't do that, then there's really no way for you to get hit by this particular attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nAnd when you are sending Bitcoin, you should check the entire address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:50\n\nI think that's something that we've said as a community for a really long time, but humans are extremely lazy individuals.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nAnd this is the type of thing that I think you've just got to keep beating people over the head about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:04\n\nAnd this, now that we are aware of it, we can continue to monitor for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:09\n\nAnd I will say props to Mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:13\n\nBecause they, just in the past few weeks, have started adding alerting within their own block explorer where transactions that appear to be poison attempts are actually getting flagged.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nSo that hopefully this will save some people from making this mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:32\n\nBut of course, this is the nature of trustless and permissionless networks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nSo it's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:37\n\nI think going to be an ongoing issue and just a question of if it gets worse, do we as like a software and wallet development ecosystem need to be more proactive and uh take more measures to help prevent people from falling victim?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:53\n\nSure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:54\n\nThanks for your talk.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:55\n\nUh two two questions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:57\n\nOne is do you think you do you think it's a single entity that did this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nBased on, I guess, the clustering.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:04\n\nAnd the second is you only looked at transactions going from the real address to the fake address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:11\n\nBut I would imagine if I'm copying an address, the wrong one, I might be putting that in an exchange, or I might be putting it in my wallet, but it's picking a different coin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nSo have you also looked at how much money actually went to the fake addresses in total?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:26\n\nRather than just from From the real to the fake?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:28\n\nNo, these are both potential avenues for further research.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:33\n\nI so I didn't do clustering analysis.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:36\n\nThat it's not my forte, but I would be interested in looking into that further.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:41\n\nI mean, you can just make a wallet that includes all those fake addresses and see what the balance is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:46\n\nTrue, fair enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nJust one one wallet with 48,000.\nkeys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nI'm sure that'll scale just fine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nJust just open a pull request if it's too slow.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:58\n\nA GitHub issue, I mean.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:00\n\nYeah, so there's definitely there's definitely room for further research on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nUh and really I as far as I'm aware, I haven't heard of anyone like publicly broadcasting and saying that they have been a victim of these attacks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nSo these type of attacks seem to happen a lot on Ethereum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:30\n\nAnd I think it's just interesting to start to try to gauge, is this same technique starting to be applied more to Bitcoin?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nAnd how aware of it do we need to be?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:41\n\nAnother thing that I think is worth pointing out is that I think that this is also a result of the fact that we are in a very low fee environment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:51\n\nIf we had 100 Satoshi per virtual byte fees going on, I think that would greatly disincentivize people from doing a lot of these dusting attacks, unless they figured out other ways to increase the attack success rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:07\n\nYeah, at last year's Bitcoin Expo we had a nice little presentation on wallet fingerprinting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:11\n\nDo you know what wallet that victim was using so I know what wallet not to use?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:16\n\nNo, but I'm you know anyone who wants to kind of build upon this research, I've got all the raw data available and I'm more than happy to share all of the data that I've collected so far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:29\n\nSo that was one of my questions as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:32\n\nAnd then the follow-up to that was going to be, you know, you said like thinking like an attacker, how you would do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:38\n\nSo clearly, if the target wallet happens to be vulnerable in that it only shows the first few beginning characters of your address, then thinking like an attacker, those are like I would look for wallets with that fingerprint and only attack that particular wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:53\n\nBut I wanted to ask also if you're to kind of show one of the things that I work on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:57\n\nUm are you familiar with the the um the Bitcoin kernel project?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:00\n\nBecause you said dealing with like calling Bitcoin core RPCs over and over becomes untenable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:06\n\nSo one of one of the exact purposes of the Bitcoin kernel project is to make it so that you can write little tools to do exactly these types of indexes very quickly and easily.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:14\n\nSo if it's not something that you've looked at before, I'd be happy to talk to you about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:18\n\nNo, cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:18\n\nNo, I've heard of the kernel project, but I have not actually tested it out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:22\n\nSo, worth looking into.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:25\n\nGreat, thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:28\n\nSo, when I was looking at your slides, something probably obvious occurred to me: that the address type in the front of an address means that you don't have to grind that part, like BC1.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:45\n\nAnd so, did you notice?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nLike based on the address type, they were doing like more or less grinding.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:50\n\nAnd do you have any idea on how to like represent an address type that doesn't, from like a human vision perspective, make it cheaper to grind?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:59\n\nI let's see, I did not, like, for example, when looking at the BEC32 style addresses, I was still only seeing like another one or two.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:09\n\nI didn't see like You know, the first three and then another three or four.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:14\n\nI don't know why that's the case, but I also haven't played around with the Vanity Gen for those addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:21\n\nI think the main interesting thing here is that I think we've seen people, at least traditionally, Vanity Gen, you're only doing the first X number of characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:37\n\nAnd this is, I think, the first time we've seen people who are also doing the end characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:43\n\nAnd I think the main reason for that is that we may have unintentionally gotten people complacent with only checking the first few characters of the address under the idea that, oh, those are the only ones that could get brute force because you have to brute force in that direction starting from the beginning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:01\n\nSo I think this is just another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:03\n\nGood practice for people to be cognizant of is that just checking the first few is not good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:10\n\nJust checking the first few and the last few is not good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:12\n\nYou really need to check the entire address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:15\n\nSo I have a tweet here from our favorite mempool monkey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:19\n\nThe DMM Japanese exchange attack was an address poisoning, but your script, I don't think, picked it up because it was going from their internal cold storage, which was a pay-do-do script hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:30\n\nTo a paid public key hash for their hot address, and that was 4,500 Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:36\n\nThat was a two of three, and they did address poisoning on their hot wallet address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:42\n\nSo they managed to trick the exchange into co-signing and then doing the 4,500 Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:47\n\nSo I think that's operationally internally, thinking if you're managing large sums too, managing that stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nBut I think I just wanted to call that out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:56\n\nYeah, I mean I think that there's well, there's definitely more research to be done to understand how some of the different wallets display like multi-output receive transactions, for example.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:11\n\nI think I saw you tweeted something recently where Mimpool was showing the poison, a potential poison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:18\n\nUh output where it was it had probably like five or ten outputs that had similar looking addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:24\n\nAnd the the the main question there is just how do those get displayed in the wallets?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:28\n\nUm and it's gonna vary from wallet to wallet and that's why I think doing the wallet fingerprinting and then looking at each wallet's own displays to understand which ones are gonna be more susceptible to that would be an important avenue of research here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:41\n\nSo that but that's why I think that m There's a lot of them that are just one input, one output, because it's so simple and it's more likely that any given wallet is gonna show both of those addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:52\n\nThank you for your talk.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:53\n\nI've had this experience um on the Solana blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nI opened a wallet one day and saw one point four million dollars worth of Solana that had exited my wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:02\n\nAnd uh I just thought of the movie Fargo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:04\n\nIf you've seen it, there's a bag of money that gets passed around and bad things happen to everybody.\ngets the bag.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:09\n\nSo I was happy that that didn't happen to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:12\n\nBut what I did learn in my investigation of dusting was that for people in that kind of case, they're being targeted for doxing and blackmail and things like that as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:22\n\nSo I really do appreciate the talk because I don't think it's talked about enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:26\n\nI'm a trader.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nI teach trading.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:28\n\nAnd when I brought it up to people in my group, they one of the guys was like, oh, yeah, you should turn.\nyour wallet off to certain types of of dusting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:36\n\nAnd I said, yes, but you're you have ten years of experience in crypto and five years of trading and regular people are not aware of this at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nSo thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:45\n\nYeah, no, I mean there's there's room, I think, for pretty much any wallet out there to try to safeguard against this attack, though this this is this is where it gets tricky.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:56\n\nLike it As a wallet software developer, it would not be difficult at all to just add in a filter rule that says do not display any incoming transactions below a value of X.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:10\n\nBut that can get tricky too, especially if we're talking about people who are using meta protocols on top of Bitcoin, where they may be sending dust, but it's representing something else.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:20\n\nSo, it's very hard, I think, to just tell everyone across the board: you know, never display any transactions that are depositing less than a thousand Satoshis or whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:30\n\nBut at the very least, I think that it could be helpful to show some sort of warning, especially if you see these addresses look the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nLike, I think it would be easy for wallets to say, oh, this came from a similar-looking address, just throw up a big red flag, um, do not interact.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:49\n\nSo do we have anything uh else to offer in terms of inspecting addresses that isn't just checking the full like long string that doesn't fit in like portrait mode on mobile?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:01\n\nLike there's SSH like ashy key art and like other things, but do you think there's any other like alternative that it would increase safety besides just If I recall correctly, Blockchain Commons had a standard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:15\n\nI don't, was that what you were referencing?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:17\n\nI'm not referencing something specific.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:19\n\nYeah, so I think Blockchain Commons had a standard for addresses that basically turns it into a like 16 by 16 or 32 by 32 pixel, you know, colorized pixel map that would just make it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:34\n\nI think it's easier for you to differentiate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:37\n\nLike, is it the same address or a different address?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:40\n\nBut as far as I'm aware, there aren't any wallets, or at least any mainstream-level adopted big wallets that have adopted that standard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:49\n\nBut it's certainly possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:51\n\nAnd really, what we're talking about is like a data visualization representation problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:56\n\nAnd I think that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:58\n\nUm as nerds, we m might be used to seeing really long hashes and stuff, but we have to understand that you know the average person who sees uh a long hash or really any long random string, their eyes are just gonna glaze over and they're just gonna start clicking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:17\n\nHi, Jamison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:18\n\nAt the end of all of this, are you gonna tell us that you're actually the attacker the whole time?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:23\n\nWell, no, that would be incredibly embarrassing because I lost so much money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:28\n\nNo, you know, if if this had turned out to be a highly profitable attack, then I would be more worried about people looking into doing it more often.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:39\n\nBut I'm a I'm the generally a security researcher.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:42\n\nI've been building multi-sig security products for ten years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:46\n\nFor me, this is just raise the alarm and if people wish to continue the research to figure out what is the severity so that this is a sort of community security problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:01\n\nWe have to be aware of this dynamic security environment and new types of attacks that seem to be popping up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:11\n\nSo yeah, I had someone pull me aside earlier and say, I hate it whenever you give a talk because I always get really scared.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nAnd unfortunately, that's the nature of the game.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:26\n\nBut the nice thing about this, you really don't have to be scared about this as long as you're not reusing addresses or not just copying addresses willy-nilly out of your transaction history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nEveryone, practice good blockchain hygiene, and you'll be just fine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:41\n\nThank you.	Speaker 0: 00:00:02\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:03\n\nYou may know me from previous dramatic episodes such as the dismantling of the software thesis, or the griefing of the Bitcoin testnet, or more recently, the burning of quantum vulnerable Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nBut today, I'm not here to provide controversy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:21\n\nI'll do that next week at OpNext.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:25\n\nSo why are we here?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:27\n\nIt actually all started with a post from our favorite space monkey, Mononaut.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:33\n\nAnd Mononaut pointed out this interesting transaction that happened back in January, said, hey, this looks like address poisoning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:43\n\nAnd I had heard of this term before, but it's not really something that had come up much in the context of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nAnd you might ask yourself, well, what is address poisoning?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:55\n\nAnd of course, there's a clue there with the highlighting that Mono Naut provided in the screenshot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:03\n\nSo what is it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:05\n\nEssentially, this is an attack where someone goes out and they find a bunch of target Bitcoin addresses that look like juicy targets.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nThey probably have a decent amount of Bitcoin in them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:18\n\nAnd then they'll use a script to generate a vanity address that looks very similar to the target address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:25\n\nAnd in particular, it'll have similar starting characters and similar ending characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:30\n\nAnd this, of course, is just a simple brute forcing type of algorithm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:34\n\nYou just keep generating private keys until you get one that produces a script with an address that has those similar characteristics.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nThen they put a little bit of money into their newly generated address, and then they send that money to the target address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:49\n\nAnd we're usually talking like 500 to 1,000 Satoshi.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nSo not a ton of money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:54\n\nJust enough to get that transaction onto the blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:58\n\nAnd then they wait.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:00\n\nAnd they hope that what happens is that this victim eventually makes a mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nThe victim has to be naive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:10\n\nThey have to go back, look at their wallet transaction history, and be using a wallet that makes this easy and possible to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:18\n\nAnd they hope that that victim basically copies and pastes the wrong address from their transaction history and end up sending it to the attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:27\n\nSo, some example transactions here, just to show you that this hits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:32\n\nPretty much every different type of address that you'll see out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:36\n\nAnd on the left will be the poison address of the attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:41\n\nAnd you can see them sending a few hundred sats over to the target victim address, which has those similar starting and ending characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:51\n\nSo why does it work?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:54\n\nLike I said, people are not checking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:57\n\nAll of the address necessarily when they're sending it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:01\n\nThere are a number of especially mobile apps out there that may truncate a lot of the address, so it's actually very difficult to check the entire thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:09\n\nOr, you know, they're just lazy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nThis is long.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nWe're talking like what, 30 to 50 characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nNobody wants to check all of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:17\n\nAnd the UX may not make it easy to do so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nAnd then, of course, If their wallet makes it easy to copy it and paste from the transaction history, then that could make it easier for someone to make this mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:31\n\nSo, what did I do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:33\n\nWell, I wanted to understand what is the practicality of this attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:38\n\nIt clearly seems to be happening, but how often is it happening?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:42\n\nAnd is the attack actually economically feasible?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:46\n\nSo, I.\nWrote a script, I scanned the entire blockchain, and I tried to find every transaction that I thought was meeting these characteristics of address poisoning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:57\n\nAnd what in particular was I looking for?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:59\n\nI was looking for transactions with one input and one output.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nThose in and of themselves are fairly rare.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:06\n\nYou almost always have a second change output when you're sending money on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:12\n\nAnd then, of course, looking for having at least four characters at the beginning and the end of the exact same, and of course, not being the exact same address sending to itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:22\n\nAnd it's possible that there may be a few false positives in here, but I don't think that that's really the case because I scanned the entire Bitcoin blockchain and did not see any hits until just about a year ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nIn particular, Block 797,570, which was July of 2023, is when we start seeing this happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nAnd it's not just one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:45\n\nIt's usually dozens of them happening in any given block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:48\n\nThis is happening in bursts, not in a consistent pattern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:52\n\nDefinitely seems to be a human-driven type of activity where they're probably doing some research, finding a new batch\nof target victim addresses and then just sending all at once.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:05\n\nFirst, they're doing a big fan out transaction that'll have hundreds and hundreds of outputs to fund all of these poison addresses, and then each of those will have the one-to-one actual deposit address into the victim's account.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:20\n\nSo it was pretty quiet for several months, and then we saw another bash of them happening.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:25\n\nAnd it was quiet again, saw some more batches.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nAnd then there was a two-month break, and then they actually just started doing this again this week.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:33\n\nSo what were the stats?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:35\n\nWhat did we find from this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:37\n\nFound about 48,000 different transactions and unique Bitcoin addresses that seem to have been targeted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:46\n\nAnd you could easily sum up how much block space did they buy?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:51\n\nThey bought about four blocks worth of block space over that year and a half, two-year period.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:58\n\nAnd they ended up spending about 0.3 Bitcoins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:01\n\nSo this attacker, they didn't spend a ton.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:05\n\nI mean, $24,000 isn't nothing, but considering that they were able to hit nearly 50,000 different targets, that's pretty decent.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:14\n\nYou know, 50 cents per target.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:17\n\nSo looking at some of the breakdowns, I was trying to understand, you know, how is this attacker trying to target the different addresses?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:26\n\nAnd this is sort of a breakdown of the wallet balances or the address balances that they were attacking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:32\n\nI don't know if there's too much to learn from this, because this is also fairly consistent with the overall distribution of balances on the blockchain, other than the fact that, of course, they're ignoring dust addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:46\n\nSo they're generally ignoring any addresses that have less than one Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:52\n\nIf you throw out all of the less than one Bitcoin addresses, this is generally what the distribution looks like anyway.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nOne thing that I found really interesting, because I put myself in the shoes of the attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:05\n\nAnd I think for an attack like this, you're going to want to go after addresses that are pretty consistently and regularly spending.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:13\n\nBecause each time they're making a spin, that's a potential mistake that they might make.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nThey might choose the wrong address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:20\n\nAnd this is where I think the attacker, from my perspective, they did not optimize this very well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:26\n\nMaybe they had some other reason for doing this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:29\n\nBut the biggest surprise to me is that of the 48,000 addresses they targeted, 12,000 of those addresses had never spent funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:39\n\nSo maybe this is really just a spray and pray, and they're hoping maybe eventually someday, of course, that person's going to spend the coin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:46\n\nBut about a quarter of these addresses to this day have never spent those funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:54\n\nSo maybe they're just playing the really long game, but I think that that is a suboptimal attribute that they probably should have filtered out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:02\n\nAnd other than that, most of these addresses are very sparingly actually spending funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:09\n\nSo I was kind of surprised.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:11\n\nI thought that they would have gone after more addresses that have a higher velocity, a higher rate of spending.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:18\n\nSimilar to that, what is the amount of Bitcoin?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nLike I said, a quarter of them, zero spending, and then most of them are spending less than one Bitcoin per transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nSo what about script types?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nThis was also kind of interesting because they're targeting the pay-to-public key hash addresses, which are not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:43\n\nThe most commonly used addresses these days, but um you know perhaps that's just because they uh are older and you know they have a lot of funds sitting in them from long time ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:57\n\nAnd I didn't try to break down uh like uh SegWit wrapped P2SH.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:02\n\nI was just looking at the high-level scripts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:05\n\nI did not see any Taproot addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nThat have been targeted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nMaybe that's just because Taproot addresses tend not to have a lot of funds in them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:14\n\nThat could be another potential interesting thing to try to understand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:17\n\nBut for whatever reason, Taproot is not being targeted by address poisoning, at least not yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:24\n\nSo is it successful?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nIs this a positive ROI?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:29\n\nWell, That took some more research because then I had to look at all of these addresses, and then I had to look to see if any of the victim addresses had sent funds back to a poison address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:43\n\nAnd that was tricky because you basically need to have an indexer where you've indexed the whole blockchain by address, not a lot of Local indexing solution to make it easy for that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nYou can't just call the Bitcoin Core RPC to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nBut I did find one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:05\n\nAnd the reason why I'm quite sure that this is actually a successful attack is because we can actually look and see what happened.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nAnd so this first transaction is the attacker sending from the poison address to Uh the target address, and you can see the time stamps in the upper right there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nAnd let's see, that was on January 18th.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:28\n\nAnd then a month and a half later, the target address sent money to the poison address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd within about 12 hours, the attacker, the owner of that poison address, moved the funds to some.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:45\n\nOther wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:47\n\nSo why do I think that this was an attack and not just, you know, something else going on?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nWell, we can then see that same victim address wallet realize they made a mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:00\n\nAnd about twelve hours after they sent that first uh 0.1 Bitcoin to the attacker, you can see they sent um 0.1 Bitcoin to some other address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nSo it looks like that second transaction was the one that they had actually intended to do, but they screwed up the first time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:21\n\nSo if we look at the ROI, it's not great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:26\n\nThey're down about 0.2 Bitcoin right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:30\n\nBut I think that this still is not great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:33\n\nThe fact that that it's a one out of forty-eight thousand uh success rate is good in and of itself, but just due to the nature of Bitcoin, this very easily could have been a highly profitable attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:47\n\nThe attacker was unlucky that the one person who has been tricked so far only sent 0.1 Bitcoin, because they had eight Bitcoin in that address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:56\n\nThey very easily could have sent that entire balance.\nwhich would have made this overall a highly, highly profitable attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nAnd of course, like I said, this is an ongoing thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:08\n\nWe should expect that given enough transactions and enough target victims, someone else will probably make the same mistake and possibly for a lot more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:20\n\nSo from a practical perspective, how do you ensure that you don't shoot yourself in the foot here?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nWell, really the easiest thing and the most important thing, you just follow the best practice of not reusing addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nLike you shouldn't be copying and pasting addresses from your own transaction history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:38\n\nIf you don't do that, then there's really no way for you to get hit by this particular attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nAnd when you are sending Bitcoin, you should check the entire address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:50\n\nI think that's something that we've said as a community for a really long time, but humans are extremely lazy individuals.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nAnd this is the type of thing that I think you've just got to keep beating people over the head about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:04\n\nAnd this, now that we are aware of it, we can continue to monitor for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:09\n\nAnd I will say props to Mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:13\n\nBecause they, just in the past few weeks, have started adding alerting within their own block explorer where transactions that appear to be poison attempts are actually getting flagged.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nSo that hopefully this will save some people from making this mistake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:32\n\nBut of course, this is the nature of trustless and permissionless networks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nSo it's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:37\n\nI think going to be an ongoing issue and just a question of if it gets worse, do we as like a software and wallet development ecosystem need to be more proactive and uh take more measures to help prevent people from falling victim?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:53\n\nSure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:54\n\nThanks for your talk.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:55\n\nUh two questions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:57\n\nOne is do you think, do you think it's a single entity that did this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nBased on, I guess, the clustering.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:04\n\nAnd the second is you only looked at transactions going from the real address to the fake address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:11\n\nBut I would imagine if I'm copying an address, the wrong one, I might be putting that in an exchange, or I might be putting it in my wallet, but it's picking a different coin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nSo have you also looked at how much money actually went to the fake addresses in total?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:26\n\nRather than just from the real to the fake?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:28\n\nNo, these are both potential avenues for further research.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:33\n\nI so I didn't do clustering analysis.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:36\n\nIt's not my forte, but I would be interested in looking into that further.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:41\n\nI mean, you can just make a wallet that includes all those fake addresses and see what the balance is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:46\n\nTrue, fair enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nJust one wallet with 48,000 keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nI'm sure that'll scale just fine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nJust, just open a pull request if it's too slow.\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:58\n\nA GitHub issue, I mean.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:00\n\nYeah, so there's definitely, there's definitely room for further research on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nUh and really, as far as I'm aware, I haven't heard of anyone like publicly broadcasting and saying that they have been a victim of these attacks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nSo these types of attacks seem to happen a lot on Ethereum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:30\n\nAnd I think it's just interesting to start to try to gauge, is this same technique starting to be applied more to Bitcoin?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nAnd how aware of it do we need to be?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:41\n\nAnother thing that I think is worth pointing out is that I think that this is also a result of the fact that we are in a very low fee environment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:51\n\nIf we had 100 Satoshi per virtual byte fees going on, I think that would greatly disincentivize people from doing a lot of these dusting attacks, unless they figured out other ways to increase the attack success rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:07\n\nYeah, at last year's Bitcoin Expo we had a nice little presentation on wallet fingerprinting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:11\n\nDo you know what wallet that victim was using? So I know what wallet not to use?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:16\n\nNo, but anyone who wants to kind of build upon this research, I've got all the raw data available and I'm more than happy to share all of the data that I've collected so far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:29\n\nSo that was one of my questions as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:32\n\nAnd then the follow-up to that was going to be, you know, you said like thinking like an attacker, how you would do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:38\n\nSo clearly, if the target wallet happens to be vulnerable in that it only shows the first few beginning characters of your address, then thinking like an attacker, those are like I would look for wallets with that fingerprint and only attack that particular wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:53\n\nBut I wanted to ask also if you're to kind of show one of the things that I work on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:57\n\nUm, are you familiar with the, the um, the Bitcoin kernel project?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:00\n\nBecause you said dealing with like calling Bitcoin Core RPCs over and over becomes untenable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:06\n\nSo one of the exact purposes of the Bitcoin Kernel project is to make it so that you can write little tools to do exactly these types of indexes very quickly and easily.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:14\n\nSo if it's not something that you've looked at before, I'd be happy to talk to you about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:18\n\nNo, cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:18\n\nNo, I've heard of the kernel project, but I have not actually tested it out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:22\n\nSo, worth looking into.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:25\n\nGreat, thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:28\n\nSo, when I was looking at your slides, something probably obvious occurred to me: that the address type in the front of an address means that you don't have to grind that part, like BC1.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:45\n\nAnd so, did you notice?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nLike based on the address type, they were doing like more or less grinding.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:50\n\nAnd do you have any idea on how to like represent an address type that doesn't, from like a human vision perspective, make it cheaper to grind?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:59\n\nI let's see, I did not, like, for example, when looking at BEC32 style addresses, I was still only seeing like another one or two.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:09\n\nI didn't see like you know, the first three and then another three or four.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:14\n\nI don't know why that's the case, but I also haven't played around with the VanityGen for those addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:21\n\nI think the main interesting thing here is that I think we've seen people, at least traditionally, VanityGen, you're only doing the first X number of characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:37\n\nAnd this is, I think, the first time we've seen people who are also doing the end characters.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:43\n\nAnd I think the main reason for that is that we may have unintentionally gotten people complacent with only checking the first few characters of the address under the idea that, oh, those are the only ones that could get brute force because you have to brute force in that direction starting from the beginning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:01\n\nSo I think this is just another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:03\n\nGood practice for people to be cognizant of is that just checking the first few is not good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:10\n\nJust checking the first few and the last few is not good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:12\n\nYou really need to check the entire address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:15\n\nSo I have a tweet here from our favorite mempool monkey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:19\n\nThe DMM Japanese exchange attack was an address poisoning, but your script, I don't think, picked it up because it was going from their internal cold storage, which was a pay-to-script-hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:30\n\nTo a pay-to-public-key-hash for their hot address, and that was 4,500 Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:36\n\nThat was a two of three, and they did address poisoning on their hot wallet address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:42\n\nSo they managed to trick the exchange into co-signing and then doing the 4,500 Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:47\n\nSo I think that's operationally internally, thinking if you're managing large sums too, managing that stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nBut I think I just wanted to call that out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:56\n\nYeah, I mean I think that there's well, there's definitely more research to be done to understand how some of the different wallets display like multi-output receive transactions, for example.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:11\n\nI think I saw you tweeted something recently where Mempool was showing the poison, a potential poison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:18\n\nUh output where it was it had probably like five or ten outputs that had similar looking addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:24\n\nAnd the the the main question there is just how do those get displayed in the wallets?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:28\n\nUm and it's gonna vary from wallet to wallet and that's why I think doing the wallet fingerprinting and then looking at each wallet's own displays to understand which ones are gonna be more susceptible to that would be an important avenue of research here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:41\n\nSo that but that's why I think that mem There's a lot of them that are just one input, one output, because it's so simple and it's more likely that any given wallet is gonna show both of those addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:52\n\nThank you for your talk.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:53\n\nI've had this experience um on the Solana blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nI opened a wallet one day and saw one point four million dollars worth of Solana that had exited my wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:02\n\nAnd uh I just thought of the movie Fargo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:04\n\nIf you've seen it, there's a bag of money that gets passed around and bad things happen to everybody who gets the bag.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:09\n\nSo I was happy that that didn't happen to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:12\n\nBut what I did learn in my investigation of dusting was that for people in that kind of case, they're being targeted for doxing and blackmail and things like that as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:22\n\nSo I really do appreciate the talk because I don't think it's talked about enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:26\n\nI'm a trader.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nI teach trading.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:28\n\nAnd when I brought it up to people in my group, they one of the guys was like, oh, yeah, you should turn your wallet off to certain types of dusting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:36\n\nAnd I said, yes, but you're you have ten years of experience in crypto and five years of trading and regular people are not aware of this at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nSo thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:45\n\nYeah, no, I mean there's there's room, I think, for pretty much any wallet out there to try to safeguard against this attack, though this this is this is where it gets tricky.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:56\n\nLike it as a wallet software developer, it would not be difficult at all to just add in a filter rule that says do not display any incoming transactions below a value of X.\n\nBut that can get tricky too, especially if we're talking about people who are using meta protocols on top of Bitcoin, where they may be sending dust, but it's representing something else.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:20\n\nSo, it's very hard, I think, to just tell everyone across the board: you know, never display any transactions that are depositing less than a thousand Satoshis or whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:30\n\nBut at the very least, I think that it could be helpful to show some sort of warning, especially if you see these addresses look the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nLike, I think it would be easy for wallets to say, oh, this came from a similar-looking address, just throw up a big red flag, um, do not interact.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:49\n\nSo do we have anything uh else to offer in terms of inspecting addresses that isn't just checking the full like long string that doesn't fit in like portrait mode on mobile?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:01\n\nLike there's SSH-like ASCII art and like other things, but do you think there's any other like alternative that it would increase safety besides just if I recall correctly, Blockchain Commons had a standard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:15\n\nI don't, was that what you were referencing?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:17\n\nI'm not referencing something specific.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:19\n\nYeah, so I think Blockchain Commons had a standard for addresses that basically turns it into a like 16 by 16 or 32 by 32 pixel, you know, colorized pixel map that would just make it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:34\n\nI think it's easier for you to differentiate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:37\n\nLike, is it the same address or a different address?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:40\n\nBut as far as I'm aware, there aren't any wallets, or at least any mainstream-level adopted big wallets that have adopted that standard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:49\n\nBut it's certainly possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:51\n\nAnd really, what we're talking about is like a data visualization representation problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:56\n\nAnd I think that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:58\n\nUm, as nerds, we might be used to seeing really long hashes and stuff, but we have to understand that you know the average person who sees uh a long hash or really any long random string, their eyes are just gonna glaze over and they're just gonna start clicking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:17\n\nHi, Jameson.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:18\n\nAt the end of all of this, are you gonna tell us that you're actually the attacker the whole time?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:23\n\nWell, no, that would be incredibly embarrassing because I lost so much money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:28\n\nNo, you know, if this had turned out to be a highly profitable attack, then I would be more worried about people looking into doing it more often.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:39\n\nBut I'm a, I'm the generally a security researcher.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:42\n\nI've been building multi-sig security products for ten years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:46\n\nFor me, this is just raise the alarm and if people wish to continue the research to figure out what is the severity so that this is a sort of community security problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:01\n\nWe have to be aware of this dynamic security environment and new types of attacks that seem to be popping up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:11\n\nSo yeah, I had someone pull me aside earlier and say, I hate it whenever you give a talk because I always get really scared.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nAnd unfortunately, that's the nature of the game.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:26\n\nBut the nice thing about this, you really don't have to be scared about this as long as you're not reusing addresses or not just copying addresses willy-nilly out of your transaction history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nEveryone, practice good blockchain hygiene, and you'll be just fine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:41\n\nThank you.	This talk investigates "address poisoning," a security vulnerability in Bitcoin where attackers create fake addresses that visually mimic real victim addresses by brute-forcing similar starting and ending characters. The speaker explains that while this attack requires only small fees (hundreds of satoshis) to broadcast, it relies entirely on human error: victims using mobile wallets often truncate long addresses or lazily copy/paste them from their transaction history instead of verifying the full string.\n\nThe speaker conducted a blockchain-wide scan since 2023 and discovered approximately 48,000 poisoning transactions spanning roughly four blocks, costing attackers about $24,000 total. Despite this relatively low investment, the attack was highly scalable due to the trustless nature of Bitcoin. A significant flaw in the current campaign was that attackers targeted nearly a quarter of addresses that had never spent funds, likely operating under a "spray and pray" mindset rather than optimizing for high-velocity targets where mistakes are more frequent. While the speaker confirmed at least one successful attack (where a victim sent 0.1 BTC to a fake address and immediately regretted it), the overall return on investment remains low because victims often send only partial funds or quickly realize the error.\n\nKey defense strategies emphasized include never reusing addresses, avoiding the copying/pasting of addresses from one's own transaction history, and meticulously verifying the entire length of any address before confirming a payment. The discussion also highlights that major blockchain explorers like Mempool are now flagging these suspicious patterns. Potential future improvements involve wallet software filtering out small dust transactions or using alternative visual representations (like pixelated color maps) to help users distinguish between similar addresses, though widespread adoption is currently limited. The talk concludes with a reminder that good blockchain hygiene remains the most effective defense against this evolving threat.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_yg6cLsQHE	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{"Address Poisoning",Self-Custody,Security}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:11:10.757342+05:30	2026-04-21 22:55:00.257139+05:30
2f9272c9-bda7-46a7-887c-d7c3a0fd296f	ckvTy0Fsc_M	conference	2025-04-08	{"Frank Corva","Sjors Provoost"}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nFrank Corva, Bitcoin magazine here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:02\n\nI've been covering the samurai case in New York in the Southern District.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:06\n\nAnd Schorst, over to my left here, if you want a quick introduction maybe.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:09\n\nYeah, I'm uh Shors Provost.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:11\n\nI'm based in the Netherlands and I normally work on Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:14\n\nUm but there's some things happening in the Netherlands that uh I'm also paying attention to, which we're gonna talk about today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:20\n\nLet's dive right in right there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:22\n\nSo you've been in the courtroom for what's happening with the Tornado Cash trial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:26\n\nThe portion of it that's happened in the Netherlands.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nIt's also going to be happening in part in the Southern District of New York as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nI think that's actually a week from Monday.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:33\n\nWe're going to have, I think, one of the first hearings in the Southern District.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:36\n\nBut let's set the stage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:37\n\nLet's sort of talk about what's at stake right now when it comes to using Bitcoin crypto mixers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:42\n\nWhat is the environment right now?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:44\n\nMaybe if you want to dive in on the Tornado Cash side, I'll give a little bit of an overview on the Samurai wallet side.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:49\n\nYeah, and and maybe we wanna go back a little bit further even.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nPlease, there have been mixers before.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nMost of these would be custodial mixers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:58\n\nSo essentially you give your co-coins to some website and that website gives you some coins back.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:06\n\nAnd hopefully then there's no link between them, but the website could screw you over.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:11\n\nAnd well, there have been arrests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:13\n\nAround that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nAnd then I guess we've mostly not been paying attention to those as a community because we're thinking, well, they're custodials and custodians have different rules, so whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:26\n\nBut now we're seeing people getting arrested for building non-custodial tools and even autonomous tools, which I guess we we can explain the differences there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:36\n\nSo let's start there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:37\n\nSo one of the custodial tools is something like Bitcoin Fog and we've seen there's been there was a crackdown on Bitcoin Fog.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:44\n\nAlso a lot of like very un like mixes that nobody's even heard of.\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:49\n\nI'm actually not as familiar with that, but I think one of the things that's really important to differentiate here is that Tornado Cache is something very diffic different than what Samurai was doing, the mixer with Samurai wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:59\n\nSo Tornado Cache, you have what you just called an autonomous tool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:02\n\nSo we have a smart contract to which the keys were burned, which means that people can now use this thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nThere's no administrator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nNobody can log in, change the whatever parameters of the smart contract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:11\n\nIt can just be used by whoever wants to use it now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nIn fact, it is still being used, even though some of the people behind it are in prison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:20\n\nSo that kind of proves that it's at least somewhat autonomous.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:23\n\nYeah, and I think something really important to keep in mind here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:25\n\nWhen we talk about smart contracts, oftentimes people use the analogy of a vending machine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:30\n\nSo a smart contract is an if-then protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:32\n\nIf this happens, then you get this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:34\n\nThe same way if you put a dollar in a vending machine, you press A1, you get a Snickers bar.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:39\n\nThe difference is that not all smart contracts are alike in that some are completely autonomous, meaning even the person who owns the vending machine, let's say, who created it, no longer has the keys, so the smart contract operates independently.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:51\n\nWhere then there are other smart contracts where people still have the keys, alterations can be made.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:56\n\nIn the case of Tornado Cache, you had an autonomous smart contract, if I'm not mistaken.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:00\n\nYes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:00\n\nSo if we could- At least the core of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nSo there was also auxiliary smart contracts that provided the user interface, and those did still have access control, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:13\n\nUnderstood.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nAnd for those who are not familiar with this as well, so we use this, whether it's Tornado Cash or Samurai, we're using this to preserve anonymity.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nThe courts have to preserve privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:23\n\nPrivacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\n\nThere's a whole fun game, at least in a Dutch courtroom, between the prosecutors using the word anonymity consistently and the defense using the word privacy consistently to the point where they started realizing they were doing that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:37\n\nBut it has a different different meaning, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:39\n\nNo no question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:40\n\nI mean, it should be yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nAnyway, go on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nLet's dive let's dive in with the Dutch courtroom.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:43\n\nSo what has been established thus far based on the hearings that have taken place in the Netherlands?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nWell, unfortunately, um so the Tornado Cash folks had one person arrested here and one person arrested in the Netherlands.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:54\n\nAnd those are completely separate cases, although they're using the same uh body of evidence as far as I know, the same chats that were taken from phones.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:02\n\nUm so Alexey Persev has been convicted in the Netherlands, at least during the first bit of the first phase of the trial, uh, for five years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nI guess that's short by US terms, but it was the maximum sentence he could get, uh, because in the Netherlands if you launder more than a million, then that's as much as you get.\nOkay.\nUm so that's bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:24\n\nThey're appealing it and that appeal should be now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:27\n\nIsh this year.\nOkay.\nUm any expectations for what you think might happen in the Southern District?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nAnd that's with um I'm gonna get the name wrong.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nIs that that's Roman Storm?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:37\n\nAm I getting the name?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nI think so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nThat's correct, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nUm do you think you'll see a comparable sort of outcome?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:41\n\nAnd and I actually I hadn't thought about this beforehand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nCan they look at what happened in the Netherland case despite the fact that the law is different and maybe use some of that as some sort of precedent for what might happen in in in the southern district?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:52\n\nSo I don't think so, because the the interesting thing is it's just two different countries with completely different legal systems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:59\n\nYou know, you guys have jury trials with witnesses and it's gonna be probably quite spectacular.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:04\n\nWhereas in the Netherlands it was you know a little courtroom with maybe five journalists, which is a lot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:11\n\nAnd just the the the lawyers talking and also they're mostly referring to dossiers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:17\n\nSo even though the trial is public, as a member if if you're just sitting there, you're just hearing them refer to page three hundred sixty seventy five of some document that you don't have access to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nSo this is a problem too.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:31\n\nUh so so I have no idea what the outcome is here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:33\n\nYou really have to ask uh lawyers here and of course the lawyers that are defending them will will say that they're definitely gonna win and the prosecutor will say that they're They're definitely going to win.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:44\n\nSo I don't know how to judge that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nWhile he's not their lawyer, Zach Shapiro from the Bitcoin Policy Institute, who's also running the P2P Rights Fund, which is helping to raise money for defense in these trials, I think he's a little bit more realistic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:57\n\nI don't think he's super optimistic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:59\n\nI think he thinks there's a chance of winning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:01\n\nBut I think also, I believe it was in one of the hearings for the Tornado Cash case here, we had the judge saying, I don't care what FinCEN guidance says.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:11\n\nYou know, in FinCEN guidance, so FinCEN guidance came out from the Treasury in 2019.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:16\n\nThere's nothing there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:17\n\nWell, basically, what a lot of the lawyers are arguing is that, well, these are non-custodial products.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nSo our team members are not actually touching this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:26\n\nWe're not actually touching funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nSo we can't be involved in illegal money transmitting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:31\n\nBut the judge has now said, I don't care if you didn't touch the keys to these.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:34\n\nTo this money, you are still part of this process of transmitting this money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:39\n\nDo you think that this is going to be something that could be superseded?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:42\n\nCan we get past this initial?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:44\n\nIt's not a ruling from the judge, but it was a statement made in one of the pretrial hearings thus far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:49\n\nHonestly, I don't know, but it is, it touches on something interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:52\n\nSo, so we have, as we said, there's custodial mixes where the mixing company does touch.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:57\n\nDoes touch the coins, and then there is non-custodial mixes where the company doesn't, where nobody has custody over the coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:04\n\nAnd as far as I know, on both sides of the pond, it's acknowledged that indeed there was no custody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:11\n\nThe question is, does that matter?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nAnd then for the tornado cache situation, it's also autonomous, which is a separate discussion.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nSo you have custody versus non-custody, and then you have autonomy versus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:23\n\nRunning a website.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:24\n\nAnd what happens in Europe is that, so you guys have a FinCEN guidance that people are like looking at as if it's like a biblical verse, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:35\n\nIt can be interpreted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nYeah, well, I guess the biblical verses are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:37\n\nIt can be interpreted, but you can also say, well, it's just guidance, so maybe you ignore the guidance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:41\n\nThat's sort of the discussion here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:43\n\nBut it is trying to, at least the way it's interpreted, is they say if you're not a custodian, Like, you don't have to do any of this money lic uh money transmitter licensing stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:54\n\nWhereas in in Europe, this is actually very clear.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nThe there is a uh European anti-money laundering directive which specifically says that if you're not a custodian, you do not need a license.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nAnd that's also not contested in the courtroom.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:07\n\nSo the even the prosecutor said, like, sure, you know, you didn't need uh a money transmitter license, but Money laundering is still a crime.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:16\n\nSo we're gonna, you know, punish you anyway.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:18\n\nAnd I think this might be a good place to switch over a little bit to the samurai trial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nSo, for those who are not familiar with the samurai trial, and also you just said you might see some kind of like hoopla in the courtroom in the United States.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nI can testify to the fact that you probably won't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nI've been the only member of the press in all four of the pre-trial hearings for the samurai devs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:36\n\nI think now we're up to maybe three members of the press that were in the fourth hearing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nSo, there is not a lot of attention on it, despite the fact that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:42\n\nThe legal precedent that will likely come from these cases will set the course for how we're able to legally transmit value across the Internet moving forward.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:53\n\nBut for those who are not familiar with the samurai case, so there are two charges that have been levied against them by the U.S.\nDepartment of Justice.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:59\n\nThe first being conspiracy to operate an unlicensed money transmitter business.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nThe second being conspiracy to launder money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:07\n\nI think what's important here is that I think it seems like based on the FinCEN guidance and also according to Zach Shapiro from BPI, the fact that we might have more FinCEN guidance come out before the trial begins in November might influence this in terms of the money transmission charges really not being thrown out, but making it much easier for the Samurai developers to win.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:33\n\nThe other side of the samurai case is that they're being tried for conspiracy to commit money laundering.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nWe know that the samurai developers, or for those who don't, they were quite flagrant in sort of poking the bear, as the analogy or the idiom people use.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:47\n\nYou know, Russian oligarchs posting on Twitter, Russian oligarchs come and use our service.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:52\n\nYou know, and then apparently, there's what's been hinted at in the courtroom by the prosecution, and I can't say this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nYou know, just it's just based on my interpretation of being there and listening is just wait until you see the correspondence that we have with the samurai devs and other people who are using the service.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:08\n\nAnd we're still waiting on this sort of discovery to be released.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:12\n\nThere's a tremendous amount of discovery in the case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:15\n\nThe way it was described in the courtroom was that there's enough discovery where if you printed it out on paper, you could stack it to the moon and back 22 times.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nSo there's a lot to be worked through here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nBut I think in thinking about the samurai case, one of the things to keep in mind is that while they might lose on the side of helping to facilitate money laundering, I hope they don't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:35\n\nI hope they didn't do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nIt seems like, based on the interpretation of FinCEN guidance, and also we had two senators in the United States, I think it's Wyden from Colorado.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:46\n\nAnd Senator Lummis wrote letters also saying that according to FinCEN guidance, they have done nothing wrong here in terms of money transmission.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nBut the big question mark is, based on what we heard in the Tornado Cash hearing, the judge saying I don't care whether you touch these keys or not, can we now interpret this guidance differently?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nAnd especially because we don't have preexisting law on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:06\n\nI think the answer is no, because again, the US has its own legal system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:11\n\nAnd hopefully this appeal in the Netherlands will also change the result a bit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:15\n\nBecause so the so um maybe go in a little bit what the prosecutor was trying to say versus what the judge eventually did.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:22\n\nSo the prosecutors were making uh for Tornado Cash a very complicated argument, basically almost using tokenomics.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:30\n\nSo they were saying, Okay, you guys have this smart contract and yes, you know, it's autonomous, uh at least parts of it, but some parts of it are not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:37\n\nBut you guys have tokens, and those tokens will go up in value if criminals are using it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:42\n\nAnd also, you guys are you have hired staff and they're doing work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:47\n\nSo so we look at it, um, you know, Tornado Cash not just as the smart contract, but as this whole organization around it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:54\n\nSo, you know, you can agree or disagree with that argument, but that's sort of what they tried to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:59\n\nAnd then the judge kind of said, Well, I don't.\ncare about any of that stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThe fact that you wrote a smart contract that is unstoppable, the fact that you did that in the first place, that and and you knew that it could have been used for money laundering, that is what makes you now responsible for that money laundering.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:15\n\nSo it the the charge actually went beyond what the prosecutor was asking for and saying, no, it doesn't matter that it's not custodial, it doesn't matter that it's uh autonomous.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:24\n\nIn fact, the fact that it's autonomous makes it worse.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nBecause you should have kept control over the system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:30\n\nI think that type of argument here would hopefully not work at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nIt sounds like a problem with free speech and kind of being responsible for something that you created.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:40\n\nI don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:41\n\nAnd I think the free speech point is important to touch on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nSo I don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:45\n\nIf you invented guns, like, are you responsible now for all that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:49\n\nHopefully, that just.\ngets blown up in appeal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:52\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:53\n\nAnd for those who are unfamiliar, it was in the nineties, I think it was during the the the the fight around PGP, pretty good privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nWe had the ruling that essentially code is free speech.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:02\n\nAnd this is something that the that the defense, at least in the samurai case I know, is going to uh put up as an argument.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:08\n\nIs there anything is that is that a argument that can be made in the Dutch courts or no?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:12\n\nWe don't have that precedent that code is free speech.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:14\n\nHowever, in practice, Uh there there's some like I guess there's some similar legal arguments around say hacking software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nSo if if you are making software that can also be used for hacking, that's not a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nIf you're making software that can only be used for hacking and you're also making money for it, that could be a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:32\n\nBut there's still still some thresholds there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:34\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nAnd for those unaware, I mean if if you're I'm I'm sure if you're sitting here you're sort of interested in this for reasons of preserving your own privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nUm I'm curious to hear, did the defense make any sort of case that activists use this and that they have to use it, that people like uh the the the Navalny organization, Alexei Navalny, who ran against Putin, that one of the only ways for them to receive their donations was to have them put through crypto mixers first, both to pr well mainly to preserve the and preserve the privacy.\nof their donors, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:04\n\nWe don't want to put the donors at risk, especially if they're donating from within Russia.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nIs any of this argument being made in the Dutch courts?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nWas this made that it's necessary in some situations?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:12\n\nI think it was kind of, but not strongly enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:15\n\nSo I'm hoping that in the appeal they're gonna put much more emphasis on the legitimate uses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nBut at the same time, uh I I think there were organizations that were using Tornado Cash for paying salary, et cetera, and they sent letters to the judge and those letters were not even mentioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nThe verdict.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nSo it's possible that these arcades might have been made on paper more than they were made orally, but they did not show up at all, which is a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:40\n\nI think another issue in the United States, and I'll just kind of put this out there as a call to action now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:44\n\nI wrote a piece in Bitcoin magazine around this, is that there's a lot of excitement right now around the strategic Bitcoin Reserve.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nThere's even good stuff like codifying the right for people to own their private keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:55\n\nBut to quote Craig Raw, who created Sparrow Wallet, one of the best Bitcoin wallets out there for those who don't use it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:01\n\nHe said, I don't think the world is really ready for the amount.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nIf we do move to more of a Bitcoin standard, Bitcoin proliferates, it becomes more widely used.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:09\n\nI don't think the world is ready for the amount of transparency that we're about to see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:13\n\nAnd as I have conversations with politicians, I recently spoke with a number of prominent politicians, senators, congresspeople, representatives at the federal level and at lower levels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:25\n\nOne of the things that comes up, We talk about all the stuff that they love to talk about right now, SPRs and the keys, and they're the heroes of the day right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nPeople in the Bitcoin community like politicians all of a sudden, which wasn't always the case in the history of the Bitcoin community.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nI watch them squirm in their seat when I bring up privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:42\n\nSo part of the reason is that if something were to happen, if someone's using privacy tools and there were a terrorist attack as a result of that or something like that, Obviously, people are going to look back to the politicians.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nAnd I think the majority of what they're thinking right now is sort of like, I don't want to be on the hook.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:57\n\nI don't want to be responsible in such a scenario.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:59\n\nSo it's easier for me to maybe either not talk about this situation or to err on the side of giving an ambiguous answer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:07\n\nSo I'm just, before I forget, the call to action is more if this isn't something that you're passionate about, even speaking with lower level, like state-level representatives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:17\n\nAnd I would actually say the state level representatives are easier to have a conversation with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:21\n\nI think, just the same way that we've educated politicians to say Bitcoin is not only used by criminals, you know, ETH is not only used by criminals, blah, blah, blah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:30\n\nThere's people who use it for very legitimate purposes, especially activists and people who need to maintain their privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nIt's going to be important to have this next discussion because as these trials move forward, There's going to be hopefully more media attention, though there hasn't been a lot thus far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nThere's going to be a lot of education that's necessary around this, same way we've educated people about, I don't know, Bitcoin as a store of value, importance of private keys, et cetera.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:53\n\nSo before I forget, just wanted to put that out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:55\n\nIf this is something that you're passionate about, education is necessary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nWriting your local politicians is necessary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:02\n\nOne of my colleagues, Shinobi, has an article online.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:05\n\nI could see if I can maybe share it via, I don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:08\n\nMIT, this venue somehow, where we created a template to start addressing politicians.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:14\n\nYou could just use this to say that this case is really important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:17\n\nAnd I think right now there's also a lot of momentum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nWe're seeing other people being pardoned, obviously, Ross being one of the bigger characters right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:26\n\nBut we haven't seen too much leniency on this front yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nSo I think it's going to take an organic push from everybody involved, everybody that cares about that, to make the case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:36\n\nThat, sure, there may be some bad actors that use it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nTo also make the case that chainalysis's stats tend to be terrible, not very good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nThey tend to say there's many more criminals using public blockchains than there actually are, but that there are many people across the globe who use this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:53\n\nBut then also saying to a politician or someone in power, a policymaker: do you want your entire balance of whatever crypto asset you're using on a public blockchain?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:02\n\nBe able to be visible to someone.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:04\n\nBecause if you, without a mixer, without being able to put this through some sort of mixer to maintain some semblance of privacy, it's going to make the world a public place in ways that I don't think people are necessarily ready for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:17\n\nLet me hand it back over to you then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:19\n\nYeah, to maybe end on a more cynical note, there is two interests really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:25\n\nYou could say there's.\nkind of roughly two ways that people care about Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:29\n\nOne of them is number go up and the other is the um um more censorship resistant money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:34\n\nAnd I think in order to have the censorship resistance you also need privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:38\n\nIt just they kind of come together because if they can't see you they can't get you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:42\n\nUh and just uh a lot of people only care about the number go up thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:46\n\nNow you can make the case for an enlightened self interest that Bitcoin will not be number go up.\nif the if the censorship resistance is undermined to the point where the government can just print more of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:57\n\nUh but I don't know if we need to do that or that people need to figure this out themselves.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:03\n\nBut I I'm not surprised that people are not uh helping these uh privacy tools at all uh when they're seeing the number go up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:10\n\nOkay uh so you're they're distracted, you're saying?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:12\n\nI say they they just they don't care about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nThey they care about the number go up and that's why they'll you know they might Let some people out of prison, but I don't think they're going to get involved in this privacy discussion because they don't want to make the argument that terrorists could use Bitcoin or something like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:27\n\nIt's a tough thing for them to have to own if that does happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:30\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nIs that fair?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:32\n\nI agree with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:32\n\nThat is part of the difficulty of the conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:34\n\nAnd I get that to some level.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:36\n\nI'm going to stop it there, though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:37\n\nLet's open the floor to QA.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:39\n\nIf anybody has any questions, we'll do our best to answer here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:44\n\nDo we put everybody to sleep?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:46\n\nSorry about that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:46\n\nBefore lunch, I know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:55\n\nHave you considered some of the irony that institutions might be your best friends in this space?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:59\n\nBecause from a trading strategy standpoint, they don't want to give up and give away front running to their info and their private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:06\n\nNo, because if you have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:07\n\nAll these institutions are holding their coins at some giant custodian, and they do all the trades off-chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:15\n\nSo I don't think they have too much of an on-chain privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:17\n\nNow, if if all these ETFs were doing self-custody, then yeah, there would definitely be an incentive for them to care about their own privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:25\n\nMaybe I'm being too cynical here, but it's good to have a cynic next to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:29\n\nI tend to be too optimistic, so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:32\n\nYeah, I've been surprised that institutions like the EFF and the ACLU haven't gotten more involved in this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:38\n\nAnd so I'm curious if, Frank, you in particular, have you tried to talk to them?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:42\n\nBecause this seems like it could leak into encrypted messaging and other areas where maybe they play more of a role.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:50\n\nNo, I think that's a really, really, really important conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:53\n\nI would say the EFF probably knows a little bit more about what's going on than the ACLU, but you've just given me some homework, some people to reach out to, but that's a great idea.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:00\n\nI think that that's really important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:01\n\nIt is sort of it's a civil rights issue essentially, and it needs to be brought to that level for sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:07\n\nAppreciate that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:08\n\nSo, this was briefly brought up in the court case in the Netherlands, the analogy with Text basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:13\n\nSo nobody is saying that Tignal is responsible for all crime organized on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:18\n\nAnd then the prosecutor just said, well, data is different.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:21\n\nData or text is different from money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:23\n\nAnd therefore, you know, that's it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:25\n\nThat's the whole argument.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nSo this is a a disconnect that you're seeing in the legal system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:30\n\nSo financial data privacy seems easier to defend than financial privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:33\n\nAnd I would say there's a political problem when it comes to Things like EFF, et cetera.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:38\n\nCrypto just has a bad rep.\nBitcoin may be even worse, and they don't want to touch it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nThe only good example there is Molly White, who is typically very critical of Bitcoin stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:48\n\nAnd she wrote an article very strongly in defense of the Tornado Cash and samurai people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:54\n\nSo there's hope.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:56\n\nOh, no, not Sydney.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:58\n\nSaid the cynic, said the cynic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nAre there any kind of like prosecutions against like the People who actually committed like fraud using these at all?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:06\n\nOr is it all just focused on the fantastic question?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:09\n\nI would say the difficulty there is that because they're using something that mixes the coins, it may be hard to tie stuff back to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:16\n\nI don't know enough about that, though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:17\n\nMaybe you know more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:18\n\nThe Tornado Cash prosecution was triggered, seems very obviously, by the fact that North Korea was using it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:24\n\nOtherwise, it would have been under the radar.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:27\n\nAnd so prosecuting North Korea is being handled, I guess, through these sanctions and all this stuff, but they're just untouchable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:35\n\nBut I would say there are, you know, criminals are getting arrested all the time and then they confiscate money and they may or may not have used the mixer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:43\n\nTo piggyback on that, one of the things that was interesting, I wrote a piece, I think it was right when the Samurai News came out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:49\n\nI interviewed two prominent activists, and one of them said to me, like, Russian oligarchs don't need this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:55\n\nThey have a bunch of other schemes that they can use to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:58\n\nLike this isn't these are like the last people we should be worried about using.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:00\n\nThey could do this through real estate schemes through other sorts of schemes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:03\n\nThis really isn't that doesn't apply to them in the way that the g you know that the the prosecution is trying to make it out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:08\n\nAnd obviously in the way that you know the samurai devs did not help by you know inviting them on Twitter to come use the the the technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:16\n\nNo, I mean the Netherlands is a paradise for Russian oligarchs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:19\n\nUh you can buy real estate, but also just these corporations are very easy to move funds around.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:24\n\nBut you know, the the argument from what aboutism basically is never uh doesn't really work that well, especially in court.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nI think that's fair.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nYeah, that he has a little bit more knowledge there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:34\n\nAny final questions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:35\n\nDo how much time do we have?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:36\n\nI just want to make sure we're okay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:37\n\nWe're good.\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:38\n\nAnd of course in the case of the North Koreans, it was clear they were using it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:41\n\nSo It's not with tornado cash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:46\n\nWhich isn't good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:50\n\nMaybe a little bit out there question, but I think with the sanctions, it's really interesting to think about it from like a global trade perspective, especially with oil.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:58\n\nThere's like a lot of bricks chatter for like a dollar alternative at the same time we have all this tariff stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:04\n\nDo you guys think any of you know that bears on Or like trickles down into the court cases where there's a higher agenda to maintain the dollar sovereignty and making sure oil trade goes through the dollar.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:16\n\nBut if you add, if you get a mixer that's really powerful, you could trade oil for Bitcoin and nobody would know if you did it in a small enough size.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:27\n\nI think it would be difficult for anyone, for the sorts of violence.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:30\n\nVolume, the sorts of money going through it, I think it would be really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:33\n\nI think Lynn Alden wrote in Broken Money something like: Bitcoin is kind of good because when somebody tries to put like serious money through the system, you can see that in the mempool, it's gonna be really, even if you do put it through a mixer, it's gonna be tough to see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:44\n\nBut also, from what I've seen about countries saying that they wanna start doing oil trade, like Bolivia just recently said we're gonna start using Bitcoin for oil payments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:53\n\nI mean, they're telegraph they're they're making it public.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:55\n\nI don't know that anonymizing the transaction is gonna do much to to change the dynamic that they're trying to establish there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:01\n\nYeah, well the fact that they're telegraphing things could also mean that they're really just trying to send a political message and not actually serious about using Bitcoin for these things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:09\n\nBut maybe then again I'm being too cynical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:11\n\nStupid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:13\n\nIt's a very yin and yang scenario here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:15\n\nIt feels good.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:16\n\nAre we good?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:16\n\nDo we have any more questions, final questions or anything?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:18\n\nI think that's all the time we have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:20\n\nAll right, sounds good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:21\n\nThank you guys so much.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nThank you, Frank.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nThanks, Charles.	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nFrank Corva, Bitcoin magazine here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:02\n\nI've been covering the samurai case in New York in the Southern District.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:06\n\nAnd Schorst, over to my left here, if you want a quick introduction maybe.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:09\n\nYeah, I'm uh Shors Provost.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:11\n\nI'm based in the Netherlands and I normally work on Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:14\n\nUm but there's some things happening in the Netherlands that uh I'm also paying attention to, which we're gonna talk about today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:20\n\nLet's dive right in right there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:22\n\nSo you've been in the courtroom for what's happening with the Tornado Cash trial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:26\n\nThe portion of it that's happened in the Netherlands.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nIt's also going to be happening in part in the Southern District of New York as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nI think that's actually a week from Monday.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:33\n\nWe're going to have, I think, one of the first hearings in the Southern District.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:36\n\nBut let's set the stage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:37\n\nLet's sort of talk about what's at stake right now when it comes to using Bitcoin crypto mixers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:42\n\nWhat is the environment right now?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:44\n\nMaybe if you want to dive in on the Tornado Cash side, I'll give a little bit of an overview on the Samurai wallet side.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:49\n\nYeah, and and maybe we wanna go back a little bit further even.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nPlease, there have been mixers before.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nMost of these would be custodial mixers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:58\n\nSo essentially you give your co-coins to some website and that website gives you some coins back.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:06\n\nAnd hopefully then there's no link between them, but the website could screw you over.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:11\n\nAnd well, there have been arrests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:13\n\nAround that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nAnd then I guess we've mostly not been paying attention to those as a community because we're thinking, well, they're custodials and custodians have different rules, so whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:26\n\nBut now we're seeing people getting arrested for building non-custodial tools and even autonomous tools, which I guess we we can explain the differences there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:36\n\nSo let's start there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:37\n\nSo one of the custodial tools is something like Bitcoin Fog and we've seen there's been there was a crackdown on Bitcoin Fog.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:44\n\nAlso a lot of like very un like mixes that nobody's even heard of.\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:49\n\nI'm actually not as familiar with that, but I think one of the things that's really important to differentiate here is that Tornado Cache is something very diffic different than what Samurai was doing, the mixer with Samurai wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:59\n\nSo Tornado Cache, you have what you just called an autonomous tool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:02\n\nSo we have a smart contract to which the keys were burned, which means that people can now use this thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:07\n\nThere's no administrator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nNobody can log in, change the whatever parameters of the smart contract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:11\n\nIt can just be used by whoever wants to use it now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nIn fact, it is still being used, even though some of the people behind it are in prison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:20\n\nSo that kind of proves that it's at least somewhat autonomous.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:23\n\nYeah, and I think something really important to keep in mind here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:25\n\nWhen we talk about smart contracts, oftentimes people use the analogy of a vending machine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:30\n\nSo a smart contract is an if-then protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:32\n\nIf this happens, then you get this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:34\n\nThe same way if you put a dollar in a vending machine, you press A1, you get a Snickers bar.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:39\n\nThe difference is that not all smart contracts are alike in that some are completely autonomous, meaning even the person who owns the vending machine, let's say, who created it, no longer has the keys, so the smart contract operates independently.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:51\n\nWhere then there are other smart contracts where people still have the keys, alterations can be made.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:56\n\nIn the case of Tornado Cache, you had an autonomous smart contract, if I'm not mistaken.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:00\n\nYes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:00\n\nSo if we could- At least the core of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nSo there was also auxiliary smart contracts that provided the user interface, and those did still have access control, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:13\n\nUnderstood.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nAnd for those who are not familiar with this as well, so we use this, whether it's Tornado Cash or Samurai, we're using this to preserve anonymity.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nThe courts have to preserve privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:23\n\nPrivacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\n\nThere's a whole fun game, at least in a Dutch courtroom, between the prosecutors using the word anonymity consistently and the defense using the word privacy consistently to the point where they started realizing they were doing that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:37\n\nBut it has a different different meaning, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:39\n\nNo no question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:40\n\nI mean, it should be yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nAnyway, go on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nLet's dive let's dive in with the Dutch courtroom.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:43\n\nSo what has been established thus far based on the hearings that have taken place in the Netherlands?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nWell, unfortunately, um so the Tornado Cash folks had one person arrested here and one person arrested in the Netherlands.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:54\n\nAnd those are completely separate cases, although they're using the same uh body of evidence as far as I know, the same chats that were taken from phones.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:02\n\nUm so Alexey Persev has been convicted in the Netherlands, at least during the first bit of the first phase of the trial, uh, for five years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nI guess that's short by US terms, but it was the maximum sentence he could get, uh, because in the Netherlands if you launder more than a million, then that's as much as you get.\nOkay.\nUm so that's bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:24\n\nThey're appealing it and that appeal should be now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:27\n\nIsh this year.\nOkay.\nUm any expectations for what you think might happen in the Southern District?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nAnd that's with um I'm gonna get the name wrong.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nIs that that's Roman Storm?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:37\n\nAm I getting the name?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nI think so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nThat's correct, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nUm do you think you'll see a comparable sort of outcome?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:41\n\nAnd and I actually I hadn't thought about this beforehand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:44\n\nCan they look at what happened in the Netherland case despite the fact that the law is different and maybe use some of that as some sort of precedent for what might happen in in in the southern district?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:52\n\nSo I don't think so, because the the interesting thing is it's just two different countries with completely different legal systems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:59\n\nYou know, you guys have jury trials with witnesses and it's gonna be probably quite spectacular.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:04\n\nWhereas in the Netherlands it was you know a little courtroom with maybe five journalists, which is a lot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:11\n\nAnd just the the the lawyers talking and also they're mostly referring to dossiers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:17\n\nSo even though the trial is public, as a member if if you're just sitting there, you're just hearing them refer to page three hundred sixty seventy five of some document that you don't have access to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nSo this is a problem too.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:31\n\nUh so so I have no idea what the outcome is here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:33\n\nYou really have to ask uh lawyers here and of course the lawyers that are defending them will will say that they're definitely gonna win and the prosecutor will say that they're They're definitely going to win.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:44\n\nSo I don't know how to judge that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nWhile he's not their lawyer, Zach Shapiro from the Bitcoin Policy Institute, who's also running the P2P Rights Fund, which is helping to raise money for defense in these trials, I think he's a little bit more realistic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:57\n\nI don't think he's super optimistic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:59\n\nI think he thinks there's a chance of winning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:01\n\nBut I think also, I believe it was in one of the hearings for the Tornado Cash case here, we had the judge saying, I don't care what FinCEN guidance says.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:11\n\nYou know, in FinCEN guidance, so FinCEN guidance came out from the Treasury in 2019.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:16\n\nThere's nothing there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:17\n\nWell, basically, what a lot of the lawyers are arguing is that, well, these are non-custodial products.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nSo our team members are not actually touching this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:26\n\nWe're not actually touching funds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nSo we can't be involved in illegal money transmitting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:31\n\nBut the judge has now said, I don't care if you didn't touch the keys to these.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:34\n\nTo this money, you are still part of this process of transmitting this money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:39\n\nDo you think that this is going to be something that could be superseded?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:42\n\nCan we get past this initial?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:44\n\nIt's not a ruling from the judge, but it was a statement made in one of the pretrial hearings thus far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:49\n\nHonestly, I don't know, but it is, it touches on something interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:52\n\nSo, so we have, as we said, there's custodial mixes where the mixing company does touch.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:57\n\nDoes touch the coins, and then there is non-custodial mixes where the company doesn't, where nobody has custody over the coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:04\n\nAnd as far as I know, on both sides of the pond, it's acknowledged that indeed there was no custody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:11\n\nThe question is, does that matter?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nAnd then for the tornado cache situation, it's also autonomous, which is a separate discussion.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nSo you have custody versus non-custody, and then you have autonomy versus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:23\n\nRunning a website.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:24\n\nAnd what happens in Europe is that, so you guys have a FinCEN guidance that people are like looking at as if it's like a biblical verse, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:35\n\nIt can be interpreted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nYeah, well, I guess the biblical verses are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:37\n\nIt can be interpreted, but you can also say, well, it's just guidance, so maybe you ignore the guidance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:41\n\nThat's sort of the discussion here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:43\n\nBut it is trying to, at least the way it's interpreted, is they say if you're not a custodian, Like, you don't have to do any of this money lic uh money transmitter licensing stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:54\n\nWhereas in in Europe, this is actually very clear.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nThe there is a uh European anti-money laundering directive which specifically says that if you're not a custodian, you do not need a license.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nAnd that's also not contested in the courtroom.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:07\n\nSo the even the prosecutor said, like, sure, you know, you didn't need uh a money transmitter license, but Money laundering is still a crime.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:16\n\nSo we're gonna, you know, punish you anyway.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:18\n\nAnd I think this might be a good place to switch over a little bit to the samurai trial.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nSo, for those who are not familiar with the samurai trial, and also you just said you might see some kind of like hoopla in the courtroom in the United States.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nI can testify to the fact that you probably won't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nI've been the only member of the press in all four of the pre-trial hearings for the samurai devs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:36\n\nI think now we're up to maybe three members of the press that were in the fourth hearing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nSo, there is not a lot of attention on it, despite the fact that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:42\n\nThe legal precedent that will likely come from these cases will set the course for how we're able to legally transmit value across the Internet moving forward.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:53\n\nBut for those who are not familiar with the samurai case, so there are two charges that have been levied against them by the U.S.\nDepartment of Justice.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:59\n\nThe first being conspiracy to operate an unlicensed money transmitter business.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nThe second being conspiracy to launder money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:07\n\nI think what's important here is that I think it seems like based on the FinCEN guidance and also according to Zach Shapiro from BPI, the fact that we might have more FinCEN guidance come out before the trial begins in November might influence this in terms of the money transmission charges really not being thrown out, but making it much easier for the Samurai developers to win.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:33\n\nThe other side of the samurai case is that they're being tried for conspiracy to commit money laundering.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nWe know that the samurai developers, or for those who don't, they were quite flagrant in sort of poking the bear, as the analogy or the idiom people use.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:47\n\nYou know, Russian oligarchs posting on Twitter, Russian oligarchs come and use our service.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:52\n\nYou know, and then apparently, there's what's been hinted at in the courtroom by the prosecution, and I can't say this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nYou know, just it's just based on my interpretation of being there and listening is just wait until you see the correspondence that we have with the samurai devs and other people who are using the service.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:08\n\nAnd we're still waiting on this sort of discovery to be released.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:12\n\nThere's a tremendous amount of discovery in the case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:15\n\nThe way it was described in the courtroom was that there's enough discovery where if you printed it out on paper, you could stack it to the moon and back 22 times.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nSo there's a lot to be worked through here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nBut I think in thinking about the samurai case, one of the things to keep in mind is that while they might lose on the side of helping to facilitate money laundering, I hope they don't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:35\n\nI hope they didn't do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nIt seems like, based on the interpretation of FinCEN guidance, and also we had two senators in the United States, I think it's Wyden from Colorado.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:46\n\nAnd Senator Lummis wrote letters also saying that according to FinCEN guidance, they have done nothing wrong here in terms of money transmission.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:53\n\nBut the big question mark is, based on what we heard in the Tornado Cash hearing, the judge saying I don't care whether you touch these keys or not, can we now interpret this guidance differently?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nAnd especially because we don't have preexisting law on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:06\n\nI think the answer is no, because again, the US has its own legal system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:11\n\nAnd hopefully this appeal in the Netherlands will also change the result a bit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:15\n\nBecause so the so um maybe go in a little bit what the prosecutor was trying to say versus what the judge eventually did.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:22\n\nSo the prosecutors were making uh for Tornado Cash a very complicated argument, basically almost using tokenomics.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:30\n\nSo they were saying, Okay, you guys have this smart contract and yes, you know, it's autonomous, uh at least parts of it, but some parts of it are not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:37\n\nBut you guys have tokens, and those tokens will go up in value if criminals are using it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:42\n\nAnd also, you guys are you have hired staff and they're doing work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:47\n\nSo so we look at it, um, you know, Tornado Cash not just as the smart contract, but as this whole organization around it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:54\n\nSo, you know, you can agree or disagree with that argument, but that's sort of what they tried to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:59\n\nAnd then the judge kind of said, Well, I don't.\ncare about any of that stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThe fact that you wrote a smart contract that is unstoppable, the fact that you did that in the first place, that and and you knew that it could have been used for money laundering, that is what makes you now responsible for that money laundering.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:15\n\nSo it the the charge actually went beyond what the prosecutor was asking for and saying, no, it doesn't matter that it's not custodial, it doesn't matter that it's uh autonomous.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:24\n\nIn fact, the fact that it's autonomous makes it worse.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nBecause you should have kept control over the system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:30\n\nI think that type of argument here would hopefully not work at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nIt sounds like a problem with free speech and kind of being responsible for something that you created.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:40\n\nI don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:41\n\nAnd I think the free speech point is important to touch on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nSo I don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:45\n\nIf you invented guns, like, are you responsible now for all that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:49\n\nHopefully, that just.\ngets blown up in appeal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:52\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:53\n\nAnd for those who are unfamiliar, it was in the nineties, I think it was during the the the the fight around PGP, pretty good privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nWe had the ruling that essentially code is free speech.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:02\n\nAnd this is something that the that the defense, at least in the samurai case I know, is going to uh put up as an argument.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:08\n\nIs there anything is that is that a argument that can be made in the Dutch courts or no?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:12\n\nWe don't have that precedent that code is free speech.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:14\n\nHowever, in practice, Uh there there's some like I guess there's some similar legal arguments around say hacking software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nSo if if you are making software that can also be used for hacking, that's not a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nIf you're making software that can only be used for hacking and you're also making money for it, that could be a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:32\n\nBut there's still still some thresholds there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:34\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nAnd for those unaware, I mean if if you're I'm I'm sure if you're sitting here you're sort of interested in this for reasons of preserving your own privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nUm I'm curious to hear, did the defense make any sort of case that activists use this and that they have to use it, that people like uh the the the Navalny organization, Alexei Navalny, who ran against Putin, that one of the only ways for them to receive their donations was to have them put through crypto mixers first, both to pr well mainly to preserve the and preserve the privacy.\nof their donors, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:04\n\nWe don't want to put the donors at risk, especially if they're donating from within Russia.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nIs any of this argument being made in the Dutch courts?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nWas this made that it's necessary in some situations?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:12\n\nI think it was kind of, but not strongly enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:15\n\nSo I'm hoping that in the appeal they're gonna put much more emphasis on the legitimate uses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nBut at the same time, uh I I think there were organizations that were using Tornado Cash for paying salary, et cetera, and they sent letters to the judge and those letters were not even mentioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nThe verdict.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nSo it's possible that these arcades might have been made on paper more than they were made orally, but they did not show up at all, which is a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:40\n\nI think another issue in the United States, and I'll just kind of put this out there as a call to action now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:44\n\nI wrote a piece in Bitcoin magazine around this, is that there's a lot of excitement right now around the strategic Bitcoin Reserve.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nThere's even good stuff like codifying the right for people to own their private keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:55\n\nBut to quote Craig Raw, who created Sparrow Wallet, one of the best Bitcoin wallets out there for those who don't use it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:01\n\nHe said, I don't think the world is really ready for the amount.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nIf we do move to more of a Bitcoin standard, Bitcoin proliferates, it becomes more widely used.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:09\n\nI don't think the world is ready for the amount of transparency that we're about to see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:13\n\nAnd as I have conversations with politicians, I recently spoke with a number of prominent politicians, senators, congresspeople, representatives at the federal level and at lower levels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:25\n\nOne of the things that comes up, We talk about all the stuff that they love to talk about right now, SPRs and the keys, and they're the heroes of the day right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nPeople in the Bitcoin community like politicians all of a sudden, which wasn't always the case in the history of the Bitcoin community.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nI watch them squirm in their seat when I bring up privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:42\n\nSo part of the reason is that if something were to happen, if someone's using privacy tools and there were a terrorist attack as a result of that or something like that, Obviously, people are going to look back to the politicians.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nAnd I think the majority of what they're thinking right now is sort of like, I don't want to be on the hook.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:57\n\nI don't want to be responsible in such a scenario.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:59\n\nSo it's easier for me to maybe either not talk about this situation or to err on the side of giving an ambiguous answer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:07\n\nSo I'm just, before I forget, the call to action is more if this isn't something that you're passionate about, even speaking with lower level, like state-level representatives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:17\n\nAnd I would actually say the state level representatives are easier to have a conversation with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:21\n\nI think, just the same way that we've educated politicians to say Bitcoin is not only used by criminals, you know, ETH is not only used by criminals, blah, blah, blah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:30\n\nThere's people who use it for very legitimate purposes, especially activists and people who need to maintain their privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nIt's going to be important to have this next discussion because as these trials move forward, There's going to be hopefully more media attention, though there hasn't been a lot thus far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:45\n\nThere's going to be a lot of education that's necessary around this, same way we've educated people about, I don't know, Bitcoin as a store of value, importance of private keys, et cetera.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:53\n\nSo before I forget, just wanted to put that out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:55\n\nIf this is something that you're passionate about, education is necessary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:59\n\nWriting your local politicians is necessary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:02\n\nOne of my colleagues, Shinobi, has an article online.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:05\n\nI could see if I can maybe share it via, I don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:08\n\nMIT, this venue somehow, where we created a template to start addressing politicians.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:14\n\nYou could just use this to say that this case is really important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:17\n\nAnd I think right now there's also a lot of momentum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nWe're seeing other people being pardoned, obviously, Ross being one of the bigger characters right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:26\n\nBut we haven't seen too much leniency on this front yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nSo I think it's going to take an organic push from everybody involved, everybody that cares about that, to make the case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:36\n\nThat, sure, there may be some bad actors that use it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nTo also make the case that chainalysis's stats tend to be terrible, not very good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nThey tend to say there's many more criminals using public blockchains than there actually are, but that there are many people across the globe who use this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:53\n\nBut then also saying to a politician or someone in power, a policymaker: do you want your entire balance of whatever crypto asset you're using on a public blockchain?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:02\n\nBe able to be visible to someone.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:04\n\nBecause if you, without a mixer, without being able to put this through some sort of mixer to maintain some semblance of privacy, it's going to make the world a public place in ways that I don't think people are necessarily ready for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:17\n\nLet me hand it back over to you then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:19\n\nYeah, to maybe end on a more cynical note, there is two interests really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:25\n\nYou could say there's.\nkind of roughly two ways that people care about Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:29\n\nOne of them is number go up and the other is the um um more censorship resistant money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:34\n\nAnd I think in order to have the censorship resistance you also need privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:38\n\nIt just they kind of come together because if they can't see you they can't get you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:42\n\nUh and just uh a lot of people only care about the number go up thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:46\n\nNow you can make the case for an enlightened self interest that Bitcoin will not be number go up.\nif the if the censorship resistance is undermined to the point where the government can just print more of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:57\n\nUh but I don't know if we need to do that or that people need to figure this out themselves.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:03\n\nBut I I'm not surprised that people are not uh helping these uh privacy tools at all uh when they're seeing the number go up.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:10\n\nOkay uh so you're they're distracted, you're saying?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:12\n\nI say they they just they don't care about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nThey they care about the number go up and that's why they'll you know they might Let some people out of prison, but I don't think they're going to get involved in this privacy discussion because they don't want to make the argument that terrorists could use Bitcoin or something like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:27\n\nIt's a tough thing for them to have to own if that does happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:30\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nIs that fair?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:32\n\nI agree with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:32\n\nThat is part of the difficulty of the conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:34\n\nAnd I get that to some level.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:36\n\nI'm going to stop it there, though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:37\n\nLet's open the floor to QA.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:39\n\nIf anybody has any questions, we'll do our best to answer here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:44\n\nDo we put everybody to sleep?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:46\n\nSorry about that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:46\n\nBefore lunch, I know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:55\n\nHave you considered some of the irony that institutions might be your best friends in this space?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:59\n\nBecause from a trading strategy standpoint, they don't want to give up and give away front running to their info and their private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:06\n\nNo, because if you have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:07\n\nAll these institutions are holding their coins at some giant custodian, and they do all the trades off-chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:15\n\nSo I don't think they have too much of an on-chain privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:17\n\nNow, if if all these ETFs were doing self-custody, then yeah, there would definitely be an incentive for them to care about their own privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:25\n\nMaybe I'm being too cynical here, but it's good to have a cynic next to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:29\n\nI tend to be too optimistic, so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:32\n\nYeah, I've been surprised that institutions like the EFF and the ACLU haven't gotten more involved in this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:38\n\nAnd so I'm curious if, Frank, you in particular, have you tried to talk to them?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:42\n\nBecause this seems like it could leak into encrypted messaging and other areas where maybe they play more of a role.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:50\n\nNo, I think that's a really, really, really important conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:53\n\nI would say the EFF probably knows a little bit more about what's going on than the ACLU, but you've just given me some homework, some people to reach out to, but that's a great idea.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:00\n\nI think that that's really important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:01\n\nIt is sort of it's a civil rights issue essentially, and it needs to be brought to that level for sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:07\n\nAppreciate that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:08\n\nSo, this was briefly brought up in the court case in the Netherlands, the analogy with Text basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:13\n\nSo nobody is saying that Tignal is responsible for all crime organized on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:18\n\nAnd then the prosecutor just said, well, data is different.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:21\n\nData or text is different from money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:23\n\nAnd therefore, you know, that's it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:25\n\nThat's the whole argument.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:27\n\nSo this is a a disconnect that you're seeing in the legal system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:30\n\nSo financial data privacy seems easier to defend than financial privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:33\n\nAnd I would say there's a political problem when it comes to Things like EFF, et cetera.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:38\n\nCrypto just has a bad rep.\nBitcoin may be even worse, and they don't want to touch it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nThe only good example there is Molly White, who is typically very critical of Bitcoin stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:48\n\nAnd she wrote an article very strongly in defense of the Tornado Cash and samurai people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:54\n\nSo there's hope.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:56\n\nOh, no, not Sydney.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:58\n\nSaid the cynic, said the cynic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nAre there any kind of like prosecutions against like the People who actually committed like fraud using these at all?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:06\n\nOr is it all just focused on the fantastic question?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:09\n\nI would say the difficulty there is that because they're using something that mixes the coins, it may be hard to tie stuff back to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:16\n\nI don't know enough about that, though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:17\n\nMaybe you know more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:18\n\nThe Tornado Cash prosecution was triggered, seems very obviously, by the fact that North Korea was using it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:24\n\nOtherwise, it would have been under the radar.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:27\n\nAnd so prosecuting North Korea is being handled, I guess, through these sanctions and all this stuff, but they're just untouchable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:35\n\nBut I would say there are, you know, criminals are getting arrested all the time and then they confiscate money and they may or may not have used the mixer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:43\n\nTo piggyback on that, one of the things that was interesting, I wrote a piece, I think it was right when the Samurai News came out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:49\n\nI interviewed two prominent activists, and one of them said to me, like, Russian oligarchs don't need this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:55\n\nThey have a bunch of other schemes that they can use to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:58\n\nLike this isn't these are like the last people we should be worried about using.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:00\n\nThey could do this through real estate schemes through other sorts of schemes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:03\n\nThis really isn't that doesn't apply to them in the way that the g you know that the the prosecution is trying to make it out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:08\n\nAnd obviously in the way that you know the samurai devs did not help by you know inviting them on Twitter to come use the the the technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:16\n\nNo, I mean the Netherlands is a paradise for Russian oligarchs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:19\n\nUh you can buy real estate, but also just these corporations are very easy to move funds around.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:24\n\nBut you know, the the argument from what aboutism basically is never uh doesn't really work that well, especially in court.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nI think that's fair.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nYeah, that he has a little bit more knowledge there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:34\n\nAny final questions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:35\n\nDo how much time do we have?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:36\n\nI just want to make sure we're okay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:37\n\nWe're good.\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:38\n\nAnd of course in the case of the North Koreans, it was clear they were using it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:41\n\nSo It's not with tornado cash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:46\n\nWhich isn't good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:50\n\nMaybe a little bit out there question, but I think with the sanctions, it's really interesting to think about it from like a global trade perspective, especially with oil.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:58\n\nThere's like a lot of bricks chatter for like a dollar alternative at the same time we have all this tariff stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:04\n\nDo you guys think any of you know that bears on Or like trickles down into the court cases where there's a higher agenda to maintain the dollar sovereignty and making sure oil trade goes through the dollar.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:16\n\nBut if you add, if you get a mixer that's really powerful, you could trade oil for Bitcoin and nobody would know if you did it in a small enough size.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:27\n\nI think it would be difficult for anyone, for the sorts of violence.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:30\n\nVolume, the sorts of money going through it, I think it would be really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:33\n\nI think Lynn Alden wrote in Broken Money something like: Bitcoin is kind of good because when somebody tries to put like serious money through the system, you can see that in the mempool, it's gonna be really, even if you do put it through a mixer, it's gonna be tough to see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:44\n\nBut also, from what I've seen about countries saying that they wanna start doing oil trade, like Bolivia just recently said we're gonna start using Bitcoin for oil payments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:53\n\nI mean, they're telegraph they're they're making it public.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:55\n\nI don't know that anonymizing the transaction is gonna do much to to change the dynamic that they're trying to establish there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:01\n\nYeah, well the fact that they're telegraphing things could also mean that they're really just trying to send a political message and not actually serious about using Bitcoin for these things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:09\n\nBut maybe then again I'm being too cynical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:11\n\nStupid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:13\n\nIt's a very yin and yang scenario here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:15\n\nIt feels good.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:16\n\nAre we good?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:16\n\nDo we have any more questions, final questions or anything?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:18\n\nI think that's all the time we have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:20\n\nAll right, sounds good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:21\n\nThank you guys so much.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nThank you, Frank.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nThanks, Charles.	# Summary of Legal Challenges and Geopolitical Implications in Crypto Mixer Cases (Tornado Cash & Samurai Wallet)\n\nBased on the transcripts provided, the legal battles surrounding **Tornado Cash** (US and Netherlands) and the **Samurai Wallet** represent a clash between non-custodial technological design and strict state enforcement of anti-money laundering (AML) laws. The following synthesis outlines the core conflicts, defense arguments, and broader geopolitical tensions.\n\n## 1. Core Legal Conflict: Custody vs. Liability\nThe central legal debate in both jurisdictions revolves around the definition of money transmission and the liability of creators for autonomous tools.\n\n*   **Non-Custodial Nature:** Both Tornado Cash (smart contracts with burned keys) and Samurai Wallet are designed as non-custodial tools where developers hold no administrative control over funds or user keys.\n*   **The Prosecution's Argument:** \n    *   **Netherlands:** The prosecutor argued that a lack of a money transmitter license invalidates the defense, making developers liable for laundering regardless of custody status. Alexey Persev was convicted of money laundering (sentenced to 5 years) based on this principle.\n    *   **United States:** Judges have indicated that they do not distinguish between touching keys or autonomy; simply creating a tool "known to be used for crime" can render developers responsible, contradicting FinCEN guidance which suggests non-custodians may avoid money transmission charges.\n*   **Samurai Specifics:** Developers face conspiracy charges (unlicensed money transmitter business and laundering). Prosecutors cite the wallet developers' open encouragement of usage by Russian oligarchs as evidence of criminal intent.\n\n## 2. Defense Strategies and Jurisdictional Divergences\nThe defense approaches in the US and Europe differ significantly due to legal frameworks regarding free speech and software liability.\n\n*   **Free Speech Arguments (US Only):** The US defense plans to argue that writing code for decentralized applications constitutes free speech, citing 1990s PGP precedents. However, this argument has **no standing** in the Dutch legal system.\n*   **Legitimate Use Cases:** Defense teams highlighted legitimate usage, such as payments to journalists (e.g., Navalny) and activists, arguing against a blanket criminalization. The transcript notes that while these arguments were raised in filings, they appear to have been largely ignored by judges during verdicts.\n*   **Custody Consensus vs. Reality:** While there is technical consensus on the non-custodial nature of these tools, prosecutors successfully argue that this does not exempt creators from AML statutes if intent or licensing criteria are met.\n\n## 3. Geopolitical Drivers and Sanctions Evasion\nThe prosecutions are deeply intertwined with international sanctions regimes and geopolitical strategy rather than purely domestic financial crimes.\n\n*   **Sanctions Enforcement:** The push against Bitcoin mixers is viewed as a mechanism to maintain US dollar hegemony. By punishing mixers, the US deters entities from bypassing sanctions (e.g., those targeting North Korea).\n    *   *Context:* North Korean actors are often untouchable via standard financial sanctions; therefore, legal systems pivot to prosecuting the mixing infrastructure itself.\n*   **Target Selection:** Prosecutions appear driven by political signaling. For instance, North Korea's use of Tornado Cash acted as a catalyst for its prosecution. Similarly, Russian oligarchs utilize traditional schemes (real estate/shells) rather than mixers because they possess the resources to move funds elsewhere; thus, crypto tools become scapegoats for high-value criminal flows.\n*   **Bitcoin Adoption Paradox:** While nations like Bolivia announce Bitcoin adoption for trade (e.g., oil), public announcements themselves can undermine the goal of anonymity.\n\n## 4. Data Accuracy and Narrative Challenges\nSignificant skepticism exists regarding the statistical narratives presented by authorities.\n\n*   **Chainalysis Inflation:** Speakers note that Chainalysis statistics often overestimate criminal activity on public blockchains, suggesting the "bad actor" narrative is inflated compared to legitimate usage.\n*   **Mixer Effectiveness:** While powerful mixers can obscure small transactions via the mempool, large-scale laundering remains visible regardless of mixing technology.\n*   **The "Fantastic Question":** Prosecutors often target the technology or developers rather than specific individuals committing fraud because the nature of mixing makes it difficult to trace specific illicit actors back to their actions.\n\n## 5. Broader Implications and Call to Action\nThe transcript concludes with warnings about privacy, surveillance, and the need for civic engagement.\n\n*   **Privacy vs. Surveillance:** Institutions currently champion Bitcoin for wealth storage but rely on custodians, lacking privacy. If they moved to self-custody, they would likely demand robust privacy protections against front-running and surveillance. Politicians avoid supporting privacy tools due to fears of association with crime/terrorism, leading to ambiguous policies that risk civil rights.\n*   **Legal Disconnects:** Financial *data* privacy is legally defensible, but financial *anonymity* (true privacy) faces significant political and legal stigma. Few figures (e.g., Molly White) defend both Bitcoin and its privacy tools simultaneously due to this reputational risk.\n*   **Call to Action:** There is an urgent need for the community to educate state-level representatives on the civil rights implications of these trials. Experts suggest engaging organizations like the **EFF** (Electronic Frontier Foundation) and **ACLU** to advocate for privacy as a fundamental right, noting that widespread adoption without privacy tools will eventually lead to unacceptable levels of global financial surveillance.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ckvTy0Fsc_M	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{Privacy,"Samourai Wallet","Tornado Cash","Bitcoin Fog",Cryptography}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:07:36.460377+05:30	2026-04-21 18:26:29.20795+05:30
11710087-90e4-4992-b8e0-589cf40a1222	-LGpW2PKwHA	conference	2025-04-18	{"Antoine Poinsot",Shinobi}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHey everybody, I'm Shinobi with Bitcoin Magazine joined by Antoine Poincet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:05\n\nI hope I didn't butcher that too badly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:07\n\nPointsot from Chain Code Labs, Bitcoin Core Contributor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:12\n\nAnd we're here to talk a little bit today about the Great Consensus Cleanup.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nSo a proposal Antoine has put forward to address a couple of long-standing bugs in the Bitcoin protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:26\n\nSome of them arguably are not that big of a deal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:30\n\nOthers are serious security threats to the entire network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nSo I guess Antoine, if you want to start off and just kind of walk people through the problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\n\nSo there is three issues that are fixed by my proposal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:43\n\nTwo of which I would consider vulnerabilities and two of which I would consider weaknesses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nI think they are worth fixing at the same time as the actual vulnerabilities.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:58\n\nThe first vulnerability is time warp.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:02\n\nTime warp allows an attacker exploited to its full potential.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:07\n\nNot in all cases, but exploited to its full potential allows an attacker to basically bring down the network in 38 days.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\n\nIt works by Messing with the timestamps of blocks and massively increasing the block rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nIt's only a concern for 51% attackers, which arguably is already an assumption that Bitcoin makes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:35\n\nBut the assumption made by Bitcoin is that a 51% attacker can censor transactions for as long as they are able to.\nkeep expensing this energy to keep censoring and they have to uh maintain this cost and maintain this fifty one percent advantage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:54\n\nThis changes the threat model.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:56\n\nThe existence of this bug ex changes the threat model of you can censor some transactions or all transactions for as long as you can maintain this advantage to in thirty eight days you can bring down the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\n\nI think it's more of a concern, however, because bringing down the network is absolutely not in the interest of existing miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:18\n\nIt might be a concern for an externally motivated attacker.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:23\n\nI think, however, it's more of a concern for perverse incentives within the network, especially with the current mining.\nuh centralization, I think it's plausible for miners to coordinate and exploit time warp to some extent.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\n\nFor instance, to increase the block rates by two X.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:48\n\nSo you have one block every five minutes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:50\n\nThis means tw twice as much resource usage for owners and the network that arguably did not sign up for that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:00\n\nAnd also, it means that they are able to effectively steal the block subsidy from the future miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nAnd so it might be in the incentive of short-sighted miners because they steal the subsidy, and short-sighted users, because they have to assign as much block space, which all other things equal is our fees.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\n\nThe second issue that I'm proposing to fix in my BIP is expensive blocks, which is blocks that are very slow to validate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:36\n\nThe details of which I'm trying to keep private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:38\n\nMost of the issues are fairly well known among the developers, the protocol developers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nBut still giving away all the details and all my scripts to create those blocks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:51\n\nSo yeah, i i if you if you released the the scripts you used to to verify this, then anybody out there could craft these transactions and attack the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:59\n\nYes, with access to some hash rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nAnd that that might even y you might even create an incentive for miners to do this to gain an advantage against other miners and force them to mine empty blocks until they finish validation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\n\nExactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\n\nWell, not only fi mine empt empty blocks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:19\n\nThe issue here is that if you're a miner, what you really want to know is whether to m to start mining on top of of a new block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:28\n\nSo you want to know whether the block is valid or not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\n\nIt might not be valid, so you don't want to be mining an empty block on top of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nSo you're you're effectively s wasting your hash rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\n\nSo there there's two ways a miner could to turn that to their advantage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:43\n\nEither forcing another miner to lose revenue mining empty blocks or just actively wasting their work on an invalid block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:51\n\nYeah, I think it's not a concern of empty blocks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:53\n\nIt's just you're you're forcing another miner to be mining on a stale tip, which is by all likelihood they're wasting All the hash rates during the time of validation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:08\n\nOr these miners might start SPV mining, which is mining only on the header and trusting the proof of work and not actually validating the transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:19\n\nBut if they do that, they might be incentivized to have some agreement with other miners, which is again more centralization pressure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nSo, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:29\n\nExpensive blocks are an issue for access accessibility to the network, but more importantly for uh uh purpose in mining incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:38\n\nThe third uh maybe to give some numbers, the worst block uh so I tried to create the worst block possible pu uh today under current consensus rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:50\n\nThe worst I could come up with to An hour and a half to validate on a Raspberry Pi and a couple minutes on high-end hardware.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:03\n\nMoving on to the next issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:07\n\nThis is Merkle tree malleability, 64 bytes transaction in the way the Merkle tree in the blocks, in the Bitcoin blocks, is constructed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:19\n\nA 64-byte transaction, which would be a leaf in the Merkle tree, can be reinterpreted as an inner node of the tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:29\n\nSo it causes an issue going in both directions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:32\n\nYou could fake a whole row of inner nodes in a block to be 64-byte transactions, which is an invalid block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:41\n\nYou can turn valid blocks into an invalid blocks with the same block hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:47\n\nYou can turn a 64-byte transaction and fake it as if it was an inner node that is committing to an arbitrarily sized transaction that would be that you can create a Merkle proof for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:04\n\nSo you can create a Merkle proof for a transaction that is not in the blockchain, but it would be so it would be a fake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nSPV proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:14\n\nThis can be mitigated because in Bitcoin, all in the market reconstructions, all transactions in the block have the same depth in the tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:25\n\nAnd when you are trying to exploit this, you actually increase the depth because you take one leaf and make a fake commitment to a transaction that is not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nDepths n plus one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:37\n\nSo if they see a proof of any real transaction in the block, they'll see like this real one is a different depth than the fake one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:45\n\nLike it doesn't match how it's supposed to look in a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:49\n\nAnd so the usual way would be to ask for an SPV proof of the Coinbase transaction so that the Coinbase transaction is at this depth.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nAnd if the SPV proof you get is at a higher debt someone is trying to attack you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nSo it's a fairly simple mitigation, but people need to actually know about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:16\n\nAnd it was like it's fairly well known among protocol developers, but not necessarily among application developers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:25\n\nSo It was uh yeah, it was brought to my attention that some application did not actually check for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nAnd also it might be too expensive to do in some uh constrained environments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nFor instance, people nowadays people nowadays are talking about crazy stuff with scripts, but some people are t uh talking about checking merk proofs in scripts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:48\n\nWell, if you want to check a merk proof of transaction, that would be very Annoying to have to check also the Coinbase transaction and check that both are actually at the same depth.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:57\n\nYeah, you're adding a big inefficiency to the witness data that you need.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:23\n\nYeah, to the to the sc yeah, to the script itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nA lot of complexity.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:27\n\nSo more complexity potentially not possible at all in some constrained environment and just a foot gun for application developers to have to know and act against it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nUh this is mitigated, well, this is fixed by invalidating sixty four byte transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:47\n\nWhich is not ideal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:49\n\nBut also I think when you weigh the price and counts is the least bad option.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:55\n\nWe can we can debate it, but uh I thi I th I think so at this point of the discussion.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nUm and then the last issue that I propose to mitigate is the one related to duplicate transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:10\n\nSo very early on in Bitcoin, it was possible to have two exactly same transaction, two transactions with the very same TX ID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:21\n\nYeah, because the coinbase commits to a previous GXID, which is 0000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:27\n\nSo you have if you have exactly two same Coinbase that space to the same the same amount to the same script pubkey, they are going to have the same GXID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:35\n\nWhich is a massive issue because we refer to transaction uh by the GXID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:40\n\nAnd that's how we look up UTXOs and So uh this was fixed in uh I think twenty eighteen or twenty twelve uh through BIP thirty uh which uh introduces validation for full nodes to For any new blocks that you receive, you go through all the transactions in the block and you look up if there is no single UTXO in your UTXO set with the same TXI.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nThis is annoying, and we could just make transactions unique directly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:19\n\nSo one way this was proposed and activated on Bitcoin was BIPCOT4, which mandated that Coinbase transactions Commit to their block height in the script sig.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:34\n\nHowever, by the time that BIP34 was activated, around block height 200,000 something, miners had been using the script sig already and as an extra noun, so you had a bunch of garbage in the script sig.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:49\n\nIt turns out between block zero and block to two hundred thousand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:54\n\nThere is a bunch of coinbases with garbage in the script sig that deserializes to a valid BIP34 block height that is in the future.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThe first one of them was around block height 400,000 something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:10\n\nAnd we just had to, it was noticed two weeks before the block height was reached.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:17\n\nSo at this point, you just had to pray that it was not different.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:21\n\nBecause at this well trying to explain it to people, uh having this uh block height from the future, let's say at block one hundred thousand, I I commit to block height four hundred thousand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nAnd then coming block height four hundred thousand, there is an opportunity for a miner to create the very same transaction that was in block height uh one hundred thousand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:45\n\nUm the next uh opportunity for this is going to be block one million nine hundred thousand, which is still far in the future, uh but it means that by this point we have to resume BIP30 validation in Bitcoin Core and in other clients.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:03\n\nThat's an issue because it's unnecessarily expensive to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:07\n\nIt's unnecessary computation that we'd rather not do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:10\n\nAnd also it's just for some clients, it's just not possible to do at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nFor UTrexo, for instance, it's not possible to do, so it would make UTrexo nodes mm not fully validate blocks anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:22\n\nSo to mitigate this, I propose to commit to the block height in the Coinbase and lock time field.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:30\n\nWell, I think for starters, I want to talk a little bit about the time warp attack, because I think there are kind of two factors you touched on that make that a very serious risk for the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:45\n\nIn the case of like miners having an incentive to kind of collude a little bit to speed up block production.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:52\n\nI am very concerned about that possibility when you look at all of the American mining companies, like the publicly traded companies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:01\n\nNone of them are profitable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nLike they're losing tens of millions of dollars every quarter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:08\n\nThey're all just financing those operations on loans and debt.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nAnd the possibility of the time warp attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:17\n\nYou know, offers them a road if they were to collude to increase their revenue versus their share of the hash rate under honest rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:27\n\nAnd the fact that they're hemorrhaging money and completely unprofitable, that is a massive incentive for all those companies to actually consider something like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nI agree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:39\n\nI mean, have you seen this recent paper that was published by?\nsome people about how to accumulate bitcoins for the government at no cost for the government.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:50\n\nBasically what they propose is just to create a new Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nJust create cre mine a new Dennis block, port the UTXO set to this to this new chain and call it Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:02\n\nAt this point, they could just propose, well, all miners in America Just exploit time work and you give fifty percent of the stolen subsidy to the government.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:14\n\nGoing through your line of reasoning, that sounds plausible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:19\n\nAnd even, you know, speaking of of governments kind of inserting themselves into the situation, you know, like a fifty-one percent attack now, like, you know, mandated by government regulations or legislation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:34\n\nLike all they can do is, like you said, censor a transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nBut like this would be a road where they could kill the entire network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:44\n\nAnd that really concerns me right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:49\n\nI feel like a lot of Bitcoiners have gotten very apathetic and kind of disconnected from those types of adversarial concerns when it comes to government.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:59\n\nAnd everyone has decided Trump.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:01\n\nSaid Bitcoin is good and he's president now, so we have nothing to worry about anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:06\n\nWell, you know, even assuming that everyone is correct about 