ing, like I'm optimistic actually, that we can just be adults and treat them in isolation, treat arguments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:35\n\nI think there is arguments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:37\n\nThere might be arguments against some parts of my proposal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:42\n\nIt's been discussed a lot for the past year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:45\n\nWe've been through a lot of the arguments on delving Bitcoin already.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:50\n\nBut I'm still open to if if users at large say, for instance, take w one of them that is part of the weaknesses more than the vulnerability, like we're not going to invalidate sixty four bytes, we're not going to run such software, some point I'm going to bend, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:05\n\nI'm going to say that's not the case now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:06\n\nI think this is the least bad option and that's why I propose the bip.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:11\n\nTo be clear with users as well, I'm not taking lightly to propose a change to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:16\n\nIt's just like not one more bip.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:18\n\nIt's something that was discussed for six years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:22\n\nThe first version of Great Consensus Cleanup was published by Matt Corallo in January twenty nineteen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:28\n\nI've been researching it for a year and a half, discussed with many people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:33\n\nThe proposal itself had input from a lot of people testing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:38\n\nAnd that's why we have this BIP now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:40\n\nIt does still does not have a number.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:41\n\nThere is a way to go.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:43\n\nNot going to propose Activation Client tomorrow, but I'm hoping to make progress with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:51\n\nWell, I hope you do because there are too many problems Bitcoiners are just ignoring and pretending aren't real.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:58\n\nLike, we need to start solving some of them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:00\n\nAll right, well, thanks for sitting down with me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:02\n\nYeah, thanks for having me.	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\nHey everybody, I'm Shinobi with Bitcoin Magazine joined by Antoine Poinsot.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:05\nI hope I didn't butcher that too badly.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:07\nPoinsot from Chain Code Labs, Bitcoin Core Contributor.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:12\nAnd we're here to talk a little bit today about the Great Consensus Cleanup.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\nSo a proposal Antoine has put forward to address a couple of long-standing bugs in the Bitcoin protocol.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:26\nSome of them arguably are not that big of a deal.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:30\nOthers are serious security threats to the entire network.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\nSo I guess Antoine, if you want to start off and just kind of walk people through the problems.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\nYeah.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\nSo there is three issues that are fixed by my proposal.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:43\nTwo of which I would consider vulnerabilities and two of which I would consider weaknesses.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\nI think they are worth fixing at the same time as the actual vulnerabilities.\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:58\nThe first vulnerability is Time Warp.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:02\nTime Warp allows an attacker exploited to its full potential.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:07\nNot in all cases, but exploited to its full potential allows an attacker to basically bring down the network in 38 days.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:15\nIt works by messing with the timestamps of blocks and massively increasing the block rate.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\nIt's only a concern for 51% attackers, which arguably is already an assumption that Bitcoin makes.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:35\nBut the assumption made by Bitcoin is that a 51% attacker can censor transactions for as long as they are able to keep expending this energy to keep censoring and they have to uh maintain this cost and maintain this fifty one percent advantage.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:54\nThis changes the threat model.\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:56\nThe existence of this bug exists changes the threat model of you can censor some transactions or all transactions for as long as you can maintain this advantage to in thirty eight days you can bring down the network.\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\nI think it's more of a concern, however, because bringing down the network is absolutely not in the interest of existing miners.\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:18\nIt might be a concern for an externally motivated attacker.\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:23\nI think, however, it's more of a concern for perverse incentives within the network, especially with the current mining centralization, I think it's plausible for miners to coordinate and exploit Time Warp to some extent.\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\nFor instance, to increase the block rates by two X.\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:48\nSo you have one block every five minutes.\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:50\nThis means tw twice as much resource usage for owners and the network that arguably did not sign up for that.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:00\nAnd also, it means that they are able to effectively steal the block subsidy from the future miners.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\nAnd so it might be in the incentive of short-sighted miners because they steal the subsidy, and short-sighted users, because they have to assign as much block space, which all other things equal is our fees.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\nThe second issue that I'm proposing to fix in my BIP is expensive blocks, which is blocks that are very slow to validate.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:36\nThe details of which I'm trying to keep private.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:38\nMost of the issues are fairly well known among the developers, the protocol developers.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\nBut still giving away all the details and all my scripts to create those blocks.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:51\nSo yeah, i i if you if you released the the scripts you used to to verify this, then anybody out there could craft these transactions and attack the network.\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:59\nYes, with access to some hash rate.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\nAnd that that might even y you might even create an incentive for miners to do this to gain an advantage against other miners and force them to mine empty blocks until they finish validation.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\nExactly.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\nWell, not only fi mine empt empty blocks.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:19\nThe issue here is that if you're a miner, what you really want to know is whether to m to start mining on top of of a new block.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:28\nSo you want to know whether the block is valid or not.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\nIt might not be valid, so you don't want to be mining an empty block on top of it.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\nSo you're you're effectively wasting your hash rate.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:39\nSo there there's two ways a miner could to turn that to their advantage.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:43\nEither forcing another miner to lose revenue mining empty blocks or just actively wasting their work on an invalid block.\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:51\nYeah, I think it's not a concern of empty blocks.\n\nIt's just you're you're forcing another miner to be mining on a stale tip, which is by all likelihood they're wasting all the hash rates during the time of validation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:08\n\nOr these miners might start SPV mining, which is mining only on the header and trusting the proof of work and not actually validating the transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:19\n\nBut if they do that, they might be incentivized to have some agreement with other miners, which is again more centralization pressure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nSo, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:29\n\nExpensive blocks are an issue for access accessibility to the network, but more importantly for uh uh purpose in mining incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:38\n\nThe third uh maybe to give some numbers, the worst block uh so I tried to create the worst block possible pu uh today under current consensus rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:50\n\nThe worst I could come up with to An hour and a half to validate on a Raspberry Pi and a couple minutes on high-end hardware.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:03\n\nMoving on to the next issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:07\n\nThis is Merkle tree malleability, 64-byte transaction in the way the Merkle tree in the blocks, in the Bitcoin blocks, is constructed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:19\n\nA 64-byte transaction, which would be a leaf in the Merkle tree, can be reinterpreted as an inner node of the tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:29\n\nSo it causes an issue going in both directions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:32\n\nYou could fake a whole row of inner nodes in a block to be 64-byte transactions, which is an invalid block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:41\n\nYou can turn valid blocks into invalid blocks with the same block hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:47\n\nYou can turn a 64-byte transaction and fake it as if it was an inner node that is committing to an arbitrarily sized transaction that would be that you can create a Merkle proof for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:04\n\nSo you can create a Merkle proof for a transaction that is not in the blockchain, but it would be so it would be a fake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nSPV proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:14\n\nThis can be mitigated because in Bitcoin, all in the market reconstructions, all transactions in the block have the same depth in the tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:25\n\nAnd when you are trying to exploit this, you actually increase the depth because you take one leaf and make a fake commitment to a transaction that is not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nDepths n plus one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:37\n\nSo if they see a proof of any real transaction in the block, they'll see like this real one is a different depth than the fake one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:45\n\nLike it doesn't match how it's supposed to look in a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:49\n\nAnd so the usual way would be to ask for an SPV proof of the Coinbase transaction so that the Coinbase transaction is at this depth.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nAnd if the SPV proof you get is at a higher debt someone is trying to attack you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nSo it's a fairly simple mitigation, but people need to actually know about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:16\n\nAnd it was like it's fairly well known among protocol developers, but not necessarily among application developers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:25\n\nSo It was uh yeah, it was brought to my attention that some application did not actually check for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nAnd also it might be too expensive to do in some uh constrained environments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nFor instance, people nowadays people nowadays are talking about crazy stuff with scripts, but some people are t uh talking about checking merk proofs in scripts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:48\n\nWell, if you want to check a merk proof of transaction, that would be very Annoying to have to check also the Coinbase transaction and check that both are actually at the same depth.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:57\n\nYeah, you're adding a big inefficiency to the witness data that you need.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:23\n\nYeah, to the to the sc yeah, to the script itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nA lot of complexity.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:27\n\nSo more complexity potentially not possible at all in some constrained environment and just a foot gun for application developers to have to know and act against it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nUh this is mitigated, well, this is fixed by invalidating sixty four byte transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:47\n\nWhich is not ideal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:49\n\nBut also I think when you weigh the price and counts is the least bad option.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:55\n\nWe can we can debate it, but uh I thi I th I think so at this point of the discussion.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nUm and then the last issue that I propose to mitigate is the one related to duplicate transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:10\n\nSo very early on in Bitcoin, it was possible to have two exactly same transaction, two transactions with the very same TX ID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:21\n\nYeah, because the coinbase commits to a previous GXID, which is 0000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:27\n\nSo you have if you have exactly two same Coinbase that spend to the same script pubkey, they are going to have the same TXID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:35\n\nWhich is a massive issue because we refer to transactions by the TXID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:40\n\nAnd that's how we look up UTXOs and so uh this was fixed in uh I think twenty eighteen or twenty twelve uh through BIP 30 which uh introduces validation for full nodes to for any new blocks that you receive, you go through all the transactions in the block and you look up if there is no single UTXO in your UTXO set with the same TXID.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nThis is annoying, and we could just make transactions unique directly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:19\n\nSo one way this was proposed and activated on Bitcoin was BIP 64, which mandated that Coinbase transactions commit to their block height in the script sig.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:34\n\nHowever, by the time that BIP 34 was activated, around block height 200,000 something, miners had been using the script sig already and as an extra noun, so you had a bunch of garbage in the script sig.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:49\n\nIt turns out between block zero and block two hundred thousand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:54\n\nThere is a bunch of coinbases with garbage in the script sig that deserializes to a valid BIP34 block height that is in the future.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThe first one of them was around block height four hundred thousand something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:10\n\nAnd we just had to, it was noticed two weeks before the block height was reached.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:17\n\nSo at this point, you just had to pray that it was not different.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:21\n\nBecause at this well trying to explain it to people, uh having this uh block height from the future, let's say at block one hundred thousand, I commit to block height four hundred thousand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nAnd then coming block height four hundred thousand, there is an opportunity for a miner to create the very same transaction that was in block height uh one hundred thousand.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:45\n\nUm the next uh opportunity for this is going to be block one million nine hundred thousand, which is still far in the future, uh but it means that by this point we have to resume BIP30 validation in Bitcoin Core and in other clients.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:03\n\nThat's an issue because it's unnecessarily expensive to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:07\n\nIt's unnecessary computation that we'd rather not do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:10\n\nAnd also it's just for some clients, it's just not possible to do at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nFor Bitcoin Core, for instance, it's not possible to do, so it would make Bitcoin Core nodes mm not fully validate blocks anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:22\n\nSo to mitigate this, I propose to commit to the block height in the Coinbase and lock time field.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:30\n\nWell, I think for starters, I want to talk a little bit about the time warp attack, because I think there are kind of two factors you touched on that make that a very serious risk for the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:45\n\nIn the case of like miners having an incentive to kind of collude a little bit to speed up block production.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:52\n\nI am very concerned about that possibility when you look at all of the American mining companies, like the publicly traded companies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:01\n\nNone of them are profitable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nLike they're losing tens of millions of dollars every quarter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:08\n\nThey're all just financing those operations on loans and debt.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nAnd the possibility of the time warp attack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:17\n\nYou know, offers them a road if they were to collude to increase their revenue versus their share of the hash rate under honest rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:27\n\nAnd the fact that they're hemorrhaging money and completely unprofitable, that is a massive incentive for all those companies to actually consider something like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nI agree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:39\n\nI mean, have you seen this recent paper that was published by?\nsome people about how to accumulate bitcoins for the government at no cost for the government.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:50\n\nBasically what they propose is just to create a new Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nJust create and mine a new difficulty block, port the UTXO set to this to this new chain and call it Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:02\n\nAt this point, they could just propose, well, all miners in America just exploit time warp and you give fifty percent of the stolen subsidy to the government.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:14\n\nGoing through your line of reasoning, that sounds plausible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:19\n\nAnd even, you know, speaking of governments kind of inserting themselves into the situation, you know, like a fifty-one percent attack now, like, you know, mandated by government regulations or legislation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:34\n\nLike all they can do is, like you said, censor a transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nBut like this would be a road where they could kill the entire network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:44\n\nAnd that really concerns me right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:49\n\nI feel like a lot of Bitcoiners have gotten very apathetic and kind of disconnected from those types of adversarial concerns when it comes to government.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:59\n\nAnd everyone has decided Trump.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:01\n\nSaid Bitcoin is good and he's president now, so we have nothing to worry about anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:06\n\nWell, you know, even assuming that everyone is correct about that, I don't personally agree with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:12\n\nHe's not going to be president forever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:15\n\nLike at the end of this four-year term, somebody else is going to take that office.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:20\n\nAnd who knows what their attitude towards Bitcoin is going to be if America remains a dominant you know, share of the hash rate, this is a a very possible way that the US government could attack and kill Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:36\n\nI think going after the choke points through OFAC and everything is probably a lower hanging fruit for them than trying to what do you think that they would mandate that these miners in America not include, for instance, OFAC transactions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:55\n\nOr do you think they would mandate actually all fan any block, like do do not build on any chain that contains OFAC transactions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:04\n\nLike do you think they would mandate an actual fifty one percent attack?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:07\n\nIf there was a majority of hash rate in the US, I absolutely do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:11\n\nI I feel like America would see that as a way to leverage control over something that right now represents something they can't control.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nPower projection, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:23\n\nPlease do not go there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nBut yeah, it's like this attack represents a complete change in the risk that a malicious mining majority represents to the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:41\n\nAnd even I believe it was back in 2017.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:46\n\nI think this also represents a very bad route that developers could consider abusing someday to do something they view as an upgrade.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:58\n\nMark Friedenbach, formerly of Blockstream, proposed doing that explicitly and using the timestamp or time warp attack essentially as an indirect way of effectively increasing the block size just through Shortening the interval between blocks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:17\n\nAnd again, the same way we don't know who's going to be president in four years, we don't know who is still going to actively be contributing to Core 10 years from now, what their motivations or assessment of different risk profiles or trade-offs are going to be.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:35\n\nYeah, if I let's say, if I want to put it in a Populist rhetoric, I would say if you're a node runner and you care about the resource constraint that you impose on miners, such as you may only mine uh up to about two megabytes block per ten minutes, and I'm restricting this block size for my interest, well, you should probably fix time warp.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:02\n\nIt's in your interest as a node runner, because it might be exploited.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:06\n\nAnd it might blow up the effective block size.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:11\n\nIt's that that kind of leans the same way into the issue of very expensive to validate blocks, opening the same kind of change in terms of threat models that we have to consider.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:28\n\nJust the ability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:30\n\nTo create this block arbitrarily, you know, it's an hour to validate on a Raspberry Pi.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:35\n\nLike that, this has implications through the whole stack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:39\n\nLike that kind of cost very well could, you know, in the fringes have security implications for users of layer twos, like a lightning node or anything that has to monitor the chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:52\n\nYou'll never crash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nAnd that that can very much put a user's funds at risk if if this happens in the wrong situation or the wrong time for them, and all the way up to this is something even a a more resourceful machine, you know, just taking a few minutes, that's enough to potentially impact miners' revenue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:14\n\nAnd it's just like the whole way through the stack, it presents a different degree of risk to different classes of users depending on the context you're looking at it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:24\n\nYeah, definitely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:26\n\nYou'll, at least back when I was familiar with one of the implementations of Lightning, if we cannot reach Bitcoin D for more than a few minutes, whatever, just stop the software because you have no no way to reach the the Bitcoin network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:46\n\nSo that that's what I s meant when I said it would crash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:51\n\nAnd yeah, a block that is making your Raspberry Pi grind for more than an hour, it clogs Bitcoin D.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:00\n\nLike the process is not going to reply for that text uh mutex that we have internally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:05\n\nIt's just the software is not going to be responsive for more than an hour on your Raspberry Pi.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:12\n\nAnd yeah, for miners, I started looking at the economic incentive of actually performing the attack, taking into account the preparation cost, because it doesn't come for free uh for an attacker as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:28\n\nBut it's not clear, it depends on the block subsidy because to prepare uh you give up on fees to sneak in some preparation transactions that are hard to validate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:42\n\nTo prepare for your very bad block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:45\n\nBut yeah, even having to go into reasoning and making some assumptions in your economic model, it just shouldn't be reasoning, but we have enough to worry about miners' incentive already.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:56\n\nWe shouldn't try to factor that in.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:59\n\nWe're just leaving another variable to complicate the whole space that has to be analyzed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:04\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:05\n\nIt's very close to selfish mining, essentially, in terms of skewness of incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:12\n\nAnd I know we talked a little bit about some of these issues, you know, I think a week ago now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:19\n\nAs far as like Merkle proof validation, there are a lot of potential. I know some people will get mad that I use this phrase, but layer twos like Liquid or Rootstock or other systems that actually make use of Merkle proofs in different ways just to maintain the security of that secondary system and how that kind of presents engineering complexity around building systems like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:45\n\nYeah, Liquid if you're going to send coins to the Liquid side chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:50\n\nThe how do they call them again?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:53\n\nWatchers, whatever the watchers are going to check that uh you need, you need to provide a Merkle proof that you actually sent these coins to the chain and well, this Merkle proof could be faked.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:07\n\nThe watcher in Liquid eye checked uh actually require check the depth of the Merkle proof against the depth of the Coinbase in the block for which it's given.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:19\n\nBut yeah, it has implication.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:21\n\nWhen we when we say SPV, people often directly think about SPV wallets that have seen less usage than the uh once had.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:34\n\nThere is there is still some usage, like uh the Electrum wallet that is still very popular. It does verify SPV proofs of transactions, although most people nowadays use Electrum with a trusted back end.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:50\n\nThere are side chains and there is whatever protocol people might be using Merkle proofs for, uh and shouldn't have to think about this weird foot gun.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:01\n\nIn the per in the in the Merkle tree.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:03\n\nYou know, and I'm even aware um of some uh systems built on networks like Ethereum that are actually SPV validating some aspects of Bitcoin state and reacting based on that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:14\n\nI I know that is outside of that would be very interesting to check out the smart contracts because they cannot update smart contracts if they didn't do the Coinbase check.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:25\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:26\n\nIt it takes uh maybe something I didn't mention.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:28\n\nThe attack does not come for free.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:30\n\nUh you need to break forth between sixty five and seventy bits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:34\n\nWhat was it like a couple million dollars or something?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:37\n\nUh yeah, but it was uh couple million dollars as estimated by Sergio in twenty seventeen with ASIC from back in the day. Mhm. Nowadays the ASIC the dollar per hash rate is much lower.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:53\n\nSo uh the attack is more accessible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:56\n\nAnd it's also comparable to just mining an invalid block and providing uh an SPV proof for this invalid block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:05\n\nToday the proof of work is something like well last year it was something like eighty bits of work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:12\n\nSo this is much cheaper than a proof of work and with a fake block you can only fake a single confirmation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:19\n\nWith this bug, you can fake an arbitrary number of confirmations.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:24\n\nYeah, so you're not even it's not even a situation of I you have to convince somebody to accept the transaction in one block or they'll see it it'll just keep building and their wallet will show like this is still valid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nAnd also another sometimes people confuse this because it requires 64-byte transactions.\n64-byte transactions are just to make the link to the Merkle tree, but you can fake arbitrarily sized transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:52\n\nYeah, and so you've got together uh The BIP that has no number yet to kind of address and fix all of these in a soft fork.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:01\n\nAnd I know, at least, you know, from my perception, most of the people at Chaincode also see this as kind of like the next major step or priority we should have as far as the consensus level goes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:14\n\nI don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:16\n\nI don't really know what.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:18\n\nWell, I know some.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:20\n\nUh people working on core think it's pretty good idea to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:25\n\nUh I think uh let me think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:28\n\nWell I it it's just my my perception kind of from the side.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:35\n\nI I had no explicit buy-in as uh we should do this as the next software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:43\n\nWe can do multiple stuff work in parallel as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:46\n\nUh but even as we should do it, I had many buy-ins such as, oh, this is great research in just thinking about what's the actual worst case uh in terms of validation time for a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:59\n\nThat's very good to know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:01\n\nUh putting together mitigations for Time Warp is good to have so we can pick it up in case of emergency, even if I do not believe it's possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:12\n\nAt least get it to the point where all you'd have to do is rebase it to the current version and not build everything from scratch.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:19\n\nYeah, but I find this uh say one thing uh historically that was uh said is Time Warp is less of a concern because it's pretty obvious in chain and we would react.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:36\n\nI was like, what in thirty eight days, who are we?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:39\n\nWho are we?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:40\n\nWho what individual is going to do what action exactly?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:44\n\nBecause I do not like I do not believe we can just coordinate the whole Bitcoin industry in thirty days to decide the validity of re realistically, like you don't have thirty eight days, you have a week or two before the difficulty really starts ratcheting down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:03\n\nYeah, that's yeah, for let's say four weeks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:07\n\nAt four weeks, twenty-eight if twenty-eight days at this point, it's almost over.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:12\n\nAnd yeah, uh I just think it's very unrealistic to expect that amount of coordination from people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:21\n\nIf it was possible to coordinate people in this scale, we probably wouldn't need such a system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:27\n\nThere'd be a lot less frustration with Bitcoin Core, I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nBut yeah, it's you know, 'cause the Time Warp especially, like that's been publicly known almost the entire time I've been in this space.\n2011.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:43\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:43\n\nOr no, actually longer than than I've been in this space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:46\n\nAnd it's always, from my perspective, just every couple of years, someone's brought it up and gone, like, we should look at this and actually address it, and then nobody responds, and it goes back off into the background.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:00\n\nBut this is really a massive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:03\n\nExistential risk.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:05\n\nAnd like, we are at the point, in my opinion, as far as integration into the mainstream financial system, attention from politicians, the professional scale that mining has reached in terms of how high the bar is now to meaningfully start an operation as a miner.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:25\n\nThis isn't like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:27\n\nAn intellectual curiosity anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:29\n\nThis is a risk that very realistically could kill Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:35\n\nAnd if not, kill, like severely damage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:39\n\nYeah, severely damage, I think, because at this point, imagine it's exploited to its full potential.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:45\n\nSome people would coordinate to invalidate this chain and end up on another chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:51\n\nProbably people would just.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:52\n\nTake the opportunity to sneak more changes in, and maybe there would be multiple chains.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:58\n\nMaybe it's unclear what would happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:01\n\nSeverely harming the trust in the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:04\n\nAt what block exactly do we fork off.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:06\n\nI mean, I guarantee you, if this actually manifested in the real world, you'd have people calling for a proof of work change on top of like just addressing the Time Warp vulnerability itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:18\n\nYeah, that would be messy, which is exactly what we don't want with Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:23\n\nMm-hmm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:24\n\nYeah, you know, it's it it j it just goes to show, you know, uh a lot of the talks, a lot of the conversation here, you know, at least today, has been focused on like how much work and issues there are to deal with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:37\n\nUh like beyond, let's add a new soft fork to create new functionality for Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:43\n\nLike, there really is kind of this mountain of engineering work and issues to figure out that doesn't circulate in the Twitter spotlight the way, like, what covenant are we adding?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:54\n\nYeah, I see so many people on Twitter proudly, even big accounts, proudly boasting about how they didn't update their nodes in five versions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:04\n\nYeah, it's like, okay, well we are now vulnerable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:09\n\nAnd we just published them on the website.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:13\n\nTruth is, I'm intimately convinced that we have not seen anything yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:18\n\nNobody cares about attacking Bitcoin at the moment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:21\n\nIt's like we released TV's and nobody and you had twenty percent of the network that was still not upgraded.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:27\n\nNobody cared to attack them, to take down the nodes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:30\n\nI released this chain split bug for BTCD.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:34\n\nIt was fully accessible and completely damaging and zero cost to exploit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:41\n\nNobody exploited it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:43\n\nAnd one year later, you still have half of the BTCD nodes on the network still running vulnerable versions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:49\n\nI can only hope that that is essentially just nodes that are economically irrelevant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:55\n\nLike just the user who spun up a node and forgot about it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:59\n\nYeah, absolutely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:00\n\nAbsolutely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:01\n\nBut for the percentage of Bitcoin Core nodes that are still vulnerable to published security advisories, there's just so many of them that some of them must be somehow economically relevant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:16\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:16\n\nUpgrade your nodes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:17\n\nLet's just hope that uh the conversation around your proposal actually goes somewhere, you know, regardless of what happens with covenants or not, or how you relatively like think we should act first or what we should do first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:32\n\nLike these are very serious issues that we can't just ignore and pretend aren't a problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:38\n\nYeah, we have mechanisms to not have to choose which one needs to go first or not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:45\n\nSo I think we should treat them separately and treat them in isolation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:52\n\nI'm trying to also, to be honest, it's going to be kind of a political game of trying to get to uh activate the software, you need to get users to upgrade to consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:03\n\nSo there is no get away.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:07\n\nBut I'm trying to just be, I don't know, honest.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:11\n\nI shine on covenant issues sometimes with differing opinion from most of the people working on this topic that might harm like, um but then maybe getting some horse trading of like, I'm nice to your proposal, I'm nice to my proposal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:28\n\nI'm just hoping, like I'm optimistic actually, that we can just be adults and treat them in isolation, treat arguments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:35\n\nI think there are arguments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:37\n\nThere might be arguments against some parts of my proposal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:42\n\nIt's been discussed a lot for the past year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:45\n\nWe've been through a lot of the arguments on deliberating Bitcoin already.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:50\n\nBut I'm still open to if users at large say, for instance, take one of them that is part of the weaknesses more than the vulnerability, like we're not going to invalidate sixty four bytes, we're not going to run such software, some point I'm going to bend, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:05\n\nI'm going to say that's not the case now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:06\n\nI think this is the least bad option and that's why I propose the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:11\n\nTo be clear with users as well, I'm not taking lightly to propose a change to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:16\n\nIt's just like not one more BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:18\n\nIt's something that was discussed for six years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:22\n\nThe first version of Great Consensus Cleanup was published by Matt Corallo in January twenty nineteen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:28\n\nI've been researching it for a year and a half, discussed with many people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:33\n\nThe proposal itself had input from a lot of people testing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:38\n\nAnd that's why we have this BIP now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:40\n\nIt does still does not have a number.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:41\n\nThere is a way to go.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:43\n\nNot going to propose Activation Client tomorrow, but I'm hoping to make progress with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:51\n\nWell, I hope you do because there are too many problems Bitcoiners are just ignoring and pretending aren't real.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:58\n\nLike, we need to start solving some of them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:00\n\nAll right, well, thanks for sitting down with me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:02\n\nYeah, thanks for having me.	Antoine Poinsot of Chain Code Labs proposes a critical consensus cleanup to address four specific vulnerabilities threatening Bitcoin's security and decentralization: Time Warp, expensive block validation, Merkle tree malleability, and duplicate transactions via the coinbase script signature. The Time Warp vulnerability allows a 51% attacker or colluding miners to manipulate timestamps, artificially shortening block intervals, enabling up to 38 days of censorship, and effectively stealing future block subsidies; this alters the threat model by lowering the cost for actors to execute attacks and creates perverse incentives against unprofitable public mining. Expensive blocks require over an hour to validate on low-end hardware, stalling nodes like Bitcoin D, forcing miners into stale tips, and potentially driving users toward SPV strategies which further centralizes the network. Merkle tree malleability permits attackers to construct blocks that appear valid to light clients by manipulating transaction depth; while mitigatable via Coinbase checks, this approach imposes significant engineering complexity and resource overhead, particularly for constrained environments and Layer 2 solutions like Liquid. Finally, duplicate transactions occur when identical coinbase transactions generate duplicate TXIDs, complicating UTXO sets and necessitating costly retrospective validation; fixing this requires committing block height to the coinbase script, which risks breaking compatibility with legacy blocks containing future-dated garbage data in that field.\n\nThe speakers argue that these issues collectively pose an existential risk, exacerbated by mining centralization and the potential for exploitation by malicious actors or governments, necessitating immediate fixes rather than relying on difficult-to-coordinate industry reactions. Despite known vulnerabilities and published advisories, many nodes still run outdated software, as evidenced by past ignored threats like a BTCD bug, meaning no active attacks are currently occurring but the lack of consensus upgrades creates significant risk with economically relevant implications. The proposal aims to address accumulated technical debt through long-term cleanup of consensus rules and improved security; notably, this specific BIP lacks a number and has been under development for six years. The ultimate goal is to separate urgent security cleanup efforts from political debates regarding features like Covenants, advocating for treating security and governance as isolated priorities to encourage users to upgrade their nodes and prevent the continued ignoring of critical problems threatening network integrity.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-LGpW2PKwHA	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{"Consensus Rules","Timewarp Attack","Block Validation","SPV-proof Malleability","Mining Decentralization"}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:02:46.221915+05:30	2026-04-21 16:42:13.429073+05:30
e0d41fe5-e8c6-44f4-8f43-63cfe1e3f950	-NlgxiLgqZo	conference	2025-05-16	{}	{}	{}	## Intro: MIT Bitcoin Expo with Vitor Pomplona\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nAll right, everybody, we are here at the MIT Bitcoin and Freedom Tech Expo here in Boston, Massachusetts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:06\n\nAnd I am joined by Vita Pamplona, the creator of Amethyst, not the founder, as he still, as he told me to say, the creator of Amethyst, an open source client for Noster, a very diverse and very great client for creators to use.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:20\n\nAnd that's sort of the angle that I want to dive in here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:22\n\nSo we were talking earlier this morning, and you said, you know, like, What can we do to attract Bitcoin magazine to use Nostra more?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:29\n\nAnd my question back to you is sort of what do you see working?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nWhat is attracting creators and other people who would normally just use social media for whatever it's X or Instagram or anything else?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\n\nWe're seeing complementary sort of technologies forming, whether it's sort of like we could call them Twitter clones or Instagram clones like Olas.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:48\n\nAnd now you can get paid in sats if people like your work, value for value exchange.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nWhat do you see working and what would be sort of your call to action for creators out there who are already putting stuff on the internet that can now earn Bitcoin for it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:00\n\nWell, definitely there are a lot of apps right now that are working really well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:04\n\nIt's not only about Twitter anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:08\n\nLike you mentioned, Olaz and Fountain for podcasts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nWe have a lot of live streaming events going on, video apps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nSo there is starting to be this next level of applications that we are starting to see that are becoming good enough for real users to just navigate through and have a good experience.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:31\n\nI think they are already out of their own better stages and trying to get into the actual real world of activities.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:42\n\nAnd in each one of those, you see creators and users engaging with one another without having a platform to manage any of that conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nAnd that does include zaps or Bitcoin payments to one another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:57\n\nThat does include regular interactions like reactions and replies and conversations and clubs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:05\n\nAnd we have, say, for instance, the new I'd say Discord-like clients like Chachi and Flotilla.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:13\n\nOh, cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:14\n\nThose are more for companies so they can discuss that internal matter in a separate relay in a separate server so that no one has access to those things and then or either keep them private forever or release them when appropriate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nBut you do start to see this level of okay, now users are starting to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:33\n\nCreate and talk to one another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:35\n\nBecause until then, like it was mostly about developers and users.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:39\n\nAnd you saw that relationship, those apps were flowing, but mostly in one way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\n\nAnd I think now we are starting to see communities being formed, we're starting to see more, frankly, more money being transferred.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:52\n\nBecause we know that in the social network in general, Users want to have more than just a platform.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nThey want to have a living.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:04\n\nThey want to have hope that they can actually achieve something bigger than what they could do alone at their houses or at their cities.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nAnd also, as you probably have seen for many of the current conversations in there, it does connect people that today are mostly inside Bitcoin or around the Bitcoin community.\nallows people to find each other, allows them to just build their communities as as they prefer and as they see fit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:31\n\nBut hopefully we can take it to the next level, which is okay, now even outside of the Bitcoin ecosystem, people can start finding each other and finding reasons to one own their content, like Nostrad promises them to, and manage their own social network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nBy themselves and hopefully making a living out of this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:53\n\nI think that is our end goal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:55\n\nIf we can get creators to that point that they can have a living and excite people enough to participate, generate discussions, generate content, help each other through life, we will win as a platform.\n\n\n\n## The emergence of peer-to-peer community formation\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:07\n\nBecause then it's just like any other servers out there, but there is no control for anybody else.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\n\nWe truly enable peer-to-peer participation in society.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:17\n\nAmazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nWhen I hear all of this and when I try to keep up with what's happening in Nostr, I think to myself, you know, this is probably what like Bitcoin in 2012 felt like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:27\n\nA lot of like people wrapping their head around it, sort of like, is this real?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\n\nIs this not real?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:32\n\nWhat works good?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nWhat doesn't work good?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:34\n\nDo you feel like that's a valid comparison?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:36\n\nAre we just in the very early days of Noster?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nI think it's what, year four of Nostr?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:40\n\nAre we just in the early days?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:42\n\nHave we not even seen the you know, even the beginning, have we not even scratched the surface of what it's really capable of?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:48\n\nPlus, did it take a little bit of time to make it a little more usable?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:51\n\nLike I personally use Primal a lot more than I use other apps, only because it's just something that I grew comfortable with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:57\n\nI've tried a bunch, I still use different apps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:59\n\nBut I had to sort of like try different things out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:02\n\nAre people still feeling out what Noster is and how it works best for them is my point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:06\n\nDefinitely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:07\n\nAnd our apps are at that level that they are crossing the boundary between one to the other and to actually getting into production level applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:14\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:15\n\nI think Primo is a good example of a very good app that is super stable for most of our users.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:20\n\nBut there are many apps that are not stable enough yet and are still in that experimentation phase, trying to figure out what they want to be, what they you know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nWhat they're trying to do for their users and how their users are reacting to those features.\n\n\n\n## Nostr feels like Bitcoin in 2012\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:33\n\nBut, like you said, this is exactly like Bitcoin was about 10 years ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:38\n\nAnd when you go back there, I remember very vividly from my perspective, there was a lot of noise.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:45\n\nI was a technical person around.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nI didn't understand hard money at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:50\n\nIt was just about the tech.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:51\n\nIt was just about building something that was cool enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:54\n\nThat was resistant enough, that was living by itself kind of thing on the ether, and we could actually go there and see the transactions and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:02\n\nThat was very cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:03\n\nAnd to me, then the conversation about hard money and how economically viable this new approach was kind of showed up.\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:12\n\nAnd I think for many of us, it's kind of the same thing with Nostr.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:14\n\nWe have the platform, we have the protocols, we know more or less what it can do, but we haven't yet seen this next.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:20\n\nLevel of okay, now let's leave the technology part of it and let's get into society.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:27\n\nYes, yes, yes, yes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nWhat will be out there that will be powered by this type of network that is so free that everybody can say whatever they want, developers can do whatever they want, and they can just ship things and you know, it will just work because it's really that simple, it's really that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:44\n\nEasy to do.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:46\n\nWith amethyst, so I think as a creator of anything in this world, right, we sort of have a vision for what we want to create, we put it out there into the world, and then it then the world sort of adopts it the way that they see fit, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:57\n\nSo I'm curious, in you working to create amethyst, putting it out there in the world, what did you expect and what has happened with it?\n\n\n\n## The story behind Amethyst’s development\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:05\n\nHow has it evolved maybe in ways that you did not expect?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:07\n\nI'll put it that way as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nMost of the features we shipped were very well accepted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:15\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:15\n\nAnd I was not expecting that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:17\n\nOkay, interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:17\n\nBecause there was, you know, Ametis came in in the very same time that Elon was talking about buying Twitter and how this, like, let's make a mega app out of Twitter conversation was happening in that ecosystem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:31\n\nAnd I went.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:32\n\nFor the same thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:33\n\nLet's do a bigger app.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:34\n\nLet's see what's out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:35\n\nBecause all the nostalg apps by that time was just about very simple features and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:40\n\nI was like, I'm going to own this whole thing, and I'm going to try to make it as big as possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:45\n\nI don't think it's going to work, but I'm going to try.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:48\n\nAnd what I realized is that the fact that we have multiple, so we have, like, for instance, we have a TikTok fit in the same client as we have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nA usual Twitter feed, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nWe have the chat and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:01\n\nBy having all of them kind of together, Ametyst is helping everybody else kickstart their own applications and their own way of doing things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nSo now we have OLAS, which is the picture in our Instagram-like feed, which we had on Ametyst, but we were just alone in that ecosystem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:20\n\nAnd OLAS helped us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nWell, it it started only in the space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:25\n\nAnd from our perspective, we just helped them kind of kickstart that entire feed, that entire experience where we share pictures instead of just posts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:35\n\nRight?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:35\n\nSo from a bigger abstinent point, we can, for now, because we are so early on noster, we can help maintain the social aspect.\nof the network, just making sure the kind of the that web of trust is there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:50\n\nAnd then from that point forward, it's very likely that these specialized app implementations are going to surpass what we do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:59\n\nAnd because they can just focus in that feature more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:02\n\nAnd that was my hypothesis.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nI was like, I'm going to do something big, but it's going to fail.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nBut maybe that was necessary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nYeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:10\n\nJust to keep that in a web of trust going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:12\n\nAnd I think that is a big learning for me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:15\n\nIt still hasn't failed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:16\n\nSo maybe there is a reason to have something like Ametyst out there that kind of implements everything it can in a basic sense that you can just have all your friends, you can see what all your friends are doing, even though they're using different applications through Ametis.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nIf you use Ametyst, you can see everything everybody else is doing on every other app.\nGot it, got it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:37\n\nAnd because of that, it kind of builds, oh, you're using OLAS.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:39\n\nOkay, I'm letting you data as one as well.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:42\n\nI can we can see users hopping between clients and trying to experiment with different things just because the events are visible to to everybody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:50\n\nThat's super cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:50\n\nAnd we hope that is kinda our contribution to the space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:53\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:54\n\nLike let's see if it we see if one if that needs to be there first of all.\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nAnd if it does, we can just do more of it.\nYeah.\nInteresting.\n\n\n\n## Will Nostr drive Bitcoin adoption through user value?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:02\n\nWhen it comes to compelling creators to use something like Amethyst or any other Nostric client, I usually sort of start with like, you know, you want to earn some money for what you're, you know, potentially earn some money for what you're putting out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:12\n\nAnd even if you're just You know, if you're going to post something on Instagram, put it on, you know, a Nostra client as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nJust test it out, try different things out, see what happens.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:20\n\nDo you think that eventually Nostra becomes one of the biggest drivers of Bitcoin adoption?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:26\n\nAnd because I think it's a way for people to not have to think about Bitcoin too much, it's more like, here's the value you're getting for what you're putting out into the world.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:33\n\nAnd it's great because I think also when people receive Bitcoin that way, it's less of like, well, I bought it at this point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:38\n\nPrice with my fiat money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:40\n\nIt's more like I earned it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:41\n\nI just have it in this wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:42\n\nI use that for my circular economy, Nostra stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:45\n\nDo you see this as one of the biggest onboarding tools out there, maybe within the next two or three years?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:50\n\nFor sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:50\n\nAnd to be clear, on the past, say, two years or so, all of the good waves of new users we have had, they were never about either Bitcoin or either Nostra.\n\n\n\n## Onboarding via freedom first, Bitcoin second\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nThey came in because of Freedom.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:04\n\nRight, because of something else, because of something some censorship in their country, and they started playing with Noster, then they realized what the protocol was about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nSo first they install, then they understand what it is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nAnd then they're like, oh, but I can do Zaps now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:20\n\nInteresting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:21\n\nAnd then they get onboarded into the Bitcoin ecosystem of tools.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:26\n\nAnd then you start talking to people, you're going to say, Oh, no.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:29\n\nWhich wallet are you going to use on this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nCan you do zaps automatically?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:33\n\nAre you doing with apps that require you to open the application and kind of like confirm the transaction each step of the way?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:41\n\nOr are you doing in some other way, which because we do have that difference on our server right now?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:46\n\nSo that little conversation of like, well, you came in because of these other things you have in life.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:52\n\nAnd now you are getting introduced to a system that is just a little bit better and maybe beyond what you were expecting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:57\n\nTo me, no app should ever talk about either Noster or Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:02\n\nYeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThey should just be there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nJust what it is, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nBecause the main goal for the app is to get people to do their thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:08\n\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:08\n\nIt's to get people to be creative or to be to talk to their friends or to have a chat with their family and solve today, you know, day-to-day problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:17\n\nIt just so happens that that same platform enables them to manage their own data, become actually hold and take that into account by themselves if they want to, and use a payment protocol that is just the best we have today.\n\n\n\n## Why Bitcoin should be invisible in great UX\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nAnd you can do that at the same time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:37\n\nOn Ametas, for instance, we don't teach even basic key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:42\n\nSecurity when you are creating an account.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nYou install the app, there's a new screen that says, you know, what's your name?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nYou insert a name, you say create account, and that's it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:51\n\nWe don't explain what an NSAC is, what an NPUB is, what all these things, terms are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:56\n\nNo, no, no.\nStart using first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nLet's make sure you can do your thing, the thing you want to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:02\n\nAnd then, you know, a week or a month later, we're going to start.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:07\n\nBugging you about, like, let's make sure you have all places and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:10\n\nAnd by the way, we can do transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:12\n\nYou can actually receive on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:14\n\nSo, for me, it's never really about just the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:17\n\nAt least, I don't want to make an app that just makes everything we do transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:20\n\nYou're right, exactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nYes, yes, yes, yes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:22\n\nYeah, transactions are good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:23\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:23\n\nBut, like, we want to leave.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:25\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:25\n\nYou know, and the transactions sometimes just get in the way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nSo, I want them to be there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:31\n\nI want them to be the preferred payment flow for folks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:35\n\nI want them to be easy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:37\n\nI want them to really help us build this community of creators and users and connect them as best as we can, make them behave and develop their community further, think about their own priorities in that sense.\n\n\n\n## Improving account security via FROST/Nostr signer\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:53\n\nAnd then, if Bitcoin and Notre in general can help them do more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:57\n\nSo be it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:58\n\nThe goal is to get the users to do the thing they want to do first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nLove that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nAmazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nVery cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nAny hints, anything you can hint at in terms of what's coming down the pipeline?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nWhat's next for Amethyst, Noster in general?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nWhat's on your mind, slash, what are you seeing happening maybe behind the scenes a little bit?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nYeah, so for the technical folks out there, you know that if you use Noster today, when you create a key, all of your account is Managed by that key, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:24\n\nYou can't change it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:25\n\nThere's no way to change passwords on Noster.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nI think we are solving that problem now with a new approach that you think you folks covered, which is called Frost.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nAnd it's just a new key scheme where you can actually, you have your key, you don't need to change it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nYou take that key and you put it into a Frost signer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:47\n\nAnd that signer breaks your key into many components.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:50\n\nAnd those components go into the apps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nSo, in that way, you can rotate one of these components and insert a new password in a specific app.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:58\n\nIf you think your key has leaked, that is a way you're gonna restart your connection to that account, make sure it's safe.\n\n\n\n## The future of decentralized identity and key rotation\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:06\n\nAnd then you make sure your signer knows that you rotated that key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nBut from that endpoint, from that moment forward, We have a solution for account management that is very close to what centralized platforms can offer today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nAnd I think that is our next step, our next big step.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:23\n\nI think from like a research and scientific standpoint, we need to verify that those things actually work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nAnd if they do, it's going to be great for everybody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:31\n\nBecause we don't change Noster.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nIt's just the same protocol has always been.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:34\n\nBut it's a new signing tool that offers those that rotation and that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:39\n\nYou know, extra safety and extra security in the protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:43\n\nAmazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:43\n\nWell, Victor, thank you so much for your time.\n\n\n\n## Get started with Amethyst\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:45\n\nMuch appreciated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:46\n\nThank you guys for watching.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:47\n\nAnd yeah, keep an eye on what Victor's working on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:49\n\nIf you haven't created your Amethyst account yet, consider this your call to action.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nAnd we will talk soon.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:54\n\nTake it easy, everybody.	## Intro: MIT Bitcoin Expo with Vitor Pomplona\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nAll right, everybody, we are here at the MIT Bitcoin and Freedom Tech Expo here in Boston, Massachusetts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:06\n\nAnd I am joined by Vita Pamplona, the creator of Amethyst, not the founder, as he still, as he told me to say, the creator of Amethyst, an open source client for Nostr, a very diverse and very great client for creators to use.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:20\n\nAnd that's sort of the angle that I want to dive in here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:22\n\nSo we were talking earlier this morning, and you said, you know, like, What can we do to attract Bitcoin magazine to use Nostr more?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:29\n\nAnd my question back to you is sort of what do you see working?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:31\n\nWhat is attracting creators and other people who would normally just use social media for whatever it's X or Instagram or anything else?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:39\n\nWe're seeing complementary sort of technologies forming, whether it's sort of like we could call them Twitter clones or Instagram clones like Olas.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:48\n\nAnd now you can get paid in sats if people like your work, value for value exchange.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:52\n\nWhat do you see working and what would be sort of your call to action for creators out there who are already putting stuff on the internet that can now earn Bitcoin for it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:00\n\nWell, definitely there are a lot of apps right now that are working really well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:04\n\nIt's not only about Twitter anymore.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:08\n\nLike you mentioned, Olas and Fountain for podcasts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nWe have a lot of live streaming events going on, video apps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nSo there is starting to be this next level of applications that we are starting to see that are becoming good enough for real users to just navigate through and have a good experience.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:31\n\nI think they are already out of their own beta stages and trying to get into the actual real world of activities.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:42\n\nAnd in each one of those, you see creators and users engaging with one another without having a platform to manage any of that conversation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:53\n\nAnd that does include zaps or Bitcoin payments to one another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:57\n\nThat does include regular interactions like reactions and replies and conversations and clubs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:05\n\nAnd we have, say, for instance, the new I'd say Discord-like clients like Chachi and Flotilla.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:13\n\nOh, cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:14\n\nThose are more for companies so they can discuss that internal matter in a separate relay in a separate server so that no one has access to those things and then or either keep them private forever or release them when appropriate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nBut you do start to see this level of okay, now users are starting to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:33\n\nCreate and talk to one another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:35\n\nBecause until then, like it was mostly about developers and users.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:39\n\nAnd you saw that relationship, those apps were flowing, but mostly in one way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\n\nAnd I think now we are starting to see communities being formed, we're starting to see more, frankly, more money being transferred.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:52\n\nBecause we know that in the social network in general, Users want to have more than just a platform.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nThey want to have a living.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:04\n\nThey want to have hope that they can actually achieve something bigger than what they could do alone at their houses or at their cities.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nAnd also, as you probably have seen for many of the current conversations in there, it does connect people that today are mostly inside Bitcoin or around the Bitcoin community.\n\nIt allows people to find each other, allows them to just build their communities as they prefer and as they see fit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:31\n\nBut hopefully we can take it to the next level, which is okay, now even outside of the Bitcoin ecosystem, people can start finding each other and finding reasons to own their content, like Nostr promises them to, and manage their own social network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nBy themselves and hopefully making a living out of this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:53\n\nI think that is our end goal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:55\n\nIf we can get creators to that point that they can have a living and excite people enough to participate, generate discussions, generate content, help each other through life, we will win as a platform.\n\n\n\n## The emergence of peer-to-peer community formation\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:07\n\nBecause then it's just like any other servers out there, but there is no control for anybody else.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\n\nWe truly enable peer-to-peer participation in society.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:17\n\nAmazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nWhen I hear all of this and when I try to keep up with what's happening in Nostr, I think to myself, you know, this is probably what like Bitcoin in 2012 felt like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:27\n\nA lot of like people wrapping their head around it, sort of like, is this real?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\n\nIs this not real?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:32\n\nWhat works good?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nWhat doesn't work good?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:34\n\nDo you feel like that's a valid comparison?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:36\n\nAre we just in the very early days of Noster?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nI think it's what, year four of Nostr?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:40\n\nAre we just in the early days?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:42\n\nHave we not even seen the you know, even the beginning, have we not even scratched the surface of what it's really capable of?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:48\n\nPlus, did it take a little bit of time to make it a little more usable?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:51\n\nLike I personally use Primal a lot more than I use other apps, only because it's just something that I grew comfortable with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:57\n\nI've tried a bunch, I still use different apps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:59\n\nBut I had to sort of like try different things out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:02\n\nAre people still feeling out what Noster is and how it works best for them is my point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:06\n\nDefinitely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:07\n\nAnd our apps are at that level that they are crossing the boundary between one to the other and to actually getting into production level applications.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:14\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:15\n\nI think Primo is a good example of a very good app that is super stable for most of our users.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:20\n\nBut there are many apps that are not stable enough yet and are still in that experimentation phase, trying to figure out what they want to be, what they you know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:28\n\nWhat they're trying to do for their users and how their users are reacting to those features.\n\n\n\n## Nostr feels like Bitcoin in 2012\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:33\n\nBut, like you said, this is exactly like Bitcoin was about 10 years ago.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:38\n\nAnd when you go back there, I remember very vividly from my perspective, there was a lot of noise.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:45\n\nI was a technical person around.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nI didn't understand hard money at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:50\n\nIt was just about the tech.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:51\n\nIt was just about building something that was cool enough.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:54\n\nThat was resistant enough, that was living by itself kind of thing on the ether, and we could actually go there and see the transactions and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:02\n\nThat was very cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:03\n\nAnd to me, then the conversation about hard money and how economically viable this new approach was kind of showed up.\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:12\n\nAnd I think for many of us, it's kind of the same thing with Nostr.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:14\n\nWe have the platform, we have the protocols, we know more or less what it can do, but we haven't yet seen this next.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:20\n\nLevel of okay, now let's leave the technology part of it and let's get into society.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:27\n\nYes, yes, yes, yes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nWhat will be out there that will be powered by this type of network that is so free that everybody can say whatever they want, developers can do whatever they want, and they can just ship things and you know, it will just work because it's really that simple, it's really that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:44\n\nEasy to do.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:46\n\nWith amethyst, so I think as a creator of anything in this world, right, we sort of have a vision for what we want to create, we put it out there into the world, and then it then the world sort of adopts it the way that they see fit, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:57\n\nSo I'm curious, in you working to create amethyst, putting it out there in the world, what did you expect and what has happened with it?\n\n\n\n## The story behind Amethyst’s development\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:05\n\nHow has it evolved maybe in ways that you did not expect?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:07\n\nI'll put it that way as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nMost of the features we shipped were very well accepted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:15\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:15\n\nAnd I was not expecting that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:17\n\nOkay, interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:17\n\nBecause there was, you know, Ametis came in in the very same time that Elon was talking about buying Twitter and how this, like, let's make a mega app out of Twitter conversation was happening in that ecosystem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:31\n\nAnd I went.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:32\n\nFor the same thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:33\n\nLet's do a bigger app.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:34\n\nLet's see what's out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:35\n\nBecause all the nostalg apps by that time was just about very simple features and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:40\n\nI was like, I'm going to own this whole thing, and I'm going to try to make it as big as possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:45\n\nI don't think it's going to work, but I'm going to try.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:48\n\nAnd what I realized is that the fact that we have multiple, so we have, like, for instance, we have a TikTok fit in the same client as we have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nA usual Twitter feed, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nWe have the chat and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:01\n\nBy having all of them kind of together, Amethyst is helping everybody else kickstart their own applications and their own way of doing things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nSo now we have OLOS, which is the picture in our Instagram-like feed, which we had on Amethyst, but we were just alone in that ecosystem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:20\n\nAnd OLOS helped us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nWell, it did started only in the space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:25\n\nAnd from our perspective, we just helped them kind of kickstart that entire feed, that entire experience where we share pictures instead of just posts.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:35\n\nRight?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:35\n\nSo from a bigger abstinent point, we can, for now, because we are so early on Nostr, we can help maintain the social aspect\nof the network, just making sure the kind of the that web of trust is there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:50\n\nAnd then from that point forward, it's very likely that these specialized app implementations are going to surpass what we do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:59\n\nAnd because they can just focus in that feature more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:02\n\nAnd that was my hypothesis.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:04\n\nI was like, I'm going to do something big, but it's going to fail.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nBut maybe that was necessary.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nYeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:10\n\nJust to keep that in a web of trust going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:12\n\nAnd I think that is a big learning for me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:15\n\nIt still hasn't failed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:16\n\nSo maybe there is a reason to have something like Amethyst out there that kind of implements everything it can in a basic sense that you can just have all your friends, you can see what all your friends are doing, even though they're using different applications through Amethyst.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:32\n\nIf you use Amethyst, you can see everything everybody else is doing on every other app.\nGot it, got it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:37\n\nAnd because of that, it kind of builds, oh, you're using OLOS.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:39\n\nOkay, I'm letting you data as one as well.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:42\n\nI can we can see users hopping between clients and trying to experiment with different things just because the events are visible to to everybody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:50\n\nThat's super cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:50\n\nAnd we hope that is kinda our contribution to the space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:53\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:54\n\nLike let's see if it we see if one if that needs to be there first of all.\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nAnd if it does, we can just do more of it.\nYeah.\nInteresting.\n\n\n\n## Will Nostr drive Bitcoin adoption through user value?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:02\n\nWhen it comes to compelling creators to use something like Amethyst or any other Nostr client, I usually sort of start with like, you know, you want to earn some money for what you're, you know, potentially earn some money for what you're putting out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:12\n\nAnd even if you're just You know, if you're going to post something on Instagram, put it on, you know, a Nostr client as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nJust test it out, try different things out, see what happens.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:20\n\nDo you think that eventually Nostr becomes one of the biggest drivers of Bitcoin adoption?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:26\n\nAnd because I think it's a way for people to not have to think about Bitcoin too much, it's more like, here's the value you're getting for what you're putting out into the world.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:33\n\nAnd it's great because I think also when people receive Bitcoin that way, it's less of like, well, I bought it at this point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:38\n\nPrice with my fiat money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:40\n\nIt's more like I earned it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:41\n\nI just have it in this wallet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:42\n\nI use that for my circular economy, Nostr stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:45\n\nDo you see this as one of the biggest onboarding tools out there, maybe within the next two or three years?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:50\n\nFor sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:50\n\nAnd to be clear, in the past, say, two years or so, all of the good waves of new users we have had, they were never about either Bitcoin or either Nostr.\n\n\n\n## Onboarding via freedom first, Bitcoin second\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nThey came in because of Freedom.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:04\n\nRight, because of something else, because of something some censorship in their country, and they started playing with Nostr, then they realized what the protocol was about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nSo first they install, then they understand what it is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nAnd then they're like, oh, but I can do Zaps now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:20\n\nInteresting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:21\n\nAnd then they get onboarded into the Bitcoin ecosystem of tools.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:26\n\nAnd then you start talking to people, you're going to say, Oh, no.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:29\n\nWhich wallet are you going to use on this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nCan you do Zaps automatically?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:33\n\nAre you doing with apps that require you to open the application and kind of like confirm the transaction each step of the way?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:41\n\nOr are you doing in some other way, which because we do have that difference on our server right now?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:46\n\nSo that little conversation of like, well, you came in because of these other things you have in life.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:52\n\nAnd now you are getting introduced to a system that is just a little bit better and maybe beyond what you were expecting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:57\n\nTo me, no app should ever talk about either Nostr or Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:02\n\nYeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:03\n\nThey should just be there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nJust what it is, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nBecause the main goal for the app is to get people to do their thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:08\n\nYep.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:08\n\nIt's to get people to be creative or to be to talk to their friends or to have a chat with their family and solve today, you know, day-to-day problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:17\n\nIt just so happens that that same platform enables them to manage their own data, become actually hold and take that into account by themselves if they want to, and use a payment protocol that is just the best we have today.\n\n## Why Bitcoin should be invisible in great UX\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nAnd you can do that at the same time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:37\n\nOn Amethyst, for instance, we don't teach even basic key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:42\n\nSecurity when you are creating an account.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nYou install the app, there's a new screen that says, you know, what's your name?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nYou insert a name, you say create account, and that's it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:51\n\nWe don't explain what an NSEC is, what an NPUB is, what all these things, terms are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:56\n\nNo, no, no.\nStart using first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nLet's make sure you can do your thing, the thing you want to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:02\n\nAnd then, you know, a week or a month later, we're going to start.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:07\n\nBugging you about, like, let's make sure you have all places and so on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:10\n\nAnd by the way, we can do transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:12\n\nYou can actually receive on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:14\n\nSo, for me, it's never really about just the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:17\n\nAt least, I don't want to make an app that just makes everything we do transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:20\n\nYou're right, exactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nYes, yes, yes, yes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:22\n\nYeah, transactions are good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:23\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:23\n\nBut, like, we want to leave.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:25\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:25\n\nYou know, and the transactions sometimes just get in the way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nSo, I want them to be there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:31\n\nI want them to be the preferred payment flow for folks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:35\n\nI want them to be easy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:37\n\nI want them to really help us build this community of creators and users and connect them as best as we can, make them behave and develop their community further, think about their own priorities in that sense.\n\n## Improving account security via FROST/Nostr signer\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:53\n\nAnd then, if Bitcoin and Nostr in general can help them do more.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:57\n\nSo be it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:58\n\nThe goal is to get the users to do the thing they want to do first.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nLove that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nAmazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nVery cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nAny hints, anything you can hint at in terms of what's coming down the pipeline?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nWhat's next for Amethyst, Nostr in general?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nWhat's on your mind, slash, what are you seeing happening maybe behind the scenes a little bit?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:13\n\nYeah, so for the technical folks out there, you know that if you use Nostr today, when you create a key, all of your account is Managed by that key, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:24\n\nYou can't change it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:25\n\nThere's no way to change passwords on Nostr.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nI think we are solving that problem now with a new approach that you folks covered, which is called FROST.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nAnd it's just a new key scheme where you can actually, you have your key, you don't need to change it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nYou take that key and you put it into a FROST signer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:47\n\nAnd that signer breaks your key into many components.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:50\n\nAnd those components go into the apps.\n\nSo, in that way, you can rotate one of these components and insert a new password in a specific app.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:58\n\nIf you think your key has leaked, that is a way you're gonna restart your connection to that account, make sure it's safe.\n\n\n\n## The future of decentralized identity and key rotation\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:06\n\nAnd then you make sure your signer knows that you rotated that key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nBut from that endpoint, from that moment forward, we have a solution for account management that is very close to what centralized platforms can offer today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nAnd I think that is our next step, our next big step.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:23\n\nI think from like a research and scientific standpoint, we need to verify that those things actually work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nAnd if they do, it's going to be great for everybody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:31\n\nBecause we don't change Noster.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nIt's just the same protocol has always been.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:34\n\nBut it's a new signing tool that offers those that rotation and that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:39\n\nYou know, extra safety and extra security in the protocol.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:43\n\nAmazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:43\n\nWell, Victor, thank you so much for your time.\n\n\n\n## Get started with Amethyst\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:45\n\nMuch appreciated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:46\n\nThank you guys for watching.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:47\n\nAnd yeah, keep an eye on what Victor's working on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:49\n\nIf you haven't created your Amethyst account yet, consider this your call to action.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nAnd we will talk soon.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:54\n\nTake it easy, everybody.	At the MIT Bitcoin Expo in Boston, Vitor Pomplona (creator of Amethyst) discussed the emergence and potential of Nostr as a decentralized social network built on Bitcoin. The conversation highlights that Nostr is currently in an early "wild west" phase similar to Bitcoin around 2012, where technology and user adoption are rapidly evolving alongside societal integration.\n\nKey topics include:\nThe evolution of the ecosystem from simple Twitter clones to diverse applications supporting value exchange, such as Olos for video feeds, Fountain for podcasts, and Chachi/Flotilla for private Discord-like group chats. Amethyst is uniquely positioned as an aggregator that allows users to view content across different specialized clients and manage their entire web of trust in one place. The development path has shifted from Vitor's initial goal of building a massive "mega app" to fostering a modular ecosystem where general-purpose apps support specialized verticals, ensuring the network remains stable and accessible.\n\nOnboarding strategies prioritize user freedom and utility over technical complexity; creators are encouraged to join for content visibility and earnings (Zaps) rather than ideological alignment with Bitcoin first. This approach ensures that Bitcoin acts as an invisible payment layer within social interactions, reducing friction by making transactions part of the user experience rather than a separate hurdle.\n\nA significant technical advancement discussed is the integration of FROST multi-signatures, which addresses Nostr's historical limitation regarding key management. By using FROST signers, users can rotate password-like credentials without changing their underlying account keys, significantly improving security and usability while maintaining decentralization. The overall vision for Nostr is to empower creators to manage their own data, build sustainable communities, and earn a living through direct value exchange, eventually attracting non-crypto audiences who adopt Bitcoin organically through social utility.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-NlgxiLgqZo	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:22:53.112862+05:30	2026-04-21 16:45:30.457326+05:30
1b13f5ae-9165-45d4-afb2-208b93beb159	4NoJnPmCVdU	conference	2025-05-13	{Shinobi,"Hunter Beast"}	{}	{}	## Introduction: Shinobi & Hunter Beast\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nSo hi everybody, I'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine and I'm sitting here with Hunter Beast to talk about his BIP proposing a post-quantum cryptographic solution for Bitcoin and just the kind of risk in general of quantum computers to the cryptography underlying the entire network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:20\n\nSo I guess where do you want to start?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nYou want to go through the BIP first or just kind of talk about the the general issue a little bit?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nYeah, well I mean the the BIP is a response to the general issue, so let's go over the general issue in particular.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nSo a lot of people think that quantum computers pose a threat to mining.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:38\n\nIt's not as big a threat to mining as it is to signatures, like transaction signatures.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:42\n\nWhen you sign your transaction to be submitted to the mempool, you have to present your public key for people to validate that your transaction is correct against the address that you receive the coins to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:54\n\nAnd the problem there is that quantum computers, once they are powerful enough, if they ever become powerful enough, they can run an algorithm called Schor's algorithm, which cannot be run on classical computers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:11\n\nThis is totally a quantum computer thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:13\n\nAnd they can derive the Private key from a public key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:22\n\nAnd this is thought to be so difficult to, so as to be impossible in classical computers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nIt's called the elliptic curve discrete log problem, ECDLP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:33\n\nAnd the thing is, Shor's algorithm on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer could break ECDLP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:40\n\nAnd the interesting thing about that is, Quantum computers don't necessarily need to be super powerful to factor large numbers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:51\n\nThey, in fact, factoring large numbers in the way that could break Bitcoin is kind of like an intermediary step to the problems that a lot of these big companies want to solve, like chemical simulation or simulating fusion power, or just more.\n\n\n\n## Shor’s Algorithm and the ECDLP Problem\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:10\n\nPractical things that quantum computers could do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:13\n\nThose require like millions, tens of millions, hundreds of millions of qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nWhereas, like for Shorzago, the simplest circuit would be about like 2,000, 3,000 qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:23\n\nJust to be clear, these are logical qubits, not physical qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:26\n\nThere is a difference.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:27\n\nYeah, you want to kind of break that down for viewers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:30\n\nI'm sure they don't understand what either of those words mean.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:34\n\nYeah, so a qubit is a fundamental unit of quantum computing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:37\n\nAnd it essentially allows you, once you have multiple qubits and you can entangle them, you can essentially model the entire problem space in what's called Hilbert space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:51\n\nAnd you can essentially have all possible states encoded in these qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:59\n\nAnd then through quantum circuits, things like Quantum Fourier transform with these integral qubits, you can achieve what's called quantum parallelism.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:10\n\nAnd that allows you to explore all these states simultaneously.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:14\n\nAnd it doesn't take very long to come up with a solid result, which you can immediately verify.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:24\n\nLike if this is the private key to a public key, you can just say, Derive this private key as if it was a public key, or derive this private key into a public key, and derive a public key from that private key.\n\n\n\n## Logical vs Physical Qubits Explained\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:37\n\nAnd then you check to see if that public key matches the original input.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:41\n\nIf it does, which can be verified classically and very, very quickly, then you basically have a key to an address, and you can prove that, which can be very concerning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:55\n\nIt is essentially an existential threat to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:58\n\nThe threat is often discounted because quantum computers right now are not very good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nThey're like at the level of like maybe going from vacuum tubes to transistors.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\n\nLike so they're they're basically you know technology that's in the like 1950s equivalent of computers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nWell we'll see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nWe'll see if any of that accelerates now, you know, with all all the the stuff that that's that other technology we have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:26\n\nGoogle in particular has made use of that in their Willow processor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:30\n\nThey arrange their their qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nSo they go from physical to logical qubit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:36\n\nSo a logical qubit is usually error corrected or also kind of isolated from noise.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:45\n\nAnd This is done by cooling the quantum computer down to like zero K, essentially, very, very like freezing cold temperatures using liquid nitrogen and liquid helium in what are called dilution refrigerators.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:59\n\nAnd this allows you to run superconducting qubits using what's called a Bose-Einstein condensate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:07\n\nIt's this other state of matter that allows you to then Uh like observe effects of the quantum level uh in isolation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:18\n\nAnd one thing that helps with that is error correction codes, a quantum error correction, which then allows you to uh where you take you take like maybe a seven by seven array of physical qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:31\n\nSo it's like 49, I think, uh physical qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:35\n\nAnd then you implement quantum error correction over that to create one logical qubit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:40\n\nAnd so, um, you know, like i it it is kind of complicated and difficult to prove, especially uh theoretically, you know, where we are with quantum computers, at least at present.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:55\n\nThere there might be some ways we can kind of approximate that at some point in the future, but the uh ultimately The way we understand, at least now, commercially at least speaking, right, is quantum computers are not very good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:08\n\nThat said, Raytheon and Honeywell have both been working on this problem a lot longer than the private industry has been, like over 20 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:17\n\nAnd so we don't know what the spooks have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:21\n\nSo there's also another question, an open question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:25\n\nWe s we do see the government really pushing us towards quantum resistance, like nudging everybody towards that direction.\n\n\n\n## State of Quantum Hardware: Who’s Ahead?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:33\n\nThey want to sunset elliptic curve cryptography and RSA in new systems by 2030.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:41\n\nAnd in existing systems, it has to be completely sunset by the year 2035.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:46\n\nSo they must know something we don't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:48\n\nOr at least, like in their assessment, they're very confident that that time horizon is a meaningful benchmark.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:55\n\nHopefully.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:57\n\nSo, you know, if we were to wake up tomorrow and realize that a powerful enough computer has been created, that kind of catches us with our pants down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nWe have no implemented post-quantum cryptographic solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:14\n\nWe have no plan in place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:16\n\nFor coin migration, we have no idea what to do with coins that don't migrate and are now vulnerable to theft by whoever possesses a quantum computer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:28\n\nI think that all definit depends on the definition of we, because I've thought about all these things, and that's why I've introduced a royal we.\n\n\n\n## The U.S. Government's Post-Quantum Timeline\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:40\n\nI was about to say, because we have this BIP360, it's essentially the plan that has come up through a lot of discussion, a lot of just careful thinking and rational logical, just like evaluating the game theory behind things and incentives and also just the design engineering behind what's needed to give us The capability to secure coins in this post-quantum regime, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:12\n\nAnd so BIT360 essentially allows you to combine three additional signature algorithms that are post-quantum cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:24\n\nAnd that allows you to then add these kind of like additional locks.\nto your transactions that when you spend, you unlock them, and then when you create a new address, you actually commit to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nAnd in order to commit to these public keys, you essentially have to hash the public keys and then hash them all once again and use that hash essentially as the address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:49\n\nAnd this is for an output type called SegWit V3.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nOkay, and so you know, what's your kind of thinking about how we should approach this issue?\n\n\n\n## BIP 360: A Multi-Sig Quantum-Resistant Upgrade\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:03\n\nI mean, obviously you think it's serious enough that we should get a plan together at least right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nLike what should we be thinking in terms of migration path, uh, timetables?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:15\n\nYou know, like h how much of a priority should this be compared to all all the other work that needs to be done?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:21\n\nYeah, so what I can tell you is that the work that we're doing at Surmount Systems, we're hoping to do uh have it have a sort of um implementation and test net uh for quantum resistance uh available by the end of the year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:39\n\nAfter that, it it's a huge coordination problem between uh layer developers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:44\n\nAnybody using Taproot, for example, is vulnerable and uh additionally Just nodes, node operators, exchanges, upgrading their cold wallets, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:55\n\nAnd of course, like just explaining things to plabs and users and just making sure that you know like the people know what to do in order to keep this coin secure and under what conditions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:06\n\nLike for example A lot of there's a lot of coins out there in older public key addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nAnd they're just addresses that are just a public key out there in the open on chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nThese are very old from the year 2009 to 2012, roughly, and they contain about an approximate average.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:33\n\nBalance of each one of these addresses is about 50 Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd there's about 34,000 of these addresses out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:42\n\nAnd sum those up together, I think it's something like 1.6 million coins, somewhere on the way to 2 million coins are essentially in these older output types.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:55\n\nAnd also known as P2PK.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:58\n\nThe concern is that these might intercirculation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nThis is something like Jamison Lopp brought up in one of his recent articles.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:05\n\nAnd it's definitely a good reasonable response to the problem in that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nIn some way some people might consider it reasonable, some people might not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:18\n\nIt's fair.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:20\n\nIt's a fair response, I should say.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:23\n\nIn many ways, it might be also considered unfair.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:28\n\nBut also, there's a contingent of users who will think, well, it's unfair to have all these coins dumped on the market all at once, because you can essentially group 34,000 outputs.\n\n\n\n## Hourglass Proposal: Game Theory vs Supply Shock\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nYou can spend that within a day.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:39\n\nA potential alternative, I'm still working on the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:44\n\nI've been collaborating with a few people, but essentially, The idea is I call it hourglass, and the idea is to restrict spending pay-to-public key coins to one input per block and also prevent creation of new outputs in that variety of that kind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:09\n\nAnd so that would then allow 34,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:14\n\nThousand blocks at a minimum go by where each one of these coins could be sort of found by quantum computers, and then they have to kind of bid up each other if they find the same key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:35\n\nAnd that benefits miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:38\n\nAnd so this is an incentive-compatible.\nproposal and it's it stretches in a period that could be you know only a couple hours to uh more like um eight months.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:53\n\nAnd so it's kind of a an alternative to violating a fundamental property of Bitcoin in terms of never invalidating coins but still addressing the issue of a supply shock just flooding the market all at once.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:07\n\nExactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:08\n\nI actually I personally say out of all the ideas floated, I prefer that one, um, compared to just lighting coins on fire and hoping that that doesn't set some bad precedents.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nI I like to think the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:22\n\nSo I guess, um, yeah, this is a very serious issue hanging over Bitcoin's head.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nIt's just the problem of ambiguity of time scales.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:33\n\nLike, we just have no way of knowing when it's going to be time to act.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:38\n\nOne possibility is that the government comes out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:40\n\nLike, for example, like Luttnick, what's his name?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:46\n\nHoward Luttnick.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:47\n\nHoward Luttnick, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:49\n\nSo, Howard Luttnick, in one of his recent interviews, actually mentioned, like, he just out of the blue mentioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:56\n\nPost-quantum cryptography and the urgency of moving towards it, it was clear he'd been briefed on the topic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nLike he stumbled over a few things about how asymmetric cryptography works, but essentially, or also known as public-private key cryptography, but he Brought it up as a legitimate concern in the year of our Lord 2025.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nAnd it gives a lot of people pause, a lot of people in the know, some concern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:27\n\nAnd there could be a possibility where the government just straight up says, like, Bitcoin is broken.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nAnd especially since the government is involved with the Strategic Bitcoin Reserve, there's government involvement and all this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nAnd it's difficult for Bitcoiners to perhaps see the government as an authority.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nBut the problem with quantum computing is that only large institutional players can gain insights into this technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:01\n\nIt's not like something where this is like self-sovereign computing, unfortunately, not for a long time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:08\n\nAnd so and it's r possibly likely this technology will be restricted from the public for because it's just so powerful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:17\n\nSo you know it it's there is some like proof of authority there rather than like Proof of work or, well, but the proof of work is there's proof of work to be done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nLike, you can do your own research as well into this topic, but a lot of this stuff you just have to kind of aggregate and research and look into and validate on your own best you can.\n\n\n\n## Institutional Monopoly on Quantum Access\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:41\n\nWhat I'm reading is just it is worrying because, like, even if this is just a point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:48\n\nChance of actually proving to be a major threat to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:54\n\nI don't like those odds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:55\n\nI don't like a 1 in 1,000 chance that Bitcoin is completely broken forever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:01\n\nBitcoin is a source of hope for a lot of people, and this could be a really difficult time that we could head into.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:10\n\nAnd regardless of, you know, whether this will happen, this the stakes are so serious to the degree in which Bitcoin could be affected that it's better to be prepared r in advance.\n\n\n\n## Final Thoughts: Betting Bitcoin on Better Odds\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:28\n\nWell, it certainly makes me feel a lot better that somebody is finally working on this beyond hand waving about Lamport signatures.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:37\n\nWanna really thank you for sitting down and talking with me, Hunter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:40\n\nBeen a pleasure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:40\n\nThank you, Shinobi.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:42\n\nTake care, everybody.	## Introduction: Shinobi & Hunter Beast\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nSo hi everybody, I'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine and I'm sitting here with Hunter Beast to talk about his BIP proposing a post-quantum cryptographic solution for Bitcoin and just the kind of risk in general of quantum computers to the cryptography underlying the entire network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:20\n\nSo I guess where do you want to start?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nYou want to go through the BIP first or just kind of talk about the general issue a little bit?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nYeah, well I mean the BIP is a response to the general issue, so let's go over the general issue in particular.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nSo a lot 