n\nWell we'll see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:18\n\nWe'll see if any of that accelerates now, you know, with all all the stuff that other technology we have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:26\n\nGoogle in particular has made use of that in their Willow processor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:30\n\nThey arrange their qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nSo they go from physical to logical qubit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:36\n\n--- Transcript End ---\n\nSo a logical qubit is usually error corrected or also kind of isolated from noise.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:45\n\nAnd this is done by cooling the quantum computer down to like zero K, essentially, very, very like freezing cold temperatures using liquid nitrogen and liquid helium in what are called dilution refrigerators.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:59\n\nAnd this allows you to run superconducting qubits using what's called a Bose-Einstein condensate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:07\n\nIt's this other state of matter that allows you to then uh like observe effects of the quantum level uh in isolation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:18\n\nAnd one thing that helps with that is error correction codes, a quantum error correction, which then allows you to uh where you take you take like maybe a seven by seven array of physical qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:31\n\nSo it's like 49, I think, uh physical qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:35\n\nAnd then you implement quantum error correction over that to create one logical qubit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:40\n\nAnd so, um, you know, like it is kind of complicated and difficult to prove, especially uh theoretically, you know, where we are with quantum computers, at least at present.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:55\n\nThere there might be some ways we can kind of approximate that at some point in the future, but the uh ultimately The way we understand, at least now, commercially at least speaking, right, is quantum computers are not very good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:08\n\nThat said, Raytheon and Honeywell have both been working on this problem a lot longer than the private industry has been, like over 20 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:17\n\nAnd so we don't know what the spooks have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:21\n\nSo there's also another question, an open question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:25\n\nWe do see the government really pushing us towards quantum resistance, like nudging everybody towards that direction.\n\n## State of Quantum Hardware: Who's Ahead?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:33\n\nThey want to sunset elliptic curve cryptography and RSA in new systems by 2030.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:41\n\nAnd in existing systems, it has to be completely sunset by the year 2035.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:46\n\nSo they must know something we don't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:48\n\nOr at least, like in their assessment, they're very confident that that time horizon is a meaningful benchmark.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:55\n\nHopefully.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:57\n\nSo, you know, if we were to wake up tomorrow and realize that a powerful enough computer has been created, that kind of catches us with our pants down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nWe have no implemented post-quantum cryptographic solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:14\n\nWe have no plan in place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:16\n\nFor coin migration, we have no idea what to do with coins that don't migrate and are now vulnerable to theft by whoever possesses a quantum computer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:28\n\nI think that all definitely depends on the definition of we, because I've thought about all these things, and that's why I've introduced a royal we.\n\n## The U.S. Government's Post-Quantum Timeline\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:40\n\nI was about to say, because we have this BIP360, it's essentially the plan that has come up through a lot of discussion, a lot of just careful thinking and rational logical, just like evaluating the game theory behind things and incentives and also just the design engineering behind what's needed to give us The capability to secure coins in this post-quantum regime, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:12\n\nAnd so BIP360 essentially allows you to combine three additional signature algorithms that are post-quantum cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:24\n\nAnd that allows you to then add these kind of like additional locks.\nto your transactions that when you spend, you unlock them, and then when you create a new address, you actually commit to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nAnd in order to commit to these public keys, you essentially have to hash the public keys and then hash them all once again and use that hash essentially as the address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:49\n\nAnd this is for an output type called SegWit V3.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nOkay, and so you know, what's your kind of thinking about how we should approach this issue?\n\n## BIP 360: A Multi-Sig Quantum-Resistant Upgrade\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:03\n\nI mean, obviously you think it's serious enough that we should get a plan together at least right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nLike what should we be thinking in terms of migration path, uh, timetables?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:15\n\nYou know, like how much of a priority should this be compared to all all the other work that needs to be done?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:21\n\nYeah, so what I can tell you is that the work that we're doing at Surmount Systems, we're hoping to do uh have it have a sort of um implementation and test net uh for quantum resistance uh available by the end of the year.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:39\n\nAfter that, it it's a huge coordination problem between uh layer developers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:44\n\nAnybody using Taproot, for example, is vulnerable and uh additionally Just nodes, node operators, exchanges, upgrading their cold wallets, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:55\n\nAnd of course, like just explaining things to plabs and users and just making sure that you know like the people know what to do in order to keep this coin secure and under what conditions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:06\n\nLike for example A lot of there's a lot of coins out there in older public key addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nAnd they're just addresses that are just a public key out there in the open on chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nThese are very old from the year 2009 to 2012, roughly, and they contain about an approximate average.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:33\n\nBalance of each one of these addresses is about 50 Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd there's about 34,000 of these addresses out there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:42\n\nAnd sum those up together, I think it's something like 1.6 million coins, somewhere on the way to 2 million coins are essentially in these older output types.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:55\n\nAnd also known as P2PK.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:58\n\nThe concern is that these might intercirculation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nThis is something like Jamie Sinkos brought up in one of his recent articles.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:05\n\nAnd it's definitely a good reasonable response to the problem in that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nIn some way some people might consider it reasonable, some people might not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:18\n\nIt's fair.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:20\n\nIt's a fair response, I should say.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:23\n\nIn many ways, it might be also considered unfair.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:28\n\nBut also, there's a contingent of users who will think, well, it's unfair to have all these coins dumped on the market all at once, because you can essentially group 34,000 outputs.\n\n\n\n## Hourglass Proposal: Game Theory vs Supply Shock\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nYou can spend that within a day.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:39\n\nA potential alternative, I'm still working on the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:44\n\nI've been collaborating with a few people, but essentially, The idea is I call it hourglass, and the idea is to restrict spending pay-to-public key coins to one input per block and also prevent creation of new outputs in that variety of that kind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:09\n\nAnd so that would then allow 34,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:14\n\nThousand blocks at a minimum go by where each one of these coins could be sort of found by quantum computers, and then they have to kind of bid up each other if they find the same key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:35\n\nAnd that benefits miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:38\n\nAnd so this is an incentive-compatible.\nproposal and it's it stretches in a period that could be you know only a couple hours to uh more like um eight months.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:53\n\nAnd so it's kind of a an alternative to violating a fundamental property of Bitcoin in terms of never invalidating coins but still addressing the issue of a supply shock just flooding the market all at once.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:07\n\nExactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:08\n\nI actually I personally say out of all the ideas floated, I prefer that one, um, compared to just lighting coins on fire and hoping that that doesn't set some bad precedents.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:21\n\nI I like to think the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:22\n\nSo I guess, um, yeah, this is a very serious issue hanging over Bitcoin's head.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:29\n\nIt's just the problem of ambiguity of time scales.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:33\n\nLike, we just have no way of knowing when it's going to be time to act.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:38\n\nOne possibility is that the government comes out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:40\n\nLike, for example, like Luke Wright, what's his name?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:46\n\nHoward Lutnick.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:47\n\nHoward Lutnick, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:49\n\nSo, Howard Lutnick, in one of his recent interviews, actually mentioned, like, he just out of the blue mentioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:56\n\nPost-quantum cryptography and the urgency of moving towards it, it was clear he'd been briefed on the topic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:02\n\nLike he stumbled over a few things about how asymmetric cryptography works, but essentially, or also known as public-private key cryptography, but he Brought it up as a legitimate concern in the year of our Lord 2025.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nAnd it gives a lot of people pause, a lot of people in the know, some concern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:27\n\nAnd there could be a possibility where the government just straight up says, like, Bitcoin is broken.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:37\n\nAnd especially since the government is involved with the Strategic Bitcoin Reserve, there's government involvement and all this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:43\n\nAnd it's difficult for Bitcoiners to perhaps see the government as an authority.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:53\n\nBut the problem with quantum computing is that only large institutional players can gain insights into this technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:01\n\nIt's not like something where this is like self-sovereign computing, unfortunately, not for a long time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:08\n\nAnd so and it's possibly likely this technology will be restricted from the public because it's just so powerful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:17\n\nSo you know it it is there is some like proof of authority there rather than like Proof of work or, well, but the proof of work is there's proof of work to be done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:28\n\nLike, you can do your own research as well into this topic, but a lot of this stuff you just have to kind of aggregate and research and look into and validate on your own best you can.\n\n\n\n## Institutional Monopoly on Quantum Access\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:41\n\nWhat I'm reading is just it is worrying because, like, even if this is just a point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:48\n\nChance of actually proving to be a major threat to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:54\n\nI don't like those odds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:55\n\nI don't like a 1 in 1,000 chance that Bitcoin is completely broken forever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:01\n\nBitcoin is a source of hope for a lot of people, and this could be a really difficult time that we could head into.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:10\n\nAnd regardless of, you know, whether this will happen, this the stakes are so serious to the degree in which Bitcoin could be affected that it's better to be prepared in advance.\n\n\n\n## Final Thoughts: Betting Bitcoin on Better Odds\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:28\n\nWell, it certainly makes me feel a lot better that somebody is finally working on this beyond hand-waving about Lamport signatures.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:37\n\nI wanna really thank you for sitting down and talking with me, Hunter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:40\n\nBeen a pleasure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:40\n\nThank you, Shinobi.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:42\n\nTake care, everybody.	In this interview, Bitcoin Magazine's Shinobi speaks with Hunter Beast about BIP 360, a proposal to secure the Bitcoin network against the threat of quantum computers. The core vulnerability is that while classical computing relies on Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECDLP) for transaction signatures, sufficiently powerful quantum computers could run Shor's algorithm to derive private keys from public keys instantly. Hunter Beast emphasizes that breaking ECDLP requires far fewer qubits than other applications like chemical simulation, meaning the threat could materialize before massive-scale quantum computing is achieved for other industrial purposes.\n\nThe discussion highlights a critical timeline: while current hardware is primitive, governments and large entities are actively developing post-quantum cryptography, with the U.S. government targeting the sunset of current encryption standards by 2035. A major concern is the "harvest now, decrypt later" scenario where stolen data from old addresses could be decrypted once a quantum computer becomes available. There are significant vulnerable funds in P2PK addresses containing approximately 1.6 to 2 million Bitcoin that remain at risk if private keys are ever exposed.\n\nTo mitigate this, Hunter Beast explains the mechanics of BIP 360, which introduces post-quantum signature algorithms and a new "Taproot Version 3" address type that allows transactions to be signed with multiple algorithms simultaneously. However, implementing these changes requires complex coordination across protocol layers, node operators, and users. As an alternative strategy to prevent a sudden supply shock if old keys are compromised, Beast proposes an "Hourglass" BIP that restricts spending on public-key-only inputs to one per block and halts the creation of such addresses. This mechanism would allow vulnerable coins to be gradually spent over months or years through a bidding process rather than flooding the market at once. The speakers conclude that despite uncertainties about when quantum computers will become viable, Bitcoin must adopt these post-quantum solutions proactively to avoid an existential crisis, acknowledging that preparation is better than reacting after the network has been broken.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4NoJnPmCVdU	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{"Quantum Computing",ECDSA,"BIP 360","Post-Quantum Cryptography",Hourglass}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:26:05.265158+05:30	2026-04-21 16:48:10.037336+05:30
e481590b-075c-4ed0-abd7-f7b5c3cd2108	M-PIOaHxX4c	conference	2025-07-02	{"Robin Linus",Shinobi}	{}	{}	## Intro: Robin Linus & BitVM\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHello, everybody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nI'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine, sitting down with Robin Linus from ZeroSync and also the creator of BitVM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nI guess we're gonna sit here and talk a little bit today about the current state of BitVM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:13\n\nSo, you just told me before we sat down that you have a feature freeze on a final implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:21\n\nHow long until mainnet, sir?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nGood question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:24\n\nMaybe first of all, I should give a shout out to the BitVM Alliance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:27\n\nWe created this alliance of all the companies, of all the L2 companies that want to build on top of BitVM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nWe've been working on that bridge implementation, in particular the Snark verifier, for a bit more than half a year now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:42\n\nAnd it has been a great collaboration.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:46\n\nAnd we have finished The first implementation of the Snark verifier did a code freeze, and now in May the security audits will start.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nThen, hopefully, if no crazy bug comes up, we can go live on mainnet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:01\n\nAll right, so just for people who might be a little out of touch, you know, it's kind of faded out of the lime night a little bit since everybody put their heads down actually working on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nDo you want to kind of give like a quick Breakdown, I guess, of the original BitVM proposal and then kind of jump to BitVM2 and the differences between them that were created, I guess, to optimize use as a bridge mechanism.\n\n\n\n## BitVM1 vs BitVM2: What Changed and Why\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nThe main difference between BitVM1 and BitVM2 was that in BitVM1, verification was permissioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:34\n\nThat means during setup, you had to define a particular set of verifiers or challenges.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:40\n\nWho could challenge malicious claims?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nSo, if some operator wanted to steal from the bridge, then there were like, let's say, 100 challenges who could prevent that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:52\n\nFrom a security point of view, that is, of course, sub-optimal, because if there's a lot of money in the bridge, you could bribe all these challengers not to challenge you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:05\n\nThat was quite a suboptimal um situation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nSo we thought about how to get rid of these permission challenges.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:11\n\nThe key idea was that in BitVM one you had multiple rounds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nSo essentially uh the operator made a claim and then um a challenger could ask clever questions and um uh the operator has to respond to each question and um then uh the operator uh the the challenger kept asking clever questions to disprove the operator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:34\n\nAnd um the problem here was that if um just somebody else would keep on asking the questions, they could ask dumb questions, which would not lead to um a disproof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:45\n\nAnd um that's why it had to be permissioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:48\n\nIn Bitvi M two we got rid of all of that altogether by just um reducing the protocol to a single round protocol.\nat the expense of essentially more data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nIn like the the unhappy path, you had to post you have to post more data on chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:03\n\nBut um challenging becomes completely permissionless.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:06\n\nSo everybody can become a challenger.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:08\n\nEverybody who has a stake in the side chain and who has an incentive um to keep the side system um secure, they can um start a challenge.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:17\n\nAnd um yeah, that's drastically uh improves the security guarantees.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nSince now um yeah, you have as long as there is somebody who who who starts a challenge, the the the system is secure more or less.\nMhm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:33\n\nAnd you know, um this is mostly kind of I guess being built out by roll-up teams.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:40\n\nBut I know you in particular had a different kind of goal in mind in terms of what type of system you wanted to use BitVM to bridge into.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nDo you want to talk a little bit about, I guess your original proposal was the ZK coins proposal, but that's kind of evolved into the full client-side validated system that you did with Liam and Jonas, I believe.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nDo you want to kind of talk a little bit about how you see that working as something to take advantage of BitVM as an alternative to roll-ups?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nLike on a high level, I keep saying I'm in it for the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:17\n\nI want Bitcoin to be used as cash, as electronic cash, as in the title of the white paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:24\n\nAnd I think for that, we need essentially three things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:28\n\nWe need better scalability, you need better privacy and better usability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:32\n\nAnd um ZK coins or like that shielded CSV protocol, it tackles both privacy and scalability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:40\n\nIt's like the privacy is is comparable to Zcash or to other state of the art protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:46\n\nAnd from the scalability point of view, it improves on-chain scalability by roughly a factor of ten.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:53\n\nSo we can process About a bit more than 100 transactions per second on the base layer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:58\n\nAlso, it makes transactions cheaper.\n\n\n\n## Building Beyond Rollups: The zkCoins Vision\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:01\n\nThe key idea behind that is to use so-called client-side validation, which is actually quite an old idea.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:08\n\nI think there is a post from Peter Todd from 2014, where he started talking about client-side validation in general.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:15\n\nBut already back then, he mentioned that if you would combine client-side validation with zero-knowledge proofs, then it would become quite neat or like pretty pretty efficient and also private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:26\n\nAnd um back then there were no proof systems that were good enough or like i it was just a theoretical thing back then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:34\n\nIt wasn't really um not sure back then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:37\n\nBut um nowadays we we have all kinds we we live in that Cambrian explosion of proof systems and proof systems become faster every day and uh like more efficient to prove and um The privacy is great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:48\n\nAnd so now the time is ripe to actually implement that original vision of Peter Todd.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:54\n\nAnd I noticed that back in 2012 when we were working on a chain state proof, where we applied zero-knowledge proofs to essentially running a light client or to compressing, to making syncing more efficient.\n\n\n\n## Client-Side Validation and ZK Proofs\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:10\n\nAnd it was kind of like Obvious application when I saw other protocols like RGB or Taproot Assets that these protocols would become drastically better if you would just apply zero-knowledge proofs to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:22\n\nAnd that is for two reasons.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nFirst of all, in these client-side validation protocols, it works such that I, as the sender, send you, the recipient, a point proof, which is essentially a proof of the entire history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nIt contains All of the previous transactions which essentially make that coin valid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:42\n\nAnd you, as the recipient, you are the one who is incentivized to check that entire history to prove to yourself that this coin is actually valid and carries value.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:53\n\nAnd the problem with that is that this history grows quasi-exponentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nEvery transaction has at least one input.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:00\n\nSo on average, it has more than one input.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:03\n\nAnd um so you have that entire tree of ancestor transactions that you have to verify.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:07\n\nPretty quickly the history becomes like gigabytes and um that kinda like defeats the purpose because uh at the supermarket she has something you don't want to transfer two gigabytes of data before yeah it's like downloading a movie to to make a paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:23\n\nSo that's a bit absurd in the classical CSV.\nmodel.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:29\n\nTo be fair, in Taproot Asset, their argument is that they will mostly use it in Lightning channels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nSo there won't be many actual transactions, on-chain transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:41\n\nMost of the transaction will happen just off-chain in Lightning channels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:44\n\nSo you could say the the history doesn't grow that much.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:47\n\nBut still they have that problem and over time the history will grow a lot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:53\n\nAnd the second thing is that it's just not private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nIt's just as like the the transaction graph and also the values are just as obvious as in in regular Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nYou could make an argument that it's not obvious to everyone, it's only uh obvious to the recipient, but the recipient sees the entire kind of have the history of different coins revealed to them over time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nExactly, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nAnd the cool thing is um when you when you apply zero knowledge proofs, um first of all these zero knowledge proofs give you perfect privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nUh they you verify that entire history within that zero knowledge proof and that zero knowledge property um hides the entire transaction graph and the values, which essentially gives you perfect privacy comparable to Zcash or even better than Monero actually.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:44\n\nIn Monero you have like anonymity sets, which are sufficiently large, but it's still like from a theoretical perspective um a bit inferior to um just like having the entire system as an anonymity set.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:58\n\nAnd the second thing is that um these s snarks or like ZK snarks um like the these these zero knowledge proofs they have that nice property that they compress very well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nI keep saying that it's actually a compression technique.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:13\n\nThat's a bit of a metaphor because there's no no way to decompress it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:17\n\nIf I give you that snark, you can decompress the data from it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:20\n\nBut um yeah informationally theoretically you're destroying the information rather than actually compressing it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nYeah, you compress way beyond the information theoretical limits of compression.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:31\n\nBut um what you're actually compressing is the Verification, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:34\n\nLike I give you like you verify a bunch of data and then I give you like a receipt of the verification of the data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:41\n\nAnd um that is miraculously efficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:45\n\nLike you can compress gigabytes of data into a succinct proof that is less than a megabyte.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:51\n\nIt's it's quite astonishing that it's actually possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:54\n\nAnd long story short, you can use that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:56\n\nMagical technique to compress the coin history into a succinct proof of negligible size.\n\n\n\n## Scalability + Privacy Gains with zk-SNARKs\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:03\n\nAnd then I just send you that proof, and then you verify the proof, and that is as good as verifying the entire history of the proof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:10\n\nAnd obviously, this gives us massive scaling and privacy wins, like you said.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:16\n\nBut it does really kind of fundamentally alter the way that people interact with Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:23\n\nLike it's right now, at least for on-chain Bitcoin, like it's completely stateless.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:28\n\nLike, as long as I keep my single seed backup, I'll always be able to recover the state of my coins, like be able to produce what I need to spend them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:38\n\nBut with this kind of scheme, like keeping that relevant information totally off-chain, like if you lose that, there is no.\nregenerating that data in the same way you can a set of private keys.\nYes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:51\n\nThat's one of the fundamental drawbacks of that approach.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:55\n\nYeah, definitely backups are extremely critical, more critical than in the protocols that we are used to, even more critical than in Lightning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nIn Lightning you could still like collaborate with your channel with your channel partner and if if they agree then you can just close it even if you don't know the the the fraud proof transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:15\n\nAnd um here that's not possible at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:19\n\nThe backups become super critical, yes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:22\n\nSo wha what what's your thinking in terms of how like to approach dealing with that, especially from like a a user's point of view, if we were to kind of move in this direction long term?\nas far as like where we want to go with scaling and and really improving Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:40\n\nYeah, of course you need like some backbone network of servers that redundantly store your backups essentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:48\n\nThey are n like y you trust that one of them is is honest and will give you um your backup if you need it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:55\n\nUm you don't have to trust them in terms of privacy or something because you can simply encrypt your backup and they won't learn anything from you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:01\n\nYeah, you have to trust that one of them is honest.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nOf course, you can also use classical cloud services.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:07\n\nYou could use Google Cloud or whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:09\n\nYou don't leak m much privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:12\n\nOf course, Google could try to analyze and could identify that there is that this is a uh a backup of that particular protocol, but um they don't learn anything else from it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:25\n\nUm I hope that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nThis actually works out in practice.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:28\n\nOf course it is an issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nUm it is a UX hurdle.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nAnd uh in the beginning I said we wanna make UX better and in that sense we make it worse.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:39\n\nBut um I I think it's um it's worth a try and um uh I think the the improvements kind of justify this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nI mean it's it's a trade-off.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:49\n\nLike you don't get anything for free in a space like this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:53\n\nYeah, I I like the the kind of aspect of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:57\n\nIt's it's just purely opt-in like Lightning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nLike the reality is if you don't want to deal with those trade-offs or are kind of skeptical of them at first, like people can build and use these systems and you you can just keep using on-chain or Lightning or whatever system you want to use and just see.\n\n\n\n## Making It Lightning-Compatible\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nLike how this system evolves over time in terms of safety, resiliency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:20\n\nIt's also an important point to make it compatible with Lightning, because here you still essentially rely on on-chain transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nSo you have that settlement time of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:30\n\nSo one block every ten minutes or something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:34\n\nAnd to have instant finality, you would need something like payment channels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:37\n\nAnd ideally, you would make it uh compatible with the Lightning Network such that um you use the Lightning Network as like the central settlement layer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:45\n\nMaybe uh to mention a couple other cool things about the protocol is um first of all you can have um uh reusable addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:52\n\nSo I have just a single address and I can post it, I don't know, on my Twitter profile or whatever, and uh everybody can send me money to that address and it doesn't reveal anything about it, doesn't harm my privacy at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nNobody can figure out my transaction history from that identifier.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nAnother cool thing in comparison to these other client-side validation protocols is that you can pay fees in tokens.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:14\n\nMaybe I should mention in general that this is a token protocol, but um we can have BTC on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nThat's actually why I'm working on BitVM to um bridge Bitcoin into ZK coins or into client-side validation protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:28\n\nSo we can have private Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nBut you could also have a stable coin on there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:32\n\nAnd most other prot token protocols also on Ethereum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:36\n\nIt's it's very common that um if I send you tether on Ethereum, then you first need ETH to be able to transfer that tether.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:44\n\nAnd the same is true for pretty much every token protocol on top of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nAnd here in our protocol, um we we get rid of that um such that you can pay fees.\ndirectly in in that token.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:55\n\nSo either in Tether or in your privacy Bitcoin, which um is also better for privacy since you don't need any auxiliary um UTXO to to pay your fees.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nI know this is like a a really mature proposal at this point, but the space has kind of gotten to the point where I'm scrambling to keep abreast of everything going on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nUm have any of you, um you Liam or uh Jonas like started working at any implementation?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:22\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:22\n\nMy team at ZeroSync, we um implemented a proof of concept prototype um just I think four weeks ago.\n\n\n\n## Mainnet Timeline: Testnet Launch + What’s Next\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nAnd um yeah, we are working on um launching it on on testnet within the next couple of weeks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nAnd um hopefully within that year we will also have like a first reckless mainnet version.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:44\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:44\n\nSo if we got bit VM and feature freeze and work moving towards an actual CSV implementation, maybe we actually see something live in the next few years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:55\n\nI hope that we can go live on mainnet within this year.\nOkay.\nProbably like a a a simplified version, but uh I wanna get it into the world.\ninto the wild as soon as possible, huh?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:09\n\nYeah, it's just, you know, th these days I'm kind of torn between pessimism and optimism because it it just seems like impossible to actually accomplish any of the clean, simple improvements.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:23\n\nBut despite that, you know, we we keep coming up with more roundabout ways to at least get s an equivalent to to something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:31\n\nSo it's It's kind of a a mixed bag these days.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nOr there is that saying that there is no rational alternative to optimism.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nI guess.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:42\n\nWell, um, do you have any like final comments or words you want to leave anybody with?\n\n\n\n## Final Words: “Make Cypherpunk Again"\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:46\n\nEverybody should help make Bitcoin cypherpunk again.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:49\n\nBitcoin is a currency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:51\n\nThat's what it's about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:52\n\nThe store of value thing is just uh to lurk the rich people into it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:00\n\nBut it's a currency, it's cypherpunk money, freedom money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:03\n\nThat's what it's about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:04\n\nCould not have said it better myself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:06\n\nSo thanks for sitting down with me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:08\n\nThanks for having me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:09\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:10\n\nTake care, everybody.	## Intro: Robin Linus & BitVM\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHello, everybody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nI'm Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine, sitting down with Robin Linus from ZeroSync and also the creator of BitVM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nI guess we're gonna sit here and talk a little bit today about the current state of BitVM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:13\n\nSo, you just told me before we sat down that you have a feature freeze on a final implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:21\n\nHow long until mainnet, sir?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nGood question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:24\n\nMaybe first of all, I should give a shout out to the BitVM Alliance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:27\n\nWe created this alliance of all the companies, of all the L2 companies that want to build on top of BitVM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:34\n\nWe've been working on that bridge implementation, in particular the Snark verifier, for a bit more than half a year now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:42\n\nAnd it has been a great collaboration.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:46\n\nAnd we have finished The first implementation of the Snark verifier did a code freeze, and now in May the security audits will start.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nThen, hopefully, if no crazy bug comes up, we can go live on mainnet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:01\n\nAll right, so just for people who might be a little out of touch, you know, it's kind of faded out of the limelight a little bit since everybody put their heads down actually working on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:12\n\nDo you want to kind of give like a quick breakdown, I guess, of the original BitVM proposal and then kind of jump to BitVM2 and the differences between them that were created, I guess, to optimize use as a bridge mechanism.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nThe main difference between BitVM1 and BitVM2 was that in BitVM1, verification was permissioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:34\n\nThat means during setup, you had to define a particular set of verifiers or challengers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:40\n\nWho could challenge malicious claims?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nSo, if some operator wanted to steal from the bridge, then there were like, let's say, 100 challenges who could prevent that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:52\n\nFrom a security point of view, that is, of course, sub-optimal, because if there's a lot of money in the bridge, you could bribe all these challengers not to challenge you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:05\n\nThat was quite a suboptimal um situation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nSo we thought about how to get rid of these permissioned challenges.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:11\n\nThe key idea was that in BitVM one you had multiple rounds.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nSo essentially uh the operator made a claim and then um a challenger could ask clever questions and um uh the operator has to respond to each question and um then uh the operator uh the the challenger kept asking clever questions to disprove the operator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:34\n\nAnd um the problem here was that if um just somebody else would keep on asking the questions, they could ask dumb questions, which would not lead to um a disproof.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:45\n\nAnd um that's why it had to be permissioned.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:48\n\nIn BitVM two we got rid of all of that altogether by just um reducing the protocol to a single round protocol,\nat the expense of essentially more data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nIn like the unhappy path, you had to post, you have to post more data on chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:03\n\nBut um challenging becomes completely permissionless.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:06\n\nSo everybody can become a challenger.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:08\n\nEverybody who has a stake in the side chain and who has an incentive um to keep the side system um secure, they can um start a challenge.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:17\n\nAnd um yeah, that's drastically uh improves the security guarantees.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nSince now um yeah, you have as long as there is somebody who who who starts a challenge, the the system is secure more or less.\nMhm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:33\n\nAnd you know, um this is mostly kind of I guess being built out by roll-up teams.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:40\n\nBut I know you in particular had a different kind of goal in mind in terms of what type of system you wanted to use BitVM to bridge into.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nDo you want to talk a little bit about, I guess your original proposal was the ZK coins proposal, but that's kind of evolved into the full client-side validated system that you did with Liam and Jonas, I believe.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:03\n\nDo you want to kind of talk a little bit about how you see that working as something to take advantage of BitVM as an alternative to roll-ups?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nLike on a high level, I keep saying I'm in it for the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:17\n\nI want Bitcoin to be used as cash, as electronic cash, as in the title of the white paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:24\n\nAnd I think for that, we need essentially three things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:28\n\nWe need better scalability, you need better privacy and better usability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:32\n\nAnd um ZK coins or like that shielded CSV protocol, it tackles both privacy and scalability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:40\n\nIt's like the privacy is comparable to Zcash or to other state of the art protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:46\n\nAnd from the scalability point of view, it improves on-chain scalability by roughly a factor of ten.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:53\n\nSo we can process about a bit more than 100 transactions per second on the base layer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:58\n\nAlso, it makes transactions cheaper.\n\n\n\n## Building Beyond Rollups: The zkCoins Vision\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:01\n\nThe key idea behind that is to use so-called client-side validation, which is actually quite an old idea.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:08\n\nI think there is a post from Peter Todd from 2014, where he started talking about client-side validation in general.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:15\n\nBut already back then, he mentioned that if you would combine client-side validation with zero-knowledge proofs, then it would become quite neat or like pretty efficient and also private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:26\n\nAnd um back then there were no proof systems that were good enough or like it was just a theoretical thing back then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:34\n\nIt wasn't really um not sure back then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:37\n\nBut um nowadays we have all kinds we we live in that Cambrian explosion of proof systems and proof systems become faster every day and uh like more efficient to prove and um The privacy is great.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:48\n\nAnd so now the time is ripe to actually implement that original vision of Peter Todd.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:54\n\nAnd I noticed that back in 2012 when we were working on a chain state proof, where we applied zero-knowledge proofs to essentially running a light client or to compressing, to making syncing more efficient.\n\n\n\n## Client-Side Validation and ZK Proofs\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:10\n\nAnd it was kind of like an obvious application when I saw other protocols like RGB or Taproot Assets that these protocols would become drastically better if you would just apply zero-knowledge proofs to them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:22\n\nAnd that is for two reasons.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:24\n\nFirst of all, in these client-side validation protocols, it works such that I, as the sender, send you, the recipient, a point proof, which is essentially a proof of the entire history.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nIt contains all of the previous transactions which essentially make that coin valid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:42\n\nAnd you, as the recipient, you are the one who is incentivized to check that entire history to prove to yourself that this coin is actually valid and carries value.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:53\n\nAnd the problem with that is that this history grows quasi-exponentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:58\n\nEvery transaction has at least one input.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:00\n\nSo on average, it has more than one input.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:03\n\nAnd um so you have that entire tree of ancestor transactions that you have to verify.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:07\n\nPretty quickly the history becomes like gigabytes and um that kinda like defeats the purpose because uh at the supermarket she has something you don't want to transfer two gigabytes of data before yeah it's like downloading a movie to make a payment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:23\n\nSo that's a bit absurd in the classical CSV model.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:29\n\nTo be fair, in Taproot Assets, their argument is that they will mostly use it in Lightning channels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nSo there won't be many actual transactions, on-chain transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:41\n\nMost of the transactions will happen just off-chain in Lightning channels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:44\n\nSo you could say the history doesn't grow that much.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:47\n\nBut still they have that problem and over time the history will grow a lot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:53\n\nAnd the second thing is that it's just not private.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nIt's just as like the transaction graph and also the values are just as obvious as in regular Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nYou could make an argument that it's not obvious to everyone, it's only uh obvious to the recipient, but the recipient sees the entire kind of have the history of different coins revealed to them over time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nExactly, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:22\n\nAnd the cool thing is um when you when you apply zero-knowledge proofs, um first of all these zero-knowledge proofs give you perfect privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nUh they you verify that entire history within that zero-knowledge proof and that zero-knowledge property um hides the entire transaction graph and the values, which essentially gives you perfect privacy comparable to Zcash or even better than Monero actually.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:44\n\nIn Monero you have like anonymity sets, which are sufficiently large, but it's still like from a theoretical perspective um a bit inferior to um just like having the entire system as an anonymity set.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:58\n\nAnd the second thing is that um these SNARKs, or like ZK-SNARKS, um like these zero-knowledge proofs, they have that nice property that they compress very well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:09\n\nI keep saying that it's actually a compression technique.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:13\n\nThat's a bit of a metaphor because there's no way to decompress it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:17\n\nIf I give you that SNARK, you can decompress the data from it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:20\n\nBut um yeah, informationally theoretically you're destroying the information rather than actually compressing it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nYeah, you compress way beyond the information-theoretic limits of compression.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:31\n\nBut um what you're actually compressing is verification, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:34\n\nLike I give you like you verify a bunch of data and then I give you like a receipt of the verification of the data.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:41\n\nAnd um that is miraculously efficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:45\n\nLike you can compress gigabytes of data into a succinct proof that is less than a megabyte.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:51\n\nIt's it's quite astonishing that it's actually possible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:54\n\nAnd long story short, you can use that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:56\n\nMagical technique to compress the coin history into a succinct proof of negligible size.\n\n\n\n## Scalability + Privacy Gains with zk-SNARKs\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:03\n\nAnd then I just send you that proof, and then you verify the proof, and that is as good as verifying the entire history of the blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:10\n\nAnd obviously, this gives us massive scaling and privacy wins, like you said.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:16\n\nBut it does really kind of fundamentally alter the way that people interact with Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:23\n\nLike it's right now, at least for on-chain Bitcoin, like it's completely stateless.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:28\n\nLike, as long as I keep my single seed backup, I'll always be able to recover the state of my coins, like be able to produce what I need to spend them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:38\n\nBut with this kind of scheme, like keeping that relevant information totally off-chain, like if you lose that, there is no way of regenerating that data in the same way you can regenerate a set of private keys.\nYes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:51\n\nThat's one of the fundamental drawbacks of that approach.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:55\n\nYeah, definitely backups are extremely critical, more critical than in the protocols that we are used to, even more critical than in Lightning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:02\n\nIn Lightning you could still like collaborate with your channel partner and if they agree then you can just close it even if you don't know the fraud-proof transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:15\n\nAnd um here that's not possible at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:19\n\nThe backups become super critical, yes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:22\n\nSo what's your thinking in terms of how like to approach dealing with that, especially from like a user's point of view, if we were to kind of move in this direction long term?\nAs far as like where we want to go with scaling and really improving Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:40\n\nYeah, of course you need like some backbone network of servers that redundantly store your backups essentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:48\n\nThey are like yeah, you trust that one of them is honest and will give you um your backup if you need it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:55\n\nUm you don't have to trust them in terms of privacy or something because you can simply encrypt your backup and they won't learn anything from you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:01\n\nYeah, you have to trust that one of them is honest.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:04\n\nOf course, you can also use classical cloud services.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:07\n\nYou could use Google Cloud or whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:09\n\nYou don't leak much privacy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:12\n\nOf course, Google could try to analyze and could identify that this is a backup of that particular protocol, but um they don't learn anything else from it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:25\n\nUm I hope that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nThis actually works out in practice.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:28\n\nOf course, it is an issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nUm it is a UX hurdle.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:34\n\nAnd uh in the beginning I said we wanna make UX better and in that sense we make it worse.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:39\n\nBut um I I think it's um it's worth a try and um uh I think the improvements kind of justify this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nI mean it's it's a trade-off.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:49\n\nLike you don't get anything for free in a space like this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:53\n\nYeah, I like the aspect of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:57\n\nIt's it's just purely opt-in like Lightning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:59\n\nLike the reality is if you don't want to deal with those trade-offs or are kind of skeptical of them at first, like people can build and use these systems and you can just keep using on-chain or Lightning or whatever system you want to use and just see.\n\n## Making It Lightning-Compatible\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nLike how this system evolves over time in terms of safety, resiliency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:20\n\nIt's also an important point to make it compatible with Lightning, because here you still essentially rely on on-chain transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nSo you have that settlement time of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:30\n\nSo one block every ten minutes or something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:34\n\nAnd to have instant finality, you would need something like payment channels.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:37\n\nAnd ideally, you would make it uh compatible with the Lightning Network such that um you use the Lightning Network as like the central settlement layer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:45\n\nMaybe uh to mention a couple other cool things about the protocol is um first of all you can have um uh reusable addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:52\n\nSo I have just a single address and I can post it, I don't know, on my Twitter profile or whatever, and uh everybody can send me money to that address and it doesn't reveal anything about it, doesn't harm my privacy at all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:03\n\nNobody can figure out my transaction history from that identifier.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:07\n\nAnother cool thing in comparison to these other client-side validation protocols is that you can pay fees in tokens.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:14\n\nMaybe I should mention in general that this is a token protocol, but um we can have BTC on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nThat's actually why I'm working on BitVM to um bridge Bitcoin into ZK coins or into client-side validation protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:28\n\nSo we can have private Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:29\n\nBut you could also have a stable coin on there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:32\n\nAnd most other token protocol protocols also on Ethereum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:36\n\nIt's it's very common that um if I send you tether on Ethereum, then you first need ETH to be able to transfer that tether.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:44\n\nAnd the same is true for pretty much every token protocol on top of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nAnd here in our protocol, um we we get rid of that um such that you can pay fees directly in that token.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:55\n\nSo either in Tether or in your privacy Bitcoin, which um is also better for privacy since you don't need any auxiliary um UTXO to to pay your fees.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:04\n\nI know this is like a really mature proposal at this point, but the space has kind of gotten to the point where I'm scrambling to keep abreast of everything going on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nUm have any of you, um you Liam or uh Jonas like started working on any implementations?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:22\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:22\n\nMy team at ZeroSync, we um implemented a proof of concept prototype um just I think four weeks ago.\n\n\n\n## Mainnet Timeline: Testnet Launch + What's Next\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:32\n\nAnd um yeah, we are working on um launching it on testnet within the next couple of weeks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nAnd um hopefully within that year we will also have like a first reckless mainnet version.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:44\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:44\n\nSo if we got BitVM and feature freeze and work moving towards an actual CSV implementation, maybe we actually see something live in the next few years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:55\n\nI hope that we can go live on mainnet within this year.\nOkay.\nProbably like a simplified version, but uh I wanna get it into the world into the wild as soon as possible, huh?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:09\n\nYeah, it's just, you know, these days I'm kind of torn between pessimism and optimism because it it just seems like impossible to actually accomplish any of the clean, simple improvements.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:23\n\nBut despite that, you know, we we keep coming up with more roundabout ways to at least get an equivalent to something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:31\n\nSo it's kind of a mixed bag these days.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nOr there is that saying that there is no rational alternative to optimism.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nI guess.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:42\n\nWell, um, do you have any like final comments or words you want to leave anybody with?\n\n\n\n## Final Words: "Make Cypherpunk Again"\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:46\n\nEverybody should help make Bitcoin cypherpunk again.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:49\n\nBitcoin is a currency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:51\n\nThat's what it's about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:52\n\nThe store of value thing is just uh to lure the rich people into it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:00\n\nBut it's a currency, it's cypherpunk money, freedom money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:03\n\nThat's what it's about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:04\n\nCould not have said it better myself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:06\n\nSo thanks for sitting down with me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:08\n\nThanks for having me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:09\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:10\n\nTake care, everybody.	Robin Linus discusses the current state of BitVM, a protocol designed to bridge Bitcoin into advanced scaling solutions with improved privacy and usability. The conversation highlights the transition from BitVM1, which relied on permissioned challenges, to BitVM2, a single-round, permissionless protocol that allows any staked party to challenge malicious claims, significantly enhancing security.\n\nLinus outlines his vision for "ZK Coins" or shielded CSV protocols, aiming to make Bitcoin function as electronic cash by addressing scalability, privacy, and usability simultaneously. By combining client-side validation with zero-knowledge proofs (ZK-SNARKs), the protocol compresses gigabytes of transaction history into a succinct proof under one megabyte, enabling perfect privacy comparable to Zcash and reducing on-chain data requirements by roughly 10x. However, this approach requires users to trust a redundant network of backup servers for critical state data, representing a fundamental trade-off where backups are more critical than in standard Lightning or on-chain Bitcoin usage.\n\nThe discussion emphasizes the protocol's compatibility with the Lightning Network for instant finality and settlement, its ability to support reusable addresses that preserve privacy, and the unique capability to pay transaction fees directly in the same token (e.g., BTC or stablecoins) rather than requiring auxiliary base tokens like ETH. While the full client-side validated system involves significant complexity regarding backups, it represents an opt-in evolution for users seeking these specific gains. Linus concludes with a call to action to restore Bitcoin's identity as cypherpunk money—a currency focused on freedom and utility rather than just store of value—and aims to launch a testnet version within weeks and a mainnet version within the year following security audits.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M-PIOaHxX4c	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{BitVM,"Smart Contracts","Zero-Knowledge Proofs","Client-Side Validation",zk-SNARKs}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:18:46.343142+05:30	2026-04-21 16:51:25.408843+05:30
1934a28a-55d9-401d-8ba7-cf3626e305fe	TEVJUjOGmOI	conference	2025-04-07	{}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nMaybe just to start things off, let's do a quick round of introductions and maybe add on why you work on Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nYou want to start with Gloria?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:10\n\nOh, sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:12\n\nI hello, I'm Gloria.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:14\n\nI s went To college, not this one, but I no, I went to college.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:24\n\nSo, like a lot of college students, I really wanted to save the world.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nI was interested in technological solutions to social problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:33\n\nI met Alex Gladstein of the Human Rights Foundation and learned about all of the activism and just Billions of people who don't get access to the US dollar like we do in the US, and found this problem extremely compelling and found Bitcoin to be a really good solution to it, or at least the furthest I can think of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:59\n\nAnd so, as a computer science student who wanted to be a software engineer, this was kind of the perfect marriage of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:07\n\nTwo really good reasons to work on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:10\n\nAnd so I applied to the Chain Code residency when I was a college student, ended up starting to contribute to Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nAnd then when I graduated, I was lucky enough to get a grant from the HRF.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:22\n\nAnd it was called Square Crypto or Spiral at the time to do it full time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nAnd so since graduation, I have worked on Bitcoin Core full time for the past four or five years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:35\n\nSo I'm I'm Corey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:36\n\nI have a a background in open source C software development and I got tired of putting my credit card number in to buy things online.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:47\n\nThat that just seems stupid to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:50\n\nSo I read about Bitcoin and and the idea that you can push a payment as opposed to pull and that made More sense that that just seemed like the way that things should be.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:00\n\nSo I hopped in, and there was this idea that the code is the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:05\n\nAnd it took a long time for that to click for me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nThat, like, it's, it's as simple as that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\n\nThe code, the software is the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nAnd it just became a fascinating thing to think about to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nAnd the number of just Technology challenges.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:22\n\nIt's just that there are just hard problems in Bitcoin and they're a lot of fun.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:26\n\nSo I got hacking on the code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nI started, I was asked to join the Bitcoin Foundation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:33\n\nI had health insurance for one week at the Bitcoin Foundation before it crumbled.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:38\n\nAnd thankfully, Gavin and Patrick and some other people that were at the foundation kind of shopped us around and found us a home here at MIT.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:47\n\nSo I've been working at the DCI here at MIT, the Digital Currency Initiative, for the last 10 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:53\n\nAnd my job is just to work on Bitcoin and Bitcoin Core and keep it going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nMy name is Antoine Poincer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:04\n\nI work at Chaincode Labs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:06\n\nI think I got started working on Bitcoin.\nfor similar reasons to Gloria.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:12\n\nAnd the reason I work on Bitcoin Core in particular is probably impact.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:19\n\nProbably the most uh frustrating project you could work at, but also the most impactful one in in the space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:28\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:30\n\nSo uh we're we're gonna do a little um a nice easy warm up here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:34\n\nUm, what should we do about all these software proponents?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:39\n\nShipp all of them at once on CNN.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nAnybody else want to jump in?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nYeah, I think you alluded to this in your presentation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:55\n\nA lot of people I guess as Bitcoin devs, like, I mean, my job description is not completely clear, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:05\n\nThere's maintaining the Bitcoin Core code base, which I would say is kind of the primary hat that I would wear.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nAnd then there's protocol research and Bitcoin solution creation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:20\n\nSo I think a good example of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:24\n\nOf this is people are like, we need to make self-custody better.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:28\n\nAnd I think there are a lot of different ways to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\n\nYou know, people want to build vaults, so they want some kind of covenant to build vaults.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nBut of course, there are people who are building certain apps or companies where there's an app and a hardware wallet, and they're trying to create a solution for custody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:45\n\nOr if you're interested in scalability, like, There are many different layers of the stack of not just technology, but products that you can be working on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:57\n\nAnd so, I guess my thing is: like, yes, there is a lot of different things in Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:06\n\nAnd I think just talking about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:09\n\nConsensus is, I think people conflate consensus with protocol development, or they conflate soft fork proposal championing with Bitcoin core maintenance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:20\n\nThey're like very, very different things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:22\n\nAnd actually, Corey, you had a really good point earlier when we were chatting about how there's almost they're almost incompatible sometimes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:29\n\nSo if you want to talk about that.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:31\n\nSo I in my head, I think of this as the soft fork paradox where Basically, the core developers are tasked with keeping Bitcoin as it is known alive and maintaining Bitcoin, the reference implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nAnd that kind of is core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:49\n\nAnd because there's no spec, because there are no written rules, the reference implementation is Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:55\n\nSo if you're a core developer, if you're a maintainer, your job is to maintain Bitcoin as you found it, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:04\n\nAnd so there's this kind of fundamental problem of who is tasked with, I mean, you can, it's a stretch, but you can define every soft fork as an attack because it's an attack on the status quo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:18\n\nAnd it may be an upgrade.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:19\n\nIt may be that 90% of people want this attack, but it's an attack because it changes the rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:25\n\nSo it's kind of a, you know, there's kind of a An oath that you take, kind of an implicit oath as a maintainer that thou shalt not change the consensus rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:36\n\nAnd so to merge in a soft fork, and especially the activation parameters for a soft fork, you're kind of violating that oath.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:44\n\nSo to me, it's this big open question of who is tasked with making these changes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:49\n\nAnd I think it's easy to forget that there are people behind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:53\n\nThe merge button on GitHub or the merge script that the maintainers run, there's a human being that is sitting behind that and clicking or hitting enter and saying, boom, I've broken my oath.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nI've decided to fork Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nThat's a real thing that someone has to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:13\n\nAnd so who is that person?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:15\n\nWho can be tasked with that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:16\n\nGloria has that power.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:17\n\nDo you want to do that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nLike, that doesn't seem like a fun Thursday night.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:23\n\nYeah, I think related to that, I think people seem to have this obsession with Softworks that, like, I'll often get asked, like, oh, you work on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:34\n\nLike, what's the next soft fork?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nWhich is like, softworks, I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:39\n\nKnow, deservedly get a lot of coverage and sensationalism because, like you said, they consensus changes are changes to the rules that decide who can spend their money and who owns how much bitcoins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:53\n\nLike, that's really, really invasive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:55\n\nAnd I think that's so fundamental.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nSo, of course, it makes sense that it comes up in the news so much more often than what Port you can open as a Bitcoin node for inbound connections.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nBut changing just from eight through three through three is really important for censorship resistance, for example.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:17\n\nLike we were really concerned about ISPs shutting down traffic to that port because that's so characteristically a Bitcoin node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:26\n\nAnd so I think it was a really great change for us to work on that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:30\n\nThere's no bid for that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nThere's certainly, I didn't see any news coverage of like, oh, the porch has changed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nAnd so, yeah, I think it's almost like people watch too much Gray's Anatomy and they're like, all of medicine is brain surgeries.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:45\n\nOh, you're a doctor?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:46\n\nHow many brain surgeries have you done?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:48\n\nBut, like, most of medicine is, first of all, not brain surgery.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:52\n\nIt's certainly very impressive, very invasive, but most.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nMedicine is preventative, and you can save lots and lots of lives and do healthcare without ever doing a brain surgery, even if it is kind of this sensational, like dramatic thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nSo, similarly, I think my point here, I'll finish up soon, is even within Bitcoin Core, there's so much that is done that is not consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:17\n\nIf you look at the list of BIPs, Probably half of them are actually peer-to-peer protocol things, and there's a lot of very impactful things you can do there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nAnd a lot of things don't have BIPS because they are engineering challenges that Satoshi left as an exercise to the reader, and we had to figure out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nAnd when there were tens of thousands of transactions coming down the network, like We were like, oh shit, like we need to fit we need to fix this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:48\n\nSo like the idea of Bitcoin being robust and censorship resistant and all these things are like things that we work on every single day that you don't necessarily see.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:02\n\nSo I'd quickly like to ask Jonas.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:05\n\nBecause I've heard you give your spiel a couple times about how you're not one of us and you're the water boy and the helper and all that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:11\n\nBut that's, I mean, that's that's bullshit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nSo what would you do about all the software?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nThat's not bullshit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nI am an outside observer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:23\n\nI really, I think for some of these, the truth of the matter is that we.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:30\n\nAs a non-big brain, like need to have some trust for the keepers of the flame.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd if they, you know, it should be verified clearly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:43\n\nBut I think a lot of my opinion, of course, is informed by people that I respect the opinion of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:51\n\nAnd I think I try to understand the trade-offs and understand the.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:56\n\nWhy, like the use cases and the risks involved.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nBut I'll never be the one that has that runs the script.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:05\n\nSo it sort of absolves me from the ultimate load.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:10\n\nAnd I think that makes me, again, a muggle and a water boy, like most of the ecosystem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nAntoine, do you have opinions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:20\n\nUh three things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:22\n\nUh I wrote a blog post about my opinion on all this FFOX proposal, so for the longer version you can refer to that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nI'd like first of all to highlight what Corey said about consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nI think people severely underestimate consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:41\n\nWe have this global network And the set of rules that everyone agrees upon.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:49\n\nIf we got together and tried to decide the cancer scrolls today with everybody with divergent incentives and opinion, it would just never happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:01\n\nAnd without consensus, there is no Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nI've heard the other day on Twitter.\nsomebody referring as oh the proof of work can just decide what the consensus is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:16\n\nThe proof of work is part of the consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:19\n\nLike the consensus comes before anything else.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:22\n\nAnd Bitcoin is valuable today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:25\n\nCensorship resistant money, even if it does not come at a huge scale, solves real problem today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nSo we must be very careful in risking it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:37\n\nSecond of all, I wanted to uh give a more practical response to your initial questions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nUh I think a lot of the SAP fork proposal that we see today, um not all of them, but a lot are because there is so many of them uh kind of solutions Such of a problem, or it's not exactly clear what problem they're solving.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:03\n\nAnd it's even amplified by the fact that Bitcoin has become so big, so big, that we cannot be specialists of every single area.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:17\n\nAnd there is this area, especially with the covenant discussion, there is researchers specializing in this area.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:23\n\nSo it would be very useful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nBecause, in addition to solutions in search of a problem, there is also not all of them agree on which solution without even defining clearly the problem in the first place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nSo, I think what would be useful from a Bitcoin Corps perspective would be some sort of a research report from this loosely defined researcher community: we set to solve this problem and we find that this solution is appropriate and it has this and this trade-off and we bring it to the community.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:58\n\nI know that there is a few people I see in the room that said to do exactly this work in comparing differing different alternatives and I I think that's very useful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nCan I ask?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nBecause I think this is a point of frustration for a lot of said people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:16\n\nWhat's the right forum to do this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:17\n\nIt seems like it leaks into a lot of different places.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:20\n\nSo, you know, there's back and forth on delving, there's the mailing list.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:26\n\nI think the lowest common denominator seems to be Twitter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:31\n\nBut where's the right place to engage in these conversations?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:35\n\nNot Twitter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:39\n\nWell, if you want to be taken seriously, I mean.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:41\n\nOkay, can we can we do more better than not Twitter?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:46\n\nI yeah, I think I like delving.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nUm I I guess I'll take the bait.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nLike, I think so people will often ask, like, oh, like the maintainers are not merging s some covenant and uh You know, faculty and I will look at each other and be like, is there a PR open for that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nAnd there isn't on Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:14\n\nBut there is no.\nYeah, there is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nWell, I didn't name a specific one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nBut like, I think Bitcoin Core, the GitHub repo, is a place for code review.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:26\n\nAnd of course, we have lots of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:29\n\nCode concept discussion on core at various times.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:33\n\nAnd I'm not trying to police where you don't come on core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nBut a lot of the wider community discussions slash flame wars can be very disruptive in a GitHub repository.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:47\n\nIt's like this is where we work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:49\n\nThat doesn't, you're still, we still don't have an answer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:52\n\nWhat's the rules of engagement?\nyou know, what as I as I tried to point out, like, we have this conflation of Bitco uh, Protocol Research and Bitcoin Core, they they do have to dance together eventually.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:06\n\nSo what is the lead up what is the what is the back and forth and the social nuance of that lead up to to said dance?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:14\n\nI think there's a frustrating truth here that that venue changes over time and has changed over time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:19\n\nAnd there is no, like Bitcoin, especially Bitcoin Core, struggles with process because it's particularly hard to install process for something that's supposed to be essentially process-free.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:31\n\nThe core devs, as kind of sort of gatekeepers, as we're pretty often called, we shouldn't exist.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:39\n\nAnd so there shouldn't be a technical forum or a specific venue for this type of a discussion because it it almost it almost certainly invites discrimination as far as who's allowed to show up, who's allowed to to argue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:54\n\nSo over time, you know, it used to be Bitcoin talk is is where these discussions would happen, and then it was Reddit, and then it was the mailing list, and now it's delving and and in Google groups and They were scaling Bitcoin and there are conferences and there are papers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:07\n\nAnd I think there just is an unfortunate truth that should be recognized: that is, these discussions are going to be fluid and the venues are going to be fluid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:15\n\nAnd in order to participate, there's almost a mini consensus mechanism involved here as well, that you have to know where the conversation is taking place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:26\n\nYou know, there's a there like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nClumping is just natural in these scenarios.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:31\n\nSo these fork discussions may happen on delving, and that may be where the interesting thing happens.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nBut that's not to say it's where it's going to happen next time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:43\n\nWe've covered one topic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:44\n\nSo you get the last word.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:49\n\nJust a third point I wanted to make earlier.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:52\n\nJust a clarification for people that are maybe newer in the room to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:57\n\nCorey and Gloria talked about the merging scripts, and we talked about SaftForks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:01\n\nMerging a SaftWork is not in Bitcoin Core, it's not how it gets activated on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:07\n\nIt's what users choose to run, what software users choose to run.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:13\n\nI would tend to agree that Bitcoin Core is probably the only reasonable software to run as a full node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:21\n\nStill, merging it is not enough to activate the software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:28\n\nSo let's not talk about softworks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:31\n\nBut let's talk about how other things get done in Bitcoin Core, the software project.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:36\n\nSo you all have done big things and are in the process of doing more big things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:44\n\nAnd so, like, What's that dance look like?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:48\n\nHow are you effective as a contributor to getting big things done at Bitcoin Core?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:56\n\nI heard CMake was 300 pull requests.\n300 pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:01\n\nThat's a lot for those that don't know what that is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:04\n\nBut that's a lot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:05\n\nSo I think maybe Gloria's point there was there's a lot of boring stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:11\n\nThat goes on in the day-to-day development.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nThat's not what I've meant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:16\n\nThis is poor framing, Corey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:17\n\nWhy don't you try again?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:20\n\nOkay, look.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:21\n\nI think what Gloria meant there was Bitcoin Core is a software project like any other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:27\n\nAnd yes, it may be money and it may be a consensus mechanism like no one has ever seen before, but it's also a massive C pile of code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:39\n\nAnd and just just like any other project, there are there are refactors.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:43\n\nThere are so the the the build system is is one that I participate in a pretty good bit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:47\n\nThere's there's the The build process, the release process, the, you know, we can't even just hit merge on, we can't hit the green button on Bitcoin or on GitHub because we're too paranoid that Microsoft might start sliding and software works on us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:01\n\nSo we have, you know, there's a script that attests to certain keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:06\n\nYou know, there's, you as a user have all kinds of power to monitor us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:10\n\nI don't know if that's known or obvious, but if we're going to be able to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:13\n\nGloria is trying to do something sneaky, then it would be very quickly noticed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:18\n\nSo there's just all kinds of work that goes into not only just doing the day-to-day maintenance, the modernization, moving from C 98 to 11 to 17 to 20, and managing dependencies and refactoring code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:35\n\nAnd there's just a lot that goes on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:39\n\nAnd it's just not all sexy soft fork stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:42\n\nSo, to me, my day-to-day code, the thing that, or my day-to-day work, the thing that motivates me is just doing plumbing work, is just making sure that the code looks prettier now than it did yesterday or last week.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nAnd so, to me, the This is something I talk a a lot about, like the modularity of things, the architecture of the software, keeping the code alive and maintainable for the next 10, 20, 50 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:09\n\nIt's kind of insane to think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:11\n\nBut right now, there's only one reference implementation of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:14\n\nAnd unless that changes somehow, then 50 years from now, if we still want Bitcoin to be running, then this piece of software will still need to be running then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:23\n\nSo there's just a lot that goes on in day-to-day work that's not this soft fork stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:29\n\nI think the build system is one of the most underappreciated parts of core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:34\n\nAnd there are, I think, unique challenges that are battles that we picked in Bitcoin for a really good reason.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:40\n\nLike you mentioned, being really conservative about dependencies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:45\n\nObviously, we do that for security purposes, but also the fact that we have a build system that supports so many different platforms, so many different ways of running Bitcoin, and not just, you know, 686 Linux is so that it can be very accessible for anybody to run a node on their Windows laptop, on their Raspberry Pi, in the background on their MacBook or their Linux box.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:08\n\nHaving reproducible builds for each and every one of those targets is ultra hard, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:14\n\nAnd these are, I think, uniquely challenging, and we spend a lot more effort on things like this than.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:21\n\nMay be proportional to other things in the project that compared to other cryptocurrencies or other whatever, because we care really strongly about the security and the accessibility of running a node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:34\n\nAnd so I think, but you never hear about the build system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:38\n\nRaise your hand if you've ever seen a single.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:42\n\nBitcoin like article about CMake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:46\n\nLike, never, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:48\n\nBut that was 300 pull requests over the past couple of years, and it was executed really, really well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:53\n\nAnd it took a village to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:56\n\nYeah, I just sorry, I just wanted to show build people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:03\n\nAntoine can just do things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:10\n\nYou can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:11\n\nBut you can't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:12\n\nWe just talked about how that's hard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:14\n\nWe just had like 20 full minutes of you can't just do things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:20\n\nWell, actually, you can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:21\n\nThere is a lot of things that we just don't do yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:27\n\nThat we just kind of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:30\n\nAstonishing given the size of the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nTwo years ago, we had a CVE because there were too many transactions on the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:40\n\nWe had never stress-tested the transaction relay to this extent, and it just cloaked the CPU.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:48\n\nSo that's one thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:49\n\nYou don't need to change Bitcoin Core to just help with testing in this area.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:57\n\nThere is many people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:58\n\nUh so one recent news is that uh Eugene Siegel joined Brank to work on Bitcoin Core full time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:06\n\nThis guy was working on L and D full time and he fa he found a critical not critical, high severity um vulnerability in Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:17\n\nHe didn't need to change Bitcoin Core to do this and it's big things because it's high impact.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:25\n\nAnother one which needed small changes to Bitcoin Core is DRX Block that Janice mentioned earlier about peer-to-peer monitoring.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:35\n\nThis one guy that does it for this massive network that is Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:40\n\nHe introduced TressPoint into Bitcoin Core, which was A small way to get metrics out of the software, and then he built his own infrastructure entirely out of the software, and that's how he monitors the whole network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:55\n\nAnd he's crying for help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:58\n\nSo, if anybody is interested in doing high-impact stuff for Bitcoin, you could help with network monitoring.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:06\n\nYou can just do stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:11\n\nLet's talk about the roles that people play in the software project because I think from an outsider, it does seem really hard to just sort of like, well, where do I start?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nWhat do I do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:25\n\nBut there's also like these maintainers, and these maintainers are, you know, ordained somehow, and they have special powers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:35\n\nThey decide our future.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:36\n\nIsn't that all right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nIsn't that they're in charge?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:39\n\nIs that how it goes?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:41\n\nWhat's the let me put it to a better, more pointed question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:46\n\nWhat's the overlap between maintainership and leadership in this project?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:52\n\nYou just do stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:54\n\nDidn't you hear Avon?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:57\n\nThis panel's gone off the rails.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:03\n\nI think they're orthogonal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:04\n\nLike, what is a maintainer?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:06\n\nApart from having the keys to do the merge button, I think everyone here does some form of maintenance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:13\n\nLike, Corey knows everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:16\n\nSo, you need to, like, there are so many parts of the code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:19\n\nLike, if you think about part of our job as Bitcoin Core people to just remember how stuff works, I would say, like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:27\n\nYou remember the most how most stuff works.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:30\n\nAnd so the fact that you dedicate so much time to review and helping people with their projects and like kind of figuring out all of the kinks of CMake, for example, I think is very maintainery.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:43\n\nAnd similarly with Antoine, like the and Nicholas, the security advisory stuff, we went like many years without a proper security disclosure policy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:55\n\nAnd I think it's one of the most important things that happened last year that the two of you kind of took ownership over, like, okay, let's go through past stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:08\n\nCome up with a policy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:11\n\nLet's make sure this thing is robust and we will open ourselves.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:15\n\nI think the security mailing list is like one of the biggest human DOS vectors ever because it's like an open port to the entire public where all of the emails that come in have to be read by like trusted people and they contain extremely potentially extremely important information.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:34\n\nSo it needs to be opened immediately.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:37\n\nSo, I don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:37\n\nIt's just like very, very docible.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:40\n\nAnd so, to take ownership and be like, okay, I'm going to be in charge of this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:43\n\nThis is a very like heavy responsibility is maintenance of Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:51\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:52\n\nAnd then there's like what I do, which is like run some scripts and make sure, try to make try to get a release out the door, which, you know, I'm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:01\n\nTrying to do right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:03\n\nBut it basically involves just being like, okay, like who's gonna do it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:07\n\nLike, I'll do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:08\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:09\n\nAnd these are the things that have to happen in order for the project to keep moving.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:15\n\nAnd similarly, with, you know, there's like merging things, and then there's like the administration of the GitHub repo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:22\n\nLike, we get so much spam that has to be closed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:26\n\nOr moderated or whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:29\n\nThere are all these stale issues.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:30\n\nThere are 400 issues open and 250 pull requests open on the GitHub repo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:36\n\nAnd it would be great if they are all responded to so that people don't think Bitcoin Core is this black hole, like this abyss that you send your work into and never hear back from.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:46\n\nThere's, of course, you were talking about deletion, and what I said about remembering how things work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:52\n\nReports do come to the security list, or like, you know, this has happened to me a few times where, like, I'm really gonna get this thing done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:01\n\nI'm gonna write this code, or I'm gonna get this review done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:03\n\nAnd then I get an encrypted email.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:05\n\nIt's like, oh, I found this thing that crashed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:07\n\nOh, crap.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:08\n\nLike, I have to drop everything and go and do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:12\n\nLike, there are the idea of like, Taking responsibility for something when, like, of course, you can't, like, it's not written in anybody's job description as the volunteer fire department that, like, if a fire happens, like, it's your job to put it out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:31\n\nBut, like, you know, that if you don't do it, who will?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:35\n\nSo, that's kind of what maintenance means to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:40\n\nAnd then the leadership thing, there are no leaders, you can just do stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:45\n\nSo so I think Gloria was alluding to this in a couple of different ways, but I think that historically we've conflated leadership and maintenance, and specifically with the people who have the maintainer bit on GitHub, the people who can actually hit the button or hit the merge script.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:04\n\nThe idea that we've tried to establish over the years is that a maintainer is essentially an automaton, so no offense.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:14\n\nBut someone who could, in a lot of ways, be replaced by ChatGPT, where your job is not necessarily to weigh in on the work that others are doing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:25\n\nYour job is to kind of represent.\nwhen it looks like most of the developers or or the the developers who who matter for this particular pull request are on board and have signed off, you know, once you have a good number of acts by the people who matter, you kind of just hit the button.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:42\n\nSo so maintainers aren't necessarily supposed to or or I hope it's understood by the other developers and by outsiders that the the Maintainers aren't necessarily weighing in when they merge something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:55\n\nThey're not necessarily endorsing any particular pull request.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:58\n\nThey're simply acknowledging that this pull request seems to have the weight behind it, that the developers who have contributed to it have, you know, that it seems to basically fill the requirements.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:12\n\nAnd so it's unfortunate that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:15\n\nLike for example, the Gloria is tasked with that responsibility and with looking at the security mailing list.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:22\n\nThose are two things that I think she's kind of stumbled into both of those roles because of this conflation of maintainership and leadership.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:32\n\nAnd one of the things that we talk about pretty often, or have started talking about more recently, is trying to split up these responsibilities so that maintainers aren't necessarily tasked with all of the leadership tasks as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:43\n\nWhere the the maintainer is the maintainership is kind of a role on on I mean specifically on GitHub or in the Git repository and uh some of the the decision making and the leadership and and the the other the other roles that people take on that people take on you know release management for for example that's one that we were talking about at at lunch you don't necessarily have to be a maintainer to do like 90% of what it takes to to Create a release.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:08\n\nThere are some things at the very end.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:09\n\nYou have to do the tagging and the binary pushing and that kind of thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:12\n\nBut there are just a lot of things that go into producing a major Bitcoin Core release that you don't necessarily need to be a maintainer for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:20\n\nYou could volunteer that as a different role.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:26\n\nI'm going to do something very dangerous and disagree slightly with Curry.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:32\n\nI don't think we can replace maintainers with chat GPT because we need to keep accountability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:39\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:40\n\nYeah, but I I think I think that's something that is worth and fa fuck, I cannot pronounce this word, so I'm going to skip it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:48\n\nBut just making importance of this is that um we need accountability from um maintainers, but Again, you can just do stuff and it and there is accountability that comes from non-maintainer contributors.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:05\n\nFor instance, I'm I'm I'm can think of Marker, who keeps reviewing every single pull request that gets merged and finds bugs after it's merged.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:16\n\nAnd say just before the release, he found a bug from that was shipped and we didn't have CI for this that was merged, sorry, not shipped, that was merged and we did not have CI and it's just read the code and since, I don't know, he has a compiler in his head or something, it just realized that's not valid C and it just said, well, you mu you merged invalid C.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:42\n\nAnd yeah, that's s sort of the leadership, but just maybe make it maybe leading by example more than trying to get people to do what he wants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:53\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:54\n\nWe've we've done a lot of talking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:56\n\nUm do we want the audience to ask some very benign questions that we can be comfortable answering?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:05\n\nOr not?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:06\n\nAdam, I know you said you're less technical, so I'll ask you a less technical question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:11\n\nWhat was it like growing up at Jonas Brother, and what do you think the contributions of boy bands from the Beatles forward have been to the music scene in general?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:20\n\nYeah, those are my cousins from New Jersey, and we're very proud of their success.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:26\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:26\n\nAppreciate it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:27\n\nYou're welcome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:29\n\nAny other really important questions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:32\n\nThese are the kinds of questions actually I like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:36\n\nThanks a lot for giving us the color on a lot of what the day-to-day work is that you do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:43\n\nSo thank you all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:45\n\nOne just kind of a quick take, some of the big things that are thinking about Opcat and Op C T V.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:54\n\nAny any kind of quick takes on Are those going to happen and when?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:58\n\nI mean, uh, just I don't want to derail the whole discussion, but just kind of your your quick your quick takes on on that given your perspective.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:05\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:07\n\nMaintainers decide soft folks, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:11\n\nI mean, are are are you going to roll it out UASF?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:13\n\nIs that you?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:18\n\nOne of those has a PR.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:21\n\nBut no acts, so there's nothing for me to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:25\n\nI mean, just your just your p do you like just your personal perspective not in your account, like do you what do you think that it's gonna happen within Mighty Mm-hmm?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:37\n\nMy personal perspective is that there is still a lot of disagreement on Uh on each of those.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:44\n\nThere is one that uh seems because it's much simpler, well, not really, but it's it seems safer, seems to be further uh in the road.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:55\n\nBut also there is clearly, clearly no consensus on anything, and therefore there is no real role for Bitcoin Core at this point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:06\n\nIt seems that you would shower At Bitcoin Core for the final code review after reaching consensus among the wider Bitcoin research and developer community?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:21\n\nSo in some ways I think to not weigh in is kind of a punt.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:25\n\nBut at the same time, so I personally don't see this as my job.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:30\n\nI can say that I have I have reviewed both technically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:33\n\nI've looked at the code behind Opcat and CTV and they're both simple and like I've kind of given both the thumbs up, but I don't consider myself big-brained enough to understand what the implications of them are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:47\n\nAnd so I think that I called myself a code monkey earlier.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:51\n\nTo me, the C, sure, ship it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:54\n\nBut the activation parameters, the actually, Having it as a feature in the currency, that I have zero opinion on because I don't feel like I should.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:07\n\nGo ahead.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:10\n\nSo, with all that being said about the soft forks, do you think we could get a Boston agreement hashed out tonight or should we wait until next year?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:20\n\nLet's go.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:23\n\nSo who signs?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:25\n\nWho signs what?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:26\n\nThe the if we if we get a Boston agreement drafted, who who who signs it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:31\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:33\n\nSo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:33\n\nWhat's included?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:34\n\nDo you include the consensus cleanup or?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:39\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:39\n\nThe kitchen sink.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:40\n\nLet's keep going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:41\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:41\n\nI'll let I'll let you ask the next question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:43\n\nMine's not super important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:44\n\nYou go for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:44\n\nWhat's important next, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:46\n\nNo, after you.\nOkay.\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:48\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:49\n\nSo, in the interest of time, unfortunately, we have to wrap up the QA for this session, but maybe we can catch the speakers afterwards.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:58\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:59\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:38:00\n\nThank you so much.	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nMaybe just to start things off, let's do a quick round of introductions and maybe add on why you work on Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nYou want to start with Gloria?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:10\n\nOh, sure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:12\n\nHi, I'm Gloria.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:14\n\nI went to college, not this one, but I did go to college.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:24\n\nSo, like a lot of college students, I really wanted to save the world.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:28\n\nI was interested in technological solutions to social problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:33\n\nI met Alex Gladstein of the Human Rights Foundation and learned about all of the activism and just billions of people who don't get access to the US dollar like we do in the US, and found this problem extremely compelling and found Bitcoin to be a really good solution to it, or at least the furthest I can think of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:59\n\nAnd so, as a computer science student who wanted to be a software engineer, this was kind of the perfect marriage of...\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:07\n\nTwo really good reasons to work on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:10\n\nAnd so I applied to the Chain Code residency when I was a college student, ended up starting to contribute to Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nAnd then when I graduated, I was lucky enough to get a grant from the HRF.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:22\n\nAnd it was called Square Crypto or Spiral at the time to do it full time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:27\n\nAnd so since graduation, I have worked on Bitcoin Core full time for the past four or five years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:35\n\nSo I'm Corey...\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:36\n\nI have a background in open source C software development and I got tired of putting my credit card number into buy things online.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:47\n\nThat just seems stupid to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:50\n\nSo I read about Bitcoin and the idea that you can push a payment as opposed to pull and that made more sense, that just seemed like the way that things should be.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:00\n\nSo I hopped in, and there was this idea that the code is the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:05\n\nAnd it took a long time for that to click for me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:08\n\nThat, like, it's as simple as that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\n\nThe code, the software is the money.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:12\n\nAnd it just became a fascinating thing to think about to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:16\n\nAnd the number of just technology challenges.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:22\n\nIt's just that there are just hard problems in Bitcoin and they're a lot of fun.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:26\n\nSo I got hacking on the code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:28\n\nI started, I was asked to join the Bitcoin Foundation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:33\n\nI had health insurance for one week at the Bitcoin Foundation before it crumbled.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:38\n\nAnd thankfully, Gavin and Patrick and some other people that were at the foundation kind of shopped us around and found us a home here at MIT.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:47\n\nSo I've been working at the DCI here at MIT, the Digital Currency Initiative, for the last 10 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:53\n\nAnd my job is just to work on Bitcoin and Bitcoin Core and keep it going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:02\n\nMy name is Antoine Poinsot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:04\n\nI work at Chaincode Labs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:06\n\nI think I got started working on Bitcoin for similar reasons to Gloria.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:12\n\nAnd the reason I work on Bitcoin Core in particular is probably impact.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:19\n\nProbably the most frustrating project you could work at, but also the most impactful one in the space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:28\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:30\n\nSo we're gonna do a little nice easy warm up here.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:34\n\nUm, what should we do about all these software proponents?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:39\n\nShip all of them at once on CNN.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:47\n\nAnybody else want to jump in?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:50\n\nYeah, I think you alluded to this in your presentation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:55\n\nA lot of people, I guess as Bitcoin devs, like, I mean, my job description is not completely clear, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:05\n\nThere's maintaining the Bitcoin Core codebase, which I would say is kind of the primary hat that I would wear.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:11\n\nAnd then there's protocol research and Bitcoin software creation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:20\n\nSo I think a good example of...\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:24\n\nOf this is people are like, we need to make self-custody better.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:28\n\nAnd I think there are a lot of different ways to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\n\nYou know, people want to build vaults, so they want some kind of covenant to build vaults.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:35\n\nBut of course, there are people who are building certain apps or companies where there's an app and a hardware wallet, and they're trying to create a solution for custody.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:45\n\nOr if you're interested in scalability, like, there are many different layers of the stack, not just technology, but products that you can be working on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:57\n\nAnd so, I guess my thing is: like, yes, there is a lot of different things in Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:06\n\nAnd I think just talking about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:09\n\nConsensus is, I think people conflate consensus with protocol development, or they conflate soft fork proposal championing with Bitcoin Core maintenance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:20\n\nThey're like very, very different things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:22\n\nAnd actually, Corey, you had a really good point earlier when we were chatting about how there's almost they're almost incompatible sometimes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:29\n\nSo if you want to talk about that.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:31\n\nSo I in my head, I think of this as the soft fork paradox where Basically, the core developers are tasked with keeping Bitcoin as it is known alive and maintaining Bitcoin, the reference implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:47\n\nAnd that kind of is Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:49\n\nAnd because there's no spec, because there are no written rules, the reference implementation is Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:55\n\nSo if you're a Core developer, if you're a maintainer, your job is to maintain Bitcoin as you found it, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:04\n\nAnd so there's this kind of fundamental problem of who is tasked with, I mean, you can, it's a stretch, but you can define every soft fork as an attack because it's an attack on the status quo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:18\n\nAnd it may be an upgrade.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:19\n\nIt may be that 90% of people want this attack, but it's an attack because it changes the rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:25\n\nSo it's kind of a, you know, there's kind of a An oath that you take, kind of an implicit oath as a maintainer that thou shalt not change the consensus rules.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:36\n\nAnd so to merge in a soft fork, and especially the activation parameters for a soft fork, you're kind of violating that oath.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:44\n\nSo to me, it's this big open question of who is tasked with making these changes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:49\n\nAnd I think it's easy to forget that there are people behind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:53\n\nThe merge button on GitHub or the merge script that the maintainers run, there's a human being that is sitting behind that and clicking or hitting enter and saying, boom, I've broken my oath.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:09\n\nI've decided to fork Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nThat's a real thing that someone has to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:13\n\nAnd so who is that person?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:15\n\nWho can be tasked with that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:16\n\nGloria has that power.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:17\n\nDo you want to do that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:18\n\nLike, that doesn't seem like a fun Thursday night.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:23\n\nYeah, I think related to that, I think people seem to have this obsession with Softforks that, like, I'll often get asked, like, oh, you work on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:34\n\nLike, what's the next soft fork?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:36\n\nWhich is like, Softforks, I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:39\n\nKnow, deservedly get a lot of coverage and sensationalism because, like you said, they consensus changes are changes to the rules that decide who can spend their money and who owns how much bitcoins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:53\n\nLike, that's really, really invasive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:55\n\nAnd I think that's so fundamental.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:57\n\nSo, of course, it makes sense that it comes up in the news so much more often than what port you can open as a Bitcoin node for inbound connections.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:10\n\nBut changing just from eight through three through three is really important for censorship resistance, for example.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:17\n\nLike we were really concerned about ISPs shutting down traffic to that port because that's so characteristically a Bitcoin node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:26\n\nAnd so I think it was a really great change for us to work on that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:30\n\nThere's no bid for that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:31\n\nThere's certainly, I didn't see any news coverage of like, oh, the port has changed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:39\n\nAnd so, yeah, I think it's almost like people watch too much Gray's Anatomy and they're like, all of medicine is brain surgeries.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:45\n\nOh, you're a doctor?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:46\n\nHow many brain surgeries have you done?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:48\n\nBut, like, most of medicine is, first of all, not brain surgery.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:52\n\nIt's certainly very impressive, very invasive, but most.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nMedicine is preventative, and you can save lots and lots of lives and do healthcare without ever doing a brain surgery, even if it is kind of this sensational, like dramatic thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:08\n\nSo, similarly, I think my point here, I'll finish up soon, is even within Bitcoin Core, there's so much that is done that is not consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:17\n\nIf you look at the list of BIPs, probably half of them are actually peer-to-peer protocol things, and there's a lot of very impactful things you can do there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:26\n\nAnd a lot of things don't have BIPs because they are engineering challenges that Satoshi left as an exercise to the reader, and we had to figure out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:38\n\nAnd when there were tens of thousands of transactions coming down the network, like we were like, oh shit, like we need to fit, we need to fix this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:48\n\nSo like the idea of Bitcoin being robust and censorship resistant and all these things are like things that we work on every single day that you don't necessarily see.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:02\n\nSo I'd quickly like to ask Jonas.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:05\n\nBecause I've heard you give your spiel a couple times about how you're not one of us and you're the water boy and the helper and all that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:11\n\nBut that's, I mean, that's that's bullshit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nSo what would you do about all the software?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:17\n\nThat's not bullshit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nI am an outside observer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:23\n\nI really, I think for some of these, the truth of the matter is that we.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:30\n\nAs a non-big brain, like need to have some trust for the keepers of the flame.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nAnd if they, you know, it should be verified clearly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:43\n\nBut I think a lot of my opinion, of course, is informed by people that I respect the opinion of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:51\n\nAnd I think I try to understand the trade-offs and understand the.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:56\n\nWhy, like the use cases and the risks involved.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:01\n\nBut I'll never be the one that has that runs the script.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:05\n\nSo it sort of absolves me from the ultimate load.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:10\n\nAnd I think that makes me, again, a muggle and a water boy, like most of the ecosystem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nAntoine, do you have opinions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:20\n\nUh three things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:22\n\nUh I wrote a blog post about my opinion on all this FOO proposal, so for the longer version you can refer to that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:31\n\nI'd like first of all to highlight what Corey said about consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nI think people severely underestimate consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:41\n\nWe have this global network And the set of rules that everyone agrees upon.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:49\n\nIf we got together and tried to decide the cancer scrolls today with everybody with divergent incentives and opinion, it would just never happen.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:01\n\nAnd without consensus, there is no Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nI've heard the other day on Twitter.\nsomebody referring as oh the proof of work can just decide what the consensus is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:16\n\nThe proof of work is part of the consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:19\n\nLike the consensus comes before anything else.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:22\n\nAnd Bitcoin is valuable today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:25\n\nCensorship resistant money, even if it does not come at a huge scale, solves real problem today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:33\n\nSo we must be very careful in risking it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:37\n\nSecond of all, I wanted to uh give a more practical response to your initial questions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:44\n\nUh I think a lot of the BIP fork proposal that we see today, um not all of them, but a lot are because there is so many of them uh kind of solutions such of a problem, or it's not exactly clear what problem they're solving.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:03\n\nAnd it's even amplified by the fact that Bitcoin has become so big, so big, that we cannot be specialists of every single area.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:17\n\nAnd there is this area, especially with the covenant discussion, there is researchers specializing in this area.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:23\n\nSo it would be very useful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:26\n\nBecause, in addition to solutions in search of a problem, there is also not all of them agree on which solution without even defining clearly the problem in the first place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nSo, I think what would be useful from a Bitcoin Core perspective would be some sort of a research report from this loosely defined researcher community: we set to solve this problem and we find that this solution is appropriate and it has this and this trade-off and we bring it to the community.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:58\n\nI know that there is a few people I see in the room that said to do exactly this work in comparing differing different alternatives and I I think that's very useful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nCan I ask?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nBecause I think this is a point of frustration for a lot of said people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:16\n\nWhat's the right forum to do this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:17\n\nIt seems like it leaks into a lot of different places.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:20\n\nSo, you know, there's back and forth on Delving, there's the mailing list.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:26\n\nI think the lowest common denominator seems to be Twitter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:31\n\nBut where's the right place to engage in these conversations?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:35\n\nNot Twitter.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:39\n\nWell, if you want to be taken seriously, I mean.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:41\n\nOkay, can we do more better than Twitter?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:46\n\nI yeah, I think I like delving.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:48\n\nUm I I guess I'll take the bait.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:51\n\nLike, I think so people will often ask, like, oh, like the maintainers are not merging some covenant and uh You know, faculty and I will look at each other and be like, is there a PR open for that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nAnd there isn't on Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:14\n\nBut there is no.\nYeah, there is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:16\n\nWell, I didn't name a specific one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nBut like, I think Bitcoin Core, the GitHub repo, is a place for code review.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:26\n\nAnd of course, we have lots of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:29\n\nCode concept discussion on core at various times.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:33\n\nAnd I'm not trying to police where you don't come on core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:38\n\nBut a lot of the wider community discussions slash flame wars can be very disruptive in a GitHub repository.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:47\n\nIt's like this is where we work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:49\n\nThat doesn't, you're still, we still don't have an answer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:52\n\nWhat's the rules of engagement?\nyou know, what as I as I tried to point out, like, we have this conflation of Bitcoin Protocol Research and Bitcoin Core, they they do have to dance together eventually.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:06\n\nSo what is the lead up what is the what is the back and forth and the social nuance of that lead up to to said dance?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:14\n\nI think there's a frustrating truth here that that venue changes over time and has changed over time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:19\n\nAnd there is no, like Bitcoin, especially Bitcoin Core, struggles with process because it's particularly hard to install process for something that's supposed to be essentially process-free.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:31\n\nThe core devs, as kind of sort of gatekeepers, as we're pretty often called, we shouldn't exist.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:39\n\nAnd so there shouldn't be a technical forum or a specific venue for this type of a discussion because it it almost it almost certainly invites discrimination as far as who's allowed to show up, who's allowed to to argue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:54\n\nSo over time, you know, it used to be Bitcoin Talk is is where these discussions would happen, and then it was Reddit, and then it was the mailing list, and now it's delving and and in Google groups and they were Scaling Bitcoin and there are conferences and there are papers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:07\n\nAnd I think there just is an unfortunate truth that should be recognized: that is, these discussions are going to be fluid and the venues are going to be fluid.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:15\n\nAnd in order to participate, there's almost a mini consensus mechanism involved here as well, that you have to know where the conversation is taking place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:26\n\nYou know, there's a there like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nClumping is just natural in these scenarios.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:31\n\nSo these fork discussions may happen on delving, and that may be where the interesting thing happens.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nBut that's not to say it's where it's going to happen next time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:43\n\nWe've covered one topic.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:44\n\nSo you get the last word.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:49\n\nJust a third point I wanted to make earlier.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:52\n\nJust a clarification for people that are maybe newer in the room to Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:57\n\nCorey and Gloria talked about the merging scripts, and we talked about SatsForks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:01\n\nMerging a SatsWork is not in Bitcoin Core, it's not how it gets activated on Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:07\n\nIt's what users choose to run, what software users choose to run.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:13\n\nI would tend to agree that Bitcoin Core is probably the only reasonable software to run as a full node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:21\n\nStill, merging it is not enough to activate the software.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:28\n\nSo let's not talk about soft forks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:31\n\nBut let's talk about how other things get done in Bitcoin Core, the software project.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:36\n\nSo you all have done big things and are in the process of doing more big things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:44\n\nAnd so, like, What's that dance look like?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:48\n\nHow are you effective as a contributor to getting big things done at Bitcoin Core?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:56\n\nI heard CMake was 300 pull requests.\n300 pull requests.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:01\n\nThat's a lot for those that don't know what that is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:04\n\nBut that's a lot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:05\n\nSo I think maybe Gloria's point there was there's a lot of boring stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:11\n\nThat goes on in the day-to-day development.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nThat's not what I've meant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:16\n\nThis is poor framing, Corey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:17\n\nWhy don't you try again?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:20\n\nOkay, look.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:21\n\nI think what Gloria meant there was Bitcoin Core is a software project like any other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:27\n\nAnd yes, it may be money and it may be a consensus mechanism like no one has ever seen before, but it's also a massive C pile of code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:39\n\nAnd and just just like any other project, there are there are refactors.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:43\n\nThere are so the the the build system is is one that I participate in a pretty good bit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:47\n\nThere's there's the The build process, the release process, the, you know, we can't even just hit merge on, we can't hit the green button on Bitcoin or on GitHub because we're too paranoid that Microsoft might start sliding and software works on us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:01\n\nSo we have, you know, there's a script that attests to certain keys.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:06\n\nYou know, there's, you as a user have all kinds of power to monitor us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:10\n\nI don't know if that's known or obvious, but if we're going to be able to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:13\n\nGloria is trying to do something sneaky, then it would be very quickly noticed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:18\n\nSo there's just all kinds of work that goes into not only just doing the day-to-day maintenance, the modernization, moving from C 98 to 11 to 17 to 20, and managing dependencies and refactoring code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:35\n\nAnd there's just a lot that goes on.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:39\n\nAnd it's just not all sexy soft fork stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:42\n\nSo, to me, my day-to-day code, the thing that, or my day-to-day work, the thing that motivates me is just doing plumbing work, is just making sure that the code looks prettier now than it did yesterday or last week.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nAnd so, to me, this is something I talk a lot about, like the modularity of things, the architecture of the software, keeping the code alive and maintainable for the next 10, 20, 50 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:09\n\nIt's kind of insane to think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:11\n\nBut right now, there's only one reference implementation of Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:14\n\nAnd unless that changes somehow, then 50 years from now, if we still want Bitcoin to be running, then this piece of software will still need to be running then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:23\n\nSo there's just a lot that goes on in day-to-day work that's not this soft fork stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:29\n\nI think the build system is one of the most underappreciated parts of Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:34\n\nAnd there are, I think, unique challenges that are battles that we picked in Bitcoin for a really good reason.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:40\n\nLike you mentioned, being really conservative about dependencies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:45\n\nObviously, we do that for security purposes, but also the fact that we have a build system that supports so many different platforms, so many different ways of running Bitcoin, and not just, you know, x86 Linux is so that it can be very accessible for anybody to run a node on their Windows laptop, on their Raspberry Pi, in the background on their MacBook or their Linux box.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:08\n\nHaving reproducible builds for each and every one of those targets is ultra hard, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:14\n\nAnd these are, I think, uniquely challenging, and we spend a lot more effort on things like this than.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:21\n\nMay be proportional to other things in the project that compared to other cryptocurrencies or other whatever, because we care really strongly about the security and the accessibility of running a node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:34\n\nAnd so I think, but you never hear about the build system.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:38\n\nRaise your hand if you've ever seen a single.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:42\n\nBitcoin like article about CMake.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:46\n\nLike, never, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:48\n\nBut that was 300 pull requests over the past couple of years, and it was executed really, really well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:53\n\nAnd it took a village to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:56\n\nYeah, I just sorry, I just wanted to show build people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:03\n\nAntoine can just do things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:10\n\nYou can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:11\n\nBut you can't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:12\n\nWe just talked about how that's hard.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:14\n\nWe just had like 20 full minutes of you can't just do things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:20\n\nWell, actually, you can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:21\n\nThere is a lot of things that we just don't do yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:27\n\nThat we just kind of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:30\n\nAstonishing given the size of the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:32\n\nTwo years ago, we had a CVE because there were too many transactions on the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:40\n\nWe had never stress-tested the transaction relay to this extent, and it just choked the CPU.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:48\n\nSo that's one thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:49\n\nYou don't need to change Bitcoin Core to just help with testing in this area.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:57\n\nThere are many people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:58\n\nUh so one recent news is that uh Eugene Siegel joined Brank to work on Bitcoin Core full time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:06\n\nThis guy was working on LND full time and he found a critical, high severity um vulnerability in Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:17\n\nHe didn't need to change Bitcoin Core to do this and it's big things because it's high impact.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:25\n\nAnother one which needed small changes to Bitcoin Core is DRX Block that Janice mentioned earlier about peer-to-peer monitoring.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:35\n\nThis one guy that does it for this massive network that is Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:40\n\nHe introduced Trezor into Bitcoin Core, which was a small way to get metrics out of the software, and then he built his own infrastructure entirely out of the software, and that's how he monitors the whole network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:55\n\nAnd he's crying for help.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:58\n\nSo, if anybody is interested in doing high-impact stuff for Bitcoin, you could help with network monitoring.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:06\n\nYou can just do stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:11\n\nLet's talk about the roles that people play in the software project because I think from an outsider, it does seem really hard to just sort of like, well, where do I start?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nWhat do I do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:25\n\nBut there's also like these maintainers, and these maintainers are, you know, ordained somehow, and they have special powers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:35\n\nThey decide our future.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:36\n\nIsn't that alright?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nIsn't that they're in charge?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:39\n\nIs that how it goes?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:41\n\nWhat's the let me put it to a better, more pointed question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:46\n\nWhat's the overlap between maintainership and leadership in this project?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:52\n\nYou just do stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:54\n\nDidn't you hear Avon?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:57\n\nThis panel's gone off the rails.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:03\n\nI think they're orthogonal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:04\n\nLike, what is a maintainer?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:06\n\nApart from having the keys to do the merge button, I think everyone here does some form of maintenance.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:13\n\nLike, Corey knows everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:16\n\nSo, you need to, like, there are so many parts of the code.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:19\n\nLike, if you think about part of our job as Bitcoin Core people to just remember how stuff works, I would say, like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:27\n\nYou remember most how most stuff works.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:30\n\nAnd so the fact that you dedicate so much time to review and helping people with their projects and like kind of figuring out all of the kinks of CMake, for example, I think is very maintainery.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:43\n\nAnd similarly with Antoine, like the and Nicholas, the security advisory stuff, we went like many years without a proper security disclosure policy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:55\n\nAnd I think it's one of the most important things that happened last year that the two of you kind of took ownership over, like, okay, let's go through past stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:08\n\nCome up with a policy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:11\n\nLet's make sure this thing is robust and we will open ourselves.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:15\n\nI think the security mailing list is like one of the biggest human DOS vectors ever because it's like an open port to the entire public where all of the emails that come in have to be read by like trusted people and they contain extremely potentially extremely important information.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:34\n\nSo it needs to be opened immediately.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:37\n\nSo, I don't know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:37\n\nIt's just like very, very doable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:40\n\nAnd so, to take ownership and be like, okay, I'm going to be in charge of this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:43\n\nThis is a very like heavy responsibility is maintenance of Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:51\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:52\n\nAnd then there's like what I do, which is like run some scripts and make sure, try to make try to get a release out the door, which, you know, I'm.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:01\n\nTrying to do right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:03\n\nBut it basically involves just being like, okay, like who's gonna do it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:07\n\nLike, I'll do it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:08\n\nRight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:09\n\nAnd these are the things that have to happen in order for the project to keep moving.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:15\n\nAnd similarly, with, you know, there's like merging things, and then there's like the administration of the GitHub repo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:22\n\nLike, we get so much spam that has to be closed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:26\n\nOr moderated or whatever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:29\n\nThere are all these stale issues.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:30\n\nThere are 400 issues open and 250 pull requests open on the GitHub repo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:36\n\nAnd it would be great if they are all responded to so that people don't think Bitcoin Core is this black hole, like this abyss that you send your work into and never hear back from.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:46\n\nThere's, of course, what you were talking about deletion, and what I said about remembering how things work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:52\n\nReports do come to the security list, or like, you know, this has happened to me a few times where, like, I'm really gonna get this thing done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:01\n\nI'm gonna write this code, or I'm gonna get this review done.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:03\n\nAnd then I get an encrypted email.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:05\n\nIt's like, oh, I found this thing that crashed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:07\n\nOh, crap.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:08\n\nLike, I have to drop everything and go and do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:12\n\nLike, there's the idea of taking responsibility for something when, like, of course, you can't, like, it's not written in anybody's job description as the volunteer fire department that, like, if a fire happens, like, it's your job to put it out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:31\n\nBut, like, you know, that if you don't do it, who will?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:35\n\nSo, that's kind of what maintenance means to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:40\n\nAnd then the leadership thing, there are no leaders, you can just do stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:45\n\nSo so I think Gloria was alluding to this in a couple of different ways, but I think that historically we've conflated leadership and maintenance, and specifically with the people who have the maintainer bit on GitHub, the people who can actually hit the button or hit the merge script.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:04\n\nThe idea that we've tried to establish over the years is that a maintainer is essentially an automaton, so no offense.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:14\n\nBut someone who could, in a lot of ways, be replaced by ChatGPT, where your job is not necessarily to weigh in on the work that others are doing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:25\n\nYour job is to kind of represent.\nWhen it looks like most of the developers or or the the developers who who matter for this particular pull request are on board and have signed off, you know, once you have a good number of ACKs by the people who matter, you kind of just hit the button.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:42\n\nSo so maintainers aren't necessarily supposed to or or I hope it's understood by the other developers and by outsiders that the the Maintainers aren't necessarily weighing in when they merge something.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:55\n\nThey're not necessarily endorsing any particular pull request.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:58\n\nThey're simply acknowledging that this pull request seems to have the weight behind it, that the developers who have contributed to it have, you know, that it seems to basically fill the requirements.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:12\n\nAnd so it's unfortunate that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:15\n\nLike for example, Gloria is tasked with that responsibility and with looking at the security mailing list.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:22\n\nThose are two things that I think she's kind of stumbled into both of those roles because of this conflation of maintainership and leadership.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:32\n\nAnd one of the things that we talk about pretty often, or have started talking about more recently, is trying to split up these responsibilities so that maintainers aren't necessarily tasked with all of the leadership tasks as well.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:31:43\n\nWhere the the maintainer is the maintainership is kind of a role on on I mean specifically on GitHub or in the Git repository and uh some of the the decision making and the leadership and and the the other the other roles that people take on that people take on you know release management for for example that's one that we were talking about at at lunch you don't necessarily have to be a maintainer to do like 90% of what it takes to to Create a release.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:08\n\nThere are some things at the very end.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:09\n\nYou have to do the tagging and the binary pushing and that kind of thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:12\n\nBut there are just a lot of things that go into producing a major Bitcoin Core release that you don't necessarily need to be a maintainer for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:20\n\nYou could volunteer that as a different role.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:26\n\nI'm going to do something very dangerous and disagree slightly with Curry.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:32\n\nI don't think we can replace maintainers with ChatGPT because we need to keep accountability.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:39\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:40\n\nYeah, but I I think I think that's something that is worth and fa fuck, I cannot pronounce this word, so I'm going to skip it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:32:48\n\nBut just making importance of this is that um we need accountability from um maintainers, but Again, you can just do stuff and it and there is accountability that comes from non-maintainer contributors.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:05\n\nFor instance, I'm I'm I'm can think of Mark Karper, who keeps reviewing every single pull request that gets merged and finds bugs after it's merged.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:16\n\nAnd say just before the release, he found a bug from that was shipped and we didn't have CI for this that was merged, sorry, not shipped, that was merged and we did not have CI and it's just read the code and since, I don't know, he has a compiler in his head or something, it just realized that's not valid C and it just said, well, you mu you merged invalid C.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:42\n\nAnd yeah, that's a sort of the leadership, but just maybe make it maybe leading by example more than trying to get people to do what he wants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:53\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:54\n\nWe've we've done a lot of talking.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:33:56\n\nUm do we want the audience to ask some very benign questions that we can be comfortable answering?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:05\n\nOr not?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:06\n\nAdam, I know you said you're less technical, so I'll ask you a less technical question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:11\n\nWhat was it like growing up at Jonas Brother, and what do you think the contributions of boy bands from the Beatles forward have been to the music scene in general?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:20\n\nYeah, those are my cousins from New Jersey, and we're very proud of their success.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:26\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:26\n\nAppreciate it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:27\n\nYou're welcome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:29\n\nAny other really important questions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:32\n\nThese are the kinds of questions actually I like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:36\n\nThanks a lot for giving us the color on a lot of what the day-to-day work is that you do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:43\n\nSo thank you all.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:45\n\nOne just kind of a quick take, some of the big things that are thinking about OP_CAT and OP_CTV.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:54\n\nAny any kind of quick takes on Are those going to happen and when?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:34:58\n\nI mean, uh, just I don't want to derail the whole discussion, but just kind of your your quick your quick takes on on that given your perspective.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:05\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:07\n\nMaintainers decide soft forks, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:11\n\nI mean, are are are you going to roll out UASF?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:13\n\nIs that you?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:18\n\nOne of those has a PR.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:21\n\nBut no acts, so there's nothing for me to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:25\n\nI mean, just your just your PDP, do you like just your personal perspective not in your account, like do you what do you think that it's gonna happen within Mighty Mm-hmm?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:37\n\nMy personal perspective is that there is still a lot of disagreement on Uh on each of those.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:44\n\nThere is one that uh seems because it's much simpler, well, not really, but it's it seems safer, seems to be further uh in the road.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:35:55\n\nBut also there is clearly, clearly no consensus on anything, and therefore there is no real role for Bitcoin Core at this point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:06\n\nIt seems that you would shower at Bitcoin Core for the final code review after reaching consensus among the wider Bitcoin research and developer community?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:21\n\nSo in some ways I think to not weigh in is kind of a punt.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:25\n\nBut at the same time, so I personally don't see this as my job.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:30\n\nI can say that I have I have reviewed both technically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:33\n\nI've looked at the code behind OP_CAT and CTV and they're both simple and like I've kind of given both the thumbs up, but I don't consider myself big-brained enough to understand what the implications of them are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:47\n\nAnd so I think that I called myself a code monkey earlier.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:51\n\nTo me, the C, sure, ship it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:36:54\n\nBut the activation parameters, the actually, having it as a feature in the currency, that I have zero opinion on because I don't feel like I should.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:07\n\nGo ahead.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:10\n\nSo, with all that being said about the soft forks, do you think we could get a BIP agreement hashed out tonight or should we wait until next year?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:20\n\nLet's go.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:23\n\nSo who signs?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:25\n\nWho signs what?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:26\n\nThe the if we if we get a BIP agreement drafted, who who who signs it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:31\n\nOkay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:33\n\nSo.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:33\n\nWhat's included?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:34\n\nDo you include the consensus cleanup or?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:39\n\nCool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:39\n\nThe kitchen sink.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:40\n\nLet's keep going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:41\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:41\n\nI'll let I'll let you ask the next question.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:43\n\nMine's not super important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:44\n\nYou go for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:44\n\nWhat's important next, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:46\n\nNo, after you.\nOkay.\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:48\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:49\n\nSo, in the interest of time, unfortunately, we have to wrap up the QA for this session, but maybe we can catch the speakers afterwards.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:58\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:37:59\n\nAll right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:38:00\n\nThank you so much.	Bitcoin Core maintainers Gloria Osawa and Antoine Poinsot, alongside other core contributors, discuss the diverse motivations behind their work, which span from financial inclusion to preserving Bitcoin's "code is money" status. A central theme is the distinction between high-level consensus decisions regarding protocol changes like soft forks and the essential, often underappreciated engineering work required for daily maintenance. This day-to-day effort includes system modernization, dependency management, refactoring legacy C code, and ensuring robustness across platforms. Critical infrastructure such as security policies, network monitoring tools like P2Pool, and release coordination are highlighted as vital components that keep the software sustainable over decades, contrasting sharply with the focus on high-profile upgrades alone.\n\nThe discussion further explores the dynamics of open-source maintenance roles, arguing against conflating "maintainership" (acting as a technical gatekeeper) with actual leadership (influencing design or consensus). Panelists note that emergency security patches often force volunteers to deprioritize planned tasks and suggest decoupling these responsibilities; for instance, release management could be handled by non-maintainers. While acknowledging the theoretical capacity of AI tools like ChatGPT, the group emphasizes the enduring need for human accountability and deep code understanding to identify post-merge bugs. Regarding specific Bitcoin proposals like OP_CAT and OP_CTV, maintainers express neutrality due to a lack of broader community consensus, concluding that without alignment among researchers and developers, pushing such features forward is not currently viable. The session concludes with logistical matters regarding BIP signing agreements before transitioning to a Q&A segment.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TEVJUjOGmOI	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:09:12.631274+05:30	2026-04-21 22:40:51.139539+05:30
ab4e4139-5c50-41d5-9ca4-70bb55fabe08	lW8r9hq8-yU	conference	2025-04-08	{}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nHello everybody, I am Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine and I'm joined here by Gloria Zhao, Bitcoin Core maintainer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\n\nYes, that's me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:10\n\nSo we are here today to talk a little bit about mempools, one of the most chaotic, messy pieces of the Bitcoin protocol, which is your area of focus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\n\nSo I guess to start, um you wanna kinda just like at the very high level like explain like how it functions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:30\n\nLike Yeah, I think Mempool is sometimes underrated uh as A crucial part of Node software that people don't like to think about very much or kind of trivialize because it's not consensus, but it is kind of a really big part of things to consider when you're creating transactions and when you're building L2s.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:51\n\nAnd also, simultaneously, I think, over-dramatized sometimes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\n\nBut the way that I like to say it is: Mempool is just a cache.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:00\n\nWe have this distributed network which allows us to have censorship-resistant payments, which is really awesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:09\n\nAnd without that, we wouldn't have censorship-resistant payments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nBut so, this distributed network that serves as a kind of Obfuscated place for you to broadcast your transactions in a private/slash censorship resistant way is an emergent property of individual nodes participating in transaction relay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:36\n\nAnd my talk yesterday was about how difficult that is because the individual node software has to be really accessible to run, like on a laptop or on a Raspberry Pi, but What transaction relay is, is you're exposing yourself to random peers on the network that are going to send you lots of data that you're going to validate, you're going to spend resources like memory and bandwidth and CPU on, but ultimately might not be very useful to you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:02\n\nAnd unless you're a miner, like you don't get any compensation for this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:06\n\nAnd so, like, that kind of software engineering is really difficult.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\n\nLike, you have a lot of security problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:13\n\nBut it is what gives us this wonderful payments network that is censorship resistant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:20\n\nSo, okay, your question was: what is mempool?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:22\n\nMempool stands, it's a shortened form of memory pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:25\n\nIt is just the pool of unconfirmed transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:31\n\nSo, If you're a miner, you would obviously keep a mempool where you receive transactions from the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:37\n\nYou sort them roughly by fee rate and you put the ones that are most profitable for you to mine in your blocks, et cetera.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\n\nAnd then, if you are a non-mining node operator, you kind of collect these transactions as they're being broadcast and you serve as part of the kind of broad transaction relay network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:55\n\nBut it's also a way for you to basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\n\nSlowly validate and download transactions before blocks, before they actually become part of the blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:04\n\nSo, if you think about a long time ago, I'd hear these stories of people, they're like on a video chat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\n\nAnd you can tell who is running a Bitcoin node because roughly every 10 minutes on the video chat, their video goes really blurry because the block is being downloaded.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nAnd maybe their CPU is spending time verifying the signatures, which is very expensive computationally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:28\n\nSo to kind of avoid that, you're able to amortize it ahead of time, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:34\n\nThat's a really good reason for you to do transaction relay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:38\n\nIt also means that you have a pretty, I don't like the word trustless, but trustless fee estimation technique because you see transactions come out ahead of time and you can see roughly like when they confirm, you're able to get a better estimate of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:56\n\nLike what fee rates are going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:58\n\nThis is what I mean by trustless here is like it's not as gameable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:01\n\nSo if you only relied on block data, you'd only be looking at fees that like miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:08\n\nYou're looking at data that's controlled by the miners, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\n\nAnd so, like, you know, they can have their own zero fee transactions in there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\n\nThey can kind of stuff their blocks with artificially high fee rate transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:19\n\nLike, that's what I mean by gameable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:20\n\nAnd similarly, if you're relying on a trusted third party to tell you what's in mempool, like, what are the transactions that you're bidding up against?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\n\nYou're also kind of trusting that those are real transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:34\n\nSo, using fee estimation with your own transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:36\n\nTransaction relay node is very useful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nAnd of course, if you have your own wallet, you're receiving or sending transactions, seeing that come to your mempool is like a pretty good sign that it has been propagated across the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:50\n\nSo that's my very long-winded explanation for what mempool is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:56\n\nYeah, and so the way I kind of conceptualize the mempool is there's like these multiple functions that it fulfills.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:05\n\nLike, you know, obviously the fee estimation, as you said, the ability to kind of protect your node from denial of service, like that's a functionality the mempool has to.\num fulfill.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:18\n\nBut also I I think like the most critical one is response for second layer protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:25\n\nLike I obviously, they're they're all designed so that you can respond to things in a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:30\n\nBut being able to see ahead of time what's going on, react to it out of band in the mempool faster, and just kind of have the view of that landscape from the context of I am engaged in this layer two protocol, and I actually need for the security of my money to know things like the fee rate, like, was this transaction broadcast?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:53\n\nDo I have to react to this now?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:56\n\nAnd, like, that I think is the most critical reason why it's so important to maintain a public mumpool, because we are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:05\n\nPretty much betting everything on Bitcoin's long-term success in layering out these second-layer protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:12\n\nAnd if we don't have that public mempool, if we kind of devolve back to these centralized APIs or just trusting what miners put in blocks, like the entire landscape of those security assumptions just chips out from under us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:27\n\nYeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nLike the talk, Nahal's talk that we were just at before this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:32\n\nWhere she's talking about L2s and scaling in general.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nShe's like, all of the, like, what makes L2s L2s is this idea of unilateral exit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:42\n\nAnd we can no longer treat it as just given that, like, oh, as long as a user is able to get the transaction confirmed, then they can unilaterally exit and get their money even if they're being attacked.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:53\n\nIt's like, well, the part where they're able to get their transaction confirmed, like you said, relies on.\nlike being able to get your transaction to miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:04\n\nSometimes in competition with your counterparties transaction, where the spending path goes to them instead of you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nAnd usually, in like a really constrained time scale, there's some kind of time lock where after this point they can take the money, or before this point, you have to respond.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:21\n\nAnd I think in the past few years, we've seen very wildly fluctuating fee markets.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:28\n\nYeah, so I think it is a really, I've been saying this for a long time, it's a very important part.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:33\n\nPart that is underrated in like L2 security models.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:38\n\nYeah, so I've spent a lot of time on pinning attacks, which is like I just alluded to, where the counterparty deliberately tries to take advantage of memfool limitations because of kind of Poor heuristics or bad logic or bad algorithms for how we assess transactions and at least Bitcoin Core's mempool logic to kind of stop it from propagating or stop it from being mined.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nAnd so, like, a lot of the work that, like, packet, for example, package relay or like the truck things and even cluster mempool are about optimizing how we assess the incentive compatibility of transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:18\n\nLike, how do we tell what the fee rate is, basically?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nThis is within the context of all of the transactions it depends on, or it may or may not replace.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:28\n\nAnd there's lots of places where this breaks down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:32\n\nAnd it's like, if you run, for example, Suhas did research on all the 2023 mempool transactions, and if you do it through Cluster Mempool and those logic, the Very few transactions are impacted, and the fee difference is like less than 1%.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:52\n\nAnd so people will be like, well, what's the point of this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nFirst of all, as we said in layer twos, this can be a security concern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:01\n\nAnd also, it is really important as the subsidy decreases that these fee optimization kind of things are publicly available and are applied in the public decentralized market.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:15\n\nBecause the reason why centralized marketplaces might exist, like miners essentially being like, hey, you can pay me a little extra for me to mine your transaction, is if the public one is inefficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:31\n\nAnd you can totally understand why a centralized marketplace would be more efficient than a decentralized one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:37\n\nBut if there is room for a private miner to spend some extra money on a researcher or an engineer to optimize their mining template or do some kind of meevil kind of stuff, that's really bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:52\n\nIt creates centralization pressure and is ultimately very toxic for the Bitcoin network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:59\n\nI mean, it's really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nA kind of an existential issue in my mind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:04\n\nAnd I feel like the whole Ordinal saga, you know, over the last year and a half or so is kind of the perfect demonstration of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nYou know, this use case, if you want to be charitable and call it that, that was very unpopular with a lot of people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nYou know, it had real economic demand for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nAnd a lot of the transaction structures that they wanted to engage in, you know, for the larger inscriptions and silly things they were doing, it violated mempool relay policy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nSo, like, that kind of demonstrated this is a real concern of economic incentives driving users to go directly to minor submission and bypass the public mempool just because, like, they can make money if they do so.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:54\n\nSo like, you know, w what what do you think about how to kind of approach that in the long term and balancing uh policy restrictions versus like these use cases that you know we can be pretty sure most of them will be unpopular, but do have real economic demand behind them?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nWith all the original stuff, I was like really regretting being the mempool maintainer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:19\n\nPeople would yell at me where they're like, you need to add policies to ban inscriptions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:24\n\nAnd then there would be people who were saying, you need to relax the policies that like create these incentives for people to submit non-standard transactions to miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:35\n\nAnd like my kind of generic response.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nWas always like the policy rules exist for like pretty specific technical reasons.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:44\n\nLike we need to be DOS resistant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:46\n\nWe can't just relax like SIGOP related rules or like the max standard size rule.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:51\n\nLike they exist for really good reasons for to make it accessible to run a node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:58\n\nAnd but I agree with people that it should be minimal, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:02\n\nBecause you don't want to create these incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nBut like I have a blog post, but like reasons like preserving upgrade hooks, like the banning of 64 byte transactions, which really should be a consensus rule.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:18\n\nShout out to the consensus cleanup.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:22\n\nLike they're real con security concerns.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:24\n\nSo it doesn't really make sense.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nLike, weighing that against, like, but somebody might want it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:29\n\nI think it makes sense to keep policy rules like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:32\n\nBut I do generally agree that, like, it shouldn't be so cumbersome for bad reasons that when people have good use cases, they're forced to go through centralized pools.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:45\n\nI think I fully agree with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nAnd then, like, the other thing that I would say.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:51\n\nThat I was saying to people is like policy is for like when we're assessing if a transaction is bad, it's like network resources.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:02\n\nLike, for example, we don't like dust because that presents a long-term cost for every single node runner to have lots of tiny UTXOs in the chain state.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:13\n\nIt's not assessing good or bad on the basis of use case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:17\n\nAnd I don't think it's like protocol development's place to be like this kind of transaction is bad based on use case, 'cause that's kind of like against what Bitcoin is all about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:27\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:28\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:28\n\nIf you're if you're making those decisions, then that's not a very open access censorship resistant network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:34\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:35\n\nBut I understand like, you know, there were so many arguments where I was like, yeah, it's a it's a hard topic to talk about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:41\n\nThat's my two cents.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nYeah, I don't envy uh your position.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:48\n\nYou you wanna talk a little bit about um V three truck transactions and package relay?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:53\n\nUm, you know, the I think the work you and uh Greg have done on that is massively important for layer twos.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:00\n\nLike that's A whole class of problems and security concerns that is now very constrained in a nice, easy to reason about box.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nYeah, thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:11\n\nYeah, so I think the way that I approach this is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:16\n\nLike from a very problem-solving kind of perspective.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nAnd I picked up Package Release maybe like less than a year into working on core, which then it was like the biggest can of worms ever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:28\n\nHe's like, oh, like, just, you know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:31\n\nSend multiple transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:33\n\nAnd so, okay, so I picked it because it seemed to be like a completely uncontroversial, like pre-concept act project that everybody seemed to agree, like, we have to do package-really at some point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:45\n\nSo, I didn't, I think that's good for beginners because, like, as a beginner, you don't really know what's a good idea, but like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:54\n\nIf there's work to be done and code to be written, and tests to be written, of course, you can do that as a beginner.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:00\n\nThey can ramp up to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:02\n\nBut it's really bad to start with an idea that requires you to argue with people, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:09\n\nSo, yeah, the general idea is kind of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:13\n\nWhat I summarized earlier that I realized package relay is really about is not just sending multiple transactions, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nIt's about fee bumping inefficiency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:24\n\nSo, like, if you're the classic problem is once minimum fee rates rise, you can't CPFP your transaction because it needs to get in by itself and then be CPFP'd.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:35\n\nSo, that's kind of the whole point of package relay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:37\n\nBut, really, this general problem of like you can't bid for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:42\n\nBlock space because the mempool logic is not smart enough to understand that your package is actually really high fee rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:51\n\nIt's just for this technical limitation reason that it isn't able to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:56\n\nAnd so we discovered lots of limitations in this category where it's like, oh, sometimes you can't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:03\n\nRBF your transaction because the RBF logic only looks at this and blah, blah, blah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:08\n\nAnd we discovered it's like a really hard problem because transactions can have dependencies, packages can be all kinds of topologies that make it really, really difficult to assess.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:19\n\nAnd so that's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:21\n\nPart of, I would say this is part of the reason why Cluster Mempool came about, where Cluster Mempool is like the complete solution to generic, all to political, not alt, but like kind of more general, better fee rate assessment, essentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nAnd it requires basically a total rewrite of mempool and how we track transactions, but it's so worth it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:49\n\nAnd so that's kind of in the works, and then general package relay will come later.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:53\n\nBut we've added like a limited version where it should be enough for all basic L2s, or at least L2s get.\nkind of all the features that you want, or most of the features that you want in the topology that is minimal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:15\n\nWhere it's basically like truck stands for topologically restricted until confirmation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nIt's basically, we can do all of this perfectly if you only have one parent and one child, because we don't hit all of the cases where this gets more complex.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nSo we've added the logic to do that and it was comparatively way simpler.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:33\n\nIt's still a lot of code and it took a long time, but it's comparatively simpler.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:37\n\nAnd then, so like, there's actually like usage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nLike, I met people who are like, hey, I'm using Truck.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:43\n\nLike, can I do this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:44\n\nCan I do that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:44\n\nAnd, like, whoa.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:45\n\nAnd they're like, yeah, like, the Bitcoin Core wallet doesn't support it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:48\n\nAnd I was like, oh, yeah, we didn't even get to that yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:50\n\nWhy are you like, you're already using it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:53\n\nSo it's like way more popular than I expected, which is amazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:56\n\nIt's a great thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:57\n\nLike, you know, Lightning has their bolt proposals and stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:00\n\nI'm like, wow, this has happened really quickly.\nwhile we work on the more general solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:07\n\nAnd Peter and Suhas, like, they're the ones who were like, okay, let's like, we killed Batman.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:16\n\nLike Cluster Memple is such a hard problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:19\n\nAnd like the reason why I went after Truck was I was like, Okay, it's a really hard problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:24\n\nWe can avoid it by just having simple topologies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:29\n\nAnd honestly, if it works for 90% of use cases and it's way simpler compared to Cluster Mempool, which I was like, I cannot imagine working on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:41\n\nI thought it was a really good idea, but very, very luckily, they have taken on the Herculean effect.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:49\n\nOf completely writing that, rewriting them pool to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:52\n\nI mean, it's a totally logical path to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:55\n\nLike, if you can get 90% of the way there with 10% of the work and then kind of let the other wizards handle.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:05\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:06\n\nI mean, yeah, like it takes a village to do everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:10\n\nLike, it's, yeah, I think it's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nLucky, and I think a sign that we're doing something important, that there are people who have kind of rallied behind this general problem of I call it like efficiency of the transaction relay, efficiency of the block space marketplace, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:35\n\nAnd I guess you wanna kind of, at least at a high level, just break down like where.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:42\n\nKind of truck and minimal package relay jumps to cluster mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:47\n\nLike, how does that functionality kind of build on what you've done and how does that function in general?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nSo, from a user's perspective, what you can now do is You can have a zero fee transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:02\n\nYou put version three on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:05\n\nAnd as long as you have a child that is fee bumping it, it basically kind of gets to be treated as one transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:15\n\nWe have full package support, basically, for this very basic topology, so much so that the parent can be zero fee.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:25\n\nIt can even have an anchor, sorry, an output.\nthat is spent by the child that has zero value in it, which is awesome because it means that contracting protocols can pre-sign transactions with their counterparties, not really need to negotiate what those fees are gonna be, because you can add them later.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:44\n\nYou can have just one anchor, which is a keyless that either of you can spend instead of having two.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:50\n\nAnd it can have zero value in it, so you don't need to like shave off channel balance into these outputs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nSo, if you were to have like an L and Symmetry kind of style thing where you can have a lot of update transactions, you aren't like shaving off outputs at each step.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:05\n\nThat's really powerful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:07\n\nAnd this is not just for Lightning, any kind of shared UTXO thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:15\n\nCoin joins, for example, which are not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:18\n\nNot lightning, where you have a lot of different participants in the transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:24\n\nYou can just have one anchor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:26\n\nSo, if anybody wants to bump it later on, you're not all kind of stuck competing with each other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:31\n\nYou can just replace that anchor and anybody can spend it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:34\n\nAnd that's awesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:35\n\nAnd you can submit that to your node, it will propagate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:42\n\nMostly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nIt will propagate in the happy case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:46\n\nSo I've tried to be really, really loud about this: that it's not reliable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:50\n\nIt uses kind of an opportunistic part of the transaction relay logic, essentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:58\n\nBut this meant we could do it without a protocol change.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:01\n\nWe were able to do it without.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:03\n\nEven changing the amount of bandwidth that would be used for relaying these transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:08\n\nSo these are all kind of really minimally invasive and just magical kind of things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:17\n\nThese packages, one parent, one child packages, can also replace each other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:21\n\nSo like commitment transactions where you have two for Lightning.\nlike the parent, which is the commitment transaction and its child, can replace another commitment transaction and its child, even though the two parents spend the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:36\n\nThat was kind of complicated to figure out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nI'm just saying.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:40\n\nBut we have that functionality.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:42\n\nSo, because we're able to do that, you don't need two anchors and you don't need CPFP carve-out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:47\n\nAnd CPFP carve-out was like the last thing that we had to figure out how to remove in order to do cluster mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:54\n\nAnd so, kind of, they have this symbiotic relationship, essentially, this like truck package relay thing, where cluster mempool is enabled by the fact that This 1p1c package relay stuff negates the need to create things that require CPFP carve-out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:12\n\nSo we can have cluster mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:14\n\nAnd then cluster mempool makes the mempool logic so, so, so much better so that we can actually build more generic package relay on top of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:23\n\nSo they all kind of mesh really, really nicely together.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:28\n\nAnd together they solve this like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:31\n\nReally important class of problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:33\n\nI think it was Francis Pouille who said the mempool is a war zone.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:42\n\nYeah, I think I gave a talk at BTC like two, three years ago called Mempool as a Battleground, and it was about pinning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:51\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:52\n\nI don't know exactly what war zone that is referring to, but yes, in many ways, it's a contentious place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:00\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:01\n\nSo it's really like a kind of a whole roadmap.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:05\n\nWe're like halfway through right now, pretty much.\nYeah.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:09\n\nIt's exciting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:10\n\nExciting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:11\n\nAnd then we can work on some other things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:13\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:13\n\nI'm biting my tongue to not make the joke.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:17\n\nWhat joke?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:19\n\nOne next soft work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:27\n\nWell, so like I felt like this was more so I think there's maybe the elephant that people say in the room is like a lot of people in Bitcoin Core seem to work on things that are not soft forks, even though a lot of the community says this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:46\n\nWhen Nextsoft work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:49\n\nAnd hopefully, based on what I just said, it kind of makes sense why I would focus on this particular thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:58\n\nThere are a lot of interesting and important problems within Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:03\n\nA lot of times when I was looking at, I think there's this fault paper or like a lot of the Covenant kind of things that were about L2 scaling.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:13\n\nI was like, well, but what about like the unilateral exit problem where you could get pinned and not be able to confirm your transactions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nLike all of those design spaces depend on the problem that you and Greg fixed being fixed to be viable ideas in the first place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:31\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:32\n\nSo it's like, yeah, we all care about scaling.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:35\n\nLike we're on the same team.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nWe're just Focused on different parts of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:42\n\nAnd, you know, not to say that the fee bumping problem is the most important part, but I felt like it was really under served in terms of development attention.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:56\n\nSo, yeah, I think me personally not working on the covenant stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:03\n\nI try to follow along.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:05\n\nBut like, it's it's not because I don't care about scaling, it's because you know, working on a different different part of the elephant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:13\n\nWell, I mean, it's you know, work on the mempool is just as important as the consensus layer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:17\n\nLike, this this entire this entire like protocol stack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:26\n\nHas to scale, and you can't just focus on one area of it and you know, make changes or alterations and then not take into account how that's going to alter the functionality of something else that you left alone.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:40\n\nLike, it's a dynamic system when you start changing pieces of it, that's going to have consequences that ripple over to other places.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:47\n\nYeah, and you really do have to step back and kind of take a holistic view and approach.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:52\n\nOr you're going to wind up breaking things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:55\n\nExactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:56\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:57\n\nWe'll all get there together.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:00\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:01\n\nAnd like, I don't speak on behalf of anyone else in core, but like, there are a lot of other things that other people work on that are not softworks that are also really important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:11\n\nI mean, the kernel project, like, just so that we can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:16\n\nPretty much pull the consensus logic out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:18\n\nAnd if people think that what you and Greg did is not the best way to handle things, they can go off and do something else, but still have like a stable consensus library they know will stay in sync with core and break and fork off onto its own chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:35\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:36\n\nAnd I think so, so many people in core being so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:42\n\nSupportive of kernel, both in their time and like in their words, is also obvious evidence that the people that work on core also aren't trying to be the incumbent, like only consensus implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:59\n\nBecause the whole point, or one of the reasons for kernel that I see, is like it would be great for there to be not.\n98, 99% Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:10\n\nIt's very stressful for the whole network to need for this, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:17\n\nBut like, at the same time, what I think is probably what is more important than having diversity of clients is that all the clients have the same consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:27\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:28\n\nStaying a consensus is pretty important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:30\n\nKind of the key thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nAnd I think we've learned over and over again that like trying to re-implement consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:36\n\nFrom scratch just does not work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:40\n\nYeah, I kind of feel at this point like consensus bugs with BTCD is like Kenny getting killed in South Park.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:51\n\nBecause it's so hard, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:53\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:54\n\nI have a call at Brink.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:57\n\nI worked a lot with Nicholas, who does fuzzing and differential fuzzing of different.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:02\n\nImplementations, and yeah, it's like it's like it's not trying to dunk on these people for writing alternative clients, it's just really, really hard, and it's you know, fuzzing just finds consensus bugs left and right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:17\n\nWell, I guess you have any other things you wanted to touch on or any final comments you wanted to make?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:23\n\nYeah, so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:25\n\nI'm gonna shill test.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:27\n\nWell, I don't know when this is coming out, but test the 29 release candidates, please.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:32\n\nIn general, please test release candidates.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:35\n\nAnd in general, try to at least.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:38\n\nUpgrade to minor releases because they have security fixes and patches.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:43\n\nWe talked a lot about like 1p1c and truck and soon to come ephemeral dust.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:49\n\nThose things only work on the network if there are nodes running them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:53\n\nAnd as far as I know, like don't quote me on this, but it's been six months since 28 came out, and I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:00\n\nOnly one miner is actually mining v3 transactions, something like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:05\n\nSounds about right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:06\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:07\n\nSo, I mean, it's not, I'm not trying to like force people to run different nodes, but you know, at least keep up with the miner release updates because they have security fixes in them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:19\n\nAnd I guess, you know, before we sign off, I'd like to apologize on behalf of all of the retards in this space for the stupid shit you have to put up with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:29\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:30\n\nI appreciate it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:31\n\nThank you for sitting down with me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:33\n\nThanks for having me.	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\nHello everybody, I am Shinobi from Bitcoin Magazine and I'm joined here by Gloria Zhao, Bitcoin Core maintainer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:08\nYes, that's me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:10\nSo we are here today to talk a little bit about mempools, one of the most chaotic, messy pieces of the Bitcoin protocol, which is your area of focus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:23\nSo I guess to start, um you wanna kinda just like at the very high level like explain like how it functions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:30\nLike Yeah, I think Mempool is sometimes underrated uh as A crucial part of Node software that people don't like to think about very much or kind of trivialize because it's not consensus, but it is kind of a really big part of things to consider when you're creating transactions and when you're building L2s.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:51\nAnd also, simultaneously, I think, over-dramatized sometimes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:56\nBut the way that I like to say it is: Mempool is just a cache.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:00\nWe have this distributed network which allows us to have censorship-resistant payments, which is really awesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:09\nAnd without that, we wouldn't have censorship-resistant payments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\nBut so, this distributed network that serves as a kind of obfuscated place for you to broadcast your transactions in a private/slash censorship resistant way is an emergent property of individual nodes participating in transaction relay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:36\nAnd my talk yesterday was about how difficult that is because the individual node software has to be really accessible to run, like on a laptop or on a Raspberry Pi, but what transaction relay is, is you're exposing yourself to random peers on the network that are going to send you lots of data that you're going to validate, you're going to spend resources like memory and bandwidth and CPU on, but ultimately might not be very useful to you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:02\nAnd unless you're a miner, like you don't get any compensation for this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:06\nAnd so, like, that kind of software engineering is really difficult.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:09\nLike, you have a lot of security problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:13\nBut it is what gives us this wonderful payments network that is censorship resistant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:20\nSo, okay, your question was: what is mempool?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:22\nMempool stands, it's a shortened form of memory pool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:25\nIt is just the pool of unconfirmed transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:31\nSo, If you're a miner, you would obviously keep a mempool where you receive transactions from the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:37\nYou sort them roughly by fee rate and you put the ones that are most profitable for you to mine in your blocks, et cetera.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:44\nAnd then, if you are a non-mining node operator, you kind of collect these transactions as they're being broadcast and you serve as part of the kind of broad transaction relay network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:55\nBut it's also a way for you to basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:58\nSlowly validate and download transactions before blocks, before they actually become part of the blockchain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:04\nSo, if you think about a long time ago, I'd hear these stories of people, they're like on a video chat.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:11\nAnd you can tell who is running a Bitcoin node because roughly every 10 minutes on the video chat, their video goes really blurry because the block is being downloaded.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\nAnd maybe their CPU is spending time verifying the signatures, which is very expensive computationally.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:28\nSo to kind of avoid that, you're able to amortize it ahead of time, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:34\nThat's a really good reason for you to do transaction relay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:38\nIt also means that you have a pretty, I don't like the word trustless, but trustless fee estimation technique because you see transactions come out ahead of time and you can see roughly like when they confirm, you're able to get a better estimate of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:56\nLike what fee rates are going.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:58\nThis is what I mean by trustless here is like it's not as gameable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:01\nSo if you only relied on block data, you'd only be looking at fees that like miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:08\nYou're looking at data that's controlled by the miners, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\nAnd so, like, you know, they can have their own zero fee transactions in there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:14\nThey can kind of stuff their blocks with artificially high fee rate transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:19\nLike, that's what I mean by gameable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:20\nAnd similarly, if you're relying on a trusted third party to tell you what's in mempool, like, what are the transactions that you're bidding up against?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:31\nYou're also kind of trusting that those are real transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:34\n\nSo, using fee estimation with your own transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:36\n\nTransaction relay node is very useful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:38\n\nAnd of course, if you have your own wallet, you're receiving or sending transactions, seeing that come to your mempool is like a pretty good sign that it has been propagated across the network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:50\n\nSo that's my very long-winded explanation for what the mempool is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:56\n\nYeah, and so the way I kind of conceptualize the mempool is there's like these multiple functions that it fulfills.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:05\n\nLike, you know, obviously the fee estimation, as you said, the ability to kind of protect your node from denial of service, like that's a functionality the mempool has to fulfill.\num fulfill.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:18\n\nBut also I think like the most critical one is response for second layer protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:25\n\nLike I obviously, they're they're all designed so that you can respond to things in a block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:30\n\nBut being able to see ahead of time what's going on, react to it out of band in the mempool faster, and just kind of have the view of that landscape from the context of I am engaged in this layer two protocol, and I actually need for the security of my money to know things like the fee rate, like, was this transaction broadcast?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:53\n\nDo I have to react to this now?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:56\n\nAnd, like, that I think is the most critical reason why it's so important to maintain a public mempool, because we are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:05\n\nPretty much betting everything on Bitcoin's long-term success in layering out these second-layer protocols.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:12\n\nAnd if we don't have that public mempool, if we kind of devolve back to these centralized APIs or just trusting what miners put in blocks, like the entire landscape of those security assumptions just chips out from under us.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:27\n\nYeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:28\n\nLike the talk, Nahal's talk that we were just at before this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:32\n\nWhere she's talking about L2s and scaling in general.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nShe's like, all of the, like, what makes L2s L2s is this idea of unilateral exit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:42\n\nAnd we can no longer treat it as just given that, like, oh, as long as a user is able to get the transaction confirmed, then they can unilaterally exit and get their money even if they're being attacked.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:53\n\nIt's like, well, the part where they're able to get their transaction confirmed, like you said, relies on\nlike being able to get your transaction to miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:04\n\nSometimes in competition with your counterparties transaction, where the spending path goes to them instead of you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:12\n\nAnd usually, in like a really constrained time scale, there's some kind of time lock where after this point they can take the money, or before this point, you have to respond.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:21\n\nAnd I think in the past few years, we've seen very wildly fluctuating fee markets.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:28\n\nYeah, so I think it is a really, I've been saying this for a long time, it's a very important part.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:33\n\nPart that is underrated in like L2 security models.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:38\n\nYeah, so I've spent a lot of time on pinning attacks, which is like I just alluded to, where the counterparty deliberately tries to take advantage of mempool limitations because of kind of poor heuristics or bad logic or bad algorithms for how we assess transactions and at least Bitcoin Core's mempool logic to kind of stop it from propagating or stop it from being mined.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:05\n\nAnd so, like, a lot of the work that, like, Packet, for example, package relay or like the truck things and even Cluster Mempool are about optimizing how we assess the incentive compatibility of transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:18\n\nLike, how do we tell what the fee rate is, basically?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:21\n\nThis is within the context of all of the transactions it depends on, or it may or may not replace.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:28\n\nAnd there's lots of places where this breaks down.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:32\n\nAnd it's like, if you run, for example, Suhas did research on all the 2023 mempool transactions, and if you do it through Cluster Mempool and those logic, the Very few transactions are impacted, and the fee difference is like less than 1%.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:52\n\nAnd so people will be like, well, what's the point of this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:56\n\nFirst of all, as we said in layer twos, this can be a security concern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:01\n\nAnd also, it is really important as the subsidy decreases that these fee optimization kind of things are publicly available and are applied in the public decentralized market.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:15\n\nBecause the reason why centralized marketplaces might exist, like miners essentially being like, hey, you can pay me a little extra for me to mine your transaction, is if the public one is inefficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:31\n\nAnd you can totally understand why a centralized marketplace would be more efficient than a decentralized one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:37\n\nBut if there is room for a private miner to spend some extra money on a researcher or an engineer to optimize their mining template or do some kind of MEV kind of stuff, that's really bad.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:52\n\nIt creates centralization pressure and is ultimately very toxic for the Bitcoin network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:58\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:59\n\nI mean, it's really.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nA kind of an existential issue in my mind.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:04\n\nAnd I feel like the whole Ordinals saga, you know, over the last year and a half or so is kind of the perfect demonstration of that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:13\n\nYou know, this use case, if you want to be charitable and call it that, that was very unpopular with a lot of people.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:22\n\nYou know, it had real economic demand for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:25\n\nAnd a lot of the transaction structures that they wanted to engage in, you know, for the larger inscriptions and silly things they were doing, it violated mempool relay policy.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:37\n\nSo, like, that kind of demonstrated this is a real concern of economic incentives driving users to go directly to miner submission and bypass the public mempool just because, like, they can make money if they do so.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:54\n\nSo like, you know, what what do you think about how to kind of approach that in the long term and balancing uh policy restrictions versus like these use cases that you know we can be pretty sure most of them will be unpopular, but do have real economic demand behind them?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:13\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:14\n\nWith all the original stuff, I was like really regretting being the mempool maintainer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:19\n\nPeople would yell at me where they're like, you need to add policies to ban inscriptions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:24\n\nAnd then there would be people who were saying, you need to relax the policies that like create these incentives for people to submit non-standard transactions to miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:35\n\nAnd like my kind of generic response.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:38\n\nWas always like the policy rules exist for like pretty specific technical reasons.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:44\n\nLike we need to be DoS resistant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:46\n\nWe can't just relax like SIGOP related rules or like the max standard size rule.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:51\n\nLike they exist for really good reasons for to make it accessible to run a node.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:58\n\nAnd but I agree with people that it should be minimal, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:02\n\nBecause you don't want to create these incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:05\n\nBut like I have a blog post about reasons like preserving upgrade hooks, like the banning of 64 byte transactions, which really should be a consensus rule.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:18\n\nShout out to the consensus cleanup.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:22\n\nLike they're real consensus security concerns.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:24\n\nSo it doesn't really make sense.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:26\n\nLike, weighing that against, like, but somebody might want it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:29\n\nI think it makes sense to keep policy rules like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:32\n\nBut I do generally agree that, like, it shouldn't be so cumbersome for bad reasons that when people have good use cases, they're forced to go through centralized pools.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:45\n\nI think I fully agree with that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:48\n\nAnd then, like, the other thing that I would say.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:51\n\nThat I was saying to people is like policy is for like when we're assessing if a transaction is bad, it's like network resources.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:02\n\nLike, for example, we don't like dust because that presents a long-term cost for every single node runner to have lots of tiny UTXOs in the chain state.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:13\n\nIt's not assessing good or bad on the basis of use case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:17\n\nAnd I don't think it's like protocol development's place to be like this kind of transaction is bad based on use case, 'cause that's kind of like against what Bitcoin is all about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:27\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:28\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:28\n\nIf you're if you're making those decisions, then that's not a very open access censorship resistant network.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:34\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:35\n\nBut I understand like, you know, there were so many arguments where I was like, yeah, it's a it's a hard topic to talk about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:41\n\nThat's my two cents.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nYeah, I don't envy uh your position.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:48\n\nYou wanna talk a little bit about um BIP versioned transactions and package relay?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:53\n\nUm, you know, the I think the work you and uh Greg have done on that is massively important for layer twos.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:00\n\nLike that's A whole class of problems and security concerns that is now very constrained in a nice, easy to reason about box.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:09\n\nYeah, thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:11\n\nYeah, so I think the way that I approach this is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:16\n\nLike from a very problem-solving kind of perspective.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:19\n\nAnd I picked up Package Release maybe like less than a year into working on core, which then it was like the biggest can of worms ever.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:28\n\nHe's like, oh, like, just, you know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:31\n\nSend multiple transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:33\n\nAnd so, okay, so I picked it because it seemed to be like a completely uncontroversial, like pre-concept act project that everybody seemed to agree, like, we have to do Package Relay at some point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:45\n\nSo, I didn't, I think that's good for beginners because, like, as a beginner, you don't really know what's a good idea, but like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:54\n\nIf there's work to be done and code to be written, and tests to be written, of course, you can do that as a beginner.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:00\n\nThey can ramp up to do that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:02\n\nBut it's really bad to start with an idea that requires you to argue with people, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:09\n\nSo, yeah, the general idea is kind of.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:13\n\nWhat I summarized earlier that I realized Package Relay is really about is not just sending multiple transactions, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:20\n\nIt's about fee bumping inefficiency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:24\n\nSo, like, if you're the classic problem is once minimum fee rates rise, you can't CPFP your transaction because it needs to get in by itself and then be CPFP'd.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:35\n\nSo, that's kind of the whole point of Package Relay.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:37\n\nBut, really, this general problem of like you can't bid for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:42\n\nBlock space because the mempool logic is not smart enough to understand that your package is actually really high fee rate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:51\n\nIt's just for this technical limitation reason that it isn't able to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:56\n\nAnd so we discovered lots of limitations in this category where it's like, oh, sometimes you can't.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:03\n\nRBF your transaction because the RBF logic only looks at this and blah, blah, blah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:08\n\nAnd we discovered it's like a really hard problem because transactions can have dependencies, packages can be all kinds of topologies that make it really, really difficult to assess.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:19\n\nAnd so that's.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:21\n\nPart of, I would say this is part of the reason why Cluster Mempool came about, where Cluster Mempool is like the complete solution to generic, all to political, not alt, but like kind of more general, better fee rate assessment, essentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:41\n\nAnd it requires basically a total rewrite of mempool and how we track transactions, but it's so worth it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:49\n\nAnd so that's kind of in the works, and then general Package Relay will come later.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:53\n\nBut we've added like a limited version where it should be enough for all basic L2s, or at least L2s get.\nkind of all the features that you want, or most of the features that you want in the topology that is minimal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:15\n\nWhere it's basically like Truck stands for Topologically Restricted Until Confirmation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nIt's basically, we can do all of this perfectly if you only have one parent and one child, because we don't hit all of the cases where this gets more complex.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:29\n\nSo we've added the logic to do that and it was comparatively way simpler.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:33\n\nIt's still a lot of code and it took a long time, but it's comparatively simpler.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:37\n\nAnd then, so like, there's actually like usage.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:40\n\nLike, I met people who are like, hey, I'm using Truck.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:43\n\nLike, can I do this?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:44\n\nCan I do that?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:44\n\nAnd, like, whoa.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:45\n\nAnd they're like, yeah, like, the Bitcoin Core wallet doesn't support it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:48\n\nAnd I was like, oh, yeah, we didn't even get to that yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:50\n\nWhy are you like, you're already using it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:53\n\nSo it's like way more popular than I expected, which is amazing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:56\n\nIt's a great thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:57\n\nLike, you know, Lightning has their bolt proposals and stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:00\n\nI'm like, wow, this has happened really quickly.\nwhile we work on the more general solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:07\n\nAnd Peter and Suhas, like, they're the ones who were like, okay, let's like, we killed Batman.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:16\n\nLike Cluster Mempool is such a hard problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:19\n\nAnd like the reason why I went after Trust was I was like, Okay, it's a really hard problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:24\n\nWe can avoid it by just having simple topologies.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:29\n\nAnd honestly, if it works for 90% of use cases and it's way simpler compared to Cluster Mempool, which I was like, I cannot imagine working on this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:41\n\nI thought it was a really good idea, but very, very luckily, they have taken on the Herculean effort.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:49\n\nOf completely rewriting that, rewriting them pool to do this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:52\n\nI mean, it's a totally logical path to me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:55\n\nLike, if you can get 90% of the way there with 10% of the work and then kind of let the other wizards handle it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:05\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:06\n\nI mean, yeah, like it takes a village to do everything.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:10\n\nLike, it's, yeah, I think it is.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nLucky, and I think a sign that we're doing something important, that there are people who have kind of rallied behind this general problem of I call it like efficiency of the transaction relay, efficiency of the block space marketplace, basically.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:35\n\nAnd I guess you wanna kind of, at least at a high level, just break down like where.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:42\n\nKind of Trust and Minimal Package Relay jumps to Cluster Mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:47\n\nLike, how does that functionality kind of build on what you've done and how does that function in general?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:54\n\nSo, from a user's perspective, what you can now do is You can have a zero fee transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:02\n\nYou put version two on it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:05\n\nAnd as long as you have a child that is fee bumping it, it basically kind of gets to be treated as one transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:15\n\nWe have full package support, basically, for this very basic topology, so much so that the parent can be zero fee.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:25\n\nIt can even have an anchor, sorry, an output.\nthat is spent by the child that has zero value in it, which is awesome because it means that contracting protocols can pre-sign transactions with their counterparties, not really need to negotiate what those fees are gonna be, because you can add them later.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:44\n\nYou can have just one anchor, which is a keyspend that either of you can spend instead of having two.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:50\n\nAnd it can have zero value in it, so you don't need to like shave off channel balance into these outputs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:56\n\nSo, if you were to have like an LN and Symmetry kind of style thing where you can have a lot of update transactions, you aren't like shaving off outputs at each step.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:05\n\nThat's really powerful.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:07\n\nAnd this is not just for Lightning, any kind of shared UTXO thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:15\n\nCoin joins, for example, which are not.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:18\n\nNot Lightning, where you have a lot of different participants in the transaction.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:24\n\nYou can just have one anchor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:26\n\nSo, if anybody wants to bump it later on, you're not all kind of stuck competing with each other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:31\n\nYou can just replace that anchor and anybody can spend it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:34\n\nAnd that's awesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:35\n\nAnd you can submit that to your node, it will propagate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:42\n\nMostly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:44\n\nIt will propagate in the happy case.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:46\n\nSo I've tried to be really, really loud about this: that it's not reliable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:50\n\nIt uses kind of an opportunistic part of the transaction relay logic, essentially.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:58\n\nBut this meant we could do it without a protocol change.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:01\n\nWe were able to do it without.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:03\n\nEven changing the amount of bandwidth that would be used for relaying these transactions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:08\n\nSo these are all kind of really minimally invasive and just magical kind of things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:17\n\nThese packages, one parent, one child packages, can also replace each other.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:21\n\nSo like commitment transactions where you have two for Lightning.\nlike the parent, which is the commitment transaction and its child, can replace another commitment transaction and its child, even though the two parents spend the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:36\n\nThat was kind of complicated to figure out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nI'm just saying.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:40\n\nBut we have that functionality.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:42\n\nSo, because we're able to do that, you don't need two anchors and you don't need CPFP carve-out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:47\n\nAnd CPFP carve-out was like the last thing that we had to figure out how to remove in order to do Cluster Mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:54\n\nAnd so, kind of, they have this symbiotic relationship, essentially, this like package relay thing, where cluster mempool is enabled by the fact that this 1P1P package relay stuff negates the need to create things that require CPFP carve-out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:12\n\nSo we can have cluster mempool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:14\n\nAnd then cluster mempool makes the mempool logic so, so, so much better so that we can actually build more generic package relay on top of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:23\n\nSo they all kind of mesh really, really nicely together.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:28\n\nAnd together they solve this like.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:31\n\nReally important class of problems.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:33\n\nI think it was Francis Pouille who said the mempool is a war zone.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:42\n\nYeah, I think I gave a talk at BTC like two, three years ago called Mempool as a Battleground, and it was about pinning.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:51\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:52\n\nI don't know exactly what war zone that is referring to, but yes, in many ways, it's a contentious place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:00\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:01\n\nSo it's really like a kind of a whole roadmap.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:05\n\nWe're like halfway through right now, pretty much.\nYeah.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:09\n\nIt's exciting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:10\n\nExciting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:11\n\nAnd then we can work on some other things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:13\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:13\n\nI'm biting my tongue to not make the joke.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:17\n\nWhat joke?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:19\n\nOne next soft fork.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:27\n\nWell, so like I felt like this was more so I think there's maybe the elephant that people say in the room is like a lot of people in Bitcoin Core seem to work on things that are not soft forks, even though a lot of the community says this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:46\n\nWhen next soft fork.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:49\n\nAnd hopefully, based on what I just said, it kind of makes sense why I would focus on this particular thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:58\n\nThere are a lot of interesting and important problems within Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:03\n\nA lot of times when I was looking at, I think there's this Fault paper or like a lot of the Covenant kind of things that were about L2 scaling.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:13\n\nI was like, well, but what about like the unilateral exit problem where you could get pinned and not be able to confirm your transactions?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:22\n\nLike all of those design spaces depend on the problem that you and Greg fixed being fixed to be viable ideas in the first place.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:31\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:32\n\nSo it's like, yeah, we all care about scaling.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:35\n\nLike we're on the same team.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:37\n\nWe're just focused on different parts of it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:42\n\nAnd, you know, not to say that the fee bumping problem is the most important part, but I felt like it was really underserved in terms of development attention.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:56\n\nSo, yeah, I think me personally not working on the Covenant stuff.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:03\n\nI try to follow along.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:05\n\nBut like, it's it's not because I don't care about scaling, it's because you know, working on a different different part of the elephant.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:13\n\nWell, I mean, it's you know, work on the mempool is just as important as the consensus layer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:17\n\nLike, this this entire this entire like protocol stack.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:26\n\nHas to scale, and you can't just focus on one area of it and you know, make changes or alterations and then not take into account how that's going to alter the functionality of something else that you left alone.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:40\n\nLike, it's a dynamic system when you start changing pieces of it, that's going to have consequences that ripple over to other places.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:47\n\nYeah, and you really do have to step back and kind of take a holistic view and approach.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:52\n\nOr you're going to wind up breaking things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:55\n\nExactly.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:56\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:57\n\nWe'll all get there together.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:00\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:01\n\nAnd like, I don't speak on behalf of anyone else in core, but like, there are a lot of other things that other people work on that are not softforks that are also really important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:11\n\nI mean, the kernel project, like, just so that we can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:16\n\nPretty much pull the consensus logic out.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:18\n\nAnd if people think that what you and Greg did is not the best way to handle things, they can go off and do something else, but still have like a stable consensus library they know will stay in sync with core and break and fork off onto its own chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:35\n\nYeah, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:36\n\nAnd I think so, so many people in core being so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:42\n\nSupportive of kernel, both in their time and like in their words, is also obvious evidence that the people that work on core also aren't trying to be the incumbent, like only consensus implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:59\n\nBecause the whole point, or one of the reasons for kernel that I see, is like it would be great for there to be not.\n98, 99% Bitcoin Core.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:10\n\nIt's very stressful for the whole network to need for this, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:17\n\nBut like, at the same time, what I think is probably what is more important than having diversity of clients is that all the clients have the same consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:27\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:28\n\nStaying a consensus is pretty important.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:30\n\nKind of the key thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nAnd I think we've learned over and over again that like trying to re-implement consensus.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:36\n\nFrom scratch just does not work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:40\n\nYeah, I kind of feel at this point like consensus bugs with BTCD is like Kenny getting killed in South Park.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:51\n\nBecause it's so hard, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:53\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:54\n\nI have a call at BTCD.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:57\n\nI worked a lot with Nicolas, who does fuzzing and differential fuzzing of different.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:02\n\nImplementations, and yeah, it's like it's like it's not trying to dunk on these people for writing alternative clients, it's just really, really hard, and it's you know, fuzzing just finds consensus bugs left and right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:17\n\nWell, I guess you have any other things you wanted to touch on or any final comments you wanted to make?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:23\n\nYeah, so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:25\n\nI'm gonna shill test.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:27\n\nWell, I don't know when this is coming out, but test the 29 release candidates, please.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:32\n\nIn general, please test release candidates.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:35\n\nAnd in general, try to at least.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:38\n\nUpgrade to minor releases because they have security fixes and patches.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:43\n\nWe talked a lot about like 1P1C and Taproot and soon to come ephemeral dust.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:49\n\nThose things only work on the network if there are nodes running them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:53\n\nAnd as far as I know, like don't quote me on this, but it's been six months since 28 came out, and I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:00\n\nOnly one miner is actually mining v3 transactions, something like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:05\n\nSounds about right.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:06\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:07\n\nSo, I mean, it's not, I'm not trying to like force people to run different nodes, but you know, at least keep up with the miner release updates because they have security fixes in them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:19\n\nAnd I guess, you know, before we sign off, I'd like to apologize on behalf of all of the retard in this space for the stupid shit you have to put up with.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:29\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:30\n\nI appreciate it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:31\n\nThank you for sitting down with me.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:33\n\nThanks for having me.	The transcript argues that optimizing the Bitcoin mempool is as critical as consensus upgrades for long-term scalability, emphasizing its role as a censorship-resistant cache essential for transaction relay, trustless fee estimation, and Layer 2 security. A primary concern is maintaining unilateral exit in Layer 2 protocols; without a healthy public mempool, users face centralization risks when forced to rely on miner-controlled or centralized APIs. The discussion highlights key technical challenges such as mitigating pinning attacks and optimizing relay policies to prevent incentives for bypassing the network, particularly regarding direct miner submissions seen with Ordinals.\n\nTo address these issues, the speaker outlines a roadmap of non-consensus improvements moving from standard Package Relay limitations toward "Truck" (Topologically Restricted Until Confirmation) and eventually a Cluster Mempool. This strategy requires a total rewrite of mempool logic to assess complex transaction topologies, enabling zero-fee parents in fee-bumping bundles and reducing the need for CPFP carve-outs. The text asserts that such optimizations must be holistic, allowing future scaling solutions like L2s to function without requiring new soft forks.\n\nOn the consensus front, the dialogue prioritizes it over client diversity because re-implementing consensus is difficult and prone to bugs found through fuzzing. Consequently, there is a strong call for community engagement in testing release candidates (specifically the 29 series) and upgrading to minor releases to obtain necessary security patches. While not mandating specific nodes, miners are urged to run updated software supporting features like v3 transactions, Taproot, and ephemeral dust, as network failure occurs if these protocols lack sufficient node adoption. The transcript concludes with an apology from the Bitcoin Core team for the difficulties users face due to community inaction.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lW8r9hq8-yU	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:06:16.797456+05:30	2026-04-21 23:08:51.244623+05:30
04196117-9f33-431f-ab45-45dfe5af28a2	_TsVRZeyiAY	conference	2025-04-07	{"Hunter Beast"}	{}	{}	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nThanks everyone for coming.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:02\n\nSo first I just want to say that I'm doing my best as anyone can to have the intellectual honesty and also the curiousness that this subject deserves.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:17\n\nIt's a very complex subject.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nIt's also very provocative and Uh I just hope that people approach this with an open mind and uh in particular um just uh uh l appreciate uh some of the thought and research that goes into this that went into this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:40\n\nSo I want to talk about Bitcoin quantum security.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:43\n\nUm first I'm gonna start off with what is a quantum computer 'cause you know it's it's it's not Like, very obvious in some ways, like what they are when we talk about these things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:55\n\nWhat's pictured here is a superconducting quantum computer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:58\n\nSo it has a chip at the bottom that gets cooled to sort of like very close to zero Kelvin, so absolute zero, and uses helium, liquid nitrogen to cool itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nWith two coolants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:18\n\nAdditionally, it gets covered with this doer that then is called a dilution refrigerator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:31\n\nAnd because this thing gets so cold, they're actually like little loops in the wires that help with the Like w when metals uh contract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nUm so yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:45\n\nUh what does a quantum computer do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:48\n\nSo quantum computers have what are called fundamental units of quantum computing called qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:54\n\nAnd they uh the the they're they're they're uh they're essentially when you're able when you entangle multiple qubits together, you're able to essentially model Uh all the states of a problem in a mathematical construct called Hilbert space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:15\n\nAnd you can actually uh then connect these in circuits with gates that can perform various quantum computations uh and uh logical operations like controlled not gates or Hadamard gates, different kind of forms of quantum computation that explore this problem space in a probabilistic manner.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:45\n\nBut eventually it'll get more deterministic, actually, as they get better.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:50\n\nThe picture here is the IBM Quantum System 2.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:53\n\nThis is a commercially available piece of hardware.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:56\n\nIt's anyone's guess if it's any good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:01\n\nIt can do at least 100 qubits, maybe even closer to 1,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:07\n\nBut these are physical qubits, and I'll get into that in a minute.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:10\n\nBut that said, it's kind of pretty to look at, and also somewhat imposing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nUh how does quantum computing r pose a risk to Bitcoin?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:25\n\nUh well so essentially uh the major thing to consider is that uh there's uh there there's public private key cryptography used in Bitcoin, also known as asymmetric cryptography and uh this uh depends on A property called the elliptic curve discrete log problem, or ECDLP, which is something that Neha mentioned earlier and in an earlier talk to today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:56\n\nAnd there's kind of like this assumption that this is going to be secure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:01\n\nThere's also a lot of assumptions that go into all the things that go into making this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\n\nConcept of quantum computing a thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:13\n\nSo plenty of assumptions to go around, I suppose.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:19\n\nOne thing I want to address first off is that, yes, while technically quantum computing can affect mining through what's called Grover's algorithm, Grover's algorithm doesn't scale as well as this other algorithm I'm going to talk about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nSo it's just not.\nas a big a concern at the moment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:40\n\nOne of the bigger concerns is how trans how tr signatures work, in particular transaction signatures.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:47\n\nSo when you go and receive some coins to an address and then you want to spend them later, you have to sign the transaction and also reveal your public key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:00\n\nAnd there's an algorithm called Shors that we've known since 1995.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:06\n\nAnd well, it was originally explained in 1995.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:10\n\nThe thing that they discovered in 1995, Peter Shor explained, was that this, it was one of the first instances that had been theorized in that a quantum computer can demonstrate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:25\n\nWhat's called quantum advantage now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:30\n\nAnd also later, we discovered, or maybe around the 2000, 2001, it was explained to the cryptography community, or people just kind of found out or considered that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:45\n\nElliptic curve cryptography, which was a like burgeoning field at the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:50\n\nThe SEC P256K1 paper, for example, was written in 2000, or the specification was published in 2000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:59\n\nAnd that's the one that's used by Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:03\n\nAround 2000, 2001, it was also revealed that, or considered, that Quantum computers could pose a threat to elliptic curve cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:14\n\nAnd so essentially, elliptic curve cryptography as being the thing that gives us the concept of public keys, well, it makes this so that, in particular, whenever you expose your public keys, that makes coins vulnerable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:31\n\nSo, when are public keys exposed?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:33\n\nWell, we have four cases.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nThe first one is, Uh early dresses like the paid to public key coins, uh they start with zero four or technically also zero two or zero three.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:43\n\nUm they th these are like hex encoded uh public keys that are just sitting on chain uh for anyone to see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:54\n\nThese also are known as Satoshi's coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:56\n\nThey're also just any CPU miner operating at the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:01\n\nAnybody who hasn't spent their coins yet from those addresses, well, please do so now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:10\n\nReused addresses are also a concern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:14\n\nSo pretty much any type except for The one I'm I'm gonna talk about soon actually, um is uh that when uh when when you publish your transaction, the public key goes on chain uh along with your signature\n\nSpeaker 1: 00:07:30\n\nand that can be recovered and uh um\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:34\n\nuh go into that uh Shore's algorithm there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:38\n\nTapered addresses are also uh an entire public key, just encoded.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:43\n\nIn an address form.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:45\n\nIt's not a hash of an address or a hash of a cup key like many other addresses types are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:51\n\nIn particular, pay-to-witness public key hash is perfectly fine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:56\n\nAnd those start with BCU and Q.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nAnd they're a bit shorter than taproot addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:01\n\nThose, actually, I shouldn't say are perfectly fine because there's another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:07\n\nPotential concern is whenever you go to spend them, anything that goes in the mempool will need to reveal its public key in order to be validated and relayed and packaged up into a block by miners and consensus, past consensus, things like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:24\n\nSo, yeah, so that's kind of the high level there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nOne thing I also want to mention about Shore's algorithm is that it's Not great right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nIn fact, quantum computers aren't really great right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:37\n\nThey're uh like uh at least commercially available ones are um they they're they're s they they're they're kind of at the state of where Vacuum tubes and maybe get moving into transistor like uh the IBM System three hundred sixty of the nineteen fifties or sixties.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:01\n\nLike like that that air that era is might might be the best way to describe where we're at now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:07\n\nUh and so Shore's algorithm is kind of somewhat limited at the moment, but uh it it's possible that at some point It might not be because of just the rate at which technology progresses and all the money and time and energy being put into this problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:25\n\nIn particular, there are a number of companies working on this, in addition to IBM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:33\n\nIntel, Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Regetti, IonQ, SyQuantum, those are all just pouring billions into this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:42\n\nAnd also it's worth mentioning that Raytheon and Honeywell have been working on this problem for like probably over 20 years now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:48\n\nSo we have no idea what they have actually because those aren't necessarily very public about their intentions or their technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nYeah, and this is also like a fraction of the companies that are currently working on this technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:08\n\nThis is not all of them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:09\n\nThere's some omitted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:11\n\nSo that's also a concern because there's lots of companies here on the slide.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:19\n\nNow, coming back to Bitcoin, Peter Willa, in year 2019, said, Has said that he, you know, he sufficiently powerful quantum computers and other elliptic curve discrete log problem breaks, he calls them, are hypothetical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:38\n\nAnd if they do happen, there will likely be a long series of incremental breakthroughs that give us time for more fundamental solutions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:46\n\nAnd what's interesting is that in the six years since this has been posted, there have Been and will continue to be a long series of intervental breakthroughs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:56\n\nAnd eventually, we're going to need to establish fundamental solutions in response to these incremental breakthroughs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:04\n\nSome of these include the Microsoft Myorana 1 announcement.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:09\n\nMany physicists are, I will say, are skeptical of what they've produced, in particular, I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nSabine Hasenfelder has published some very skeptical pieces about it, and maybe rightly so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:25\n\nThat said, I also am s uh uh skeptical that Satya Nadella, maybe I'm wrong, but uh I I don't think he would s lie to his investors or his shareholders, you know?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nSo um I I would imagine that he has like a some fiduciary responsibility not to lie uh about uh all the bullish stuff he said about the development.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:45\n\nAnd so um maybe somewhere th the s the truth lies somewhere in the middle, as he usually does.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:50\n\nBut we'll see about that one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:53\n\nAlso, Google Willow.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:55\n\nBack in December, Google announced a development where they were able to use AI to sort of implement really compact quantum error correction codes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:13\n\nUh and like to the point where like they only needed a 49, like a seven by seven grid of uh of physical qubits to approximate one logical qubit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:24\n\nUh that and a logical qubit being one that is error corrected and noise isolated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:29\n\nUh one of the interesting things about the Majorana 1 uh announcement was that Majorana or what they call topological qubits Are sort of isolated from noise pretty much entirely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:43\n\nAnd so, so long as they're cooled to the correct temperature.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:50\n\nIt's also worth noting that people have thought about sort of a timeline of quantum emergency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:56\n\nRight now, hopefully, we're in the middle of preparation and design and implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:00\n\nThat's kind of where we're at.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:06\n\nThere's also been a proposal that I'll go into in a second.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:09\n\nWe're working on getting consensus for that proposal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:12\n\nThat takes time and energy and work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nAnd in some ways, this talk is a bit of that work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:19\n\nSo hopefully there will be plenty of time between the first two steps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:27\n\nOnce those are done and we have an activation client and we've activated it, that there will be a migration of coins in some way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nThat hopefully we have plenty of time for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:39\n\nSo that might be one reason why to sort of, I hate to use the word rush.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nI don't want to rush this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:45\n\nWe want a very good, reliable, comprehensive solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:49\n\nBut there is a sense of urgency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:52\n\nAnd the sooner the better, because there will be time needed to update all the nodes and applications and wallets and things like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:01\n\nThere's also commercial, there's a CNSA 2.0 timeline.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:08\n\nSo the government is very concerned about this thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:14\n\nIt's very interesting how concerned they are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:17\n\nBecause you just have to wonder, like, why are they so concerned?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nAnd maybe there's, I don't know, it's really hard to say, but it seems like they were concerned about this before we even had good solutions for these things in terms of post-quantum cartography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:34\n\nBut regardless, they have Timelines that go out to 2030, 2033.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:41\n\nIn particular, they hope to sunset entirely RSA and elliptic cryptography by the year 2035.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:50\n\nAs for Bitcoin, what is like one thing you might be thinking is like, how severe is this concern?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:57\n\nWe estimate about four million coins are Vulnerable somewhere between four to six million coins are vulnerable at present on chain against what are called long exposure attacks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nAnd whereas like a short exposure attack, on the other hand, would be just a p a transaction that's been signed and submitted to the mempool without having been mined yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:25\n\nAnd the reason there's a distinction is because the kind of quantum computer that's required to sort of recover the key behind a transaction that's in the mempool within a timely manner is a very much more powerful quantum computer than one that could just take as much time as it needed to look at.\nkeys to coins on chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nThe one thing I'd like to stress is just that this is not something we can fix overnight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:58\n\nIt will take a lot of time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:00\n\nIt'll take a lot of, it's a big, it's kind of an immense coordination problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:06\n\nAnd in some ways, like hopefully we're very early to this problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:12\n\nAnd we're going to have plenty of time to implement satisfactory solutions and answer everybody's questions and implement nodes and wallets and layers, especially fixes to layers, because a lot of them use Taproot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:28\n\nAnybody using Taproot should be very concerned about this issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nSo what do we do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:37\n\nBecause I don't want to just scare you guys and leave you, you know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:42\n\nSo, first of all, let's just give you an idea of how big elliptic curve cartography signatures and public keys are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:52\n\nThey're not big, as you can see: 33 bytes, 70 bytes, 32 bytes, 64 bytes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:58\n\nPost-quantum cartography, on the other hand, it's more like Hundreds or thousands of bytes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:05\n\nIn particular, in BIP360, which implements a it's called pay-to-quantum resistant hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:14\n\nIt specifies three NIST approved algorithms.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nOne we originally came up with, was called Ski Sign.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:25\n\nWe had it also in the spec.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:28\n\nLater, we came to find that ski sign took 15,000 times longer to verify than elliptic curve cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:39\n\nAnd so these other ones, not nearly as bad, but they're still quite large and expensive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:47\n\nSo I was really curious about, like, not just curious, but also like, one of the big problems we have is sort of like how do we have a sort of ergonomic and comprehensive solution for implementing these algorithms, the specific ones that are specified in the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:12\n\nAnd so I made a library called libitcoin bqc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:19\n\nYou can find it on GitHub slash crypto quick slash libitcoin bqc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:25\n\nAnd it implements FN DSA, also known as Falcon, ML DSA, also known as Crystal Zolithium, and SLH DSA, also known as Sphinx.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:37\n\nAnd to give you a better idea of the performance comparison between these, there's like some factors there that you can kind of get a sense for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:49\n\nI'd like to publish these slides.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:51\n\nSo maybe go on a X and maybe Nostra I'll also side note, I'm not a big fan of Nostra just because Every profile is a public key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:06\n\nSo that said, it's very cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:08\n\nAnd I do have an account.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:09\n\nI'm just expecting that one day I might not be able to trust it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nAnd yeah, and so this is a side comparison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:18\n\nThere's also, this is just like an overview of some BIPs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:23\n\nPainted corner resistant hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:25\n\nI'm going to talk about Hourglass in a second.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:27\n\nAnd then we'd also need qubit activation, maybe some for queue derivation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nPSPTs is a very complex and difficult endeavor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:35\n\nArglass, in particular, I want to discuss because it's essentially a consensus-level change that I want to introduce to Bitcoin as a potential solution for the problem of there being 34,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:53\n\nPay to public key coins out there that contain about like 1.6 or 1.8 million Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:03\n\nAnd these are largely considered either lost coins or Satoshi's coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:08\n\nAnd what I would want to do is I wouldn't want to disable all of the P2PK coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:17\n\nThat would be like essentially burning them, which has been proposed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:23\n\nNot a lot of people like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:25\n\nIt's sort of like taking everything over 21 million and being like, oh, actually, no, it's actually everything over 21 million minus some subset of coins, which is just not as doesn't sound as good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:39\n\nAnd Uh instead, just let's just first of all disable making nuance, because that's bad, and then uh spend uh make it so that people can only like anybody who has the key, let's just say that for one of these in for one of these uh coins, they can only include one one P2PK input per block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:02\n\nAnd so instead of having 34,000 transactions, Hit Bitcoin within a span of a few hours, we get to spread that across eight months at a minimum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:16\n\nAnd it cannot be more than one block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:18\n\nWe can't just be like, oh, let's solve this problem for one a day and then we can solve it for 80 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:24\n\nUnfortunately, that messes with the mining incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:29\n\nBut I've discussed this at length with a few miners, and I think that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:33\n\nThis proposal is incentive compatible with miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:36\n\nAnd I'd be very interested in hearing everyone's thoughts on this potential solution to the problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:43\n\nAlso, at Surmount Systems, we worked on something called Satoshi Shield.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:47\n\nWe're looking essentially at how many Bitcoin are there out there that are in P2PK keys, and also how many.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:57\n\nUTXOs are out there that are that have uh pay-to-public key commitments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:03\n\nAnd uh this this graph is unfortunately not complete.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:06\n\nUh I'm still working on building a better indexer for this, but uh uh that's about as far as I've gotten so far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:13\n\nAnd uh once it's done then uh uh hopefully there will be a um uh like uh uh a website people can go to to just kind of monitor You know, how many of these coins are out there so that once they're moved, this graph goes lower, and you kind of get a sense for, oh no, is this actually happening?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nSo, you know, of course, like, we have to, in order for this to work out, we need to convince certain stakeholders, individual node runners, economic node runners, exchanges in particular, have cold wallets that are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:52\n\nReused addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:54\n\nMany of the top addresses are reused in terms of their address balances.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:01\n\nAlso, enthusiasts, investors, influencers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:03\n\nWe have to convince a meaningful, in fact, more like more than meaningful, a vast majority of these people that this is a worthwhile thing for them to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:14\n\nAnd I think, you know, generally speaking, It is just maybe I'm biased, but I just think that even if there's a one in 1000, like a 0.1% chance that quantum computers can't come to break Bitcoin in our lifetimes, yeah, I think it's such a serious problem that we should do our best to try to solve it as soon as we can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:41\n\nIn a way that balances all the considerations and that everybody's happy, and this is an optional, non-coercive sort of approach that's hopefully less controversial and more approachable and practical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:02\n\nSo, yeah, that's Uh that's all I have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:06\n\nUh uh please check out surmount.systems and also uh follow surmount systems on x.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:14\n\nAnd uh that's my nonprofit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:16\n\nAnd uh also Isabel, you want me to say something?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:19\n\nBIP360.org.\nOh yes, of course, BIP360.org.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:23\n\nPlease check out the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:24\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:34\n\nHey, FYI, I think you can find that chart that you want on txtats.com.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:41\n\nIt has the pay-to-pub key chart.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:44\n\nSo there's like 1.72 million Bitcoin across 45.8 thousand UTXOs.\n45.8 thousand?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:53\n\nWow.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:54\n\nSo that's basically a year's worth of UTXOs then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:58\n\nYeah, good point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:59\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:00\n\nI've seen that side.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:01\n\nI just, you know, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:02\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:03\n\nYeah, so question though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:05\n\nI guess one, the first issue that comes to mind with the sort of hourglass gating of spinning stuff is: sure, I guess you can gate it by UTXO, but not by value, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:17\n\nHave you looked at the value distribution across the center?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:20\n\nCorrect.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:20\n\nWell, the average value of these outputs is about 50 Bitcoin because That was the first block award.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:27\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:28\n\nSo I don't know, as far as you're aware, you haven't seen too much else consolidation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:35\n\nI think the one, what is it, the guy whose hard drive is in a landfill, for example, I think that he consolidated about 8,000 coins into one UTXO.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:46\n\nSo there's probably a few of those.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:47\n\nOkay, good to know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:49\n\nThank you a lot, James.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:51\n\nUh what kind of uh number of qu logical qubits uh is it for speeding up the hashing and and that's like a quadratic uh scaling instead of uh like an exponential speed up for correct doing rovers?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:04\n\nYeah, for grovers, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:05\n\nUh and then I guess uh another question too is uh how many like qubits, you know, once we break your use shores to break uh ECC?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:15\n\nHow far does that get to between the short term and like just breaking it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:20\n\nIs that a couple orders of magnitude?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:24\n\nSo the gap between shores and grovers, I'm not quite sure I can answer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:30\n\nBut what I can say about shores in particular is that I think it takes about 2,000 or so at a very minimum.\nlogical qubits, logical qubits, to run shores over ECC.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:48\n\nAnd so 2,000, 3,000, that's going to be a number we're going to wanna watch.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:53\n\nAlso for like physical qubits, uncorrected qubits, we might be more like 100,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:01\n\nAnd so those are both kind of numbers to keep an eye out for in the news, I guess, see how close we're getting to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:08\n\nI actually just want to add to that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:09\n\nSo, Juan Maldasena, the guy who did the ADS CFT paper, he actually wrote a paper on this and he showed that it's about 7,000 logical qubits to simulate a black hole, and that's about the same for actually breaking RSA.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:22\n\nSo, it's like, if you want to, I forget the name of the paper, but it's super interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:25\n\nAnd it was interesting that he wrote that paper because he really knows about all the mathematical details of those types of models.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:33\n\nThat's not my question, though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:36\n\nYeah, it's interesting that it doesn't seem like there's a lot of concern from Bitcoin Core devs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:40\n\nLike, for instance, Taproot was introduced.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:43\n\nThey knew that quantum computers in theory could be coming.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:47\n\nAnd they seem to be pushing a lot of people into Taproot for privacy reasons.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:52\n\nHow do you deal with the fact that it seems like no one really seems to be taking the problem seriously?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:57\n\nWell, one way to deal with that is just to come to places like this and try to explain the problem and advocate for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:04\n\nAnd another way is also just to build the solution and put it out there for people to evaluate upon its merit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:11\n\nAnd hopefully, you know, it is the right thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:15\n\nAlso, one thing I forgot to mention is, and this is a little bit of alpha for this conference, is that I have been joined by a co-author.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:23\n\nEthan Howman, so he will be helping me co-author this paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:28\n\nThis bit, 360.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:30\n\nYes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nSo thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nI just want to add one more, which is it seems like, again, because of no one's taking it as as seriously, they you know, Tapro, one of the big benefits of Taproot was they're trying to get more and more people to use it for the privacy enhancement, because like it it be it kind of all of the um It just becomes a single pub key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:49\n\nYou can't really tell the difference between a script span and a regular key span.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:52\n\nSo, do you see the community like, should we still be pushing it for the privacy reasons, or should we be more cautious?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:58\n\nLike, it seems like you don't get as many of the privacy benefits if we're not really pushing Taproot, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:03\n\nWell, yeah, what's interesting about Taproot in particular is it is so good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:09\n\nLike we have Schnorr signatures and those are so good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:11\n\nLike we don't know how good we have it when we compare it to you know the current state of post-quantum cartography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:17\n\nSignature aggregation or key aggregation, like things like Frost, multi-sigs, Musig2, lightning channels that are indistinguishable from ordinary taproot spends.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:34\n\nThat's just so cool.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:38\n\nIt's just kinda depressing because, you know, man, they are so vulnerable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:45\n\nAnd anyway, anybody who's really into Taproot should be definitely taking a look at this BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:52\n\nI want to maintain Taproot compatibility, just add on post-Chron cryptography as kind of like additional locks.\nfor those transactions that you can commit to in an address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:03\n\nAnd so in BIP360, we basically allow you to generate multiple public keys and multiple against these multiple signature algorithms and combine them and use them to secure your coins in a way that's hopefully somewhat compact and efficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:22\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:23\n\nThat's all the time we have for Hunter, so thank you.	Speaker 0: 00:00:00\n\nThanks everyone for coming.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:02\n\nSo first I just want to say that I'm doing my best as anyone can to have the intellectual honesty and also the curiousness that this subject deserves.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:17\n\nIt's a very complex subject.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:18\n\nIt's also very provocative and Uh I just hope that people approach this with an open mind and uh in particular um just uh uh I appreciate uh some of the thought and research that goes into this that went into this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:40\n\nSo I want to talk about Bitcoin quantum security.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:43\n\nUm first I'm gonna start off with what is a quantum computer 'cause you know it's it's it's not Like, very obvious in some ways, like what they are when we talk about these things.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:55\n\nWhat's pictured here is a superconducting quantum computer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:00:58\n\nSo it has a chip at the bottom that gets cooled to sort of like very close to zero Kelvin, so absolute zero, and uses helium, liquid nitrogen to cool itself.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:17\n\nWith two coolants.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:18\n\nAdditionally, it gets covered with this doer that then is called a dilution refrigerator.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:31\n\nAnd because this thing gets so cold, they're actually like little loops in the wires that help with the Like w when metals uh contract.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:43\n\nUm so yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:45\n\nUh what does a quantum computer do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:48\n\nSo quantum computers have what are called fundamental units of quantum computing called qubits.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:01:54\n\nAnd they uh the the they're they're they're uh they're essentially when you're able when you entangle multiple qubits together, you're able to essentially model Uh all the states of a problem in a mathematical construct called Hilbert space.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:15\n\nAnd you can actually uh then connect these in circuits with gates that can perform various quantum computations uh and uh logical operations like controlled not gates or Hadamard gates, different kind of forms of quantum computation that explore this problem space in a probabilistic manner.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:45\n\nBut eventually it'll get more deterministic, actually, as they get better.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:50\n\nThe picture here is the IBM Quantum System 2.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:53\n\nThis is a commercially available piece of hardware.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:02:56\n\nIt's anyone's guess if it's any good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:01\n\nIt can do at least 100 qubits, maybe even closer to 1,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:07\n\nBut these are physical qubits, and I'll get into that in a minute.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:10\n\nBut that said, it's kind of pretty to look at, and also somewhat imposing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:21\n\nUh how does quantum computing r pose a risk to Bitcoin?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:25\n\nUh well so essentially uh the major thing to consider is that uh there's uh there there's public private key cryptography used in Bitcoin, also known as asymmetric cryptography and uh this uh depends on A property called the elliptic curve discrete log problem, or ECDLP, which is something that Neha mentioned earlier and in an earlier talk to today.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:03:56\n\nAnd there's kind of like this assumption that this is going to be secure.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:01\n\nThere's also a lot of assumptions that go into all the things that go into making this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:10\n\nConcept of quantum computing a thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:13\n\nSo plenty of assumptions to go around, I suppose.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:19\n\nOne thing I want to address first off is that, yes, while technically quantum computing can affect mining through what's called Grover's algorithm, Grover's algorithm doesn't scale as well as this other algorithm I'm going to talk about.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:33\n\nSo it's just not.\nas a big a concern at the moment.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:40\n\nOne of the bigger concerns is how trans how tr signatures work, in particular transaction signatures.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:04:47\n\nSo when you go and receive some coins to an address and then you want to spend them later, you have to sign the transaction and also reveal your public key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:00\n\nAnd there's an algorithm called Shors that we've known since 1995.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:06\n\nAnd well, it was originally explained in 1995.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:10\n\nThe thing that they discovered in 1995, Peter Shor explained, was that this, it was one of the first instances that had been theorized in that a quantum computer can demonstrate.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:25\n\nWhat's called quantum advantage now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:30\n\nAnd also later, we discovered, or maybe around the 2000, 2001, it was explained to the cryptography community, or people just kind of found out or considered that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:45\n\nElliptic curve cryptography, which was a like burgeoning field at the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:05:50\n\nThe SEC P256K1 paper, for example, was written in 2000, or the specification was published in 2000.\n\nAnd that's the one that's used by Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:03\n\nAround 2000, 2001, it was also revealed that, or considered, that Quantum computers could pose a threat to elliptic curve cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:14\n\nAnd so essentially, elliptic curve cryptography being the thing that gives us the concept of public keys, well, it makes this so that, in particular, whenever you expose your public keys, that makes coins vulnerable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:31\n\nSo, when are public keys exposed?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:33\n\nWell, we have four cases.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:35\n\nThe first one is, Uh early addresses like the paid to public key coins, uh they start with zero four or technically also zero two or zero three.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:43\n\nUm they these are like hex encoded uh public keys that are just sitting on chain uh for anyone to see.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:54\n\nThese also are known as Satoshi's coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:06:56\n\nThey're also just any CPU miner operating at the time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:01\n\nAnybody who hasn't spent their coins yet from those addresses, well, please do so now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:10\n\nReused addresses are also a concern.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:14\n\nSo pretty much any type except for The one I'm I'm gonna talk about soon actually, um is uh that when uh when when you publish your transaction, the public key goes on chain uh along with your signature\n\nSpeaker 1: 00:07:30\n\nand that can be recovered and uh um\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:34\n\nuh go into that uh Shor's algorithm there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:38\n\nTapered addresses are also an entire public key, just encoded.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:43\n\nIn an address form.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:45\n\nIt's not a hash of an address or a hash of a pub key like many other address types are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:51\n\nIn particular, pay-to-witness public key hash is perfectly fine.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:56\n\nAnd those start with BCU and Q.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:07:59\n\nAnd they're a bit shorter than taproot addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:01\n\nThose, actually, I shouldn't say are perfectly fine because there's another.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:07\n\nPotential concern is whenever you go to spend them, anything that goes in the mempool will need to reveal its public key in order to be validated and relayed and packaged up into a block by miners and consensus, past consensus, things like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:24\n\nSo, yeah, so that's kind of the high level there.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:29\n\nOne thing I also want to mention about Shor's algorithm is that it's Not great right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:34\n\nIn fact, quantum computers aren't really great right now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:08:37\n\nThey're uh like uh at least commercially available ones are um they they're they're s they they're they're kind of at the state of where vacuum tubes and maybe moving into transistor like uh the IBM System three hundred sixty of the nineteen fifties or sixties.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:01\n\nLike like that that air that era is might might be the best way to describe where we're at now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:07\n\nUh and so Shor's algorithm is kind of somewhat limited at the moment, but uh it it's possible that at some point It might not be because of just the rate at which technology progresses and all the money and time and energy being put into this problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:25\n\nIn particular, there are a number of companies working on this, in addition to IBM.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:33\n\nIntel, Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Rigetti, IonQ, Silica Quantum, those are all just pouring billions into this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:42\n\nAnd also it's worth mentioning that Raytheon and Honeywell have been working on this problem for like probably over 20 years now.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:09:48\n\nSo we have no idea what they have actually because those aren't necessarily very public about their intentions or their technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:01\n\nYeah, and this is also like a fraction of the companies that are currently working on this technology.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:08\n\nThis is not all of them.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:09\n\nThere's some omitted.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:11\n\nSo that's also a concern because there's lots of companies here on the slide.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:19\n\nNow, coming back to Bitcoin, Peter Wladis, in year 2019, said, Has said that he sufficiently powerful quantum computers and other elliptic curve discrete log problem breaks, he calls them, are hypothetical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:38\n\nAnd if they do happen, there will likely be a long series of incremental breakthroughs that give us time for more fundamental solutions.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:46\n\nAnd what's interesting is that in the six years since this has been posted, there have been and will continue to be a long series of incremental breakthroughs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:10:56\n\nAnd eventually, we're going to need to establish fundamental solutions in response to these incremental breakthroughs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:04\n\nSome of these include the Microsoft Mycorana 1 announcement.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:09\n\nMany physicists are, I will say, are skeptical of what they've produced, in particular, I think.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:17\n\nSabine Hassenfelder has published some very skeptical pieces about it, and maybe rightly so.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:25\n\nThat said, I also am sort of skeptical that Satya Nadella, maybe I'm wrong, but uh I I don't think he would lie to his investors or his shareholders, you know?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:36\n\nSo um I I would imagine that he has like a some fiduciary responsibility not to lie uh about uh all the bullish stuff he said about Bitcoin development.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:45\n\nAnd so um maybe somewhere there the truth lies somewhere in the middle, as he usually does.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:50\n\nBut we'll see about that one.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:53\n\nAlso, Google Willow.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:11:55\n\nBack in December, Google announced a development where they were able to use AI to sort of implement really compact quantum error correction codes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:13\n\nUh and like to the point where like they only needed a 49, like a seven by seven grid of uh physical qubits to approximate one logical qubit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:24\n\nUh that and a logical qubit being one that is error corrected and noise isolated.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:29\n\nUh one of the interesting things about the Majorana 1 uh announcement was that Majorana or what they call topological qubits are sort of isolated from noise pretty much entirely.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:43\n\nAnd so, so long as they're cooled to the correct temperature.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:50\n\nIt's also worth noting that people have thought about sort of a timeline of quantum emergency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:12:56\n\nRight now, hopefully, we're in the middle of preparation and design and implementation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:00\n\nThat's kind of where we're at.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:06\n\nThere's also been a proposal that I'll go into in a second.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:09\n\nWe're working on getting consensus for that proposal.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:12\n\nThat takes time and energy and work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:15\n\nAnd in some ways, this talk is a bit of that work.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:19\n\nSo hopefully there will be plenty of time between the first two steps.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:27\n\nOnce those are done and we have an activation client and we've activated it, that there will be a migration of coins in some way.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:36\n\nThat hopefully we have plenty of time for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:39\n\nSo that might be one reason why to sort of, I hate to use the word rush.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:43\n\nI don't want to rush this.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:45\n\nWe want a very good, reliable, comprehensive solution.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:49\n\nBut there is a sense of urgency.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:13:52\n\nAnd the sooner the better, because there will be time needed to update all the nodes and applications and wallets and things like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:01\n\nThere's also commercial, there's a CNSA 2.0 timeline.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:08\n\nSo the government is very concerned about this thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:14\n\nIt's very interesting how concerned they are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:17\n\nBecause you just have to wonder, like, why are they so concerned?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:21\n\nAnd maybe there's, I don't know, it's really hard to say, but it seems like they were concerned about this before we even had good solutions for these things in terms of post-quantum cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:34\n\nBut regardless, they have Timelines that go out to 2030, 2033.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:41\n\nIn particular, they hope to sunset entirely RSA and elliptic cryptography by the year 2035.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:50\n\nAs for Bitcoin, what is like one thing you might be thinking is like, how severe is this concern?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:14:57\n\nWe estimate about four million coins are vulnerable somewhere between four to six million coins are vulnerable at present on chain against what are called long exposure attacks.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:11\n\nAnd whereas like a short exposure attack, on the other hand, would be just a P2P transaction that's been signed and submitted to the mempool without having been mined yet.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:25\n\nAnd the reason there's a distinction is because the kind of quantum computer that's required to sort of recover the key behind a transaction that's in the mempool within a timely manner is a very much more powerful quantum computer than one that could just take as much time as it needed to look at keys to coins on chain.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:53\n\nThe one thing I'd like to stress is just that this is not something we can fix overnight.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:15:58\n\nIt will take a lot of time.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:00\n\nIt'll take a lot of, it's a big, it's kind of an immense coordination problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:06\n\nAnd in some ways, like hopefully we're very early to this problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:12\n\nAnd we're going to have plenty of time to implement satisfactory solutions and answer everybody's questions and implement nodes and wallets and layers, especially fixes to layers, because a lot of them use Taproot.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:28\n\nAnybody using Taproot should be very concerned about this issue.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:35\n\nSo what do we do?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:37\n\nBecause I don't want to just scare you guys and leave you, you know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:42\n\nSo, first of all, let's just give you an idea of how big elliptic curve cryptographic signatures and public keys are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:52\n\nThey're not big, as you can see: 33 bytes, 70 bytes, 32 bytes, 64 bytes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:16:58\n\nPost-quantum cryptography, on the other hand, it's more like Hundreds or thousands of bytes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:05\n\nIn particular, in BIP326, which implements what it's called pay-to-PoQ resistant hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:14\n\nIt specifies three NIST approved algorithms.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:20\n\nOne we originally came up with was called SPHINCS+.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:25\n\nWe had it also in the spec.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:28\n\nLater, we came to find that SPHINCS+ took 15,000 times longer to verify than elliptic curve cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:39\n\nAnd so these other ones, not nearly as bad, but they're still quite large and expensive.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:17:47\n\nSo I was really curious about, like, not just curious, but also like, one of the big problems we have is sort of like how do we have a sort of ergonomic and comprehensive solution for implementing these algorithms, the specific ones that are specified in the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:12\n\nAnd so I made a library called libbitcoin_bqc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:19\n\nYou can find it on GitHub slash bitcoin-core slash libbitcoin_bqc.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:25\n\nAnd it implements DSA, also known as Falcon, ML-DSA, also known as Kyber (though typically referred to as Kyber or Classic McEliece in that context), and SLH-DSA, also known as SPHINCS+.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:37\n\nAnd to give you a better idea of the performance comparison between these, there's like some factors there that you can kind of get a sense for.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:49\n\nI'd like to publish these slides.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:18:51\n\nSo maybe go on X and maybe Nostr... I'll also side note, I'm not a big fan of Nostr just because every profile is a public key.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:06\n\nSo that said, it's very cool.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:08\n\nAnd I do have an account.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:09\n\nI'm just expecting that one day I might not be able to trust it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:14\n\nAnd yeah, and so this is a side comparison.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:18\n\nThere's also, this is just like an overview of some BIPs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:23\n\nPay-to-public-key resistant hash.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:25\n\nI'm going to talk about Taproot in a second.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:27\n\nAnd then we'd also need UTXO activation, maybe some softfork derivation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:31\n\nP2PKs is a very complex and difficult endeavor.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:35\n\nTaproot, in particular, I want to discuss because it's essentially a consensus-level change that I want to introduce to Bitcoin as a potential solution for the problem of there being 34,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:19:53\n\nPay-to-public-key coins out there that contain about like 1.6 or 1.8 million Bitcoin.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:03\n\nAnd these are largely considered either lost coins or Satoshi's coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:08\n\nAnd what I would want to do is I wouldn't want to disable all of the P2PK coins.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:17\n\nThat would be like essentially burning them, which has been proposed.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:23\n\nNot a lot of people like that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:25\n\nIt's sort of like taking everything over 21 million and being like, oh, actually, no, it's actually everything over 21 million minus some subset of coins, which is just not as... doesn't sound as good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:20:39\n\nAnd uh, instead, just let's just first of all disable making new ones, because that's bad, and then uh spend... make it so that people can only like anybody who has the key, let's just say that for one of these, for one of these uh coins, they can only include one P2PK input per block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:02\n\nAnd so instead of having 34,000 transactions hitting Bitcoin within a span of a few hours, we get to spread that across eight months at a minimum.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:16\n\nAnd it cannot be more than one block.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:18\n\nWe can't just be like, oh, let's solve this problem for one a day and then we can solve it for 80 years.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:24\n\nUnfortunately, that messes with the mining incentives.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:29\n\nBut I've discussed this at length with a few miners, and I think that...\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:33\n\nThis proposal is incentive compatible with miners.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:36\n\nAnd I'd be very interested in hearing everyone's thoughts on this potential solution to the problem.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:43\n\nAlso, at Surmount Systems, we worked on something called Satoshi Shield.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:47\n\nWe're looking essentially at how many Bitcoin are there out there that are in P2PK keys, and also how many...\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:21:57\n\nUTXOs are out there that have pay-to-public-key commitments.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:03\n\nAnd uh, this graph is unfortunately not complete.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:06\n\nUh I'm still working on building a better indexer for this, but uh uh that's about as far as I've gotten so far.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:13\n\nAnd uh once it's done then uh uh hopefully there will be a um uh like uh uh a website people can go to to just kind of monitor You know, how many of these coins are out there so that once they're moved, this graph goes lower, and you kind of get a sense for, oh no, is this actually happening?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:39\n\nSo, you know, of course, like, we have to, in order for this to work out, we need to convince certain stakeholders, individual node runners, economic node runners, exchanges in particular, have cold wallets that are.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:52\n\nReused addresses.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:22:54\n\nMany of the top addresses are reused in terms of their address balances.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:01\n\nAlso, enthusiasts, investors, influencers.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:03\n\nWe have to convince a meaningful, in fact, more like more than meaningful, a vast majority of these people that this is a worthwhile thing for them to do.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:14\n\nAnd I think, you know, generally speaking, It is just maybe I'm biased, but I just think that even if there's a one in 1000, like a 0.1% chance that quantum computers can't come to break Bitcoin in our lifetimes, yeah, I think it's such a serious problem that we should do our best to try to solve it as soon as we can.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:23:41\n\nIn a way that balances all the considerations and that everybody's happy, and this is an optional, non-coercive sort of approach that's hopefully less controversial and more approachable and practical.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:02\n\nSo, yeah, that's Uh that's all I have.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:06\n\nUh uh please check out surmount.systems and also uh follow surmount systems on x.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:14\n\nAnd uh that's my nonprofit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:16\n\nAnd uh also Isabel, you want me to say something?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:19\n\nBIP360.org.\nOh yes, of course, BIP-360.org.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:23\n\nPlease check out the BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:24\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:34\n\nHey, FYI, I think you can find that chart that you want on txtats.com.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:41\n\nIt has the pay-to-pub key chart.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:44\n\nSo there's like 1.72 million Bitcoin across 45.8 thousand UTXOs.\n45.8 thousand?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:53\n\nWow.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:54\n\nSo that's basically a year's worth of UTXOs then.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:58\n\nYeah, good point.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:24:59\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:00\n\nI've seen that side.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:01\n\nI just, you know, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:02\n\nThank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:03\n\nYeah, so question though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:05\n\nI guess one, the first issue that comes to mind with the sort of hourglass gating of spinning stuff is: sure, I guess you can gate it by UTXO, but not by value, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:17\n\nHave you looked at the value distribution across the center?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:20\n\nCorrect.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:20\n\nWell, the average value of these outputs is about 50 Bitcoin because That was the first block award.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:27\n\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:28\n\nSo I don't know, as far as you're aware, you haven't seen too much else consolidation.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:35\n\nI think the one, what is it, the guy whose hard drive is in a landfill, for example, I think that he consolidated about 8,000 coins into one UTXO.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:46\n\nSo there's probably a few of those.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:47\n\nOkay, good to know.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:49\n\nThank you a lot, James.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:25:51\n\nUh what kind of uh number of logical qubits is it for speeding up the hashing and and that's like a quadratic uh scaling instead of uh like an exponential speed up for correct doing Grover's?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:04\n\nYeah, for Grover's, yeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:05\n\nUh and then I guess uh another question too is uh how many like qubits, you know, once we break Shor's to break ECC?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:15\n\nHow far does that get to between the short term and like just breaking it?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:20\n\nIs that a couple orders of magnitude?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:24\n\nSo the gap between Shor's and Grover's, I'm not quite sure I can answer.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:30\n\nBut what I can say about Shor's in particular is that I think it takes about 2,000 or so at a very minimum.\nlogical qubits, logical qubits, to run Shor's over ECC.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:48\n\nAnd so 2,000, 3,000, that's going to be a number we're going to wanna watch.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:26:53\n\nAlso for like physical qubits, uncorrected qubits, we might be more like 100,000.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:01\n\nAnd so those are both kind of numbers to keep an eye out for in the news, I guess, see how close we're getting to.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:08\n\nI actually just want to add to that.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:09\n\nSo, Juan Maldesana, the guy who did the ADS CFT paper, he actually wrote a paper on this and he showed that it's about 7,000 logical qubits to simulate a black hole, and that's about the same for actually breaking RSA.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:22\n\nSo, it's like, if you want to, I forget the name of the paper, but it's super interesting.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:25\n\nAnd it was interesting that he wrote that paper because he really knows about all the mathematical details of those types of models.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:33\n\nThat's not my question, though.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:36\n\nYeah, it's interesting that it doesn't seem like there's a lot of concern from Bitcoin Core devs.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:40\n\nLike, for instance, Taproot was introduced.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:43\n\nThey knew that quantum computers in theory could be coming.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:47\n\nAnd they seem to be pushing a lot of people into Taproot for privacy reasons.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:52\n\nHow do you deal with the fact that it seems like no one really seems to be taking the problem seriously?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:27:57\n\nWell, one way to deal with that is just to come to places like this and try to explain the problem and advocate for it.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:04\n\nAnd another way is also just to build the solution and put it out there for people to evaluate upon its merit.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:11\n\nAnd hopefully, you know, it is the right thing.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:15\n\nAlso, one thing I forgot to mention is, and this is a little bit of alpha for this conference, is that I have been joined by a co-author.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:23\n\nEthan Heilman, so he will be helping me co-author this paper.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:28\n\nThis bit, BIP-360.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:30\n\nYes.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nSo thank you.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:31\n\nI just want to add one more, which is it seems like, again, because no one's taking it as seriously, they, you know, Taproot, one of the big benefits of Taproot was they're trying to get more and more people to use it for the privacy enhancement, because like it makes all of the public keys look the same.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:49\n\nYou can't really tell the difference between a script pubkey and a regular key pubkey.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:52\n\nSo, do you see the community like, should we still be pushing it for the privacy reasons, or should we be more cautious?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:28:58\n\nLike, it seems like you don't get as many of the privacy benefits if we're not really pushing Taproot, right?\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:03\n\nWell, yeah, what's interesting about Taproot in particular is it is so good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:09\n\nLike we have Schnorr signatures and those are so good.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:11\n\nLike we don't know how good we have it when we compare it to you know the current state of post-quantum cryptography.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:17\n\nSignature aggregation or key aggregation, like things like Frost, multi-sigs, MuSig2, lightning channels that are indistinguishable from ordinary Taproot spends.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:34\n\nThat's just so cool.\nYeah.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:38\n\nIt's just kinda depressing because, you know, man, they are so vulnerable.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:45\n\nAnd anyway, anybody who's really into Taproot should be definitely taking a look at this BIP.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:29:52\n\nI want to maintain Taproot compatibility, just add on post-quantum cryptography as kind of like additional locks.\nfor those transactions that you can commit to in an address.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:03\n\nAnd so in BIP360, we basically allow you to generate multiple public keys and multiple against these multiple signature algorithms and combine them and use them to secure your coins in a way that's hopefully somewhat compact and efficient.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:22\n\nAwesome.\n\nSpeaker 0: 00:30:23\n\nThat's all the time we have for Hunter, so thank you.	Speaker 0 presents a talk on Bitcoin quantum security, addressing the risks posed by quantum computers to the network's cryptographic foundations. The presentation explains that while early "Satoshi" coins stored in pay-to-public-key (P2PK) addresses and reused or taproot addresses are vulnerable, modern pay-to-witness-pubkey-hash (Bech32) addresses are currently safe, provided their keys are not revealed during spending. The core threat is Shor's algorithm, which can solve the Elliptic Curve Discrete Log Problem (ECDLP) used by Bitcoin to secure signatures, allowing an attacker to steal funds once a public key is exposed on-chain or in a transaction.\n\nThe speaker notes that while current quantum computers are primitive, major tech companies and governments are investing heavily in quantum development with timelines targeting the 2030s to transition away from classical cryptography (NIST CNSA 2.0). This creates urgency for Bitcoin, as there are an estimated 4 million vulnerable coins exposed to "long exposure" attacks or mempool transactions susceptible to "short exposure" attacks. A potential emergency scenario involves a race condition where a quantum computer attempts to spend UTXOs before they are mined into a block.\n\nTo mitigate this without burning vulnerable funds or requiring immediate, drastic consensus changes that could disrupt the ecosystem, Speaker 0 proposes BIP-360 (Pay-to-Public-Key Resistant Hash). This proposal implements post-quantum cryptography (PQC) via "hourglass gating," allowing new transactions to use PQC signatures alongside standard ECDSA to secure existing legacy coins gradually. The solution aims to maintain Taproot compatibility and privacy while providing a non-coercive path for wallets, nodes, and miners to upgrade over time. The speaker highlights the significant computational cost of current post-quantum algorithms compared to standard signatures and shares data on the scale of vulnerable UTXOs. Q&A follows, clarifying the qubit counts required for quantum attacks (approx. 2,000 logical qubits) and discussing the trade-offs between privacy incentives driving Taproot adoption versus the necessity of implementing post-quantum safeguards.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_TsVRZeyiAY	completed	MIT Bitcoin Expo 2025	{"Quantum Security",Cryptography,"Quantum Computing"}	Bitcoin Magazine	2026-04-21 17:10:17.689831+05:30	2026-04-21 22:45:27.632114+05:30
\.


